        "Actual native basin auto_solve_conjugate_gradient instantiated with an owned native tape cost and exact reverse gradient. Inputs and parameters retain independent reference domains; target_cost is normalized objective target,not a gradient tolerance. Oracle budget covers both cost and gradient calls. PSO uses initial/upper as bounds and the native randomness semantics. Other methods ignore upper. Generic auto_solve explicitly instantiates the native steepest-descent solver. Minimises `problem` with nonlinear conjugate gradients. # Errors Propagates [`minimize`]'s errors.",
        "Actual native basin auto_solve instantiated with an owned native tape cost and exact reverse gradient. Inputs and parameters retain independent reference domains; target_cost is normalized objective target,not a gradient tolerance. Oracle budget covers both cost and gradient calls. PSO uses initial/upper as bounds and the native randomness semantics. Other methods ignore upper. Generic auto_solve explicitly instantiates the native steepest-descent solver. Runs any [`SolverKind`] on `problem`. # Errors Propagates [`minimize`]'s errors.",
        "Actual native band LU; exact triplet bounds and band widths are checked before allocation; singularity remains None. Factors the matrix given by triplets `(row, col, value)` (entries outside the band are an error). Returns `None` when singular or when an entry lies outside the band.",
        "Actual native band-factor solve in normalized coordinates. Solves `A x = b`.",
        "Native complex mobius_apply; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The image of `z` (infinite at the pole `-d / c`).",
        "Native complex mobius_compose; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `self \u2218 other`.",
        "Native complex mobius_identity; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The identity map.",
        "Native complex mobius_inverse; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The inverse map (up to the projective scale).",
        "Native complex mobius_new; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `(a z + b) / (c z + d)`.",
        "Native complex_scalar arg; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The argument, `arg z`, in `(-\u03c0, \u03c0]`.",
        "Native complex_scalar complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `re + im i`.",
        "Native complex_scalar cos; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `cos z`.",
        "Native complex_scalar magnitude; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The modulus, `|z|`.",
        "Native complex_scalar method_abs; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The modulus.",
        "Native complex_scalar method_arg; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The argument, in `(-\u03c0, \u03c0]`.",
        "Native complex_scalar method_conj; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The conjugate.",
        "Native complex_scalar method_div; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `self / other`. Finite inputs with a nonzero divisor use binary scaling when their components are large or small enough to lose intermediate products. The zero divisor and nonfinite arguments follow the direct IEEE componentwise formula.",
        "Native complex_scalar method_exp; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `exp(self)`.",
        "Native complex_scalar method_ln; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `ln(self)`, the principal branch.",
        "Native complex_scalar method_mul; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `self * other`.",
        "Native complex_scalar method_new; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. A complex number.",
        "Computes the complex reciprocal 1/z with a scaled evaluation when intermediate products leave the ordinary floating-point range. Returns the inverse physical dimension of the input. Zero or a nonfinite result produces a domain error. The reciprocal `1 / self`, with the scaling and special points of [`Self::div`].",
        "Returns the principal complex square root, with the imaginary sign selecting the side of the negative real axis. Rescaling protects finite large inputs and the quotient preserves representable small components. The result carries the square root of the input dimension. The principal square root, with a nonnegative real part for finite inputs. The sign of the imaginary part selects the side of the negative real axis, including when it is signed zero. Binary scaling avoids an intermediate overflow when a finite input's modulus exceeds `f64::MAX`. Nonfinite inputs follow the componentwise IEEE midpoint formula.",
        "Native complex_scalar norm_squared; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `z * conj(z)` is real; this returns that real part, `|z|^2`.",
        "Native complex_scalar pow; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `z^n` for an integer exponent, by repeated squaring.",
        "Native complex_scalar powc; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `z^w = exp(w ln z)`.",
        "Native complex_scalar sin; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `sin z`.",
        "Native convergence levin_condition_number; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Condition number `kappa = sum_j |w_j|` at the highest order the term count supports. [`LevinVariant::V`] consumes one extra look-ahead term. The transform is a weighted mean of the partial sums, `L = sum_j w_j s_j` with `sum_j w_j = 1`. Those weights are the solution of the Vandermonde-type system in the nodes `beta + n + j` that the Fessler-Ford-Smith recurrence inverts implicitly, so `kappa` \u2014 the 1-norm of that solution \u2014 is the factor by which rounding in `s_j` is amplified into the estimate, and a lower bound for the 1-norm condition number of the system. The accuracy floor of the transform is `kappa * eps`. `kappa` depends on the series, which is why `terms` is an argument: alternating terms give a convex combination with `kappa = 1`, while positive logarithmically convergent terms give weights of alternating sign whose absolute sum grows by a factor `~3.5` per order (for `1/(n+1)^2`: `4.6e5` at order 11, `2.3e8` at order 16). Returns infinity for a nonpositive or non-finite `beta`, an invalid remainder in the required prefix, or a singular or unrepresentable condition number. Fewer than two terms return `1.0` by convention.",
        "Returns one dimensionless Levin condition number per available order, from order zero. Infinite values indicate an undefined or unbounded condition number. Measured terms may share any dimension, beta is dimensionless, and the input budget is 4096 terms. Estimates the Levin weight condition number for each order starting at zero. Each entry is the absolute sum of the normalized weights for its order. This measures sensitivity to the partial sums with fixed remainder estimates. A singular or unrepresentable order contributes infinity at its original position. The profile ends at the first invalid remainder estimate. [`LevinVariant::V`] uses one additional term beyond each order's prefix. A nonpositive or non-finite `beta` gives an empty profile. The recurrence uses quadratic time in the valid prefix length and linear workspace in the number of terms. ``` use rssn::kernels::convergence::LevinVariant; use rssn::kernels::convergence::levin_condition_numbers; let profile = levin_condition_numbers(&[1.0, -0.5, 0.25], LevinVariant::T, 1.0); assert_eq!(profile.len(), 3); assert!(profile.iter().all(|kappa| (kappa - 1.0).abs() < 1e-14)); ```",
        "Native convergence levin_sum_lazy; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Sums a series whose terms arrive lazily, one at a time. Pulls at most `max_terms` items from `terms` \u2014 an unbounded generator is fine \u2014 and transforms the terms directly with [`levin_transform_terms`], so the acceleration never depends on differencing rounded partial sums. `beta` is the shift of the remainder estimate (`1.0` is usual). Returns `(estimate, error_estimate)`, chosen by the same smallest-successive-difference rule as [`levin_sum`]. # Errors Returns an error if `max_terms` is below four, if the iterator yields fewer than four terms, if a term is not finite, or if no finite transform can be formed.",
        "Native convergence levin_transform; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Levin-type sequence transformation of the partial sums `s_0, s_1, ...`. With the term `a_n = s_n - s_{n-1}` (`a_0 = s_0`) and the remainder estimate `omega_n` of the chosen [`LevinVariant`], the transform `L_k^{(0)}` is computed with the stable Fessler-Ford-Smith recurrence for numerator and denominator, `L_k^{(n)} = N_k^{(n)} / D_k^{(n)}` with `N_{k+1}^{(n)} = N_k^{(n+1)} - (beta+n)(beta+n+k)^{k-1} / (beta+n+k+1)^k N_k^{(n)}`. Order zero uses the first partial sum and later orders use more terms. `beta` is the shift (`1.0` is the usual choice). The result lists finite estimates in order and omits any non-finite estimates. The terms are recovered by differencing, which cancels the leading digits that consecutive partial sums share; when the terms are available directly, [`levin_transform_terms`] avoids that loss.",
        "Native convergence levin_transform_terms; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Levin-type transformation of the terms `a_0, a_1, ...` themselves. Same transform as [`levin_transform`], but the terms are used as given instead of being recovered from rounded partial sums by `a_n = s_n - s_{n-1}`. That difference throws away the leading digits `s_n` and `s_{n-1}` share \u2014 most of them late in a slowly converging series \u2014 so this entry point keeps the full precision of the terms. The partial sums are accumulated in the order the terms are given. This is the entry point for a series whose terms are known in closed form or produced one at a time (see [`levin_sum_lazy`]).",
        "Converts the fixed shape [x, y, z] to [radius, polar angle from +z, azimuth from +x]. Express all lengths in one reference and use radians for both angles. The polar angle is zero at the origin. Converts Cartesian `[x, y, z]` to spherical `[r, theta, phi]`. `theta` is measured from the positive z axis in `[0, \u03c0]` and `phi` is the azimuth from the positive x axis in `[-\u03c0, \u03c0]` (both in radians). With horizontal length `h = hypot(x, y)`, the polar angle is `atan2(h, z)`. It is zero at the origin. Finite coordinates can produce an infinite radius if the norm overflows, with both angles still defined. An infinite z component produces a NaN polar angle.",
        "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Converts a Cartesian point to the system `to`. # Errors Returns an error if `point` has the wrong number of components (2 for polar, 3 for cylindrical and spherical).",
        "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Converts a Cartesian point to the system `to`, without checking its length: missing components are taken as zero. The polar angle of the origin in spherical coordinates is 0.",
        "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Determinant of the Jacobian of the map `from -> to` at `at_point`. This is the local volume-element factor `dV_to = |det J| dV_from` of the two coordinate charts: for example `r` for polar-to-Cartesian and `r^2 sin(theta)` for spherical-to-Cartesian, while the inverse maps carry the reciprocal determinant. It is built from the central-difference Jacobian of [`numerical_jacobian`] and inherits its accuracy and precision caveat (no analytic Jacobian). # Errors As [`numerical_jacobian`].",
        "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Jacobian `J[i][j] = d(to_i) / d(from_j)` of the map `from -> to` at `at_point`, by central differences. # Errors Returns an error if the point is invalid for the transformation, see [`transform_point`].",
        "Converts [radius, polar angle from +z, azimuth from +x] to [x, y, z]. The incoming angles are radians and the result uses the same length reference as the radius. A negative radius reflects the point through the origin. Converts spherical `[r, theta, phi]` in radians to Cartesian `[x, y, z]`. Uses the angles in [`cartesian_to_spherical`]. A negative radius reflects the Cartesian point through the origin. See [DLMF 1.5.16] for the map. [DLMF 1.5.16]: https://dlmf.nist.gov/1.5.E16",
        "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Inverse map to Cartesian: converts a point of `from` to Cartesian. # Errors Returns an error if `point` has the wrong number of components.",
        "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Converts a point of `from` to Cartesian coordinates, without checking its length: missing components are taken as zero.",
        "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Transforms `point` from the system `from` to the system `to`, going through Cartesian coordinates, with validation of the dimensions. # Errors Returns an error if `point` has the wrong number of components for `from`, or if the dimension of `from` and `to` differ (2D polar against 3D systems).",
        "Normalized coordinate conversion: radial/Cartesian coordinates are expressed in one chosen positive reference length, angles in radians. Result coordinates and Jacobians use the same normalization; mixed physical units are not erased silently. Like [`transform_point`] but without validation: a direct, allocation light evaluation for hot loops and for differentiating the map. Missing components are taken as zero.",
        "Native Genz-Malik cubature: physical same-unit coordinates, independently measured output, explicit evaluation limit and volume-dimensioned absolute tolerance. Adaptive Genz-Malik cubature over the box `[lo, hi]`. Each region is integrated with the degree-7 Genz-Malik rule (`1 + 4n + 2n(n-1) + 2^n` points) whose embedded degree-5 rule gives the error estimate; the region with the largest error is bisected along the axis with the largest fourth divided difference. The box is first split uniformly into a few sub-boxes so that narrow peaks are not missed. Stops when the total error is below `max(abs_tol, rel_tol * |value|)` or after `max_evals` integrand evaluations (`converged == false`). Intended for 2 to about 8 dimensions. A bisection only replaces a region by its two halves, so the regions always tile the box exactly: their volumes sum to the box volume and no two overlap. `value` and `error` are the sums of the region values and of the region estimates; the estimate is the embedded degree-5/7 difference, a heuristic rather than a rigorous bound (see the module limitations).",
        "Native serial nested cubature; absolute tolerance has output times coordinate-volume dimension. Nested adaptive Gauss-Kronrod cubature over the box `[lo, hi]` (practical up to about four dimensions).",
        "True native parallel nested cubature; absolute tolerance has output times coordinate-volume dimension. Parallel [`cubature_nested`]: the 15 nodes of every outer panel are inner integrals evaluated concurrently ([`integrate_gk_par`]); each node owns its inner state. Value and evaluation count are identical to the serial function.",
        "Actual native index1 DAE consistency projection with independently measured variables, explicit constraint metric and original oracle errors. Computes consistent initial algebraic states `z0` and differential derivatives `dy0` for a semi-explicit Index-1 DAE system `y' = f(t, y, z)`, `0 = g(t, y, z)` at `t0`. Given fixed initial differential variables `y0` and an initial guess `z0_guess`, solves `g(t0, y0, z0) = 0` via damped Newton-Raphson iteration (`dg/dz` must be nonsingular for an index-1 DAE). Returns `(dy0, z0)`. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] for non-finite values, [`OdeGeomError::ShapeMismatch`] for mismatched lengths, [`OdeGeomError::Singular`] if `dg/dz` is singular (e.g. higher-index DAE), and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if Newton iteration does not converge.",
        "Actual native index2 DAE consistency projection with independently measured variables, explicit constraint metric and original oracle errors. Computes consistent initial conditions `(y0, z0)` for a semi-explicit Hessenberg Index-2 DAE system `y'(t) = f(t, y, z)`, `0 = g(t, y)` at `t0`. An index-2 DAE has two constraint manifolds: 1. The explicit position/state constraint `g(t, y) = 0`. 2. The hidden velocity/index-1 constraint obtained by differentiating `g(t, y) = 0` with respect to time: `G(t, y) f(t, y, z) + \\\\partial_t g(t, y) = 0`, where `G = \\\\partial g / \\\\partial y` and `(G \\\\cdot \\\\partial f / \\\\partial z)` is nonsingular. This function first projects `y0_guess` onto `g(t0, y) = 0` via minimum-norm Gauss\u2013Newton projection, then solves the hidden constraint for `z0`. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Empty`] if `y0_guess` or `z0_guess` is empty, [`OdeGeomError::Singular`] if `G (\\\\partial f / \\\\partial z)` is singular, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if projection fails to converge.",
        "Actual native DAE dense evaluation restoring differential/algebraic component units; outside the integrated interval remains None. Evaluates `(y(t), z(t))` at any time `t` inside the integrated span using cubic Hermite interpolation for `y(t)` and linear/Hermite interpolation for `z(t)`.",
        "Actual native fixed backward-Euler index1DAE, with independently normalized differential/algebraic variables and physical time step. Returned states concatenate the original differential/algebraic order. Backward Euler for the semi-explicit index-1 DAE `y' = f(t, y, z)`, `0 = g(t, y, z)` with fixed step `h`. Returns the trajectory of `(y, z)` concatenated. # Errors [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if the nonlinear solve fails.",
        "Actual native maximum constraint violation in the explicit independently scaled normalized constraint metric. No common physical residual unit is invented for heterogeneous equations. Maximum infinity-norm constraint violation `max_k ||g(t_k, y_k, z_k)||_inf`.",
        "Build a genuine semi-explicit native DAE model. f tape receives(time,y,z) and outputs physical dy/dt. Constraint tape receives(time,y,z) for index1or(time,y) for index2. Positive independent constraint references define its normalized Newton/violation metric; physical differential/algebraic units may differ by component.",
        "Actual adaptive native index1 DAE solve with independent differential/algebraic physical domains, normalized error/constraint metrics, physical step bounds and explicit Newton/attempt/evaluation budgets. Adaptive semi-explicit DAE solver with bounded callback work. # Errors Invalid controls, malformed/nonfinite callbacks, failed consistency or stage solves, exhausted budgets, or an unsatisfied tolerance at minimum step.",
        "Actual adaptive native index2 DAE solve with independent differential/algebraic physical domains, normalized error/constraint metrics, physical step bounds and explicit Newton/attempt/evaluation budgets. Adaptive semi-explicit DAE solver with bounded callback work. # Errors Invalid controls, malformed/nonfinite callbacks, failed consistency or stage solves, exhausted budgets, or an unsatisfied tolerance at minimum step.",
        "Actual native DDE Hermite history evaluation, restoring independent physical component units; outside the integrated interval returns None. Evaluates `y(t)` at any time `t` inside `[t_0, t_end]` using cubic Hermite interpolation.",
        "Actual native fixed-delay RK4 method of steps. History tape(time)->state sets the initial condition; RHS tape receives(time,state,delayed state). tau/h are physical times, tau>=h, and the final native step clips the endpoint. Returned samples carry true physical coordinates, not an invented dense interpolant. Constant-delay DDE integrator (method of steps with RK4). Constant-delay DDE `y'(t) = f(t, y(t), y(t - tau))` with history `hist(t)` for `t <= t0`, integrated by classical RK4 (method of steps) with cubic Hermite interpolation of the stored solution. Requires `tau >= h`. Returns the grid and states. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for invalid span, delay or step size, or [`OdeError::StepLimit`] when the requested grid cannot advance in floating-point time.",
        "Actual native state-dependent DDE. Each query tape(time,state)->physical past time invokes the native scoped history lookup at that stage; RHS tape receives(time,state,queried states in declared order). No lookup closure escapes or is retained. Independent state/history/rate references and explicit query/RHS evaluation budgets are preserved. Solves a general (possibly state-dependent or time-dependent) Delay Differential Equation (DDE) system `y'(t) = rhs(t, y(t), &|t_past| ...)` over `span = (t0, t1)`. The third argument passed to `rhs` is a continuous state lookup closure `&dyn Fn(f64) -> Vec<f64>` that evaluates `history(t_past)` for `t_past <= t0` and the continuous cubic Hermite extension of the solution for `t0 < t_past <= t`. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Empty`] if `y0` is empty, [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] for invalid span or tolerances, [`OdeGeomError::ShapeMismatch`] for mismatched state dimensions, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if step size collapses.",
        "Actual native fd_jacobian_colored on explicitly normalized time/state/rate data; RHS retains physical references and returned matrix reports them. Parallel mode calls the genuine Sync native implementation on an owned immutable tape. Finite-difference Jacobian restricted to `pattern`, computed with one evaluation of `f` per colour of `colors` (from [`color_columns`]). `f0 = f(t, y)`; `nfev` counts the evaluations.",
        "Actual native fd_jacobian_colored_par on explicitly normalized time/state/rate data; RHS retains physical references and returned matrix reports them. Parallel mode calls the genuine Sync native implementation on an owned immutable tape. Evaluates the columns of each colour together and writes the result on `pattern`. Each evaluation gets a zero-initialized output. Worker jobs reuse their perturbed state. Large colour sets on sparse patterns retain only the differences referenced by the pattern. Valid colours come from [`color_columns`].",
        "Actual native fd_jacobian_dense on explicitly normalized time/state/rate data; RHS retains physical references and returned matrix reports them. Parallel mode calls the genuine Sync native implementation on an owned immutable tape. Dense finite-difference Jacobian (one evaluation per column).",
        "Actual native fd_jacobian_dense_par on explicitly normalized time/state/rate data; RHS retains physical references and returned matrix reports them. Parallel mode calls the genuine Sync native implementation on an owned immutable tape. Dense finite-difference Jacobian from independent right-hand side columns. Independent column groups share one work vector per job. Below 16 columns or with one active worker, evaluation runs in index order. Each evaluation receives a zero-initialized output buffer. # Panics Panics if `f0` has fewer components than `y`.",
        "Instantiate the real native parallel finite-difference mode for immutable owned RHS functions; no host callback is shared unsafely. Columns / colours evaluated concurrently ([`fd_jacobian_dense_par`], [`fd_jacobian_colored_par`]).",
        "Instantiate the real native serial finite-difference mode for owned compiled RHS functions. Sequential evaluation (any right-hand side).",
        "Native fft fft_axes; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The transform over `axes` of a row-major tensor of the given `shape`, computed one axis at a time (row-column decomposition) with the 1-D transform above. An empty `axes` is the identity. # Errors An axis out of range or repeated, or a `shape` that disagrees with the number of elements.",
        "Native fft fft_norm; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The forward transform under a chosen normalisation. `Backward` is the plain forward transform, `Forward` scales it by `1/N` (so the inverse needs none) and `Unitary` by `1/sqrt(N)`.",
        "Native fft ifft_norm; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The inverse transform under a chosen normalisation: `Backward` scales by `1/N`, `Forward` not at all, `Unitary` by `1/sqrt(N)`.",
        "Native fftconv fft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Discrete Fourier transform of any length (`inverse` includes the `1/n` scaling).",
        "Native2D Filon cosine/sine quadrature: phase coefficients have one dimension P,omega has1/(P*coordinate),amplitude references preserve arbitrary ordinate units. Two-dimensional Filon quadrature of an oscillatory integrand over a box. The integrand is `f(x, y) * cos(omega * phi(x, y))` (`cosine = true`) or `f(x, y) * sin(omega * phi(x, y))` (`cosine = false`), for the linear phase `phi(x, y) = a x + b y` with `phase = [a, b]`. The amplitude is interpolated by a tensor product of quadratic Lagrange polynomials, one per double panel and axis, and each oscillatory moment is integrated exactly (it is the 1-D [`filon`] functional), so the accuracy does not degrade as `|omega|` grows. The panel count is doubled from one until the difference between successive sweeps is at most `tol * max(|value|, 1)`, so `omega = 0` reproduces the classical Simpson rule (exact for a quadratic amplitude). Invalid input (a box dimension list other than two, `hi <= lo`, a non-finite frequency/slope/tolerance, `tol <= 0`) returns `NaN` values with `converged == false`; it never panics.",
        "Native complex2D Filon quadrature with physical coordinate,amplitude,linear-phase and frequency contracts. Two-dimensional complex-exponential Filon quadrature, `integral f(x, y) exp(i omega (a x + b y)) dx dy` over the box `[lo, hi]`, with `phase = [a, b]`. Same method and validity rules as [`filon2d`]; the value is returned as a complex number so both the cosine and sine components are available, and the error is the norm of the complex error.",
        "Gaussian model with same-unit physical mean and positive standard deviation. Creates a Gaussian with the given mean and standard deviation.",
        "Physical Gaussian density has the reciprocal coordinate dimension. Density at `x`.",
        "Sample a measured Gaussian using the retained shared native RNG handle. Draws one sample with `rng`.",
        "Dimensionless importance weight between measured Gaussian models in the same coordinate domain. Importance weight `target(x) / proposal(x)` of a point `x` drawn from `proposal` when `self` is the target density. The weight has mean one under the proposal whenever the proposal has the same support, which is what makes the estimator of [`importance_sampling_gaussian`] unbiased.",
        "Actual parallel native Gauss-Kronrod panel; returns physical integral/error and evaluation count without inventing a convergence verdict. [`gk_panel`] with the `2n - 1` node evaluations done in parallel. The panel's arithmetic is identical to the serial rule. Parallel evaluation is most useful when one evaluation of `f` is expensive and the callback is `Sync`.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. A differential operator annihilating `expr`. It is the operator of least order found in the vector space that the closure construction provides, which is usually but not always the minimal operator of the function. Returns `None` when an atom is invalid or a limit is exceeded.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. A differential operator annihilating the atom.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. Whether the atom meets the conditions stated on its variant.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The first `n` Taylor coefficients, or `None` for an invalid atom.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. How many leading Taylor coefficients suffice to decide whether a solution of `op` is zero. Returns `None` for the zero operator or when the coefficient or search bound exceeds `limits.max_terms`.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The first `n` Taylor coefficients at the origin. Higher integer powers use a truncated recurrence over the nonzero base terms.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. Whether `expr` is identically zero. `None` when it cannot be decided within the limits (or an atom is invalid); `Some` is a proof either way.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The sum.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The constant `c`.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The derivative.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The quotient, or `None` when dividing by zero.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. Whether this is zero.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The product.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The negation.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. `num / den` in lowest terms, or `None` for a zero denominator.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. A polynomial as a rational function.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The first `n` Taylor coefficients at the origin, or `None` when the function has a pole there.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The difference.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. Zero.",
        "Native exact holonomic operation in explicitly normalized rational coordinates. Session/kind and bounded exact coefficient/expression work are checked. The recurrence of the Taylor coefficients `a_n` of a solution of `op`: pairs `(s, c_s)` with `sum_s c_s(m) a_{m+s} = 0` for every integer `m` (coefficients with a negative index are zero), shifts ascending.",
        "Actual native square polynomial homotopy for normalized coefficients/exponent rows/checked polynomial offsets. Positive coordinate/output references restore physical complex roots/residuals. Maximum6variables,4096terms,degree16,and256total-degree paths. Failed paths retain false status and optional residual; their undefined roots occupy empty offset ranges. Solves a square polynomial system by total-degree homotopy continuation with the default (fixed, generic) `gamma`; see [`homotopy_system_with_gamma`]. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for a non-square or malformed system.",
        "Actual gamma-selected native homotopy using normalized polynomial descriptors and independent physical root/residual references; all path statuses and defined coordinates are retained. Total-degree homotopy continuation for a square polynomial system `f_i(x_1..x_n) = 0` (`system[i]` lists the terms of `f_i`). The start system is `g_i = x_i^{d_i} - 1` with `d_i` the total degree of `f_i`; its `prod d_i` solutions are tracked along `H(x, t) = (1-t) gamma g(x) + t f(x)` from `t = 0` to `t = 1` with an RK4 predictor, a Newton corrector and adaptive step control. The complex constant `gamma` (the \"gamma trick\") makes the paths non-crossing with probability one. One [`PathResult`] is returned per start solution; paths diverging to infinity are marked `finite == false`. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for a non-square or malformed system, a zero or nonfinite gamma, or more than 200 000 total-degree paths.",
        "Native hypergeometric gamma_c; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The complex gamma function; poles (non-positive integers) give NaN.",
        "Native hypergeometric hyp1f1_cplx; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Kummer's confluent hypergeometric function `M(a, b, z) = 1F1(a; b; z)`. All arguments are complex. For `Re z < 0` Kummer's transformation `M(a, b, z) = e^z M(b - a, b, -z)` is applied so that the dominant solution is the one being continued.",
        "Native hypergeometric hyp2f1_cplx; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Gauss hypergeometric function `2F1(a, b; c; z)` for complex parameters and argument (principal branch, cut along `[1, inf)`; the value on the cut is the limit from `Im z -> 0+`). For positive real parameters at `z = 1`, the gamma ratio in [Gauss's theorem](https://dlmf.nist.gov/15.4.E20) is evaluated in logarithmic form without forming large gamma values.",
        "Native hypergeometric hyp_pfq; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The generalised hypergeometric function `pFq(a; b; z)`. See the module documentation for the algorithm. `z = 0` gives `1`. Returns NaN for divergent series (`p > q + 1` without termination, `z != 0`), a denominator parameter at a non-positive integer (without earlier termination), and `+inf` at `z = 1` when `Re(sum b - sum a) <= 0`; [`hyp_pfq_checked`] tells which.",
        "Native hypergeometric hyp_pfq_checked; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. [`hyp_pfq`] with the reason when there is no value. # Errors [`HypError`]: NaN input, a denominator parameter at a non-positive integer (without earlier termination), or a divergent series (`p > q + 1`, `z != 0`). `z = 0` gives `1` in every case.",
        "Native hypergeometric hyp_u_cplx; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Tricomi's confluent hypergeometric function `U(a, b, z)` for complex arguments on the principal branch (cut along the negative real axis; the upper side is returned for `z < 0`).",
        "Native hypergeometric ln_gamma_c; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Logarithm of the complex gamma function. Complex log-gamma function, delegating to [`super::special_complex::ln_gamma_complex`].",
        "Native Gaussian importance sampling using measured target/proposal models; integrand references match their coordinate domain. Output has ordinate units, not ordinate times length; ESS remains dimensionless. Importance-sampling estimate of `\u222b f(x) p_target(x) dx`. Draws `n` points from `proposal` and averages `f(x) * p_target(x) / p_proposal(x)`. Because the weight has mean one under the proposal, the estimator is unbiased for the target integral whatever the shift; what the shift costs is variance, visible in the effective sample size. The proposal must be non-degenerate and have at least the support of the target, otherwise the weights are not finite and the estimate is not defined. The error is the sample standard error of the weighted terms, and the effective sample size is `(\u03a3 w)^2 / \u03a3 w^2`.",
        "Normalized native integrate::adaptive_quadrature: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. # Adaptive Quadrature (Adaptive Simpson's Method) Recursively refines the interval until the estimated error satisfies the tolerance or the refinement limit is reached. The integrand must return a consistent result for a given coordinate because refinement reuses the parent panel's samples. A panel with consecutive floating-point bounds uses a trapezoid through its two finite endpoint samples, or returns `NaN` if either sample is nonfinite. ## Arguments * `f` - Function to integrate. * `range` - Interval `(a, b)`. * `tolerance` - Error tolerance (e.g., 1e-6). ## Example ``` use rssn::kernels::integrate::adaptive_quadrature; let f = |x: f64| x.sin(); let res = adaptive_quadrature(f, (0.0, std::f64::consts::PI), 1e-6); assert!((res - 2.0).abs() < 1e-6); ```",
        "Normalized native integrate::gauss_kronrod_any: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Adaptive Gauss\u2013Kronrod quadrature over an interval that may be infinite at either end, by mapping it to a finite one.",
        "Normalized native integrate::gauss_kronrod: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Globally adaptive Gauss\u2013Kronrod quadrature of `f` over the finite interval `[a, b]`. The panel with the largest error estimate is bisected until the total estimate drops below `tolerance` or `max_panels` panels exist. The returned [`Quadrature::error`] is the sum of the panel estimates, so a caller can tell a converged result from an exhausted budget. Reversing the limits negates the integral. Non-finite integrand values are propagated to the result rather than treated as zero.",
        "Normalized native integrate::gauss_legendre_quadrature: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. # Gauss-Legendre Quadrature Uses standard weights and nodes for n = 5 (hardcoded for now as it's efficient for general purpose). Can be extended to arbitrary n in the future. ## Example ``` use rssn::kernels::integrate::gauss_legendre_quadrature; let f = |x: f64| x.powi(3); let res = gauss_legendre_quadrature(f, (0.0, 1.0)); assert!((res - 0.25).abs() < 1e-10); ```",
        "True native parallel Gauss-Kronrod quadrature using physical coherent bounds and compiled reference scales; rule0=G7K15,1=G10K21. [`integrate_gk`] with the nodes of each panel evaluated by [`gk_panel_par`]. For a pure callback the partition, arithmetic and result are bit-identical to the serial driver, regardless of the number of Rayon workers.",
        "Normalized native integrate::romberg_integration: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. # Romberg Integration Uses Richardson extrapolation on the Trapezoidal rule to improve accuracy. ## Arguments * `f` - Function to integrate. * `range` - Interval. * `max_steps` - Order of extrapolation (e.g., 5-10). ## Example ``` use rssn::kernels::integrate::romberg_integration; let f = |x: f64| x.exp(); let res = romberg_integration(f, (0.0, 1.0), 6); assert!((res - (std::f64::consts::E - 1.0)).abs() < 1e-10); ```",
        "Normalized native integrate::simpson_rule: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. # Pure Numerical Simpson's Rule Performs numerical integration using Simpson's 1/3 rule. ## Arguments * `f` - The function to integrate. * `range` - Interval `(a, b)`. * `n_steps` - Number of steps (must be usually even, but we handle it). ## Returns `Result<f64, String>` ## Errors Returns an error if the number of steps is zero or if an internal calculation fails. ## Example ``` use rssn::kernels::integrate::simpson_rule; let f = |x: f64| x * x; let res = simpson_rule(f, (0.0, 1.0), 10).unwrap(); assert!((res - 1.0 / 3.0).abs() < 1e-10); ```",
        "Normalized native integrate::trapezoidal_rule: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. # Pure Numerical Trapezoidal Rule Performs numerical integration using the trapezoidal rule for a given function. ## Arguments * `f` - The function to integrate, as a closure. * `range` - A tuple `(a, b)` representing the integration interval. * `n_steps` - The number of steps to use. ## Returns The approximate value of the definite integral. ## Example ``` use rssn::kernels::integrate::trapezoidal_rule; let f = |x: f64| x * x; let res = trapezoidal_rule(f, (0.0, 1.0), 1000); assert!((res - 1.0 / 3.0).abs() < 1e-4); ```",
        "Native adaptive triangle quadrature on three physical planar vertices; compiled function has matching coordinate references and absolute tolerance has area times ordinate units. Adaptive integration over a planar triangle. The panel is the seven-point Dunavant rule of degree five; its error is estimated against the embedded three-point midpoint rule of degree two, and the region with the largest estimate is bisected into four congruent triangles until the summed estimate is below `max(abs_tol, rel_tol * |I|)` or `max_evals` integrand evaluations have been spent. The estimate is a heuristic, not a rigorous bound \u2014 the same contract as the box cubature in this module. The vertices may be given in either orientation; the affine map uses the absolute area, so the result is the integral over the triangle, not a signed one. A degenerate triangle (zero area) integrates to zero.",
        "Actual native normalized band sparsity pattern with checked dimension/bandwidth budgets. Pattern of the band `-kl <= j - i <= ku` of an `n x n` matrix.",
        "Actual native stage-block assembly in normalized coordinates. cm/cj are immutable two-index compiled coefficient programs with dimensionless input/output; mass is None or a normalized matrix handle. n*stages<=128 bounds output triplets before allocation. Assembles the triplets of the block system `sum over blocks (bi, bj) of cm(bi, bj) M + cj(bi, bj) J` in stage-major numbering for `stages` blocks of size `n`.",
        "Actual native graph coloring retained with its original sparsity pattern; prevents mismatched or forged colored finite-difference descriptors. Greedy colouring of the columns of a sparsity pattern: columns that share a row get different colours. Returns the colour of each column and the number of colours.",
        "Inspect the actual native color assignment/count retained with its sparsity pattern.",
        "Retain a native normalized dense Jacobian/mass matrix. Measured quantities require explicit prior normalization; RHS-derived Jacobians retain their physical reference frame separately.",
        "Actual native dense/banded/sparse stage-block factorization. n*stages<=512; solver Dense,Banded,Sparse; all triplets are checked before native indexing. Factors the system with the given triplets in stage-major numbering (`stages` blocks of size `n`) according to `structure`; `band` is the `(kl, ku)` of one block (used for banded structure only).",
        "Actual native factor solve in its stage-major normalized coordinate layout. Solves the factored system.",
        "Genuine serial native matrix visitor on normalized coefficients. Ordinary native handle is authenticated by its session bank and never enters the callback. Visitor receives exact row/column Int and Float value,returns an ignored Bool acknowledgement. Shared evaluation budget and original source errors stop subsequent source invocations; native iteration order is preserved. Calls `g(i, j, value)` for every stored entry.",
        "Inspect normalized native matrix data and actual evaluation count. RHS-derived Jacobians additionally expose their original time/state reference quantities; normalized coefficients are never mislabeled as one common physical matrix domain.",
        "Actual native provider evaluation on explicitly normalized time/state/rate data; updated caller evaluation count and physical frame remain with the returned matrix. Evaluates the Jacobian at `(t, y)` with `f0 = f(t, y)`.",
        "Actual native provider evaluation with explicit real serial/parallel FD mode; normalized frame contracts and actual updated nfev are preserved. As [`JacobianProvider::eval`] with the finite-difference routines of `fd`.",
        "Actual native JacobianProvider builder over an owned RHS tape. Structure Dense,Banded,Sparse; analytic0 finite differences,1 exact native tape dense AD,2 exact native tape sparse AD. Sparse structure requires its normalized matrix pattern. Provider borrows are reconstructed per invocation to retain safe owned lifetimes; every evaluation uses the real native provider. Prepares the provider for a system of dimension `n`. A sparse callback with `Dense` structure makes the structure sparse. Returns `None` if a banded/sparse pattern does not fit `n`.",
        "Retain checked native normalized CSR data from exact row/column triplets.",
        "Actual native KKT stationarity residual in the normalized coordinate/objective metric. Physical gradient E/D,constraint Jacobians C/D,G/D,multipliers E/C,E/G are normalized by four positive references; result is dimensionless. Infinity norm of the gradient of the Lagrangian, i.e. the KKT stationarity residual for the multipliers `lambda` (on `h`) and `mu` (on `g`, with `L = f + lambda . h + mu . g`).",
        "Actual native adaptive Levin collocation with positive absolute physical tolerance and bounded collocation/evaluation work. [`levin`] with the number of points doubled until two successive results agree to `tol` (absolute, or relative to the result when that is larger), starting from 8 points and stopping at `max_points`. # Errors A message when no pair of successive collocations agrees: typically a stationary point of the phase, or an integrand that is itself oscillatory.",
        "Actual native Levin collocation with independent physical amplitude,phase,and derivative programs. Absolute reference tolerance is checked for interface consistency; fixed collocation supplies no error estimate. `integral from a to b of f(x) exp(i w g(x)) dx` by Levin collocation at `n` Chebyshev points (`n >= 2`); `dg` is the derivative of `g`. # Errors A message when `n < 2`, the data is not finite, or the collocation system cannot be solved.",
        "Integrates a compiled real amplitude with the prepared weights. The complex result has amplitude units times coordinate units. Each call evaluates only the amplitude program. Integrates an amplitude sampled once at each prepared node. # Errors A non-finite sample, an unrepresentable result, or cancellation.",
        "Integrates one real amplitude sample per ordered plan node. The result has amplitude units times coordinate units. No compiled function is required for this call. Integrates amplitude values already sampled at [`Self::nodes`]. # Errors The wrong sample count, a non-finite sample, an unrepresentable result, or cancellation.",
        "Prepares repeated integration on one interval at fixed frequency. Supply compiled scalar phase and phase derivative, with derivative units equal to phase units divided by the coordinate dimension. Frequency is inverse phase. The handle retains only the prepared nodes and weights.",
        "Returns the measured quadrature nodes in the order required by the sampled amplitude operator. An empty interval has no nodes. The physical nodes, in the order required by [`Self::integrate_samples`].",
        "Construct an actual native regression CostFunction with independently measured predictor/response domains and positive reference quantities; coefficients retain intercept E and slope E/D units. Builds a least-squares problem from predictors and responses. # Errors Returns an error when the row counts differ, the design is empty, or a value is not finite.",
        "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Levenberg-Marquardt for nonlinear least squares `min |f(x)|^2` with a finite-difference Jacobian. Works for `m >= n` residuals. # Errors [`RootError::NoConvergence`] when work is exhausted or steps stagnate; [`RootError::Invalid`] for invalid input or malformed residuals and [`RootError::Singular`] if the augmented decomposition fails.",
        "Actual native normalized least-squares report convergence predicate. All original report fields/status are supplied explicitly; this does not synthesize convergence from an iteration count. Whether the reported termination passed a convergence test.",
        "Actual native least-squares status predicate; budget/stagnation statuses never imply convergence. Whether an explicit residual or first-order convergence test passed.",
        "Actual native normalized mass-matrix band. Half bandwidths `(kl, ku)` of the stored nonzeros.",
        "Actual native normalized mass-matrix cols. Dimension (columns) of the matrix.",
        "Genuine serial native matrix visitor on normalized coefficients. Ordinary native handle is authenticated by its session bank and never enters the callback. Visitor receives exact row/column Int and Float value,returns an ignored Bool acknowledgement. Shared evaluation budget and original source errors stop subsequent source invocations; native iteration order is preserved. Calls `g(i, j, value)` for every nonzero entry.",
        "Actual native mass-matrix action in explicitly normalized coordinates; vector length is checked before native indexing. Matrix-vector product `M x`.",
        "Actual native normalized mass-matrix rows. Dimension (rows) of the matrix.",
        "Owned position-only acceleration tape. Positive time reference and each coordinate reference restore acceleration coordinate/time\u00b2, preventing hidden dependence on velocity or time in the MechanicalSystem trait.",
        "Actual native generic backward Euler on the retained constant matrix with physical time/state reconstruction and bounded allocation. Solves a linear ODE system y' = Ay using the backward (implicit) Euler method. This method is A-stable, making it excellent for stiff equations. The update rule is y_{n+1} = (I - dt*A)^-1 * `y_n`. # Returns A `Result` containing the solution path, or an error string if matrix inversion fails. Solves a linear ODE system `y' = Ay` using the backward (implicit) Euler method. This method is A-stable, making it excellent for stiff equations where explicit methods would require prohibitively small time steps. The update rule is `y_{n+1} = (I - dt*A)^-1 * y_n`. # Arguments * `system` - The linear ODE system to solve, implementing the `LinearOdeSystem` trait. * `y0` - The initial state vector. * `t_span` - The time interval `(t_start, t_end)`. # Returns A `Result` containing the solution path as `Vec<(f64, Vec<f64>)>`. # Errors This function will return an error if the matrix `(I - dt*A)` is not invertible, which can occur if the system is singular for the given time step `dt`.",
        "Actual native MechanicalSystem acceleration evaluation with physical coordinate/time\u00b2 result components.",
        "Actual native LinearOdeSystem dimension.",
        "Actual native LinearOdeSystem matrix in its declared normalized reference coordinate metric; mixed physical row/column domains remain on the retained model.",
        "Declare a constant linear ODE from a normalized native dense Jacobian carrying its physical RHS frame. The resulting coefficient matrix is the caller's explicit constant linear model, not a linearity inference from sampled values.",
        "Actual native mechanical semi-implicit Euler. State contains independently measured positions then corresponding velocities; source tape depends only on position. Solves a second-order ODE system (typically mechanical systems) using the semi-implicit Euler method. This method is often used in mechanics for its energy conservation properties (it is symplectic). It is particularly well-suited for systems where the state vector can be partitioned into positions and velocities, and accelerations depend only on positions. It assumes the state vector `y` is ordered as `[x0, x1, ..., v0, v1, ...]`. # Arguments * `system` - The mechanical system to solve, implementing the `MechanicalSystem` trait. * `y0` - The initial state `[initial_positions..., initial_velocities...]`. * `dt` - The fixed time step. # Returns A `Vec` of tuples `(time, state_vector)` representing the solution path. # Errors This function will return an error if the length of the initial state vector `y0` is not twice the spatial dimension of the system.",
        "Actual instantiated native MechanicalSystem spatial dimension.",
        "Actual native symplectic_trajectory with physical position, momentum, mass and time. The mass itself defines the positive mass reference, so native mass1is a checked normalization and physical momentum is restored with m*coordinate/time. No numerical update is implemented in glue. Integrates a separable Hamiltonian system for `steps` steps with the symplectic method of the given `order` (2: Verlet, 4: Yoshida), returning the states `(q, p)` after every step.",
        "Actual native verlet_step with physical position, momentum, mass and time. The mass itself defines the positive mass reference, so native mass1is a checked normalization and physical momentum is restored with m*coordinate/time. No numerical update is implemented in glue. One step of the St\u00f6rmer\u2013Verlet (leapfrog) method for `q'' = a(q)` (`a` the acceleration, `-\u2207V/m`): kick, drift, kick.",
        "Actual native yoshida4_step with physical position, momentum, mass and time. The mass itself defines the positive mass reference, so native mass1is a checked normalization and physical momentum is restored with m*coordinate/time. No numerical update is implemented in glue. One step of the fourth-order Forest\u2013Ruth (Yoshida) composition of Verlet steps with weights `w1, w0, w1`, `w1 = 1/(2 - 2^(1/3))`.",
        "Native meijer meijer_g; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Value of `G^{m,n}_{p,q}(z | a; b)`, the Meijer G-function, at complex parameters and complex `z` (principal branch; see the module documentation). `p = a.len()` and `q = b.len()`. Returns `None` for invalid orders (`m > q` or `n > p`), non-finite inputs, `z = 0` where the limit does not exist, or when a finite value cannot be resolved. The single-pole exponential reduction uses its closed form. Residue series use the Mellin-Barnes integral as an independent cross-check where it applies.",
        "Native meijer meijer_g_real; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Value of `G^{m,n}_{p,q}(z | a; b)` for real parameters and real `z`. Returns `None` when the value is complex (a branch cut was crossed), when the imaginary part is not negligible, or under the conditions of [`meijer_g`].",
        "Native antithetic Monte Carlo with physical compiled-function references and bounded pair count. Antithetic-variate Monte Carlo integral over `[lo, hi]`. Each of the `pairs` iterations draws one uniform point `u` and evaluates the integrand at both `u` and its reflection `1 - u` in every axis; the estimator is the mean of the `pairs` pair averages, so it costs `2 * pairs` evaluations. Both points are marginally uniform, hence the estimator is unbiased whatever the correlation between them. For an integrand that is monotone along the sampled axes the two evaluations are negatively correlated and the variance is below that of [`monte_carlo`] with the same number of evaluations; for an integrand that is symmetric about the centre of the box the correlation is positive and the variance can be slightly worse. The reported error is the standard error of the pair averages, which is the correct standard error of this estimator and already accounts for the correlation.",
        "Native serial Monte Carlo over physical bounds using an owned immutable compiled program; same-unit coordinates, arbitrary output units, actual native error/status and explicit sample/evaluation limits. Plain Monte Carlo integral over `[lo, hi]` with a seeded generator; the error is the standard error of the mean.",
        "True native parallel Monte Carlo over physical bounds using an owned immutable compiled program; same-unit coordinates, arbitrary output units, actual native error/status and explicit sample/evaluation limits. Parallel [`monte_carlo`]: every block of 4096 samples has its own generator stream (seeded from `seed` and the block index), the block sums are combined in block order. Deterministic for a given `seed` and `n` whatever the thread count; the samples differ from the serial single-stream function.",
        "Native stratified Monte Carlo with checked exponential grid/sample cost and independent physical output units. Stratified Monte Carlo integral over `[lo, hi]`. Every axis is cut into `strata_per_axis` equal cells, so the box has `strata_per_axis^dim` strata and each stratum receives `per_stratum` uniform samples. The estimator is the volume-weighted sum of the stratum means and the error is the root of the sum of the squared stratum standard errors, which is unbiased and never assumes that the strata are equally variable. Stratification removes the between-stratum part of the variance, so it pays when the integrand is smooth on the scale of a cell (the within-cell variance falls like the square of the cell width) and buys nothing when the integrand varies inside every cell, as a discontinuity finer than the grid does. The cost is exponential in the dimension: `strata^dim * per_stratum` evaluations. Arguments below one are treated as one. Returns `converged = false` with a `NaN` value and no evaluations when `strata_per_axis^dim * per_stratum` does not fit in a `usize`.",
        "Returns a three-component gravitational acceleration per body in the original input order. The output carries length per time squared. Reading the accelerations leaves the chain state and time intact. Accelerations per body in the original input order. The returned vectors are Cartesian accelerations. In states mutated after construction, coincidences retain the gravitational singularity.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Angular momentum about the centre of mass.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Cartesian positions and velocities (original body order, absolute frame) at the current time.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Total energy.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Advances to the physical time `t_end` with adaptive fictitious steps (initial `ds0`) and local error tolerance `tol`; the chain is re-selected before every step and the last step is solved for so that the final time is hit to round-off. Returns the number of accepted steps. # Errors Fails if the step size collapses or the final-time solve does not converge.",
        "Actual native GBS step. Its returned error is explicitly in the native normalized state metric, while fictitious time has physical energy*time units. One Gragg-Bulirsch-Stoer extrapolated step of size `ds` with `kmax` columns (order `2 kmax`) at fixed chain. Returns the relative error estimate.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Kinetic energy.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. One second-order time-transformed leapfrog step of size `h` in `s` (chain coordinates).",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Builds the chain system from Cartesian data. # Errors Fails for fewer than two bodies, length mismatch, non-positive or non-finite masses, non-finite inputs, or an unrepresentable initial chain or energy. The unsoftened interaction is singular at coincidence.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Potential energy (negative for attractive gravity).",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Re-selects the chain from the current configuration; the new chain vectors are exact sums of the old ones, so no precision is lost.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Full 1PN (EIH) accelerations (Newtonian part included) of all particles, given the Newtonian accelerations `acc_newton` of the sources.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Conserved 1PN energy of the EIH N-body Lagrangian (Landau-Lifshitz section 106).",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Registers particles `a` and `b` (indices into the *current* particle arrays) as a KS-regularised binary: they are replaced by one centre-of-mass particle (stored at index `a`; index `b` becomes a massless passive placeholder that is excluded from all sums). # Errors Fails with the sixth-order scheme, PN, equal indices or out-of-range indices.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Runs complete blocks until the next block would end after `t_end`. To query a state between block times, use [`Self::state_at`]. # Errors Rejects a non-finite end time and propagates [`step`](Self::step) errors.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Enables the 1PN EIH corrections with speed of light `c` and re-initialises. # Errors Fails with the sixth-order scheme, with softening, or with binaries.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Total energy at the current system time (Newtonian, or the 1PN energy with PN enabled). # Errors Propagates binary propagation errors.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Creates a system (all particles at time `t0`) and evaluates the initial derivatives. # Errors Fails for mismatched array lengths, fewer than two particles, a non-finite initial time, invalid step parameters, or a block interval with no power of two.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Time at which the next block ends.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Positions and velocities of all particles synchronised to time `t` (binary components are reported at the position of their regularised internal state; the placeholder particle of a binary reports its second component). # Errors Propagates binary propagation errors.",
        "Actual native N-body operation. Positive SI mass/length/time references preserve physical input/output domains. Particle, extrapolation and attempted-work bounds are checked before native computation. Advances the system by one block: the particles that are due at the earliest end time. # Errors Rejects invalid step bounds and clocks that cannot advance to a finite time. Propagates binary propagation errors.",
        "Normalized native rootfind::newton_bracketed: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then2 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Safeguarded Newton on a sign-changing bracket `[a, b]`. Newton steps must lie in the central half of the current bracket; otherwise bisection guarantees progress. Convergence uses bracket width, not the Newton step size, which can be tiny far from a root. `tol` is a relative/absolute width tolerance scaled by `1 + |x|`. # Errors [`RootError::NotBracketed`] / [`RootError::NoConvergence`]. [`RootError::Invalid`] for non-finite inputs, function or derivative values, or a non-positive tolerance.",
        "Genuine matrix-free trust-region Newton-CG: physical point,positive coordinate/objective references,explicit normalized-coordinate metric options,shared callback evaluation limit,then normalized objective/gradient/Hessian-vector callbacks. Exact derivative actions come from the supplied callbacks; no finite-difference substitution. Returns physical x,value,gradient norm plus actual native status and work counters. Minimize a smooth objective using only vector actions. `gradient(x, out)` and `hessian_vector(x, direction, out)` must fill every output element. Buffers are reset to NaN before each callback, so missing writes are detected. Non-finite trial objectives/gradients are rejected; the last accepted point is retained. No parallel callback calls occur. # Errors Returns an error for invalid options/input, a non-finite initial value or gradient, or a non-finite Hessian action/CG intermediate.",
        "Actual native Tape Newton-CG. Tape input, parameter and output references preserve mixed physical coordinate units. Initial and parameter vectors may hold separately measured Value cells. Options use the normalized coordinate metric. The normalized gradient norm is dimensionless. Iteration, CG-action and instruction work is bounded before the solve. Minimize one tape output using analytic reverse gradients and HVPs. Parameters are constant. The objective tape must have matching input arity. No finite-difference Hessian is substituted when a second-order rule is absent. # Errors Returns the same errors as [`minimize`], or `InvalidInput` for an invalid output index/arity. Opaque second-order rules report `NonFiniteModel`.",
        "Solve a prepared tape objective from a new measured initial point and parameters, using the plan's retained buffers. Each solve has its own normalized options and work budget. Results and counters have the same units and meaning as the one-shot tape solver. Minimize a tape output with reusable Newton and CG buffers. # Errors Returns the same errors as [`minimize_tape`].",
        "Retain an independent snapshot of a native tape and select one objective output. The plan also holds the Newton and CG buffers used by repeated solves. Releasing the input tape does not invalidate the plan.",
        "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Newton's method for `f(x) = 0` with a backtracking (Armijo) line search on `|f|^2`. Pass `jac = None` to use finite differences. # Errors [`RootError::Singular`] on a singular Jacobian, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] when the iteration limit is hit.",
        "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Newton's method for `f(x) = 0` with a backtracking (Armijo) line search on `|f|^2`. Pass `jac = None` to use finite differences. # Errors [`RootError::Singular`] on a singular Jacobian, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] when the iteration limit is hit.",
        "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Forward-difference Jacobian of `f: R^n -> R^m` at `x`. This compatibility function returns a same-shaped NaN matrix when a callback is malformed or a derivative cannot be represented. Prefer [`try_numerical_jacobian`] when the failure reason is needed.",
        "Native ode::solve_adaptive: this legacy driver supports rtol/atol/max_steps; h0/hmin must be zero and hmax must cover the whole span, with unsupported requests rejected. independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Integrates `y' = f(t, y)` over `t_span` with the adaptive Dormand\u2013Prince 5(4) method, keeping the local error below `atol + rtol * |y|`. # Errors Returns an error when the step size underflows, `max_steps` accepted or rejected steps are exhausted, or the state stops being finite.",
        "Adaptive BDF with finite-difference Jacobians and event detection. The positive maximum order controls[0] defaults to one and is capped at five. Config rtol and atol apply to the normalized state. Config h0, hmin and hmax are physical step magnitudes, and zero h0 selects the initial step automatically. The required config max_steps bounds attempts, including rejected steps. Each event supplies program, terminal and direction (-1, 0 or 1). The maxeval argument bounds tape evaluations. Variable-order BDF with event detection on its backward-difference interpolant. # Errors As [`bdf`].",
        "Legacy adaptive BDF with the Jacobian of the retained RHS tape and event detection. The positive maximum order controls[0] defaults to one and is capped at five. Config rtol and atol apply to the normalized state. Config h0, hmin and hmax are physical step magnitudes, and zero h0 selects the initial step automatically. The required config max_steps bounds attempts, including rejected steps. Each event supplies program, terminal and direction (-1, 0 or 1). The maxeval argument bounds tape evaluations. Variable-order BDF with dense output and event detection; superseded by [`bdf_with`]. # Errors As [`bdf_with`].",
        "Adaptive BDF with the Jacobian of the retained RHS tape. Pass an empty events list. The positive maximum order controls[0] defaults to one and is capped at five. Config rtol and atol apply to the normalized state. Config h0, hmin and hmax are physical step magnitudes, and zero h0 selects the initial step automatically. The required config max_steps bounds attempts, including rejected steps. The maxeval argument bounds tape evaluations. Variable-order BDF with a user-supplied Jacobian `jac(t, y)`. The solution is advanced in the fixed-leading-coefficient form on a backward-difference array; the order (capped at `max_order`, `1..=5`) and the step size are chosen after every `order + 1` equal steps by comparing the error estimates of the neighbouring orders. The Jacobian and its LU factorisation are reused while the Newton iteration converges. # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::Invalid`].",
        "Adaptive BDF with dense output and event detection. Set controls[1] to 1 to use the Jacobian of the retained RHS tape. An omitted or other value uses finite differences, evaluated concurrently for systems with at least 64 state components. The positive maximum order controls[0] defaults to one and is capped at five. Config rtol and atol apply to the normalized state. Config h0, hmin and hmax are physical step magnitudes, and zero h0 selects the initial step automatically. The required config max_steps bounds attempts, including rejected steps. Each event supplies program, terminal and direction (-1, 0 or 1). The maxeval argument bounds tape evaluations. [`bdf_with`] with concurrently evaluated finite-difference Jacobians. The right-hand side must be `Sync`; the solution is bit-identical. It pays from `n` of a few hundred when one evaluation of `f` is expensive (measured 1.35 at `n = 100` to 1.56 at `n = 400`); below `STIFF_PAR_MIN_N` it uses the serial Jacobian. The Radau solver gains nothing (Newton / LU dominate: 1.08, 0.99, 0.92), so there is no `radau_with_par`. # Errors As [`bdf_with`].",
        "Adaptive BDF with dense output and event detection. Set controls[1] to 1 to use the Jacobian of the retained RHS tape. An omitted or other value uses finite differences. The positive maximum order controls[0] defaults to one and is capped at five. Config rtol and atol apply to the normalized state. Config h0, hmin and hmax are physical step magnitudes, and zero h0 selects the initial step automatically. The required config max_steps bounds attempts, including rejected steps. Each event supplies program, terminal and direction (-1, 0 or 1). The maxeval argument bounds tape evaluations. Variable-order BDF with dense output, event detection and structured Jacobians. Identical to [`bdf_jac`] (the Jacobian is optional) but additionally locates `bo.events` on the backward-difference interpolating polynomial of the last step (direction-aware; a terminal event ends the run), uses a banded or sparse LU when [`BdfOptions::structure`] says so, and [`OdeSolution::eval`] interpolates anywhere in the integrated range. # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::Invalid`].",
        "Actual native finite-difference BVP y''=f(x,y,y') with physical coordinateD/stateE references. f inputs have domains D,E,E/D and output E/D\u00b2; guess is D->E. Boundary values, true native samples and explicit evaluation budget retain those domains. Finite-difference BVP solver for `y'' = f(x, y, y')` with Dirichlet ends. Second-order finite-difference solution of the BVP `y'' = f(x, y, y')`, `y(a) = ya`, `y(b) = yb` on `n` interior points, by Newton iteration on the discrete system. Returns `(x, y)` including the end points; `guess` gives the initial profile. # Errors [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if Newton fails.",
        "Physical accepted samples and native event/order statistics. Fixed endpoint-only solvers return only their actual initial/final states.",
        "Actual native matrix ETDRK4 with independent physical state/time reference frame. Linear matrix coefficients are explicitly normalized in that same metric; RHS tape is the actual nonlinear term. ETDRK4 for `u' = L u + N(t, u)` with a dense matrix `L`. Takes `steps` equal steps from `t0` to `t1` and returns the states at the `steps + 1` grid points. Fourth-order accurate for any `L`, independent of its stiffness. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for mismatched dimensions or `steps == 0`; [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if the matrix functions cannot be formed.",
        "Use real exponential Runge\u2013Kutta weights for an independent linear rate per state component. The diagonal has inverse-time units. The retained tape encodes the nonlinear part of the RHS. The trajectory includes the initial and each accepted fixed-step state, with each component restored to its physical units. The evaluation budget bounds the four tape evaluations per step. Integrates `u_i' = lambda_i u_i + N_i(t, u)` by diagonal ETDRK4. Returns the states at the `steps + 1` equally spaced grid points, including the initial state. Real scalar weights use a Taylor expansion near zero and reciprocal-power formulas for large linear rates. The nonlinear callback writes the rate vector and may couple the state components. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for an empty state, mismatched dimensions, zero steps or non-finite inputs. [`OdeError::NonFinite`] if the step or an updated state is non-finite. [`OdeError::StepLimit`] if the trajectory cannot be reserved.",
        "Actual native dense output; no invented interpolant for a discrete trajectory. Outside the solved interval returns None. Evaluates the continuous extension at `t` (available for [`dopri5`], [`rosenbrock23`], the Radau and the BDF integrators); `None` outside the integrated range.",
        "Actual native matrix exponential on explicitly dimensionless normalized matrix coefficients. Physical-rate Jacobians must first be multiplied by a matching physical time in the declared metric. Matrix exponential helper re-exported for the exponential integrators. # Errors [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if the matrix exponential cannot be computed.",
        "Actual native adaptive exponential Rosenbrock3/2 with exact native tape Jacobian, physical mixed state reconstruction and explicit attempts/evaluation budgets. Adaptive exponential Rosenbrock method `exprb32` (order 3 with an embedded order-2 estimate) for `y' = f(t, y)`. Each step linearises `f` at the current point (Jacobian `jac(t, y)` or a finite-difference one; time dependence is handled by augmenting the state with `t`), evaluates `phi_1` and `phi_3` of `h J` and applies `y+ = y + h phi_1(hJ) f + 2 h phi_3(hJ) D` with `D = N(U) - N(y)`, `U = y + h phi_1(hJ) f`, `N(y) = f(y) - J y`. Linear problems are integrated exactly. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`], [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`] or [`OdeError::SolveFailed`].",
        "Native ode::solve_fixed; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Integrates `y' = f(t, y)` from `t_span.0` to `t_span.1` in `steps` equal steps. # Errors Rejects zero steps, non-finite inputs, unrepresentable steps, or a trajectory that cannot be reserved. Returns an error when the state stops being finite.",
        "Native ode::solve_fixed_events; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Integrates in `steps` equal steps and locates events. # How an event is located without a dense output A fixed-step method has no interpolant, so there is nothing to Brent-iterate on. It is a *one-step* method, however, which means the state at any intermediate time is just another application of the same step from the same start: the locator bisects the step length, re-integrating the partial step each time, until the bracket is below `1e-13`. That is exact for the method \u2014 the located state is a point of the method's own trajectory, not of an interpolant \u2014 and it costs about fifty evaluations per event, which is the right trade for a method that is cheap by construction. A `terminal` event stops the integration; the trajectory then ends at the event point. # Errors As [`solve_fixed`].",
        "Returns only the final measured state of a retained RHS tape, preserving each component's dimension. Steps are equal; method 0 is Euler, 1 is Heun and 2 is classical Runge\u2013Kutta. maxeval bounds RHS evaluations before integration. Intermediate samples are not retained. Integrates on the same fixed grid as [`solve_fixed`] and returns its final state using scratch space proportional to the state dimension. # Errors Rejects zero steps, non-finite inputs, unrepresentable steps, non-finite states, or cancellation.",
        "Actual native gauss_legendre_step. Physical time/state/rate references are retained by the RHS tape; no alternate method is substituted. stages selects the native1/2/3-stage Gauss-Legendre method where applicable. Advance one implicit Gauss\u2013Legendre collocation step. One, two and three stages give orders two, four and six, respectively. The method is symmetric, A-stable and B-stable. In exact arithmetic it is symplectic for canonical Hamiltonian systems and preserves quadratic invariants. The returned count is the number of right-hand-side evaluations. When fixed-point iteration stalls, Newton's method linearizes the right-hand side independently at each collocation node. # Errors Returns an error for invalid or non-finite data, a callback dimension mismatch, a singular stage Jacobian, or failure of the implicit iteration.",
        "Actual tolerance-controlled native geometric driver; method-specific full/two-half-step error control, physical step bounds, native events and explicit attempt/evaluation budgets. Integrate the selected RK4 or geometric method forward using step doubling. The finite interval uses `start <= end`. The accepted state is two half steps of the requested method, without extrapolation or method substitution. Both the endpoint estimate and the midpoint defect of its cubic Hermite extension control acceptance. A trial needs a representable time strictly between its endpoints. A bracketed event between adjacent times is reported at the later timestamp. Adaptive state-dependent step selection does not retain fixed-step symplecticity. Gauss\u2013Legendre trials reuse stage storage for the duration of the solve. # Errors Invalid inputs or callback outputs, exhausted attempts/evaluations, a tolerance requiring a smaller step than `h_min`, an unsplittable time grid, or failed implicit stages.",
        "Actual fixed geometric driver with physical state components, native Hermite event localization, explicit method/steps/optional stages controls and budgets. Integrate a fixed geometric ODE method with Hermite event localization. # Errors Invalid state, time interval, callback dimensions, or failed implicit iteration.",
        "Actual native fixed-geometric requested-time sampler. Times have the RHS time dimension; mixed physical state components retain independent references. Sample a requested fixed method with `steps` substeps per output interval. # Errors Invalid grids, callback values, or geometric-stage convergence failures.",
        "Native Hermite segment; endpoints are physical state vectors and endpoint rates have each state dimension divided by the RHS time dimension. Evaluate one checked cubic Hermite segment in linear state-vector work. # Errors Empty or mismatched vectors, nonfinite inputs, a query outside the segment, or arithmetic overflow during the evaluation.",
        "Actual native imex_rk on explicit/implicit physical RHS tapes sharing one declared metric, with all3native schemes, exact implicit tape Jacobian, checked steps and shared evaluation budget. [`imex_rk_with`] with the options passed as separate arguments. # Errors See [`imex_rk_with`].",
        "Actual native imex_rk_with on explicit/implicit physical RHS tapes sharing one declared metric, with all3native schemes, exact implicit tape Jacobian, checked steps and shared evaluation budget. Additive IMEX Runge-Kutta integration of `y' = f_ex(t, y) + f_im(t, y)` with `steps` equal steps. `f_ex` (non-stiff) is treated explicitly, `f_im` (stiff) implicitly: each stage solves `Y - h gamma f_im(t, Y) = rhs` by Newton iteration with the Jacobian `jac_im(t, y)` of `f_im` (finite differences when `None`), factored once per step. Returns the states on the `steps + 1` grid points. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Actual general native Hamiltonian integrator with all8native methods. dq/dt and dp/dt tapes receive(time,q,p); q/p components carry independent physical references and rates preserve each component/time. Returned object retains actual native Hermite trajectory. Integrates a canonical Hamiltonian or partitioned system `dq/dt = dq_dt(t, q, p)`, `dp/dt = dp_dt(t, q, p)` over `span = (t0, t1)` using `steps` steps of `method`. Explicit splitting methods (`StormerVerlet`, `Ruth3`, `Ruth4`, `Yoshida6`, `Yoshida8`) are symplectic for separable Hamiltonians `H(t, q, p) = T(t, p) + V(t, q)`. Implicit Gauss\u2013Legendre Runge\u2013Kutta methods (`ImplicitMidpoint`, `GaussLegendre4`, `GaussLegendre6`) are symplectic for **arbitrary** (including non-separable) canonical Hamiltonian systems and preserve all quadratic invariants to machine precision. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] for non-finite inputs or `t1 < t0`, [`OdeGeomError::ShapeMismatch`] when `q0.len() != p0.len()`, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] when implicit Newton iteration fails.",
        "Actual native separable symplectic integrator. Acceleration tape receives(time,position) and outputs position/time\u00b2; velocity references are derived from position/time. All8native methods and original native dense data are preserved. Integrates a second-order separable system `q' = p`, `p' = accel(t, q)` using a symplectic or geometric integrator over `span = (t0, t1)` with `steps` steps. For autonomous acceleration `accel(q)` with splitting methods (`StormerVerlet`, `Ruth4`, `Yoshida6`, `Yoshida8`), this uses the exact velocity-Verlet composition matching `rssn::kernels::ode_ext::symplectic_split` (`Rssn_Numeric::ode_symplectic`), extended to support time-dependent forces, `Ruth3`, and implicit Gauss\u2013Legendre methods (`ImplicitMidpoint`, `GaussLegendre4`, `GaussLegendre6`). # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] for non-finite inputs or invalid span, [`OdeGeomError::ShapeMismatch`] when `q0.len() != p0.len()` or `accel` changes length, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if an implicit stage fails to converge.",
        "Actual native trajectory last; only valid on a native discrete trajectory. The final state, or an empty slice for an empty trajectory.",
        "Actual native leapfrog over a pure-position acceleration tape with physical position/velocity/time references and explicit evaluation budget. Velocity-Verlet (leapfrog) for `q'' = acc(q)`; returns `(q, p)` after `steps` steps of size `dt` (unit mass, `p = q'`).",
        "Actual native maximum absolute energy drift on accepted phase nodes. Hamiltonian tape receives(q,p); positive energy reference must match every q_i*p_i/time domain. Native reverse/finite-difference substitution is not involved. Maximum absolute Hamiltonian drift `max_k |H(q_k, p_k) - H(q_0, p_0)|` along the trajectory.",
        "Actual native symplectic trajectory Hermite evaluation. Independent position/momentum or velocity units are preserved; out-of-span queries remain None. Continuous cubic Hermite evaluation of `(q(t), p(t))` at any `t` in the span.",
        "Actual legacy native variable-stage Radau entry, including its true collocation dense output and event handling. Physical frames and explicit bounds match the modern Radau adapter. Variable-order Radau IIA integration; superseded by [`radau_with`]. # Errors As [`radau_with`].",
        "Native ode_stiff::radau5_fixed; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Constant-step Radau IIA(5): `n_steps` equal steps from `t0` to `t1`. The Jacobian is a finite-difference one refreshed every step and the Newton iteration is fully converged. Returns the final state; used to measure the order of convergence (5). # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Native ode_stiff::radau5_events; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Radau IIA with event detection on its collocation polynomial. The extension is the collocation polynomial through the internal stages, so an event time is located to the order of the method, not to the step size. # Errors As [`radau5`].",
        "Native ode_stiff::radau_fixed; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Constant-step Radau IIA with `stages` (3, 5 or 7) stages, i.e. order `2 stages - 1` (5, 9, 13). # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Native ode_stiff::radau5_jac; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Radau IIA(5) integration with a user-supplied Jacobian `jac(t, y)`. The method is L-stable, stiffly accurate and of order 5. The stage system is solved by simplified Newton iteration with a single LU factorisation reused across iterations and across steps whose step size is (nearly) unchanged; the Jacobian is refreshed only when the Newton iteration fails to converge. The error estimate is the embedded third-order formula of Hairer and Wanner. # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::Invalid`].",
        "Native ode_stiff::radau_with; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Variable-order Radau IIA integration (orders 5, 9 and 13) of `M y' = f(t, y)` with dense output (the collocation polynomial), event detection, structured Jacobians and index-1, 2 and 3 DAEs. The number of stages is raised while the simplified Newton iteration converges very fast (smooth, tightly toleranced problems profit from the high orders) and lowered when it converges slowly; the accepted orders are reported in [`OdeSolution::orders`]. Events are handled exactly as in [`crate::kernels::ode_adaptive::dopri5`]: direction-aware, located on the collocation polynomial, terminal events end the run. With a singular mass matrix the system is a DAE; the initial values must be consistent, and unless `opts.h0` is given the first step is `1e-5 |t1 - t0|`. For index 2 or 3 give the variable blocks in [`RadauOptions::index`] (see the module documentation). # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::Invalid`] (also for unsupported stage counts, mismatched mass/pattern/index sizes).",
        "Build an owned native tape RHS: inputs(time,state components),outputs corresponding rates. Each state may have independent physical units; tape references define normalized solver coordinates. Parameter quantities retain their own domains.",
        "Actual native rk4_step. Physical time/state/rate references are retained by the RHS tape; no alternate method is substituted. stages selects the native1/2/3-stage Gauss-Legendre method where applicable. A classical fourth-order Runge-Kutta step. # Errors Callback dimension or nonfinite-value errors, or an unrepresentable result.",
        "Actual native Rosenbrock2/3 with native event localization and dense output in independently scaled physical state coordinates. The same integrator, locating events on its Hermite extension. The extension is third order, so an event time is located to the accuracy of the step rather than to the step size; the bracket is refined by Brent iteration to `1e-14`. # Errors As [`rosenbrock23`].",
        "Actual native partitioned_ruth4_step. Physical time/state/rate references are retained by the RHS tape; no alternate method is substituted. stages selects the native1/2/3-stage Gauss-Legendre method where applicable. Partitioned 4th-order Ruth (Forest\u2013Ruth / Yoshida) step on a flat state `y = [q, p]`.",
        "Native ode_sdirk::sdirk3; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Integrates `y' = f(t, y)` from `t0` to `t1` with the adaptive SDIRK3(2) method, using the analytic Jacobian `jac(t, y)`. The step size is controlled by the embedded second-order estimate with exponent `1/3`; steps whose Newton iteration fails are rejected and retried with half the step. The cubic Hermite continuous extension of the accepted steps is exposed by [`SdirkSolution::eval`]. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for an empty state, `t0 == t1` or a Jacobian of the wrong shape; [`OdeError::NonFinite`] when the state or the estimate stops being finite; [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] when Newton iteration does not converge at the smallest step; [`OdeError::StepLimit`] when [`SdirkOptions::max_steps`] or the step-size floor is reached.",
        "Native ode_sdirk::sdirk3_events; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Adaptive SDIRK3(2) with event detection on its cubic Hermite dense output. Events follow the conventions of the other integrators: a sign change of `g(t, y)` is located by Brent iteration on the continuous extension, `direction` selects rising, falling or both crossings, and a `terminal` event ends the run at the event point, which is recorded as the last sample of [`OdeSolution::t`]. Non-terminal events are recorded in [`OdeSolution::events`] and integration continues to `t1`. The located event time inherits the `O(h\u00b3)` accuracy of the interpolation (see the module limitations), not the `1e-14` bracket tolerance of the root finder. # Errors As [`sdirk3`].",
        "Native ode_sdirk::sdirk3_fd; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. The same integrator with a forward-difference Jacobian (`n + 1` right-hand-side evaluations per Newton iterate). # Errors As [`sdirk3`].",
        "Native ode_sdirk::sdirk3_fixed; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Integrates `y' = f(t, y)` from `t0` to `t1` in `steps` equal steps with no step-size control, returning the final state. This is the entry point for convergence studies: with `steps = 2^k` the error of a third-order method falls by `8` per refinement of the step. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for an empty state or `steps == 0`, and the errors of [`sdirk3`] otherwise.",
        "Native ode_sdirk::sdirk3_fixed_events; independently measured initial components and physical times are normalized by the retained RHS tape references. Config rtol/atol are explicit normalized-state tolerances; h0/hmin/hmax are physical times and max_steps is mandatory. Controls contain steps/order/stages as documented by the native function; events contain program/terminal/direction. Real native evaluation and retained sample budgets are enforced. Fixed-step SDIRK3(2) with event detection on the cubic Hermite dense output. The `steps` equal steps are the same as in [`sdirk3_fixed`]; events follow the conventions of [`sdirk3_events`]. Returns the full solution (accepted samples, located events and dense output), so [`OdeSolution::eval`] also works. The right-end slope of every step is the last stage of the method, so the extension adds no right-hand-side evaluations. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for an empty state or `steps == 0`, and the errors of [`sdirk3_fixed`] otherwise.",
        "Actual native damped shooting-parameter solve. Positive independent parameter/residual references define the normalized Newton metric; returned parameters retain their own physical units. Original tape failures take precedence over native nonconvergence. Initial state solving a boundary residual by damped finite-difference Newton. The caller supplies an integration-backed residual. At most 60 Newton trials, each with at most 30 backtracking residuals, preserve the legacy work bound. # Errors Original residual errors or a malformed, singular, nonconverged iteration.",
        "Actual native ODE shooting with true initial-state and terminal-residual tapes, independently measured parameters/residuals and original errors. RHS uses its mixed physical state frame; config controls inner native integration rather than an unrelated substitute. General shooting method: `params -> initial state` and `final state -> residual` define the boundary conditions; the parameters are adjusted by Newton iteration so the residual vanishes. # Errors [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if the parameter iteration or an inner integration fails.",
        "Actual native symplectic split with pure-position acceleration; state contains physical positions then velocities. SplitMethod is explicit Verlet/TripleJump/Yoshida6/Yoshida8 and triple-jump order is bounded2..10. Integrates `q' = p`, `p' = accel(q)` (unit masses; for `H = |p|^2/2 + V(q)` the acceleration is `-grad V`) with a symplectic composition method and `steps` equal steps from `t0` to `t1`. Each Verlet sub-step of the composition is time-reversible and the whole map is symplectic. Returns the final `(q, p)`. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for mismatched lengths, `steps == 0` or an odd or too large order in [`SplitMethod::TripleJump`].",
        "Actual native Galerkin-Lobatto variational integrator with physical mass matrix, position and momentum. A native tape reverse gradient supplies the potential force; explicit positive M/L/T references preserve energy/force units and stage/work budgets. [`variational_lobatto_with`] with the options passed as separate arguments. # Errors See [`variational_lobatto_with`].",
        "Actual native Galerkin-Lobatto variational integrator with physical mass matrix, position and momentum. A native tape reverse gradient supplies the potential force; explicit positive M/L/T references preserve energy/force units and stage/work budgets. Galerkin variational integrator with `stages >= 2` Gauss-Lobatto nodes for `L(q, q') = q'^T M q' / 2 - V(q)`. The path inside each step is the degree-`stages - 1` polynomial through the node values; the discrete action is the Lobatto quadrature of the Lagrangian. The discrete Euler-Lagrange equations are solved by Newton's method (finite-difference Jacobian) for the interior values and the next position; momenta follow from the discrete Legendre transforms. Order `2 stages - 2`; symplectic and momentum-preserving by construction. Returns the final `(q, p)`. # Errors [`OdeError::Invalid`] for bad dimensions or `stages < 2`; [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] if Newton's iteration does not converge.",
        "Actual native partitioned_verlet_step. Physical time/state/rate references are retained by the RHS tape; no alternate method is substituted. stages selects the native1/2/3-stage Gauss-Legendre method where applicable. Partitioned St\u00f6rmer\u2013Verlet step on a flat state `y = [q, p]` (or implicit midpoint if `y.len()` is odd).",
        "Actual native named solution view; native component naming/padding is preserved while samples and events retain physical dimensions. This solution as a structured result, naming each state component. The integrator works with anonymous columns; a model knows what they are. Passing the names here is what turns `y[3][1]` into `velocity`, and it is the only place the two views meet. A name list shorter than the state is padded with `y{k}`, and one longer is truncated: a mismatch is a caller's slip, not a reason to lose the solution.",
        "Actual native fourth-order Yoshida over a pure-position acceleration tape, preserving physical phase/time references and original native evaluation errors. Fourth-order symplectic Yoshida (triple-jump) composition of leapfrog.",
        "Native analyzer of retained optimizer state; native labels use normalized objective thresholds and are heuristics,not convergence certificates. Labels the convergence quality of `result`.",
        "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Central-difference gradient of `f` at `x`.",
        "Print native normalized optimizer-state diagnostics. Particle populations project their actual best position,cost,iteration and evaluation counts to the vector State required by the native printer. Prints a short report for `result`.",
        "Physical best point/objective and actual native termination from retained optimizer state. Missing native best values remain None; mixed coordinate domains are preserved per component.",
        "Native general-phase2D oscillatory cubature; owned phase program references match physical coordinates and omega has inverse phase dimension. Evaluation limit applies separately to each compiled program. General-phase fallback for `integral f(x, y) exp(i omega phi(x, y)) dx dy` over the box `[lo, hi]`. [`filon2d`] is moment-corrected only for a linear phase. For a nonlinear phase this fallback integrates the real and imaginary oscillating integrands directly with [`cubature_genz_malik`]: deterministic and reliable while the adaptive partition resolves the oscillation, but its cost grows with `|omega|` (there is no frequency-independent 2-D Levin collocation). The tolerance is used as both absolute and relative tolerance of the two cubatures, with an integrand budget of `OSC2D_MAX_EVALS` evaluations per component. A non-finite phase at the box corners, edge midpoints or centre, or any other invalid argument, returns `NaN` with `converged == false`.",
        "Native advection_1d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t + a u_x = 0` on the periodic domain `[x0, x1)` with `n` nodes (`x1` is identified with `x0`). Stable for `|a| dt / dx <= 1`, checked up front. # Errors [`PdeError::Unstable`], [`PdeError::InvalidInput`] or [`PdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Native advection_2d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t + a_x u_x + a_y u_y = 0` on the periodic rectangle `[x0, x1) x [y0, y1)` (the upper ends are identified with the lower ones). # Errors [`PdeError::Unstable`] when a directional Courant number exceeds one, [`PdeError::InvalidInput`] or [`PdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Native advection_3d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t + a . grad(u) = 0` on the periodic box `[x0, x1) x [y0, y1) x [z0, z1)`. # Errors As [`advection_2d`].",
        "Retain an immutable compiled two-coordinate physical boundary program, with lifetime evaluation budget and dependency-tracked program storage. Dirichlet condition with a position-dependent value.",
        "Constant measured Dirichlet data in the retained grid reference frame. Constant Dirichlet value.",
        "Native maximum nodal error against an owned physical exact-solution program; returns measured ordinate error. Maximum absolute difference to `exact(x)` over all nodes.",
        "Checked native2D field sample retains measured ordinate units. Value at node `(i, j)`.",
        "Native maximum nodal error against an owned physical exact-solution program; returns measured ordinate error. Maximum absolute difference to `exact(x, y)` over all nodes.",
        "Checked native2D field node coordinates retain measured length units. Coordinates of node `(i, j)`.",
        "Checked native3D field sample with physical field units.",
        "Actual native3D maximum nodal error against an immutable physical exact-solution program, with original evaluation failures preserved. Maximum absolute difference to `exact(x, y, z)` over all nodes.",
        "Checked actual native3D node coordinates in physical length units. Coordinates of node `(i, j, k)`.",
        "Retrieve native field samples,shape,and physical snapshot time with preserved measurement units.",
        "Construct an actual native1D/2D/3D rectangular grid from physical bounds and explicit positive length/time/value references. Shape product is bounded before native allocations.",
        "Physical native grid spacing in the retained length domain. Grid spacing for a bounded grid.",
        "Native1D grid nodes restored to measured coordinates. Node coordinates of a bounded grid.",
        "Physical native grid spacing in the retained length domain. Spacing in `x`.",
        "Physical native grid spacing in the retained length domain. Spacing in `y`.",
        "Actual native3D grid method; physical spacings retain the grid length reference. Spacing in `x`.",
        "Actual native3D grid method; physical spacings retain the grid length reference. Spacing in `y`.",
        "Actual native3D grid method; physical spacings retain the grid length reference. Spacing in `z`.",
        "Actual native3D grid method; physical spacings retain the grid length reference.",
        "Actual native3D grid method; physical spacings retain the grid length reference.",
        "Native unit-cube grid interpreted through explicit physical references, bounded before native allocation. Unit cube with `n` nodes per axis.",
        "Native unit-square grid interpreted through explicit physical references. Unit square with `n x n` nodes.",
        "Native heat_1d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t = alpha u_xx` on `grid` with Crank-Nicolson time stepping (`steps` equal steps up to `t_end`); `bc = [left, right]`. The scheme is second-order in space and time and unconditionally stable. # Errors [`PdeError::InvalidInput`] for a bad grid, step count or `alpha <= 0`; [`PdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`PdeError::NonFinite`] otherwise.",
        "Native heat_2d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t = alpha (u_xx + u_yy)` with Crank-Nicolson; `bc = [left, right, bottom, top]`. # Errors As [`heat_1d`].",
        "Native heat_3d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_t = alpha (u_xx + u_yy + u_zz)` with Crank-Nicolson; `bc = [x-, x+, y-, y+, z-, z+]`. # Errors As [`heat_1d`](super::heat_1d).",
        "Native laplace_1d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_xx = 0` on a 1D grid. # Errors As [`poisson_1d`].",
        "Native laplace_2d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `Laplace(u) = 0` (see [`poisson_2d`]). # Errors As [`poisson_2d`].",
        "Native laplace_3d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `Laplace(u) = 0` on a 3D grid. # Errors As [`poisson_3d`].",
        "Native stiff1D method of lines. Compiled RHS inputs have domains(time,length,value,value/length,value/length^2),output value/time. Initial program is length->value. Method Radau5 or Bdf1..Bdf5; physical atol and all step bounds retain the grid time/value domains; explicit attempted-step and per-program evaluation budgets. field_data exposes native accepted steps/evaluations. Method of lines for `u_t = F(t, x, u, u_x, u_xx)` on a 1D grid with a stiff ODE solver. Semi-discretises with second-order central differences (ghost nodes for Neumann ends, Dirichlet nodes held fixed) and integrates the ODE system to `t_end`. Each boundary callback is evaluated once at `(endpoint, 0)` and its spatial value is used throughout the solve. # Errors [`PdeError::InvalidInput`] for a bad grid or `t_end`, [`PdeError::Ode`] if the integrator fails.",
        "Stiff 1D method of lines with two time-dependent compiled boundary programs in left-to-right order. Set each kind to Dirichlet for a measured field value or Neumann for a measured outward derivative. Each boundary program takes a measured Time. The returned field retains physical samples and exact prescribed Dirichlet endpoint values at the requested final time. Integrates a 1D method-of-lines problem with time-dependent endpoint data. The endpoint callbacks receive each RHS evaluation time, including internal integrator stages. Dirichlet endpoints are excluded from the ODE unknowns. Neumann data are outward normal derivatives. The returned field includes the prescribed Dirichlet values at `t_end`. # Errors Returns [`PdeError::InvalidInput`] for an invalid grid or final time, [`PdeError::NonFinite`] for non-finite boundary data, or [`PdeError::Ode`] when the integrator fails.",
        "Retain an immutable compiled outward-gradient boundary program; field/length output and physical tangential coordinates are checked. Neumann condition with a position-dependent outward flux.",
        "Constant measured outward normal gradient has field/length dimension. Constant Neumann flux (`0.0` for an insulated / reflecting wall).",
        "Native poisson_1d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `-u_xx = f` on a 1D grid; `bc = [left, right]`. # Errors As [`poisson_2d`](super::poisson_2d).",
        "Native poisson_2d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `-(u_xx + u_yy) = f` with the 5-point stencil; `bc = [left, right, bottom, top]`. A problem with Neumann conditions on all four sides is singular and is rejected with [`PdeError::Unsupported`], unless `solver` is [`PoissonSolver::MeanFree`], which solves it in the zero-mean subspace when the data are compatible. # Errors [`PdeError::InvalidInput`], [`PdeError::Unsupported`] (pure Neumann, or CG with a Neumann side), [`PdeError::SolveFailed`] (singular matrix or CG not converged), [`PdeError::Incompatible`] or [`PdeError::NotConverged`] ([`PoissonSolver::MeanFree`]).",
        "Native poisson_3d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `-(u_xx + u_yy + u_zz) = f` with the 7-point stencil; `bc = [x-, x+, y-, y+, z-, z+]`. # Errors As [`poisson_2d`](super::poisson_2d).",
        "Native PdeProblem dispatch. Kind0..2heat1D..3D,3..5wave,6..8Poisson,12..14advection; use dedicated Laplace endpoints for9..11. Reference and compiled-function contracts match dedicated native solvers. Solves a [`PdeProblem`] with the matching finite-difference scheme. # Errors Any [`PdeError`] of the underlying solver.",
        "Native wave_1d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_tt = c^2 u_xx` with the explicit leapfrog scheme; `v0` is the initial velocity `u_t(x, 0)`. The scheme is second-order and stable for the Courant number `c dt / dx <= 1`, which is checked up front. # Errors [`PdeError::Unstable`] when the CFL condition fails, or the errors of [`heat_1d`].",
        "Native wave_2d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_tt = c^2 (u_xx + u_yy)` with leapfrog; stable for `c dt sqrt(1/dx^2 + 1/dy^2) <= 1`. # Errors As [`wave_1d`].",
        "Native wave_3d in the grid physical reference frame. Boundary handles share that frame. Heat coefficient L^2/T,wave/advection L/T; primary compiled output V for initial data or V/L^2 for Poisson; secondary V/T only for wave. Method SparseLu/Cg/MeanFree for elliptic,Upwind/LaxWendroff for advection; relative>0. Dedicated Laplace ignores primary; other unused coefficients/secondary are explicit placeholder Values. Solves `u_tt = c^2 (u_xx + u_yy + u_zz)` with leapfrog; stable for `c dt sqrt(1/dx^2 + 1/dy^2 + 1/dz^2) <= 1`. # Errors As [`wave_1d`](super::wave_1d).",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. One backward Euler step: `y1 = y0 + h f(t + h, y1)`.",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE system using the Bogacki-Shampine 2(3) method.",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE system using the Cash-Karp 4(5) method.",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE system forward in time using the Dormand-Prince 5(4) method. The output includes the initial state and every accepted step. The last time is exactly the requested endpoint when it is reached. Invalid controls, non-finite rates, or a step that cannot advance return the accepted prefix. Tolerances are nonnegative and at least one must be positive.",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Creates a new Dormand-Prince 5(4) solver.",
        "Evolves a native measured ODE tape using Forward, Midpoint or Heun with a signed time step. The last interval is shortened to reach the requested endpoint. Returns time and mixed physical state for each accepted grid point. Forward needs one tape evaluation per step and Midpoint and Heun need two. Solves an ODE with a fixed Euler formula, ending exactly at the requested time. The sign of `dt` must agree with the direction of travel. Times are located on the nominal grid from the start, without accumulating rounding drift. State updates use the represented interval between successive grid points. The last step ends at `t_span.1`, shortening the interval if necessary. A zero-length span returns the initial state without evaluating the RHS. # Errors Returns an error for invalid times, a zero or oppositely signed step, mismatched dimensions, non-finite states or right-hand sides, unrepresentable progress, or a history whose size cannot be represented or reserved.",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE with first-order forward Euler and a complete step at each sample. For an endpoint between grid points the final sample uses a full step. Use [`solve_explicit_euler`] for a checked solve ending at the requested time.",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE with second-order Heun and complete steps. Use [`solve_explicit_euler`] to terminate exactly at the requested time.",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. One implicit midpoint step: `y1 = y0 + h f(t + h/2, (y0 + y1)/2)` (symplectic and A-stable, order 2).",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE with second-order explicit midpoint and complete steps. Use [`solve_explicit_euler`] to terminate exactly at the requested time.",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Solves an ODE system using the classic 4th-order Runge-Kutta method with a fixed step size. This method is a widely used, robust, and relatively accurate explicit method for approximating the solutions of ordinary differential equations. # Arguments * `system` - The ODE system to solve, implementing the `OdeSystem` trait. * `y0` - The initial state vector. * `t_span` - A tuple `(t_start, t_end)` specifying the time interval. * `dt` - The fixed time step. # Returns A `Vec` of tuples `(time, state_vector)` representing the solution at each time step.",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors. Integrates `y' = f(t, y)` from `t0` to `t1` with the adaptive Rosenbrock method ROS2 (L-stable, order 2; `\u03b3 = 1 + 1/\u221a2`), the error estimated against the linearly implicit Euler step. Returns the accepted `(t, y)` pairs, or `None` when a linear system is singular or the step size underflows.",
        "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Simulates a damped harmonic oscillator.",
        "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Solves a 2D orbital mechanics problem using the energy-conserving semi-implicit Euler method. This scenario simulates the orbit of a celestial body around a much more massive star, demonstrating the semi-implicit Euler method's ability to preserve energy and produce stable, realistic orbits over long simulation times. # Returns A `Vec` of tuples `(time, state_vector)` representing the solution path. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_semi_implicit_euler` function encounters an error, e.g., if the initial state vector dimensions are incorrect.",
        "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Simulates the Lorenz attractor system.",
        "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Simulates the Lotka-Volterra predator-prey system.",
        "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Solves the damped harmonic oscillator with the less stable forward Euler method. This scenario demonstrates the numerical energy gain over time that can occur with the forward Euler method when solving oscillatory systems, highlighting its limitations for such problems. # Returns A `Vec` of tuples `(time, state_vector)` representing the solution path.",
        "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Solves a stiff ODE system, demonstrating the stability of the backward Euler method. This scenario highlights how the backward Euler method can handle stiff equations with relatively large time steps, whereas a forward method would become unstable. # Returns A `Result` containing the solution path as `Vec<(f64, Vec<f64>)>`. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_backward_euler_linear` function encounters an error, e.g., if matrix inversion fails.",
        "Actual native fixed built-in scenario with its literal normalized parameters. Returned normalized time/state values are explicitly dimensionless; use a physical RHS model for user-specified quantities. Simulates the Van der Pol oscillator.",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors.",
        "Actual native generic ODE solver instantiated on an immutable tape implementing the real OdeSystem trait. Independent physical state/time references, normalized rtol/atol and a shared explicit oracle budget are preserved; incomplete native paths are errors.",
        "Uses the prepared positions and weights with one measured charge per particle. Returns a periodic mesh density in charge per unit length. Deposits one charge per prepared particle as density per unit length. # Errors The charge array length differs from the prepared particle count.",
        "Interpolates one measured value per cell at the prepared particle positions. A positive field reference with the same dimension specifies the normalization. Results keep the field dimension and original particle order. Evaluates the prepared stencils on a mesh field. # Errors The field does not have one entry per grid cell.",
        "Prepares the periodic grid stencils of fixed measured positions in particle order. Select Linear (or CIC) or Cubic to share the same interpolation weights between deposition and gathering. Prepares the periodic weights for repeated transfers at fixed positions. # Errors A position is non-finite.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Validates a configuration. # Errors If the mesh or the box length is invalid.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Deposits `q` at `pos` and returns the charge density per unit length. Positions are wrapped periodically. The two slices are zipped, so a trailing element of the longer slice is ignored.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. The electric field at the cell centres from the last half step.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Gathers a mesh quantity to the particles with the same shape weights. # Errors If `field` does not have one entry per cell.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. The mesh.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Number of cells.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Box length.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Creates a periodic mesh with `cells` cells on a box of length `length`. # Errors If `cells` is not a power of two of at least 8, `length` is not finite and positive, or the spacing rounds to zero.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Cell size `length / cells`.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Creates a system from equal-length particle arrays. # Errors If the configuration is invalid, the arrays differ in length, there are no particles, or a mass is not finite and positive.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Particle positions.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. The shape function.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Number of mesh cells a particle contributes to (the stencil width).",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Order of the shape's interpolation error (the field error at particles).",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Solves `-d^2 phi / dx^2 = rho` and returns `phi` and `E = -d phi / dx`. The zero mode is dropped, i.e. a uniform neutralising background is assumed. The `CIC` structure factor is divided out of the linear mesh density; the cubic kernel reproduces cubics, so it needs no correction. # Errors If `rho` does not have one entry per cell.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. One leapfrog step: half kick, drift, field solve, half kick. # Errors If `dt` is not finite and positive, or the mesh solve fails.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Total charge.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Total momentum `sum m v`.",
        "Actual native electrostatic PIC operation. SI length/time/charge domains and an explicit positive permittivity-times-transverse-area reference define the native unit-coefficient1D Poisson model. Mass and electric references follow Q\u00b2T\u00b2/(epsilon_area L) and Q/epsilon_area; no SI constant is guessed. Particle velocities.",
        "Native normalized polynomial value and exact polynomial derivative, restored with independent positive coordinate and output references. Evaluates a polynomial (coefficients in ascending order) and its derivative at a complex point with Horner's scheme.",
        "Evaluate F(to) minus F(from) using a divided difference and the measured interval width. Both endpoints share the prepared coordinate dimension. The result keeps the polynomial coefficient dimension. Evaluate `F(to) - F(from)` without subtracting two rounded values.",
        "Evaluate the prepared polynomial antidifference at a measured coordinate. Its result keeps the polynomial coefficient dimension. Evaluate the antidifference at one point.",
        "Prepare the discrete antidifference for up to 128 ascending polynomial coefficients. Use normalized coordinate u = (x - origin) / step. The origin and positive step have the same dimension, and all coefficients have the common value dimension. The prepared function F is zero at the origin and F(x + step) - F(x) is the polynomial evaluated at u. Compile the antidifference of the given ascending power coefficients.",
        "Native active-set QP with positive coordinate,objective,equality and inequality references. H has E/D^2,g E/D,Aeq C/D,beq C,Aineq G/D,bineq G; result step D and multipliers E/C,E/G. Solves `min 0.5 d^T H d + c^T d` subject to `A_eq d = b_eq` and `A_ineq d <= b_ineq` by a primal active-set method started from `d = 0`, which must be feasible (`b_ineq >= 0`). The KKT system of the working set is solved exactly at every step, so the returned multipliers are those of the Lagrangian `0.5 d^T H d + c^T d + lambda . (A_eq d - b_eq) + mu . (A_ineq d - b_ineq)`. Returns `None` if a KKT system stays singular after regularisation or the working set fails to settle within the iteration budget.",
        "Normalized native quadrature::exp_sinh: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Exp-sinh quadrature on `[a, inf)`; suitable for algebraic or exponential decay and endpoint singularities at `a`.",
        "Normalized native quadrature::filon: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length2, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Filon quadrature for `integral_a^b f(x) cos(w x) dx` (`cosine = true`) or `sin(w x)` (`cosine = false`), using `2 * panels` sub-intervals. Exact when `f` is quadratic on each double panel, so the accuracy does not degrade as the frequency `w` grows.",
        "Integrates a normalized callback with a shared tolerance and panel budget across known interior breakpoints. The Float parameters are `[a, b, abs_tol, rel_tol, ...breakpoints]` and the integer controls are `[rule, max_panels]` with rule 0 for G7K15 or 1 for G10K21. Pass positive coordinate and integrand reference scales and a callback evaluation budget. The result value and error have the product of the two reference dimensions. Adaptive Gauss\u2013Kronrod integration split at known interior breakpoints. Supply breakpoints in increasing coordinate order even for reversed limits. Kronrod samples each subinterval's interior, avoiding the breakpoints for ordinary representable intervals. The panel budget and the tolerance `max(abs_tol, rel_tol * |value|)` apply to the entire partition. Exhausting the refinement budget returns an estimate with `converged = false` if the error is still above tolerance. # Errors Returns an error for invalid endpoints, tolerances or breakpoints, an insufficient initial panel budget, a nonfinite estimate, or cancellation.",
        "Normalized native quadrature::gk_panel: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. One Gauss-Kronrod panel on `[a, b]` returning `(kronrod, error, evals)`. The error is the QUADPACK heuristic built from the Gauss/Kronrod difference and the integral of `|f - mean|`.",
        "Normalized native quadrature::integrate: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Adaptive G7K15 integration with a default panel budget (2000). `tol` is the relative tolerance (absolute is `1e-3 * tol`).",
        "Normalized native quadrature::sinh_sinh: parameters Float vector length1, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Sinh-sinh quadrature on `(-inf, inf)` for rapidly decaying integrands.",
        "Normalized native quadrature::tanh_sinh: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Tanh-sinh quadrature on `[a, b]`. Handles integrable endpoint singularities (`1/sqrt(x)`, `log x`, ...) because the nodes cluster double-exponentially at the end points and the integrand is never evaluated at them. `tol` is the relative tolerance.",
        "Native serial Halton/Sobol cubature with checked physical compiled-function references; sequence0=Halton,1=Sobol. Quasi-Monte Carlo integral of `f` over the box `[lo, hi]`. The error estimate is the difference to the estimate that uses only the first half of the points. Matching bounds may describe a reversed axis. Returns an undefined integral without evaluating `f` for mismatched bounds, non-finite axis widths, unsupported dimensions, or a zero sample count.",
        "True native parallel Halton/Sobol cubature with checked physical compiled-function references; sequence0=Halton,1=Sobol. Parallel [`quasi_monte_carlo`] with a fixed summation order. Each block starts at its exact sequence index and then advances locally. Blocks of 4096 points are summed concurrently, and their sums are added in block order. Different thread counts give the same result. The serial implementation adds the same samples with a different association.",
        "Evaluate the retained native regression CostFunction with measured intercept/slope coefficients; returns physical half mean-squared residual units.",
        "Return the half mean-squared residual and its coefficient gradient using the same residual pass. If predictor units are D and response units are E, coefficients use E then E/D, value uses E squared, and the gradient uses E then E times D. The gradient preserves the intercept-first coefficient order. Half the mean squared residual and its coefficient gradient. The intercept is the first coefficient, followed by one slope per column of the design. Both outputs come from the same residual pass. # Errors Returns an error when the design is empty, the row counts differ, or the coefficient count is not `columns + 1`.",
        "Normalized native rootfind::root_near: parameters Float vector length1, exact nonnegative integer controls length0, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. A root of `f` near `guess`, by Newton's method with a bisection fallback. If no sign change is found, a local minimum of the residual magnitude can identify a touching root. A callback that rounds residuals to zero limits positional accuracy. Positive offsets below the local resolution can be indistinguishable from a touching root. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for a non-finite guess or a non-finite value inside a bracket. [`RootError::NoConvergence`] when no sign change or resolved touching root is found.",
        "Native homotopy_univariate on normalized polynomial coefficients with a positive physical coordinate reference; returned complex roots restore its units. Degree and iteration work are bounded before native calls. Homotopy continuation for the roots of a real-coefficient polynomial. Homotopy continuation for a polynomial with real coefficients: tracks the roots of `H(z, t) = (1-t) * gamma * (z^n - 1) + t * p(z)` from the roots of unity at `t = 0` to those of `p` at `t = 1` with predictor (Euler) and Newton corrector steps and adaptive step size. This is the one-variable instance of total-degree homotopy continuation; the random complex `gamma` is fixed (deterministic) so generic paths do not cross. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for constant polynomials, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] if a path fails to be tracked.",
        "Native polynomial_roots_companion on normalized polynomial coefficients with a positive physical coordinate reference; returned complex roots restore its units. Degree and iteration work are bounded before native calls. Roots of a polynomial as eigenvalues of its companion matrix. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for constant polynomials, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] if the QR iteration fails.",
        "Native polynomial_roots_jenkins_traub_complex on normalized polynomial coefficients with a positive physical coordinate reference; returned complex roots restore its units. Degree and iteration work are bounded before native calls. All complex roots of a complex polynomial by Jenkins-Traub. All complex roots of a polynomial with complex coefficients (`(re, im)` pairs, ascending order) by the three-stage Jenkins-Traub algorithm (no-shift, fixed-shift and variable-shift stages with shifts on a circle just inside the smallest root modulus), polished by Newton iteration against the original polynomial. Roots are found in order of increasing modulus and deflated forward, which is the numerically stable direction. Roots at zero are detected from vanishing low-order coefficients. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for constant polynomials, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] if the shift iteration fails.",
        "Native polynomial_roots_jenkins_traub on normalized polynomial coefficients with a positive physical coordinate reference; returned complex roots restore its units. Degree and iteration work are bounded before native calls. All complex roots of a real-coefficient polynomial (ascending order) by the Jenkins-Traub algorithm; see [`polynomial_roots_jenkins_traub_complex`]. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for constant polynomials, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] if the shift iteration fails.",
        "Actual native sde_adaptive with independently scaled physical state, normalized rtol/atol, seed, max_steps, and explicit evaluation budget. Noncommutative variants require bounded cells/terms and retain genuine conditional Levy areas. [`sde_adaptive_with`] with the options passed as separate arguments. # Errors See [`sde_adaptive_with`].",
        "Actual native sde_adaptive_noncommutative_stratonovich_with with independently scaled physical state, normalized rtol/atol, seed, max_steps, and explicit evaluation budget. Noncommutative variants require bounded cells/terms and retain genuine conditional Levy areas. Adaptive-step integration of a **Stratonovich** SDE with **non-commutative** noise on `[t0, t1]`, by exact conversion to Ito form. As [`sde_adaptive_stratonovich_with`], with the controller of [`sde_adaptive_noncommutative_with`] (fine cell grid, Chen's relation); the returned path is the controlled Ito-Milstein solution of the equivalent Ito SDE, which converges to the Stratonovich solution. The Brownian path is the same as for the Ito solver ([`noncommutative_noise_grid`]). # Errors As [`sde_adaptive_noncommutative_with`].",
        "Actual native sde_adaptive_noncommutative_with with independently scaled physical state, normalized rtol/atol, seed, max_steps, and explicit evaluation budget. Noncommutative variants require bounded cells/terms and retain genuine conditional Levy areas. Adaptive-step Milstein integration of an Ito SDE with **non-commutative** noise on `[t0, t1]`. The Brownian path is fixed in advance on a grid of `opts.cells` cells, each with its increment and its Levy areas ([`levy_areas`]); a step is a union of cells, whose increment and areas are combined *exactly* by Chen's relation `A_{0,2h} = A_1 + A_2 + (W_1 x W_2 - W_2 x W_1) / 2`. Splitting a rejected step is therefore merging fewer of the same cells: the realised path is the same for every step sequence, the errors of the controller cannot bias the noise, and a fixed-step reference on the cells sees exactly the path the adaptive solver used ([`noncommutative_noise_grid`]). The error control is that of [`sde_adaptive_with`], with the Milstein correction built from the areas (for commutative noise the correction is the same function of the increments, so the estimate and the controller agree with it in law). A step of a single cell that is still rejected ends the run with [`SdeError::StepLimit`]: use more cells. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`], [`SdeError::NonFinite`] or [`SdeError::StepLimit`].",
        "Actual native sde_adaptive_stratonovich_with with independently scaled physical state, normalized rtol/atol, seed, max_steps, and explicit evaluation budget. Noncommutative variants require bounded cells/terms and retain genuine conditional Levy areas. Adaptive-step integration of a **Stratonovich** SDE `dX = a dt + g o dW` with commutative noise on `[t0, t1]`, by exact conversion to Ito form. The drift is replaced by the Ito-equivalent drift ([`stratonovich_to_ito_drift`]) and [`sde_adaptive_with`] runs on the Ito SDE `dX = a~ dt + g dW`, which has the same solution process. The returned path is therefore the *controlled Ito-Milstein solution of the equivalent Ito SDE*; it converges to the Stratonovich solution of the original coefficients (for `dX = sigma X o dW` to `X_0 exp(sigma W_T)`), with the error control of [`sde_adaptive_with`] applied to the converted drift. The conversion uses finite-difference derivatives of `g`, which costs `2m + 1` extra diffusion evaluations per drift evaluation. # Errors As [`sde_adaptive_with`].",
        "Actual native sde_adaptive_with with independently scaled physical state, normalized rtol/atol, seed, max_steps, and explicit evaluation budget. Noncommutative variants require bounded cells/terms and retain genuine conditional Levy areas. Adaptive-step integration of an Ito SDE with commutative noise on `[t0, t1]`. Each step computes the Euler-Maruyama and the Milstein update from the same Brownian increment; the Milstein correction plus the trapezoidal drift correction `h |a(y_EM) - a(y)| / 2` estimate the local error of the former (so additive noise and strongly varying drifts both control the step). A step is accepted when the scaled error `max_i |delta_i| / (atol + rtol |y_i|)` is at most one (the tolerance bounds the local, not the global, pathwise error; the global error accumulates like a random walk). Rejected increments are split by Brownian bridges and re-used, so the realised path is a genuine Brownian sample independent of the step sequence. The returned solution is the Milstein one. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`], [`SdeError::NonFinite`] or [`SdeError::StepLimit`].",
        "Actual native Chen aggregation retains the same physical Brownian path and iterated integrals. Merges groups of `group` consecutive steps. The increments add up and the iterated integrals combine exactly as `I_jl = sum_k I_jl^k + sum_{k<k'} dW_j^k dW_l^{k'}`. Used to compare a coarse scheme with a fine reference on the same Brownian path.",
        "Actual native Brownian increments in physical sqrt(time) units. h must be strictly positive; shape and allocation budgets are checked. Generates `steps` Brownian increments (`m` components each, variance `h`) from `rng`.",
        "Native drift-implicit path over retained Brownian increments. All five scheme variants use physical time and state references and share a checked evaluation budget. Fixed-step drift-implicit integration over prescribed Wiener increments. Rejects inconsistent shapes, invalid timesteps, non-finite coefficients and nonconvergent implicit equations. Milstein includes all commutative cross terms.",
        "Runs all drift-implicit steps over the retained Brownian increments and returns the initial state, every state at a positive multiple of the stride, and the final state once. State units and callback evaluation limits match the full path. Runs every fixed drift-implicit step while retaining selected states. The result starts at `y0`, includes the state after every `save_every` increments, and ends at the final state without duplicating it. A path with no increments returns only `y0`. Saved states use the same arithmetic and callback evaluations as [`drift_implicit_core`]. No randomness is drawn internally. `t0` and `dt` are times, `increments` are Wiener increments, and the states retain the coordinate units of `y0`. # Errors Rejects zero retention stride, inconsistent shapes, a non-advancing grid, non-finite coefficients or states, and nonconvergent implicit equations.",
        "Native drift-implicit single step using the specified retained conditional Levy-area sample and its iterated integrals. One drift-implicit step with prescribed Brownian increments and iterated It\u00f4 integrals. Noncommutative Milstein uses the supplied full iterated-integral matrix; Euler/theta schemes do not need those integrals. Invalid shapes and nonconvergent Newton iterations return errors.",
        "Actual parallel native ensemble on immutable owned drift/diffusion tapes. The explicit global evaluation budget is shared atomically across real parallel members; original failures are preserved. Returned final states retain independent physical units. Simulates `paths` independent sample paths of `dX = a dt + g dW` with `steps` fixed steps of size `h` and returns the end state of each, in path order. Path `i` draws its Brownian increments from `Rng::new(ensemble_seed(seed, i))`, so the result is independent of the thread count and equals running the paths one after the other. The first error (in path order) is returned. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`] from a member.",
        "Actual deterministic native ensemble member seed. Seed of member `index` of an ensemble with base `seed`: a function of the pair only, so the members do not depend on how many threads run them or in which order.",
        "Construct the actual native invalid-parameter error object; text is bounded and preserved. Convenience constructor for [`Self::Invalid`].",
        "Inspect the retained native SDE extension error text.",
        "Actual native euler_maruyama over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Euler-Maruyama path with explicit increments; `dws[k]` is the increment over `[t0 + k h, t0 + (k + 1) h]`. Returns the `dws.len() + 1` states. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Actual native heun_stratonovich over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Heun predictor-corrector for the *Stratonovich* interpretation `dX = a dt + g o dW` (strong order 1 for commutative noise, converges to the Stratonovich solution). # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Evaluate the actual native converted Ito drift. Each result component has its state unit divided by the time unit, with a checked shared evaluation budget.",
        "Actual native conditional Levy areas. Supplied increments have sqrt(time) units and output flattened m by m Ito integrals have time units. Samples the iterated Ito integrals `I_jl` of one step of length `h`. The integrals are drawn conditionally on the increments `dw` by the Fourier expansion of the Brownian bridge (Kloeden, Platen and Wright) with the Gaussian approximation of the truncated tail that reproduces its full conditional covariance (Wiktorsson's improvement): with `xi = dw / sqrt(h)`, `I_jl / h = xi_j xi_l / 2 + A_jl + sqrt(rho) (mu_j xi_l - mu_l xi_j) + sqrt(rho) nu_jl` for `j < l`, where `A` is the Fourier sum over `terms` modes, `rho` the tail sum divided by `2 pi^2`, and `mu`, `nu` standard normal. The remainder error decays like `1 / terms^{3/2}` in distribution; a few terms suffice for strong order one. `dw` contains the `m` supplied increments. The returned matrix is flat and row-major. Pair evaluation can run in parallel, with the same RNG draws and floating-point summation order for every worker count.",
        "Actual native milstein over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Milstein scheme (strong order 1) for commutative noise; arguments as in [`euler_maruyama`]. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Actual native milstein_noncommutative over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Milstein scheme (strong order 1) for **non-commutative** noise. The correction uses the sampled iterated integrals of `noise` instead of the symmetric products `(dW_j dW_k - delta_jk h) / 2`. For commutative noise it is equivalent to [`milstein`] in law. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Actual native milstein_noncommutative_stratonovich over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Milstein scheme (strong order 1) for **non-commutative** noise in the *Stratonovich* interpretation `dX = a dt + g o dW`. The correction is `sum_{j,l} (D_j g)_{.l} J_jl` with the Stratonovich iterated integrals `J_jl = I_jl + delta_jl h / 2 = dW_j dW_l / 2 + A_jl` (`A` the Levy area), i.e. the same sampled areas as [`milstein_noncommutative`] with the diagonal shifted by `h / 2`. The drift is the Stratonovich drift, unchanged. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Actual native milstein_stratonovich over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Milstein scheme (strong order 1) for the *Stratonovich* interpretation `dX = a dt + g o dW` of the same coefficients; arguments as in [`euler_maruyama`]. It differs from the Ito [`milstein`] only in the iterated integral of the correction, `I_jk = dW_j dW_k / 2` instead of `(dW_j dW_k - delta_jk h) / 2`: for scalar noise the correction is `g g' dW^2 / 2` rather than `g g' (dW^2 - h) / 2`, so the two differ by the Ito-Stratonovich drift correction `g g' h / 2` and converge to different solutions. For `dX = sigma X dW` they are `X_0 exp(sigma W_T)` (here) and `X_0 exp(sigma W_T - sigma^2 T / 2)` ([`milstein`]). # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Owned native drift/diffusion tapes. Diffusion outputs are row-major n by m and have component dimensions state/sqrt(time); all input/output reference scales are explicit and may differ from the drift tape frame.",
        "Physical Brownian/Levy arrays from a retained sample; increments have sqrt(time) units and areas have time units.",
        "Actual native reproducible noncommutative grid. The normalized [0,1] reference frame is explicitly a physical time reference equal to the requested positive span; state-independent noise retains genuine conditional areas. The fine-cell Brownian path of [`sde_adaptive_noncommutative_with`] as a [`NoiseSample`]. Step `h = (t1 - t0) / cells` on `[t0, t1]`; use it to compare the adaptive solution with a fine fixed-step [`milstein_noncommutative`] reference on the *same* path.",
        "Physical native accepted samples and original rejection/seed statistics; no interpolant is invented for stochastic paths.",
        "Native callback-based alpha-stable sampler using the retained RNG. Scale and location have the same physical domain; alpha/beta are dimensionless. Samples an alpha-stable random variable `S_alpha(scale, beta, location)` via Chambers-Mallows-Stuck (1976). At `alpha = 2` this uses one standard normal draw. Otherwise it consumes two uniform draws from `[0, 1)` and uses the S1 scale and location parametrization, including its logarithmic correction at `alpha = 1`. # Errors Invalid parameters, invalid uniform draws, or a non-finite sample.",
        "Actual native conditional Brownian increments/Levy areas. Positive time reference defines normalized stochastic time; physical h has the same dimension. RNG state advances exactly through the native routine. Samples `steps` Brownian increments and their iterated integrals ([`levy_areas`]) for `m` Wiener processes and step size `h`.",
        "Native callback-based Poisson sampler instantiated with the retained native RNG's actual unit/normal streams. Exact Poisson sampling: Knuth multiplication for small means and Hoermann transformed rejection (PTRS) for large means. The normal callback is retained for source compatibility and is unused.",
        "Actual native srk_noncommutative over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Derivative-free stochastic Runge-Kutta scheme of strong order 1 for **non-commutative** Ito noise (Kloeden-Platen, eq. 11.1.7). With the supporting values `U^j = Y + a h + g^j sqrt(h)` (`g^j` the `j`-th column of `g`, one per noise channel), `Y' = Y + a h + sum_j g^j dW_j + h^{-1/2} sum_{j,l} (g^l(U^j) - g^l(Y)) I_jl`, where `I_jl` are the sampled iterated Ito integrals of `noise` ([`sample_noise`]). It needs `m` extra diffusion evaluations per step and no derivative of `g`. On commutative noise the Levy-area part cancels and it agrees with [`srk_platen`] up to the `O(h)` of the supporting values. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Actual native srk_noncommutative_stratonovich over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. The Stratonovich counterpart of [`srk_noncommutative`]. After Kloeden-Platen, eq. 11.1.10: with the Stratonovich iterated integrals `J_jl = I_jl + delta_jl h / 2` and the Stratonovich drift it converges to the solution of `dX = a dt + g o dW` with strong order 1. Because `J_jj` has mean `h / 2` (unlike `I_jj`), the difference quotient must be accurate to `O(h)`: the scheme uses the *central* supporting values `U^j_{+-} = Y +- g^j sqrt(h)` and `Y' = Y + a h + sum_j g^j dW_j + (2 sqrt(h))^{-1} sum_{j,l} (g^l(U^j_+) - g^l(U^j_-)) J_jl` (a forward quotient would leave a systematic `O(sqrt(h))` error and only strong order 1/2). It costs `2m` extra diffusion evaluations per step. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Actual native srk_platen over one retained physical Brownian sample. The caller explicitly selects Ito/Stratonovich and commutative/noncommutative methods; no scheme substitution. Independent state dimensions and a shared native evaluation budget are preserved. Platen's derivative-free stochastic Runge-Kutta scheme for commutative It\u00f4 noise, with strong order 1. Each supplied Brownian increment has `m` components. The scheme replaces the Milstein term with differences of the diffusion at supporting values `x + a h + g_j sqrt(abs(h))`. Corrections at extreme scales retain an out-of-range intermediate product in scaled form when the weighted result is representable. An empty increment sequence returns just `y0` without evaluating either callback. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] or [`SdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Owned native Stratonovich-to-Ito drift recipe. Evaluation calls the actual native derivative-based conversion over the immutable physical reference frame, retaining no source callback lifetime. The Ito-equivalent drift of a Stratonovich SDE `dX = a dt + g o dW`: `a~_i = a_i + (1/2) sum_j sum_k g_{kj} d_k g_{ij}`. The correction is `(1/2) sum_j (D_j g)_{.j}` with the directional derivative of column `j` along itself, with the same scaled probes as the Milstein term. The ordinary step is `eps = 1e-6 max(1, |y|_inf) / |g^j|`. A probe uses one side at the floating-point range boundary when only that side is finite. The closure writes `a~` into its output, so any Ito integrator applied to `(a~, g)` converges to the Stratonovich solution of `(a, g)`.",
        "Actual native SDIRK Hermite dense output in physical state coordinates. Evaluates the cubic Hermite continuous extension at `t`. Within an accepted step `[t_n, t_{n+1}]` the interpolant is the cubic Hermite polynomial matching `y_n`, `y_{n+1}` and the endpoint slopes `f(t_n, y_n)`, `f(t_{n+1}, y_{n+1})`. Returns `None` outside the integrated range or when no step was accepted.",
        "Normalized native series::sum_to_infinity: parameters Float vector length1, exact nonnegative integer controls length2, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Sum of `term(k)` for `k = from, from + 1, ...` to infinity. The integer index advances through `i64::MAX`. See [`sum_terms_to_infinity`] for the stopping rules. Use that entry point for recurrences or to keep indices exact beyond the integer precision of the `f64` callback.",
        "Normalized native series::sum_range: parameters Float vector length0, exact nonnegative integer controls length2, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available.",
        "Estimates an infinite sum from a finite measured prefix, stopping at the requested absolute tolerance, the end of the prefix, or the term budget. The record's value and heuristic error have the term dimension. Missing estimates are None, and terms reports the number consumed. Estimates an infinite sum from terms supplied in order. This accepts an unbounded generator or a finite prefix. At each doubling checkpoint it compares Wynn's epsilon algorithm on the most recent partial sums, Richardson extrapolation on the doubling checkpoints, and the Levin `u` transform of the first terms. A final partial block may still contribute Wynn or Levin estimates. The calculation stops on the requested tolerance, input exhaustion, or after evaluating `max_terms` terms. The reported error is a change between estimates and is heuristic. When extrapolation has insufficient data, the result is the available partial sum with infinite error. A non-finite term or partial sum yields `NaN` and infinite error, counting the triggering term in `terms`.",
        "Native signal fft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the one-dimensional discrete Fourier Transform. This function uses the `rustfft` library to perform the FFT. # Arguments * `input` - A slice of complex samples. Its contents are preserved. # Returns A vector of complex numbers representing the FFT of the input. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::signal::fft; use rustfft::num_complex::Complex; let mut input = vec![ Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), ]; let output = fft(&mut input); assert!((output[0].re - 4.0).abs() < 1e-9); ```",
        "Native signal ifft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the one-dimensional inverse discrete Fourier Transform. This is the positive-sign transform followed by the `1/n` normalisation, so `ifft(fft(x)) == x` (up to round-off) for every length. # Arguments * `input` - A mutable slice of complex frequency-domain samples. # Returns A vector of complex numbers representing the inverse transform. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::signal::fft; use rssn::kernels::signal::ifft; use rustfft::num_complex::Complex; let mut input = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 0.5), Complex::new(-2.0, 0.0)]; let spectrum = fft(&mut input); let back = ifft(&mut spectrum.clone()); assert!((back[0] - input[0]).norm() < 1e-12); ```",
        "Native signal stft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Short-time Fourier transform of complete frames with starts `hop` samples apart. Returns full unnormalised complex spectra. The number of bins per frame is the window length. Incomplete trailing frames are discarded. Empty windows, zero hops and signals shorter than a window yield an empty result.",
        "Native simplex LP with normalized coefficient conversion using positive coordinate/objective/constraint references; preserves Optimal,Infeasible,Unbounded without fabricated coordinates or objective. Two-phase simplex: minimise `c . x` subject to rows `a_i . x (rel_i) b_i` and `x >= 0`, with steepest-edge pricing and the Bland anti-cycling fallback; see [`simplex_with`].",
        "Native simplex LP with actual selectable Dantzig,SteepestEdge,Bland pricing and retained pivot statistics; independent positive coordinate/objective/constraint references. Two-phase tableau simplex with a selectable pricing rule. For [`Pricing::Dantzig`] and [`Pricing::SteepestEdge`] the solver monitors degeneracy: after a run of consecutive pivots that do not improve the objective it switches to Bland's rule (smallest-index entering variable, ties in the ratio test broken by smallest basic index) until a strictly improving pivot occurs, which guarantees termination on cycling examples such as Beale's. The leaving-variable tie-break is always Bland's.",
        "Create a real native Sobol sequence in1..8 normalized dimensions; rejects unsupported dimensions instead of clamping. Creates a generator for `dim` (clamped to 1..=8) dimensions.",
        "Advance the retained native Sobol sequence, returning normalized unit-cube coordinates. Next point in `[0, 1)^dim`, starting at index one. After the last `u32` index the sequence continues from index zero, the origin.",
        "Returns the indexed normalized unit-cube point without advancing the sequence. Index zero is the origin. The index must fit in an unsigned 32-bit integer. Returns the indexed point without changing the sequence's position. Index zero returns the origin.",
        "Sets a retained sequence position by its unsigned 32-bit index. The next draw is at index plus one, wrapping the largest index to the origin. Sets the position so that [`Self::next_point`] returns index `index + 1`, modulo `2^32`. Seeking to zero restarts generation from the first nonzero point.",
        "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Solves the square nonlinear system `f(x) = 0` by Newton's method with a finite-difference Jacobian. `f` writes the residuals for the point given as its first argument into its second argument; both have the length of `start`. # Errors Returns an error when a Jacobian is singular, a residual is not finite, or the iteration does not converge within `max_iter` steps.",
        "Normalized native solve::solve_root_bisection: parameters Float vector length3, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Finds a root of `f` in `interval` by bisection. Endpoints may be in either order. A finite endpoint or midpoint whose residual is zero or has magnitude less than `tolerance` is accepted. Otherwise the absolute interval width is compared with the tolerance. Zero tolerance disables approximate stopping. The two endpoint evaluations are followed by at most `max_iter` midpoint evaluations. At the budget or when no representable point remains inside the bracket, returns its midpoint. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite endpoints or function values, a negative or non-finite tolerance, or endpoints with the same sign outside the tolerance.",
        "Normalized native solve::solve_root_newton: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then2 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Finds a root of `f` by Newton's method, using `df` for the derivative. # Errors Returns an error when the derivative vanishes or the iteration does not converge.",
        "Normalized native solve::solve_root: parameters Float vector length2, exact nonnegative integer controls length1, positive coordinate reference D,positive value reference E,evaluation budget,then1 normalized Float callbacks. Results restore D*E for integrals,D for roots,E for sums. Error=None means native method supplies no finite error estimate; convergence flag retains native stopping status where available. Finds a root of `f` near `guess`: Newton's method with a central difference derivative from several starting points, then bisection over growing brackets. A non-finite sample discards the current attempt. # Errors Returns an error for a non-finite guess or a negative or non-finite tolerance, or when every strategy fails.",
        "Actual native basin solve_with_bfgs instantiated with an owned native tape cost and exact reverse gradient. Inputs and parameters retain independent reference domains; target_cost is normalized objective target,not a gradient tolerance. Oracle budget covers both cost and gradient calls. PSO uses initial/upper as bounds and the native randomness semantics. Other methods ignore upper. Generic auto_solve explicitly instantiates the native steepest-descent solver. Minimises `problem` with BFGS. # Errors Propagates [`minimize`]'s errors.",
        "Actual native basin solve_with_gradient_descent instantiated with an owned native tape cost and exact reverse gradient. Inputs and parameters retain independent reference domains; target_cost is normalized objective target,not a gradient tolerance. Oracle budget covers both cost and gradient calls. PSO uses initial/upper as bounds and the native randomness semantics. Other methods ignore upper. Generic auto_solve explicitly instantiates the native steepest-descent solver. Minimises `problem` with steepest descent. # Errors Propagates [`minimize`]'s errors.",
        "Actual native basin solve_with_pso instantiated with an owned native tape cost and exact reverse gradient. Inputs and parameters retain independent reference domains; target_cost is normalized objective target,not a gradient tolerance. Oracle budget covers both cost and gradient calls. PSO uses initial/upper as bounds and the native randomness semantics. Other methods ignore upper. Generic auto_solve explicitly instantiates the native steepest-descent solver. Minimises `problem` with a global-best particle swarm. `bounds` is `(lower, upper)`; the swarm needs a finite box. # Errors Propagates [`minimize`]'s errors.",
        "Native special_complex bessel_i_complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The modified Bessel function of the first kind `I_\u03bd(z)` for real order `\u03bd` and complex `z` (principal branch).",
        "Native special_complex bessel_j_complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The Bessel function of the first kind `J_\u03bd(z)` for real order `\u03bd` and complex `z` (principal branch of `z^\u03bd`, cut along the negative real axis).",
        "Native special_complex digamma_complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The digamma function `\u03c8(z) = \u0393'(z) / \u0393(z)`; poles give NaN.",
        "Native special_complex gamma_complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `\u0393(z)`; poles (non-positive integers) give `+\u221e`.",
        "Return the dimensionless complex solution to w exp(w) = z on integer branch k. When the imaginary component is signed zero on the negative real axis, its sign selects the side of the branch cut. The principal branch preserves small real and imaginary components near the origin. The branch `k` of the Lambert W function: the solution of `w e^w = z` in the strip of branch `k`. On the negative real axis, a positive zero imaginary part selects the upper lip and a negative zero selects the lower lip. `W_0(0) = 0` and `W_k(0) = -\u221e` for `k != 0`; the branch point `-1/e` gives `-1`.",
        "Native special_complex ln_gamma_complex; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The analytic branch of `ln \u0393(z)`, defined and continuous on the plane cut along the non-positive real axis, with `ln \u0393(z + 1) = ln \u0393(z) + ln z`. Non-positive integers (the poles) give `+\u221e`.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The default number of Fourier modes of the simulated L\u00e9vy areas for a step (or Brownian cell) of size `h`: `clamp(ceil(1 / sqrt(h)), 16, 4096)`. The truncation error of the area decays like `h p^(-3/2)` with the kernel's Gaussian tail correction, so this rule keeps it far below the `O(h)` strong error of the scheme at an `O(m^2 / sqrt(h))` sampling cost per step.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Integrates one path per ensemble member, sequentially and order-preserving. Member `i` uses [`ensemble_seed`] with `(seed, i)` as its own seed, so the result does not depend on execution order or concurrency. The paths run one after another because the Aevia interpreter closures are `!Send`; the kernel's rayon ensemble would need a `Send + Sync` callback, which this build cannot produce. The returned paths are in index order. # Errors The first member error, in index order.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Seed of path `index` of an ensemble with base `seed`. A function of the pair only (the kernel's derivation), so path `i` is the same whichever order or concurrency runs the ensemble; this build runs the paths sequentially because an interpreter closure is `!Send`.",
        "Actual native optional capability diagnostic. The capability an `E1001` names, when it is one.",
        "Actual native stable diagnostic code. The stable Aevia diagnostic code.",
        "Native stochastic error builder with exact Invalid/Shape/NonFinite/StepLimit/Unsupported variants; unsupported capability is an explicit closed known capability name.",
        "Actual native sign-change event detection on the native piecewise-linear stochastic path; this does not claim to detect unobserved between-node process crossings. Programs receive coherent physical time/state components. Detects sign changes of `g_i(t, y)` along a path. Between two consecutive nodes the path is the chord of [`Path::eval`]; a crossing is a sign change of `g` at the two nodes in an allowed direction (falling `g0 > 0 >= g1`, rising `g0 < 0 <= g1`, the kernel's convention for ODE events), and its time is the root of `g` along the chord, found by bisection. A node where `g` is exactly zero counts once (as the end of the step that reaches it). Only the discretised path is watched: the continuous process may cross and return between two nodes, which no discrete scheme can see. Hits come in time order. If an event is `terminal`, the returned path ends at the first terminal hit (the hit is appended as its last node) and later hits are dropped. The returned path is the input when nothing terminates.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Integrates `dX = a(t, X) dt + G(t, X) dW` from `y0`. `drift` returns `n` values; `diffusion` returns `n` values for [`Structure::Diagonal`] (the diagonal entries) or `n \u00d7 m` values in row-major order for [`Structure::General`]. Fixed/uniform-grid schedules use prescribed increments. Adaptive schedules retain the requested method and use a bounded noise tree with coupled step doubling, a separate drift-error check, and explicit resolution/work errors. See the module documentation for tolerance scaling and finite allocations. # Errors `E0802` for shapes/parameters, `E0806` for a non-finite state, `E0807` for a step collapse. No method/structure/step-policy combination is refused with `E1001` any more.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Integrates a (potentially stiff) SDE `dX = a(t, X) dt + G(t, X) dW` using the specified [`DriftImplicitScheme`]. Supports `BackwardEuler` (`theta = 1`), `Trapezoidal` (`theta = 0.5`), general `Theta(theta)`, `DriftImplicitMilstein` (strong order 1.0), and `SplitStepBackwardEuler` (for nonlinear stiff superlinear drifts). # Errors Returns [`SdeError`] on invalid options, mismatched callback dimensions, non-finite values, or Newton non-convergence.",
        "Actual native compensated jump diffusion. Poisson intensity has inverse time units; retained marks have independent physical references. Jump tape receives(time,state,mark) and outputs state increments; expected-jump tape receives(time,state) and outputs the exact supplied expected state increment, rather than evaluating at the mean mark. Native arrivals/marks remain fixed before adaptive rejection. Integrate a jump diffusion with an explicit compensator. Integrate a compensated jump diffusion with an explicit expected jump coefficient. `expected_jump(t, y)` must return the expected coefficient `E[c(t, y, Z)]`. For nonlinear coefficients, evaluate the coefficient under the mark distribution before taking the expectation. # Errors Invalid integration inputs, callback shapes, or non-finite expected coefficients.",
        "Physical native jump path samples and actual total/per-step jump counts.",
        "All native JumpDistribution variants over an explicit positive physical mark reference. data1/data2 contain normalized vectors, controls contain normalized distribution parameters. Exact native validation is called before retention.",
        "Actual native optional analytical mean mark; infinite or undefined stable means remain None. Analytical mean mark `E[Z]` when finite and known (used for compensated Poisson jumps).",
        "Actual native jump mark sampling on the retained native stream, returning physical mark units. Samples one jump mark vector `Z` from the distribution using `stream`. # Errors Invalid shapes or parameters, non-finite inputs, or numerical solver failure.",
        "Draws consecutive marks into a rank-two tensor with one row per sample. Columns use the physical mark reference. Successful calls advance the stream in the same order as single draws. Zero samples and rejected batches keep the stream state. Fills caller-owned storage with consecutive jump marks, in sample-major order. Each mark has the same component count as [`Self::sample`]. Fills consume the same stream draws as repeated calls to that method, and an empty output is a no-op. The mark shape is checked once per nonempty batch. # Errors Invalid parameters, output length not divisible by the mark dimension, or a numerical failure while sampling. A numerical failure can leave partial output and advances the stream through the failing draw.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Generates `steps` vectors of `dim`-dimensional `\\\\alpha`-stable L\u00e9vy increments `\\\\Delta L_k ~ S_\\\\alpha(dt^{1/\\\\alpha}, \\\\beta, 0)` from the deterministic language stream. # Errors Returns [`SdeError::Invalid`] if `dim == 0`, `dt <= 0`, or `\\\\alpha, \\\\beta` are out of range.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The integral the scheme converges to.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The method named `name`, if it is one this build knows.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The spelling used by the language surface and diagnostics.",
        "Actual native MLMC with coupled fine/coarse Brownian paths. Payoff compiled program receives physical state components; target RMSE and estimate/standard-error have payoff units and variance has their square. Explicit hierarchy/sample/work caps bound all native levels. Estimate a terminal SDE expectation with multilevel Monte Carlo. Estimates the expected value `E[payoff(X_{t1})]` of a terminal functional of an SDE `dX = a(t, X) dt + G(t, X) dW` using Giles' Multilevel Monte Carlo (MLMC) method with coupled fine/coarse Brownian paths (`\\\\Delta W^{(c)}_k = \\\\Delta W^{(f)}_{2k} + \\\\Delta W^{(f)}_{2k+1}`) and optimal sample allocation `N_l \\\\propto \\\\sqrt{V_l / C_l}`. Each (level, sample index) draws from its own deterministic seeded stream. A coupled fine/coarse pair shares that stream. The estimates for a given seed may differ from versions that reused streams across levels. # Errors Returns [`SdeError`] if options or callback dimensions are invalid or if any simulated trajectory becomes non-finite. Returns [`SdeError::StepLimit`] if cancelled at a checkpoint between solves and payoff evaluations.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The next standard normal, Box\u2013Muller (cosine branch).",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The next uniform in `[0, 1)`.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Adaptive integration on `[t0,t1]` using bounded dyadic coupled noise. `cells=0` selects the default finest resolution for every method.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. `steps` fixed steps of `dt` from `t0`.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Fixed steps covering `[t0, t1]`: the step count is rounded, and `dt` is adjusted to `(t1 - t0) / steps` so the end time is reached exactly.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. An explicit uniform grid.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Sets the number of Fourier modes for the simulated L\u00e9vy areas.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The state at time `t`, by linear interpolation between the accepted nodes; `None` outside `[t_first, t_last]`. A numerical SDE path is only known at its nodes. Between them the interpolant is the chord, which is continuous and deterministic. It is as accurate as the scheme itself (strong order at most 1, and a Brownian path is only H\u00f6lder-1/2 between nodes anyway); it does not add the within-step Brownian-bridge variance, so statistics of `eval` between nodes understate the within-step variation by `O(dt)`. Use the node values (`y`) when exact distributional statistics at the nodes are wanted.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The final state, when the path has one.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The states at the requested times (`saveat`): [`Path::eval`] at each. # Errors `E0802` when a time lies outside the path's span.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Samples a general `\\\\alpha`-stable random variable `S_\\\\alpha(scale, beta, location)` using the exact Chambers\u2013Mallows\u2013Stuck (1976) algorithm. # Errors Invalid shapes or parameters, non-finite inputs, or numerical solver failure.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Samples a `Poisson(mean)` random variable from `stream` using Knuth's exact multiplication algorithm for `mean <= 30` and exact transformed rejection for `mean > 30`.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The strong/weak order of `method` on **commutative** noise (see [`sde_order_for`] for a given structure), as the kernel's documentation states it.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The strong/weak order of `method` on noise of the given `structure`. As [`sde_order`], except that `HeunStratonovich` on non-commutative noise is only strong order 1/2 (it has no L\u00e9vy-area term), and that the schemes which dispatch to the L\u00e9vy-area kernels on non-commutative noise keep strong order one.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. A stream seeded with `seed` (the same state `rng(seed)` starts from).",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. How many values the diffusion callback returns.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Whether the structure is (declared) commutative.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. The number of noise channels (`m`) for a state of `n` components.",
        "Actual native stochastic method in explicit physical reference coordinates. Session/kind, storage, shape and evaluation limits are checked. Brownian increments for `steps` steps of `dt`, `m` components each, on the language stream seeded with `seed`. The argument order is the kernel's `sde_wiener(seed, m, steps, h)` so the language surface, the manifest operator and this helper cannot drift: `wiener(seed, m, steps, dt)[k][j]` is the increment of the `j`-th Wiener process over `[t0 + k dt, t0 + (k+1) dt]`, drawn step-major (component order inside a step), which is exactly the layout the kernel's fixed-step solvers consume. A fixed-step integration with the same seed and structure therefore consumes these numbers.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Step-size multipliers of a symmetric composition of even `order`. Step-size multipliers `c_i` (summing to 1) of the symmetric composition `S(c_1 h) S(c_2 h) ...` that lifts a symmetric second-order step `S` to the even `order` (Yoshida sequences for 6 and 8, Suzuki triple jumps beyond). # Errors Fails for odd or zero `order`.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. **Exact** free rotation for time `t` of an asymmetric rigid body, in closed form. With `d` the principal axis about which the polhode circulates, the body-frame angular momentum is `L_d = +-A_d dn`, `L_p = A_p cn`, `L_s = A_s sn` (Jacobi functions of `tau = tau_0 + omega t`, parameter `m`; Landau and Lifshitz, section 37). The orientation follows from the Euler angles with the (space-fixed) angular momentum as polar axis: `theta` and `psi` are algebraic in `L`, and the precession angle is `phi(t) = |L| t / I_d + |L| (2T - L^2 / I_d) int_0^t dt' / (L^2 - L_d^2)`, an elliptic integral of the **third kind** ([`crate::sim::elliptic::elliptic_pi`]). Symmetric tops and steady rotations use the exact symmetric-top flow. Valid for any `t` (including negative).",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. One symmetric (`NO_SQUISH` / DLM) step of the free rotor, second order.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Free rotation for time `h` with the chosen splitting composed to the even `order` (2, 4, 6, 8, ...) by [`composition_weights`]. # Errors Fails for an invalid `order`.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Second-order symmetric step of the free rotor built on exact **symmetric-top flows**: `exp(h/2 R_a) exp(h/2 R_b) exp(h S) exp(h/2 R_b) exp(h/2 R_a)` where `S` is the symmetric top and `R_a`, `R_b` the single-axis remainders. Exact when the body is itself a symmetric top and much more accurate than [`free_step`](Self::free_step) for nearly symmetric bodies.",
        "Native Hamiltonian splitting with six schemes, physical coordinate, velocity and time references, and a shared evaluation budget. Integrates `n` steps of signed size `h` with `scheme`, reusing acceleration storage. # Panics Panics if `n` is positive and `p` is shorter than `q`.",
        "Integrates every step with a separable symplectic scheme. Retains step zero, each positive save_every multiple, and the final step. Each state preserves the physical positions followed by velocities. Step indices are exact and the signed step size uses the acceleration tape's time dimension. Integrates a separable Hamiltonian and samples its phase state by step count. Retains step zero, each multiple of `save_every`, and the final step. `h` may be negative for backward integration. With zero `h` the sampled state stays constant and the acceleration callback is skipped. Otherwise `acc(q, a)` must write every element of `a` at each kick. # Errors Empty or unequal state slices, non-finite inputs, zero cadence, a non-finite state or acceleration, an unavailable snapshot allocation, or cancellation.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Adds `h * torque` (space-frame torque) to the angular momentum.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Rotational kinetic energy.",
        "Actual native physical-time KS advance with explicit positive gravitational parameter mu(L\u00b3/T\u00b2), bounded eta and a genuine tape perturbation acceleration. Callback budget failures and incomplete native endpoint errors are preserved. Advances a perturbed two-body problem in KS variables for a given physical time. The gravitational parameter is `mu`, the physical time `dt > 0` (RK4 in the fictitious time, step `ds = eta / omega` with the harmonic frequency `omega` of the regularised oscillator; the last steps are solved for so that the physical time lands exactly on `dt`). `pert(x, v, tau)` is the perturbing relative acceleration at elapsed time `tau`. Returns the relative position and velocity. # Errors Fails for a non-positive `dt`, `mu` or `eta`, or a collision (`r = 0`).",
        "Actual native Cartesian-to-KS transform. Cartesian units are L,L/T; regular coordinates have sqrt(L) and du/ds has L^(3/2)/T, with fictitious time ds=T/L. KS map of a regular state: returns `(u, u')` for position `x` and velocity `v` (`u'` is the derivative with respect to the fictitious time `ds = dt / r`).",
        "Actual native KS matrix, linear in regular coordinates; input and matrix entries have sqrt(length) units. The KS matrix `L(u)` (4x4, rows give `x1, x2, x3, 0`).",
        "Actual native fixed fictitious-time KS propagation. Native central mu=1is normalized by an explicit positive physical mu reference: time reference=sqrt(Lref\u00b3/mu). Fictitious step has T/L units, outputs retain regular-coordinate/time/specific-energy units. Propagates a (perturbed) Kepler problem in KS variables with RK4. Propagates a (perturbed) Kepler problem with gravitational parameter 1 in KS variables with RK4 in the fictitious time `s`, for `n` steps of size `ds`. `pert(x, v, t)` is the perturbing acceleration. Without perturbation the equations are a harmonic oscillator of frequency `sqrt(-E/2)`, so near-collision orbits are integrated without singularity.",
        "Actual native KS-to-Cartesian transform with physical sqrt(L),L^(3/2)/T regular coordinates and L,L/T outputs. Cartesian position and velocity from a KS state.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Angular momentum in the space frame.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Unit quaternion (scalar first) of a proper rotation matrix.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Quaternion product `a * b` (scalar first).",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Rotation matrix (body to space) of a unit quaternion.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. One **implicit RATTLE-type** step with a space-frame torque: the discrete rigid-body (Moser-Veselov) free step, in which the rotation increment `F` solves the implicit constraint equation `F J - J F^T = h Pi^` (`J = tr(I)/2 - I` in the principal frame, Newton iteration), sandwiched between half kicks of the torque. The map is symplectic, time reversible and second order; it conserves the space-frame angular momentum exactly in the absence of torque and keeps `R^T R = 1` to round-off. # Errors Fails if the Newton iteration for the rotation increment does not converge (very large steps) or a principal moment is not positive.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Order of accuracy of the scheme.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Stage table: pairs `(drift, kick)` applied in sequence (drift first).",
        "Actual native custom-stage drift/kick step. State contains physical positions then velocities and stage coefficients are explicitly normalized; a pure-position acceleration tape preserves coordinate/time\u00b2 units. One step of a composition scheme. `acc(q, a)` writes the acceleration. # Panics Panics if a stage is executed and `p` is shorter than `q`.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. One step with a space-frame torque `torque(q)`: half kick, free rotation, half kick.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Strang step with space-frame torque built on the **exact** free flow ([`free_flow_exact`](Self::free_flow_exact)): half kick, exact free rotation, half kick. Symplectic, time reversible and second order, with no splitting error in the free part.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Exact flow for time `h` of the **symmetric top** obtained by replacing the two inverse moments about the axes other than `ax` with their mean: `L` is constant in space, so `q(h) = q_L(h) * q(0) * q_ax(h)` with `q_L` a rotation about `L` by `|L| h / I_perp` and `q_ax` a rotation about the symmetry axis by `h L_ax (1/I_ax - 1/I_perp)`.",
        "Actual native geometric operation; physical rigid-body moments/torques use explicit positive SI M/L/T references, while orientations and composition coefficients are dimensionless. Suzuki triple jump: lifts a symmetric scheme of order `p` to order `p + 2`.",
        "Physical retained native TTL time, positions and momenta.",
        "Actual native ttl_gbs_step with a true native tape reverse gradient of the physical negative potential. Binding has energy units and may be negative when initial T+B>0; fictitious step has energy*time units; the returned error is explicitly in the native normalized state metric. One Gragg-Bulirsch-Stoer extrapolated step of the time-transformed leapfrog. One Gragg-Bulirsch-Stoer extrapolated step of the time-transformed leapfrog ([`ttl_step`]) of size `ds` in the fictitious time, using `kmax` columns. Returns the relative error estimate. The scheme has order `2 kmax` in `ds`.",
        "Build actual native TTL phase state with physical positions, momenta, masses and initial time. The fictitious integration increment has energy*time dimensions.",
        "Actual native ttl_step with a true native tape reverse gradient of the physical negative potential. Binding has energy units and may be negative when initial T+B>0; fictitious step has energy*time units; the returned error is explicitly in the native normalized state metric. Time-transformed leapfrog for `H = T(p) + V(q)` with `V < 0`. Uses the extended Hamiltonian `K = ln(T + B) - ln(-V)` with `B = -E0` (`E0` the energy to preserve); the fictitious step `h` corresponds to the physical step `h / (T + B)`, which is `h r / (GM)` for Kepler (a Sundman transformation `dt = r ds`). The scheme is symmetric and symplectic in the extended phase space. `mass` holds one mass per coordinate; `potential_grad(q, g)` writes `grad V` and returns `V`.",
        "Actual native ttl_step_composed with a true native tape reverse gradient of the physical negative potential. Binding has energy units and may be negative when initial T+B>0; fictitious step has energy*time units; the returned error is explicitly in the native normalized state metric. Time-transformed leapfrog composed to an even `order`. Time-transformed leapfrog composed to the even `order` (4, 6, 8, ...): the TTL step is a symmetric second-order integrator of the extended Hamiltonian `ln(T + B) - ln(-V)`, so composition with [`composition_weights`] removes the `O(h^2)` energy error of the plain scheme. # Errors Fails for an invalid `order`.",
        "Native transforms fft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) of a sequence of complex numbers. Works at any length and leaves the length unchanged using a shared, runtime-selected SIMD FFT plan from [`super::fft`]. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable slice of `Complex<f64>` representing the input sequence. # Example ```rust use num_complex::Complex; use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft; let mut data = vec![ Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(0.0, 0.0), Complex::new(0.0, 0.0), ]; fft(&mut data); assert!((data[0].re - 2.0).abs() < 1e-9); ``` A length that is not a power of two keeps its length: ```rust use num_complex::Complex; use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft; let mut data = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 0.0); 3]; fft(&mut data); assert_eq!(data.len(), 3); // [3, 0, 0] for a constant signal. assert!((data[0].re - 3.0).abs() < 1e-12); ```",
        "Native transforms fft_many_par; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Forward FFT of every row, concurrently, in row order. Each row runs the serial [`fft`] kernel and only different rows run concurrently, so the result is bit-identical to transforming the rows one at a time and therefore independent of the thread count. Fewer than `grain` rows (or `grain < 2`) run sequentially, so a small batch does not pay for the task spawn. Use the batch entry point instead of a hand-written `rayon` map when the rows are independent (the usual row/column pass of a 2-D transform).",
        "Native transforms fft_slice; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the Forward FFT for a slice. **Use this for efficient, parallel chunk processing (e.g., with rayon).** Works at any length and operates in place: a power-of-two length uses the scalar radix-2 kernel and other lengths use the shared FFT planner. Bit-identical to [`fft`]. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable slice of `Complex<f64>` representing the input sequence. # Example ```rust use num_complex::Complex; use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft_slice; let mut data = vec![ Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(0.0, 0.0), Complex::new(0.0, 0.0), ]; fft_slice(&mut data); assert!((data[0].re - 2.0).abs() < 1e-9); ```",
        "Native transforms ifft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the Inverse Fast Fourier Transform (IFFT). Works at any length and leaves the length unchanged, so `ifft(fft(x))` returns `x` at every length. The `1/n` normalisation is included. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable `Vec<Complex<f64>>` representing the input frequency-domain sequence. # Example ```rust use num_complex::Complex; use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft; use rssn::kernels::transforms::ifft; let mut data = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 2.0), Complex::new(3.0, 4.0)]; let original = data.clone(); fft(&mut data); ifft(&mut data); assert!((data[0].re - original[0].re).abs() < 1e-9); assert!((data[0].im - original[0].im).abs() < 1e-9); ```",
        "Native transforms ifft_many_par; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. [`fft_many_par`] for the inverse transform.",
        "Native transforms ifft_slice; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Computes the Inverse FFT for a slice. **Use this for efficient, parallel chunk processing (e.g., with rayon).** Works at any length and operates in place; the `1/n` normalisation is included. Bit-identical to [`ifft`]. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable slice of `Complex<f64>` representing the input frequency-domain sequence. # Example ```rust use num_complex::Complex; use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft_slice; use rssn::kernels::transforms::ifft_slice; let mut data = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(0.0, 0.0)]; let original = data.clone(); fft_slice(&mut data); ifft_slice(&mut data); assert!((data[0].re - original[0].re).abs() < 1e-9); ```",
        "Genuine native method with physical point and positive coordinate/output references. Callbacks operate normalized vectors; residual/gradient/Jacobian results restore their proper E,D units. Solver args x,xref,yref,normalized tolerance,iterations,budget,callback(s); numerical Jacobian args x,physical fx,xref,yref,budget,f; gradient args x,xref,yref,budget,f. Shared callback budget and original errors are preserved. Validated forward-difference Jacobian, with a backward-side retry at domain boundaries and checked callback length and representability. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] if inputs, samples, shape or a derivative are invalid.",
        "Native wavelets dwt; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. One level of the periodised discrete wavelet transform: returns `(approximation, detail)`. `a[k] = sum_m h~[m] x[(2k + m) mod n]`, `d[k] = sum_m g~[m] x[(2k + m) mod n]` with the analysis banks. An even signal of length `n` maps to `n/2` coefficients of each kind. For odd lengths or another boundary rule use [`dwt_with`]. # Errors [`WaveletError::OddLength`] for an empty or odd-length signal.",
        "Native wavelets dwt2; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. One level of the separable two-dimensional transform, `x` row-major with `rows x cols` entries. The rows are transformed first, then the columns, and the four sub-bands are packed into a `(2*ra) x (2*ca)` row-major array with `ra = w.coeff_len(rows, b)` and `ca = w.coeff_len(cols, b)`: `LL` at rows `0..ra`, columns `0..ca`; `LH` at rows `0..ra`, columns `ca..2ca`; `HL` at rows `ra..2ra`, columns `0..ca`; `HH` at rows `ra..2ra`, columns `ca..2ca`. In each label, the first letter gives the filter within each row (horizontal) and the second the filter within each column (vertical). # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when `rows * cols` does not match `x.len()`, or when a row or column transform fails.",
        "Computes one level of approximation and detail for the plan's input length. The two vectors both retain the physical dimension of the original signal. Writes one level of approximation and detail coefficients in the plan's layout. # Errors Returns [`WaveletError::Shape`] for a length mismatch, without changing the output slices.",
        "Returns the number of entries in each approximation and detail band for this signal length and boundary. Number of entries in each approximation or detail band.",
        "Prepares one-level analysis and synthesis for a fixed nonzero signal length. Use a wavelet such as Haar or Db10 and one of Periodic, Zero, Symmetric or Reflect. Shares filter taps and boundary layout across independent signals. Prepares one level for the given length, wavelet and boundary. # Errors [`WaveletError::OddLength`] for an empty signal and [`WaveletError::Shape`] for a length that cannot fit in a slice of `f64` values.",
        "Returns the signal length fixed by the plan and used for synthesis output. Number of samples in the input or reconstructed signal.",
        "Reconstructs the original signal length from two equal-dimension coefficient bands of the plan's required length. The output retains the shared dimension. Reconstructs one level into the given output slice. # Errors Returns [`WaveletError::Shape`] for a length mismatch, without changing the output slice.",
        "Native wavelets dwt_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. One level of the discrete wavelet transform with an explicit [`Boundary`] mode. Returns `(approximation, detail)`, each of the length that [`Wavelet::coeff_len`] reports for `x.len()` and `b`. In [`Boundary::Periodic`] mode this is the circular transform of [`dwt`], extended by one sample for an odd length. Other modes use the documented boundary extension. Use [`DwtPlan`] to reuse the banks and output storage. # Errors [`WaveletError::OddLength`] for an empty signal.",
        "Native wavelets extend; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Sample `i` of the boundary extension of `x` in mode `b`. `Periodic` has period `n`, `Symmetric` period `2n` (mirror with the edge sample repeated) and `Reflect` period `2(n-1)` (mirror without repeating it); `Zero` returns `0.0` outside `[0, n)`. For `n = 1` the nonzero extension modes return `x[0]`, and an empty signal has no samples.",
        "Native wavelets idwt; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`dwt`] (exact up to rounding). # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the two coefficient lists differ in length or are empty.",
        "Native wavelets idwt2; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`dwt2`]: `rows` and `cols` are the shape of the original array. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the packed array does not have the expected `(2*ra) x (2*ca)` size, or when a column or row transform fails.",
        "Native wavelets idwt_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`dwt_with`]. `n` is the length of the signal that produced the coefficients. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the lists differ in length, are empty, or do not match `w.coeff_len(n, b)`.",
        "Native wavelets method_analysis_high; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The analysis high-pass filter `g~`, the alternating mirror of the synthesis low-pass.",
        "Native wavelets method_analysis_low; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The analysis low-pass filter `h~` (the same as [`Wavelet::low_pass`]).",
        "Native wavelets method_coeff_len; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The number of coefficients [`dwt_with`] returns for a signal of length `n`. `ceil(n/2)` in [`Boundary::Periodic`] mode, and `j_hi - j_lo + 1` \u2014 about `n/2 + (m_hi - m_lo)/2` \u2014 in the extension modes, where `[m_lo, m_hi]` is the support of the synthesis banks (see the module documentation).",
        "Native wavelets method_high_pass; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The analysis (decomposition) high-pass filter `g~[n] = (-1)^n h[L-1-n]`.",
        "Native wavelets method_is_orthogonal; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. `true` when one bank serves both analysis and synthesis (orthonormal filters).",
        "Native wavelets method_low_pass; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The analysis (decomposition) low-pass filter `h~`, normalised to `sum h~ = sqrt 2`.",
        "Native wavelets method_synthesis_high; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The synthesis high-pass filter `g[n] = (-1)^n h~[L-1-n]`.",
        "Native wavelets method_synthesis_low; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The synthesis (reconstruction) low-pass filter `h`; equal to [`Wavelet::low_pass`] for the orthogonal families.",
        "Native wavelets method_taps; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Number of filter taps (the length of the banks).",
        "Native wavelets method_vanishing_moments; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. The vanishing moments of the analysis and of the synthesis wavelet, `(Nr, Nd)`. Counted from the filters: the analysis wavelet annihilates polynomials of degree below `Nr`, the synthesis wavelet those below `Nd`. For the orthogonal families the two agree and equal the Daubechies order.",
        "Native wavelets wavedec; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Multilevel decomposition: `[approximation_L, detail_L, ..., detail_1]`. Requires `2^levels` to divide the length, as before; use [`wavedec_with`] for the general case. # Errors [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when `2^levels` does not divide the length, or the length is shorter than the filter at some level.",
        "Native wavelets wavedec2; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Multilevel separable two-dimensional decomposition: `[LL_L, packed_L, ..., packed_1]`. Every level applies [`dwt2`] to the current `LL` band and keeps the whole packed array (`LL`, `LH`, `HL`, `HH`) for the inverse; the next level then transforms the extracted `LL` band. The arrays therefore follow the [`wavedec`] convention: the first array is the final approximation and the remaining ones are the details of the levels from coarsest to finest. The periodic transform requires the dyadic shape `2^levels | rows` and `2^levels | cols` (the sizes halve exactly at every level); use [`wavedec2_with`] for another [`Boundary`] mode or a non-dyadic shape. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when `rows * cols` does not match `x.len()` or a dimension is zero; [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when `levels` is zero or exceeds the dyadic depth.",
        "Native wavelets wavedec2_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Multilevel separable two-dimensional decomposition with an explicit [`Boundary`] mode. The recursion is the same as in [`wavedec2`], but any shape is accepted as long as every level strictly shrinks the approximation band in both dimensions. The extension modes return about `n/2 + (m_hi - m_lo)/2` coefficients, so once the filters reach the whole band the count stops shrinking; that is detected per level and reported as [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] instead of recursing on a band that does not get smaller. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when `rows * cols` does not match `x.len()` or a dimension is zero; [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when `levels` is zero, or a level would not shrink the approximation.",
        "Native wavelets wavedec_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Multilevel decomposition with a [`Boundary`] mode, for any non-empty signal. Every level keeps the number of coefficients that [`Wavelet::coeff_len`] reports for its length and `b`, so the levels stop shrinking once the length is comparable to the filter length (see the module documentation). # Errors [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when `levels` is zero or `2^(levels-1) > x.len()`.",
        "Native wavelets waverec; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`wavedec`]. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] for an inconsistent list.",
        "Native wavelets waverec2; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`wavedec2`]: `rows` and `cols` are the shape of the decomposed array. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the coefficient list or one of its shapes does not fit `rows x cols` and the periodic rule.",
        "Native wavelets waverec2_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`wavedec2_with`]; `rows` and `cols` are the shape of the decomposed array. The shape of every level is recomputed from `rows`, `cols` and the mode, exactly like [`waverec_with`]; the `LL` band of each stored level is overwritten with the reconstruction of the next finer level and [`idwt2`] recovers its input. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the list is empty, one of its arrays has the wrong size, or the shape chain does not fit `rows x cols` and the mode.",
        "Native wavelets waverec_with; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names. Inverse of [`wavedec_with`]; `n` is the length of the decomposed signal. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the list or one of its lengths does not fit `n` and the mode.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Adds a soft current source.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Largest time step for which the coupled ADE scheme is stable, `2 / sqrt(\u03c90\u00b2 + a/\u03b5\u221e + 4 \u03a3_a h_a^{-2}/\u03b5\u221e)` for the stiffest pole and cell; infinite when the medium is non-dispersive. [`Yee::new`] reduces `dt` to this bound.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). PEC level sets may have any scalar output domain normalized by their explicit positive program reference; only signs and scale-invariant fractions are used. Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Vacuum configuration with PEC walls everywhere. # Errors For a bad dimension or sizes.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Conserved energy: `\u00bd \u03a3 (\u03b5\u221e E\u00b2 + H\u00b2) \u0394V` and, for a dispersive medium, the oscillator energy `\u03a3 (P_t\u00b2 + \u03c90\u00b2 P\u00b2) / (2 a) \u0394V` of every pole, both taken at `n - 1/2`. Without poles and without ohmic conduction this is the electromagnetic energy alone; with conduction it excludes the dissipated part, which accumulates in [`Yee::ohmic_loss`]. `E` sits at `n` and `H` at `n + 1/2`, so the estimator itself wobbles at `O(\u03c9 dt)` (a few per cent on a coarse grid) even where the discrete scheme conserves energy exactly; the mean over a period is constant.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Field values of one component.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Mutable field values of one component (for initial conditions).",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Cell size along an axis.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Sets `H^{-1/2}` from `E^0` assuming `H^0 = 0` (call once after setting `E` for a standing-wave initial condition; not for PML runs).",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Flat index of cell `(i, j, k)`.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Cells per axis.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Creates a solver. The time step is `cfl / sqrt(\u03a3 1/h\u00b2)`, reduced to the ADE stability bound ([`Yee::ade_dt_limit`]) when a dispersive pole is present and to keep the subcell PEC scheme stable. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions, inconsistent periodic faces, a CFL number above one, or a PML thicker than half the grid.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Total ohmic energy dissipated so far, `\u222b \u03a3 \u03c3 E\u00b2 dV dt` (zero for a lossless medium). It is accumulated with the midpoint rule, so `energy() + ohmic_loss()` is conserved up to the estimator wobble.",
        "Reads electric and magnetic amplitudes in their separate physical units at an auxiliary normal-incidence TF/SF line index. The magnetic field is sampled one half-cell ahead of the electric field. Returns the actual electric and magnetic sample times. No installed source or an out-of-range index returns None. Samples the auxiliary line of the normal-incidence TF/SF source. Returns `None` without an installed source or past the auxiliary endpoint. The sample carries the electric and magnetic times of the actual stored values, including after time-step changes.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Drude pole with plasma frequency `\u03c9_p`: `\u03c7 = -\u03c9_p\u00b2 / (\u03c9\u00b2 + i \u03b3 \u03c9)`.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Lorentz pole with static contribution `\u0394\u03b5`: `\u03c7 = \u0394\u03b5 \u03c90\u00b2 / (\u03c90\u00b2 - \u03c9\u00b2 - i \u03b3 \u03c9)`.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Runs `steps` steps.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Installs an oblique plane wave on an arbitrary TF/SF box. # Errors For a zero direction or polarisation, a polarisation parallel to the direction, or components along inactive axes.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Installs an oblique plane wave on a **rotated** box: the box `[lo, hi]` of `w` is rotated about its centre by the orthonormal matrix `rotation` (columns are the images of the axes). The incident wave itself keeps the direction and polarisation given in `w`. # Errors As [`Yee::set_oblique_plane_wave`], and for a matrix that is not orthonormal or that mixes in an inactive axis.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Installs the TF/SF plane-wave source. The auxiliary line starts at rest. The first drive sample is taken at the next electric time, after the first magnetic half-step. # Errors The slab is outside the grid, a correction touches the PML along the propagation axis, or the auxiliary line cannot be allocated.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Ohmic conductivity at every cell (empty when the medium is lossless).",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Number of cells.",
        "Native Yee state. The solver reserves48MiB: up to16MiBgrid/auxiliary/history storage and32MiBfully independent compiled snapshots, including at most4mutable sources. Snapshots remain valid after their original source handles are released.",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. One leapfrog step (`H` then `E`).",
        "Actual native Yee FDTD operation. Explicit positive SI length, wave-speed, impedance and electric-field references define c=epsilon0=mu0=1native coordinates. H uses E/Z, time L/c, energy E\u00b2L\u00b3/(Zc), conductivity1/(ZL). Grid\u22644096and source/work bounds precede execution. Susceptibility `\u03c7(\u03c9)` (time dependence `e^{-i\u03c9t}`) as `(re, im)`.",
        "Angle in radians; zero for a zero vector. Computes the angle between two vectors in radians. Formula: $\\\\theta = \\\\arccos\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\mathbf{a} \\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{b}}{||\\\\mathbf{a}|| ||\\\\mathbf{b}||}\\\\right)$ # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "Value of the `i`-th B-spline basis function at `x`. Uses the Cox\u2013de Boor recurrence on non-decreasing knots, with right-continuous spans and a closed last span. An invalid index or a non-finite local knot gives NaN.",
        "Evaluates a B-spline curve `sum c_i B_{i,p}(x)` by de Boor's algorithm. Requires finite, non-decreasing `knots`, `degree < coeffs.len()` and `knots.len() == coeffs.len() + degree + 1`. The coordinate is clamped to the valid range. Returns NaN for an invalid shape or endpoint.",
        "Fits an interpolating B-spline and returns `(knots, coefficients)`. Degree zero places boundaries at the midpoints between consecutive sites. Higher degrees use a clamped knot vector with averaged interior knots. # Errors [`ApproxError::Invalid`] for inconsistent or non-finite samples, an invalid degree, unordered sites, or an unrepresentable final degree-zero interval. [`ApproxError::Singular`] if the collocation system is singular or overflows.",
        "Chebyshev points of the second kind (extrema) `cos(pi k / n)` mapped to `[a, b]`, `k = 0..=n`.",
        "Clenshaw evaluation of `sum c_k T_k(t)` at `t in [-1, 1]`.",
        "Evaluates a polynomial with ascending coefficients by Horner's scheme.",
        "Pade approximant `[m/n]` from Taylor coefficients `c_0, c_1, ...` (needs `m + n + 1` coefficients). Returns `(numerator, denominator)` with ascending coefficients and `denominator[0] = 1`.",
        "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.",
        "List valid backend selectors discovered on this host. The first device query can initialize GPU adapters.",
        "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.",
        "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.",
        "Evaluate a retained scalar program over measured rank-one columns in declared symbol order. Each column has its own physical unit and matches the explicit row count. Returns a single measured vector in input order. The active CPU pool splits sufficiently large work into fixed independent blocks. Cancellation and the program's row and work limits apply.",
        "Evaluate a retained scalar program over a tensor of session-owned input handles with shape `[arity, rows]`. Columns follow declared symbol order and retain their individual physical dimensions. The executor estimates the work per row from the compiled program and uses the active CPU pool for independent blocks when there is enough work. The measured result preserves row order. Cancellation is checked between blocks, and shape, units, and the program\u2019s row and work limits apply.",
        "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.",
        "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy. Compiles `root` with the [current] backend. # Errors See [`BackendError`].",
        "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy. The backend numeric kernels compile with: the innermost [`with_backend`] scope, else the process default ([`set_default`]), else the [`Interpreter`].",
