        "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.",
        "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.",
        "Set a named process default and return its resolved selector. Use auto to clear the override. Thread-local overrides keep priority.",
        "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy. Sets the process-wide default backend (`None` restores the interpreter).",
        "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy.",
        "Session-owned native backend/compiled/scalar handles. Compile an elementary ASCII expression in declared symbol order, normalized by positive physical coordinate references and a positive output reference. Scalar-value handles preserve mixed dimensions individually; call columns are [arity,rows] handles, not mixed-unit numeric tensors. The compiled object retains its private elementary RSSN Session and actual backend. Compilation enforces source, allocation and per-call work bounds for the selected backend. Current snapshots environment; scoped compile selects its backend explicitly. Process-default changes require an explicit action string. Kind/session/lifetime and units are checked; release invalidates every handle copy. Runs `f` with `backend` as the current backend on this thread.",
        "Modified Bessel I of order zero. Computes the modified Bessel function of the first kind, I\u2080(x). Power series for |x| \u2264 30 and the asymptotic expansion beyond; relative accuracy about 1e-15.",
        "Modified Bessel I of order one. Computes the modified Bessel function of the first kind, I\u2081(x) (odd in `x`). Same method and accuracy as [`bessel_i0`].",
        "Bessel J of order zero. Computes the Bessel function of the first kind, J\u2080(x). Accurate to roughly 1e-15 (absolute): power series for |x| < 5, Miller backward recurrence for 5 \u2264 |x| \u2264 25 and the Hankel asymptotic expansion beyond.",
        "Bessel J of order one. Computes the Bessel function of the first kind, J\u2081(x) (odd in `x`). Same method and accuracy as [`bessel_j0`].",
        "`K\u2080(x)`.", "`K\u2081(x)`.",
        "Bessel Y of order zero. Computes the Bessel function of the second kind, Y\u2080(x), for x \u2265 0 (`NaN` for x < 0, `-\u221e` at 0). Series (A&S 9.1.13) for x < 5, the Neumann expansion `Y\u2080 = (2/\u03c0)(ln(x/2) + \u03b3) J\u2080 - (4/\u03c0) \u03a3 (-1)^k J_{2k}/k` (A&S 9.1.88) for 5 \u2264 x \u2264 25 and the Hankel expansion beyond; accurate to about 1e-14.",
        "Bessel Y of order one. Computes the Bessel function of the second kind, Y\u2081(x), for x \u2265 0 (`NaN` for x < 0, `-\u221e` at 0). Series (A&S 9.1.11) for x < 5; for 5 \u2264 x \u2264 25 the derivative of the Neumann expansion of Y\u2080 (`Y\u2081 = -Y\u2080'`, `J\u2080' = -J\u2081`, `J_{2k}' = (J_{2k-1} - J_{2k+1})/2`); Hankel expansion beyond. Accurate to about 1e-14.",
        "Dimensionless serial optimization: initial vector,[lower bounds,upper bounds for lbfgs_b],positive tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,objective callback,gradient callback. Both callbacks share a strict evaluation budget. Gradient length matches initial vector; L-BFGS methods retain ten correction pairs. Returns x,value,iterations,evaluations,converged; callback failures propagate unchanged. BFGS quasi-Newton method with a weak-Wolfe line search. If `grad` is `None` a central-difference gradient is used.",
        "Measured nonlinear problem with explicit coordinate_scale and value_scale, dimensionless tolerance, max_iterations and max_evaluations controls. Solves in normalized coordinates/residuals. Arguments: initial, controls, optional lower/upper, objective/residual, optional gradient. Gradient units are objective/coordinate. Homogeneous coordinate and residual vectors; callback failures propagate and budgets are shared. BFGS quasi-Newton method with a weak-Wolfe line search. If `grad` is `None` a central-difference gradient is used.",
        "Floating-point binomial coefficient; returns zero when k exceeds n. Computes the binomial coefficient C(n, k) = n! / (k! * (n-k)!)",
        "Seeded nonparametric bootstrap: sample vector, positive replicate count, nonnegative u64 seed, statistic-evaluation budget, statistic callback. The callback accepts a sample-unit vector and returns a finite scalar with independently declared statistic units; every summary/replicate retains those units. Budget must cover the original estimate and every replicate. Work is capped at 100000000 resampled entries and storage at 8388608 values. Percentile confidence bounds are 95 percent and are statistical estimates, not coverage guarantees. Non-parametric bootstrap resampling with `n_resamples` draws. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Empty`] when `samples` is empty, [`StatsExtError::Domain`] when the resample count is zero or cannot be allocated, a uniform draw is outside `[0, 1)`, or a statistic is non-finite. Callback errors propagate.",
        "Serial bracketed Brent root solver: lower bound, upper bound, positive argument tolerance, positive iteration budget, callback. The root and tolerance have callback-domain dimensions; residual dimensions are independent. Returns root/evaluations without an extra residual evaluation. Endpoint samples count as evaluations, callback failures propagate unchanged, and iteration exhaustion is an explicit error. Brent's method on a bracketing interval `[a, b]`. # Errors [`RootError::NotBracketed`] if the endpoints have the same nonzero sign, [`RootError::Invalid`] for non-finite inputs, non-positive tolerance, or non-finite callback values, and [`RootError::NoConvergence`] at the limit.",
        "Bracketed Brent root solver returning the root, actual evaluation count and final two endpoints in ascending order. The argument tolerance and all returned coordinates use the callback's input dimension. An exact zero repeats its coordinate at both endpoints.",
        "Dimensionless nonlinear system solver: initial vector,positive tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,residual callback. Callback input and output retain the initial vector length. Returns x,evaluations; callback failures and iteration exhaustion are explicit errors. Broyden's \"good\" method: finite-difference Jacobian at the start, then rank-one updates of its inverse-applied form (Sherman-Morrison on `B`). # Errors As [`newton_system`].",
        "Measured nonlinear problem with explicit coordinate_scale and value_scale, dimensionless tolerance, max_iterations and max_evaluations controls. Solves in normalized coordinates/residuals. Arguments: initial, controls, optional lower/upper, objective/residual, optional gradient. Gradient units are objective/coordinate. Homogeneous coordinate and residual vectors; callback failures propagate and budgets are shared. Broyden's \"good\" method: finite-difference Jacobian at the start, then rank-one updates of its inverse-applied form (Sherman-Morrison on `B`). # Errors As [`newton_system`].",
        "Fit a portable B-spline model. Axis and ordinate units are carried independently by canonical witnesses. Fits an interpolating B-spline of `degree` through `(xs, ys)`. # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`], [`InterpError::Domain`], or [`InterpError::Singular`].",
        "Evaluate a fitted portable B-spline. Validates opaque kind/version, payload bounds and knot structure; query and returned values retain their independent physical units. Evaluates the B-spline at `x`. A degree-zero fit to one sample gives a constant at every finite query. # Errors [`InterpError::Domain`] when the query is nonfinite, or an error from [`bspline_eval`] if the stored spline representation is invalid.",
        "Build immutable portable BVH defining data from same-unit N x 3 lower/upper corner tensors. Payload kind rssn/bvh-defining-data/v1 stores only canonical finite corner rows, never pointers or internal tree nodes. Queries validate/version-check the payload and reconstruct the native tree; portable reconstruction is limited to one million work-array coefficients. Builds a BVH over `aabbs`.",
        "Nearest AABB on a portable BVH. Query coordinates share the descriptor's coordinate units; index/distance tensors have zero or one entry, with measured distances. An empty definition gives empty results. Returns `(aabb_index, distance)` for the [`Aabb`] closest to `point`. If its distance overflows `f64`, the index remains the nearest and the distance is positive infinity.",
        "Indices of AABBs hit by origin+t*direction, t>=0, on an immutable portable BVH. Origin shares coordinate units; direction is dimensionless and nonzero. Payload kind/version, finite corners and allocation bounds are checked before reconstruction. Returns the indices of all [`Aabb`]s intersected by the ray `origin + t * dir` (`t >= 0`).",
        "Classic Cauchy upper bound from a complex coefficient vector in ascending order. Coefficients may share ordinate units. The polynomial coordinate and the result are dimensionless. Classic Cauchy upper bound on the modulus of all roots of $p(z) = \\\\sum_{k=0}^n `c_k` z^k$ (ascending coefficients): $R = 1 + \\\\max_{0 \\\\le k < n} |`c_k` / `c_n`|$.",
        "Compute the sharp Cauchy radius from ascending complex coefficients with a common ordinate dimension. Returns zero for a nonconstant monomial. The polynomial coordinate and the result are dimensionless. Computes the sharp Cauchy radius, the positive solution of $|`c_n`| R^n = \\\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} |`c_k`| R^k$ for a non-monomial polynomial. Coefficients are ascending. A monomial has radius zero.",
        "Lower Cholesky factor of a positive definite matrix. Cholesky factor `L` (lower triangular) with `A = L L^T`. # Errors [`DenseError::Singular`] if `A` is not symmetric positive definite.",
        "Stefan-Boltzmann law: total power radiated by a blackbody. P = \u03c3 * A * T\u2074 # Arguments * `area` - Surface area (m\u00b2) * `temperature` - Temperature (K)",
        "Compton wavelength of a particle: `\u03bb_C` = h/(mc)",
        "Calculates the electric force between two point charges using Coulomb's law. F = k * q1 * q2 / r\u00b2 # Arguments * `q1`, `q2` - Charges (C) * `r` - Distance between charges (m) # Returns force magnitude (N), positive for repulsion",
        "Calculates the cyclotron radius (Larmor radius) for a charged particle. r = mv / (|q|B) # Arguments * `mass` - Particle mass (kg) * `velocity` - Particle speed (m/s) * `charge` - Particle charge (C) * `b_field` - Magnetic field magnitude (T)",
        "De Broglie wavelength: \u03bb = h/p # Arguments * `momentum` - Particle momentum (kg\u00b7m/s)",
        "Calculates the electric field magnitude from a point charge. E = k * q / r\u00b2 # Arguments * `q` - Charge (C) * `r` - Distance from charge (m)",
        "Calculates the electric potential from a point charge. V = k * q / r # Arguments * `q` - Charge (C) * `r` - Distance from charge (m)",
        "Heisenberg uncertainty principle minimum: \u0394x\u00b7\u0394p \u2265 \u0127/2 Returns minimum position uncertainty given momentum uncertainty.",
        "Hydrogen atom energy levels (Bohr model). `E_n` = -13.6 eV / n\u00b2 # Arguments * `n` - Principal quantum number (1, 2, 3, ...) # Returns energy in Joules",
        "Ideal gas law: PV = nRT, solving for pressure. # Arguments * `n` - Amount of substance (mol) * `t` - Temperature (K) * `v` - Volume (m\u00b3)",
        "Ideal gas law: PV = nRT, solving for temperature.",
        "Ideal gas law: PV = nRT, solving for volume.",
        "Length contraction: L = L\u2080 / \u03b3 # Arguments * `proper_length` - Proper length (m) * `velocity` - Relative velocity (m/s)",
        "Lorentz factor \u03b3 = 1 / \u221a(1 - v\u00b2/c\u00b2) # Arguments * `velocity` - Velocity (m/s)",
        "Calculates the Lorentz force on a charged particle. F = q(E + v \u00d7 B) - returns force magnitude assuming v \u27c2 B # Arguments * `charge` - Particle charge (C) * `velocity` - Particle speed (m/s) * `e_field` - Electric field magnitude (V/m) * `b_field` - Magnetic field magnitude (T)",
        "Calculates the magnetic field magnitude at distance r from an infinite wire. B = \u03bc\u2080 * I / (2\u03c0 * r) # Arguments * `current` - Current in wire (A) * `r` - Perpendicular distance from wire (m)",
        "Mass-energy equivalence: E = mc\u00b2",
        "Mean speed from Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution. \u27e8v\u27e9 = \u221a(8kT/(\u03c0m))",
        "RMS speed from Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution. `v_rms` = \u221a(3kT/m)",
        "Maxwell-Boltzmann speed distribution probability density. f(v) = 4\u03c0 * (m/(2\u03c0kT))^(3/2) * v\u00b2 * exp(-mv\u00b2/(2kT)) # Arguments * `v` - Speed (m/s) * `mass` - Particle mass (kg) * `temperature` - Temperature (K)",
        "Photon energy: E = hf = hc/\u03bb # Arguments * `wavelength` - Wavelength (m)",
        "Photon wavelength from energy.",
        "Quantum harmonic oscillator energy levels. `E_n` = \u0127\u03c9(n + 1/2) # Arguments * `n` - Quantum number (0, 1, 2, ...) * `omega` - Angular frequency (rad/s)",
        "Relativistic kinetic energy `KE = (\u03b3 - 1)mc\u00b2`, accurate also in the low-speed limit. # Arguments * `mass` - Rest mass (kg) * `velocity` - Velocity (m/s)",
        "Relativistic momentum: p = \u03b3mv # Arguments * `mass` - Rest mass (kg) * `velocity` - Velocity (m/s)",
        "Total relativistic energy: E = \u03b3mc\u00b2 # Arguments * `mass` - Rest mass (kg) * `velocity` - Velocity (m/s)",
        "Relativistic velocity addition: u = (v + w) / (1 + vw/c\u00b2)",
        "Simple harmonic oscillator solution. Returns position x(t) = A * cos(\u03c9t + \u03c6) # Arguments * `amplitude` - Amplitude A * `omega` - Angular frequency \u03c9 (rad/s) * `phase` - Initial phase \u03c6 (radians) * `time` - Time t",
        "Time dilation: \u0394t = \u03b3 * \u0394t\u2080 # Arguments * `proper_time` - Proper time interval (s) * `velocity` - Relative velocity (m/s)",
        "Wien's displacement law: peak wavelength of blackbody radiation. `\u03bb_max` = b / T, where b \u2248 2.898 \u00d7 10\u207b\u00b3 m\u00b7K",
        "Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget. Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh: a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules: finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule: tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations. Callback failures propagate unchanged. Clenshaw-Curtis quadrature of `f` on `[a, b]` with `n + 1` points.",
        "Computes the Bell number `B(n)` as an `f64`. Counts all partitions of a set of `n` elements, including the one partition of the empty set. Returns positive infinity when the count overflows `f64` (`n >= 219`).",
        "Returns a Bell number for each unsigned integer index in input order. Finite values are Some, and counts that exceed Float are None. The batch reuses the same finite-count table as the scalar Bell function. Computes [`bell`] for each index using the shared table of finite counts. Accepts unsorted indices and preserves their order and repetitions. An empty input yields an empty output.",
        "Computes the nth Catalan number `C_n`. `C_n` = (1 / (n + 1)) * C(2n, n)",
        "Computes the number of combinations `C(n, k) = n! / (k! * (n-k)!)`. # Arguments * `n` - The total number of items. * `k` - The number of items to choose. # Returns The number of combinations as an `f64`. Returns `0.0` if `k > n`.",
        "Computes the factorial of `n` (`n!`) as an `f64`. # Arguments * `n` - The non-negative integer for which to compute the factorial. # Returns The factorial of `n` as an `f64`. Returns `f64::INFINITY` if `n` is too large to fit in `f64`.",
        "Computes the falling factorial (x)_n = x(x-1)...(x-n+1).",
        "Computes the number of permutations `P(n, k) = n! / (n-k)!`. # Arguments * `n` - The total number of items. * `k` - The number of items to choose. # Returns The number of permutations as an `f64`. Returns `0.0` if `k > n`.",
        "Computes the rising factorial (Pochhammer symbol) x^(n) = x(x+1)...(x+n-1).",
        "Numerically solves a linear recurrence relation by unrolling it. The recurrence relation is assumed to be of the form: `a_n = coeffs[0]*a_{n-1} + coeffs[1]*a_{n-2} + ... + coeffs[order-1]*a_{n-order}`. # Arguments * `coeffs` - A slice of `f64` representing the coefficients of the recurrence relation. * `initial_conditions` - A slice of `f64` representing the initial values `a_0, a_1, ..., a_{order-1}`. * `target_n` - The index `n` for which to compute `a_n`. # Returns A `Result` containing the numerical value of `a_n`. # Errors Returns an error if: - The number of initial conditions does not match the order of the recurrence. - The internal storage fails to compute values (e.g., empty result vector).",
        "Computes the Stirling number of the second kind `S(n, k)` as an `f64`. Counts partitions of `n` elements into `k` nonempty subsets. The empty set has one partition into zero subsets. Returns zero for `k > n` and for `k = 0 < n`, and positive infinity when the count overflows `f64`.",
        "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. `f^(n)(z) = n! / (2 pi i) \u222e f(w) / (w - z)^(n+1) dw` over the circle of `radius` around `z` (Cauchy's integral formula); accurate for high orders where finite differences are not.",
        "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. `\u222e f(z) dz` over the circle `|z - center| = radius`, counter-clockwise, with `n` equally spaced nodes.",
        "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. `\u222b f(z) dz` along the polygon through `path`, by Simpson's rule on each segment subdivided `per_segment` times.",
        "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. The number of zeros minus the number of poles of `f` inside a circle. The circle is `|z - center| = radius` (argument principle), and the count is the winding number of the polygon through the sampled values of `f`. Samples `f` once per node, in counterclockwise order, with at least 64 nodes. Returns `None` if a sampled value is zero or nonfinite or if a sampled change of argument exceeds `pi / 2`. Undersampling can still alias a winding whose changes between nodes are not observed.",
        "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. `f'(z)` by the central difference along the real axis (valid for analytic `f`).",
        "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. Newton's method for an analytic `f` with derivative `df`, from `z0`. Returns the root once a step is below `tolerance`, or `None` after `max_iterations` or at a vanishing derivative.",
        "Native complex analysis with positive coordinate/value scales, independent D/E physical units and a shared actual callback budget. Circle/residue/count: center,radius,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Contour: path,panels,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Derivative: z,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f. Newton: z,xscale,yscale,tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,f,df. Cauchy: z,radius,order,n,xscale,yscale,max_evaluations,f; dynamic result explicitly retains exact E/D^order units. Analyticity and contour isolation are caller assumptions; native singular/nonconvergence outcomes remain errors. The residue of `f` at `z0`: `(1 / 2 pi i) \u222e f dz` over a small circle that must enclose no other singularity.",
        "Returns the winding number about zero of the polygon formed by a rank-one tensor of complex vertices with a common physical dimension. The last vertex connects to the first. Counterclockwise winding is positive. Empty paths and paths through zero return a domain error. Winding number about the origin of the closed polygonal path through `points`. Joins the last vertex to the first. Counterclockwise turns count as positive. Returns `None` for an empty path, a nonfinite vertex or an edge through the origin. A single nonzero finite vertex has winding number zero.",
        "Converts degrees to radians.",
        "Computes the dot product of two vectors of any dimension. # Errors Returns an error when the slices have different lengths.",
        "Converts radians to degrees.",
        "2-norm condition number; infinity for a singular matrix. 2-norm condition number `sigma_max / sigma_min` (infinite when singular). # Errors As [`svd`].",
        "Discretise a measured continuous state-space model under a zero-order hold. Accepts positive sample time and returns a portable record with a, b, c, d and dt. Matrix a has inverse-time units before discretisation, and b gains one factor of Time. Zero-order-hold discretisation with sample time `dt`. Computes `exp([A B; 0 0] dt)` by scaling and squaring with a diagonal Pade approximant of order six. The upper-right block is the exactly integrated input matrix, not a Euler approximation. # Errors Returns an error when `dt` is not finite and positive, or when the matrix exponential fails.",
        "Simulate a measured continuous state-space model on a strictly increasing time grid. Hold each input row until the next time and return one measured output row per grid point. Simulates a continuous-time model on the strictly increasing time grid `t` from initial state `x0` under piecewise-constant (zero-order-hold) inputs `u`, where `u[k]` is held across `[t[k], t[k + 1])`. Each step uses exact zero-order-hold discretisation via [`StateSpace::c2d_zoh`], caching `(Ad, Bd)` whenever consecutive step sizes match to a relative tolerance of `1e-12`. Uniform grids compute the matrix exponential only once. Returns the output `y[k] = C x(t[k]) + D u[k]` at each grid point. # Errors Returns an error when the model is discrete-time, when `x0` or `u` dimensions are wrong, when `u.len() != t.len()`, when any entry is non-finite, or when `t` is not strictly increasing.",
        "Simulate a portable discrete state-space model from an initial state and one input row per sample. Return one measured output row per input row, including the initial output and direct feedthrough. Simulates a discrete-time model from `x0` under the input sequence `u`. The recursion `x[k+1] = A x[k] + B u[k]`, `y[k] = C x[k] + D u[k]` is applied directly. For continuous-time models, use [`StateSpace::simulate_continuous`] (with an explicit time grid) or [`StateSpace::simulate_with_dt`] (with a uniform sample time). Returns the output at each input sample. # Errors Returns an error for a wrong `x0` or input length, or when the model has no sample time.",
        "One pass of Aitken's delta-squared process over `sequence`. Entry `i` is `s_i - (s_{i+1} - s_i)^2 / (s_{i+2} - 2 s_{i+1} + s_i)`. Entries whose second difference is below `1e-9` in magnitude (already converged, or numerically meaningless) are skipped, so the result can be shorter than `len - 2`; fewer than three inputs give an empty vector.",
        "Richardson extrapolation of approximations `A(h), A(h/2), A(h/4), ...`. For errors that expand in powers of `h^2` (Romberg integration, central differences), the standard factors are `4^j`. The result is the diagonal of the extrapolation table, with the highest order estimate last.",
        "Richardson extrapolation with a configurable error-term growth factor. Column `j` of the table eliminates one error term by the factor `growth^j`: `growth = 4` for errors in `h^2, h^4, ...` as `h` halves, `growth = 2` for errors in `1/n, 1/n^2, ...` as the number of terms `n` doubles. Returns the diagonal and retains only one working column. For finite `growth > 1`, a change of scale avoids overflow in intermediate powers or products when the estimate is finite.",
        "Wynn's epsilon algorithm (Shanks transformation) on `sequence`. Returns the accelerated estimates: the first entry is the last element of the sequence itself, and each further entry is the last element of the next even epsilon column, so later entries are higher-order estimates of the limit. Estimates that are not finite are dropped, and the process stops when two consecutive values coincide (exact convergence).",
        "Pearson correlation of equally sized samples. Computes the Pearson correlation coefficient of two slices of data. # Arguments * `data1` - The first slice of `f64` data points. * `data2` - The second slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The Pearson correlation coefficient as an `f64`.",
        "The hyperbolic cosine integral `Chi(x) = \u03b3 + ln x + \u222b_0^x (cosh t - 1)/t dt` for `x > 0`.",
        "The cosine integral `Ci(x) = \u03b3 + ln x + \u222b_0^x (cos t - 1)/t dt` for `x > 0`.",
        "Cosine similarity; zero vectors are invalid. Computes the cosine similarity between two vectors. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths or if any vector is zero.",
        "Sample covariance of equally sized samples. Computes the sample covariance of two slices of data. # Arguments * `data1` - The first slice of `f64` data points. * `data2` - The second slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The covariance of the two data sets, or NaN when the slices differ in length or hold fewer than two points.",
        "Computes the cross product of two 3D vectors. # Arguments * `v1` - First 3D vector. * `v2` - Second 3D vector. # Returns * `Ok(Vec<f64>)` - The 3D cross product vector. # Errors Returns an error if the input vectors are not 3D.",
        "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Solves `A x = b` by repeated cycles. # Panics Panics if the right-hand side or supplied initial iterate has the wrong length.",
        "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Solves `A x = b` by repeated cycles; returns the solution, the relative residual history and whether `tol` was reached.",
        "BiCGSTAB with identity preconditioning. Convergence reports the independently recomputed relative residual. Preconditioned `BiCGSTAB` for general nonsymmetric operators. Uses the same finite-input, complete linear-operator, zero-RHS, and verified convergence contract as [`cg`]. Coefficient ratios and preconditioned directions retain their scales to avoid spurious arithmetic breakdown.",
        "Conjugate gradients with identity preconditioning; the matrix must be symmetric positive definite. Convergence reports the independently recomputed relative residual. Preconditioned conjugate gradients for SPD operators. `apply(x, y)` computes `y = A x`; `precond(r, z)` computes `z = M^-1 r`. Both operators must be linear and overwrite every output entry. Inputs, output entries, and tolerance must be finite; tolerance is nonnegative and an initial guess must have the RHS length. Invalid inputs or incomplete operator outputs produce a nonconverged result rather than false success. The solver normalizes the initial residual correction and search directions; dot products and coefficient ratios retain binary exponents until vector updates. Convergence is checked against the original system, not just the recursive residual. Residual correction is refined under one shared iteration budget when normalization loses tiny components. A zero iteration budget still checks the initial guess.",
        "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Preconditioner action `z = M^{-1} r` (one cycle from zero), symmetric positive definite for symmetric `A`.",
        "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Implicitly restarted Arnoldi for the `k` wanted eigenpairs of a general real operator. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k >= n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if the small dense eigenproblem fails.",
        "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Sparse-LU shift-invert eigensolve with verified residuals in the original problem. Duplicate CSR entries are accumulated, and the shift is subtracted once per row. # Errors Invalid CSR data, singular shifted factorization, or failed eigensolve.",
        "Validated sorted structural CSR sparse eigen/AMG adapters. Lanczos/Arnoldi: matrix,k,selection text,positive relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv (0 auto). Shift-invert: matrix,k,shift,symmetric,relative tolerance,max_restarts,ncv. Eigenvalues and independently recomputed original-system residual norms carry matrix dimensions; eigenvectors are normalized and dimensionless. Symmetric methods reject nonsymmetric coefficients. AMG: matrix,RHS,positive relative tolerance,cycle/iteration budget; solution has RHS/matrix dimensions, history/residual are relative and dimensionless. Smoothed aggregation requires SPD-compatible setup; general AMG uses its positive-diagonal setup contract. Convergence flags require verified original-system residuals. Thick-restart Lanczos for the `k` wanted eigenpairs of a symmetric operator on `R^n`. # Errors [`EigsError::Invalid`] for `k == 0` or `k > n`; [`EigsError::Dense`] if the projected eigenproblem fails.",
        "Restarted GMRES with identity preconditioning. Convergence reports the independently recomputed relative residual. Restarted GMRES(`restart`) with right preconditioning. Uses the same finite-input and complete-output contract as [`cg`]. The preconditioned basis vectors are retained, and Arnoldi uses reorthogonalized modified Gram-Schmidt. A zero restart is clamped to one, as before. Final convergence uses the original system residual.",
        "MINRES for symmetric indefinite systems, identity preconditioning; reports independently recomputed original-system residual. MINRES for a symmetric (indefinite allowed) operator with an SPD preconditioner `precond`. The reported `residual` is the true relative residual `|b - A x| / |b|` of the returned iterate; the iteration stops when the recurrence estimate (preconditioned residual norm) drops below `tol` relative to its initial value.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Approximate minimum degree ordering (quotient graph with element absorption, supervariables and mass elimination); see [`crate::kernels::ordering::amd_order`]. Returns `perm[new] = old`.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Half bandwidth `max |i - j|` over the stored entries.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Diagonal of the matrix.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Builds a matrix from `(row, col, value)` triplets; duplicates are summed.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Jacobi preconditioner apply function data: the inverse diagonal.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Matrix-vector product `y = A x`. Every row is an independent sequential dot product, so large matrices are processed in parallel blocks of rows without changing a bit of the result.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Minimum-degree ordering on the elimination graph of the symmetrised pattern: repeatedly eliminates a vertex of smallest current degree, turning its neighbourhood into a clique (exact degrees, lazily maintained heap). Returns `perm[new] = old`.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Nested dissection ordering; see [`crate::kernels::ordering::nested_dissection_order`].",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Symmetric permutation `B[i][j] = A[perm[i]][perm[j]]`.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Reverse Cuthill-McKee ordering of the symmetrised pattern, with a George-Liu pseudo-peripheral start vertex per connected component. Uses the leading `min(rows, cols)` square, with every stored off-diagonal entry contributing an edge regardless of its value. Components, including isolated vertices, are processed in ascending order of their smallest vertex, then the complete order is reversed. Returns `perm` with `perm[new] = old`.",
        "Coefficient of variation. Computes the coefficient of variation (CV = `std_dev` / mean). Useful for comparing variability across datasets with different means.",
        "Dimension-aware residual DAE BDF1/BDF2 with genuine step-doubling control: t0,t1,y0,yp0,atol,rtol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,newton_tolerance,max_newton_iterations,residual. State has arbitrary E dimensions, derivatives E/D, residuals independent R; Newton tolerance carries R. Algebraic initial values must be consistent. Returns times/states/rates/evaluations/rejected. Solves a fully implicit Differential-Algebraic Equation `F(t, y, y') = 0` over `span = (t0, t1)` using variable-step BDF1/BDF2 with Newton iteration. Refines differential derivatives when possible; algebraic initial states must already be consistent. Step doubling controls the BDF local error; Newton failure alone is never treated as an accuracy estimate. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Empty`] if `y0` is empty, [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] for invalid inputs, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if Newton iteration or step control fails.",
        "Dimension-aware semi-explicit DAE with explicit nondimensionalization: t0,t1,y0,z0,time_scale,state_scale,algebraic_scale,constraint_scale,normalized_atol,rtol,normalized_h0,normalized_h_min,normalized_h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,normalized_newton_tolerance,max_newton_iterations,rhs,constraint. Four positive physical reference scales make mixed state/constraint Newton blocks dimensionless. Index1 constraint receives t,y,z; index2 receives t,y. Returns physical times,states,algebraic,rates and actual work counts. Adaptive semi-explicit DAE solver with bounded callback work. # Errors Invalid controls, malformed/nonfinite callbacks, failed consistency or stage solves, exhausted budgets, or an unsatisfied tolerance at minimum step.",
        "Dimension-aware semi-explicit DAE with explicit nondimensionalization: t0,t1,y0,z0,time_scale,state_scale,algebraic_scale,constraint_scale,normalized_atol,rtol,normalized_h0,normalized_h_min,normalized_h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,normalized_newton_tolerance,max_newton_iterations,rhs,constraint. Four positive physical reference scales make mixed state/constraint Newton blocks dimensionless. Index1 constraint receives t,y,z; index2 receives t,y. Returns physical times,states,algebraic,rates and actual work counts. Adaptive semi-explicit DAE solver with bounded callback work. # Errors Invalid controls, malformed/nonfinite callbacks, failed consistency or stage solves, exhausted budgets, or an unsatisfied tolerance at minimum step.",
        "Dimension-aware constant-delay method-of-steps DP5(4): t0,t1,y0,positive_delays,atol,rtol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,rhs,history. RHS receives t,y,delay-by-state matrix and returns state/time. History receives time and returns state. Strict total RHS/prehistory callback budget; no fabricated intra-step history. Returns times/states/Hermite rates/evaluations/rejected. Integrate a constant-delay differential equation. Solves a constant-delay Delay Differential Equation (DDE) system: `y'(t) = rhs(t, y(t), &[y(t - \\\\tau_1), ..., y(t - \\\\tau_k)])` over `span = (t0, t1)` given a pre-initial history function `history(t)` for `t <= t0`. Uses the **Method of Steps** with an adaptive Dormand\u2013Prince 5(4) pair, continuous cubic Hermite history interpolation, and automatic breakpoint tracking at integer combinations of the delays `t0 + \\\\sum m_j \\\\tau_j` so that derivative discontinuities at `t0` do not degrade high-order convergence. # Errors [`OdeGeomError::Empty`] if `y0` is empty, [`OdeGeomError::Domain`] if any delay is non-positive or inputs are non-finite, [`OdeGeomError::ShapeMismatch`] if `rhs` or `history` returns a vector of length `!= y0.len()`, and [`OdeGeomError::NotConverged`] if the step size collapses or `max_steps` is exceeded.",
        "Solves `A x = b` for symmetric positive definite `A` using Cholesky. # Errors As [`cholesky`].",
        "Determinant with runtime matrix-order dimension exponent. The Value result retains entry-units raised to matrix order; only square matrices are accepted. Determinant of the factored matrix.",
        "Minimum-norm least-squares solution through the SVD (handles rank deficiency).",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Computes the LU factorisation of a square matrix. # Errors [`DenseError::Shape`] for non-square input or incompatible matrix storage, [`DenseError::Singular`] when a pivot is exactly zero, or [`DenseError::NonFinite`] for NaN or infinite entries.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Determinant of the factored matrix.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Inverse of the factored matrix.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Solves `A x = b`, returning a new solution vector. Returns an empty vector for a short right-hand side and ignores any trailing entries. Use [`Lu::solve_into`] to check shape and finiteness.",
        "Orthonormal basis of the null space `{x : A x = 0}` as the columns of the result (`cols x (cols - rank)`), from the SVD.",
        "Orthonormal basis of the column space (range) of `A` as the columns of the result (`rows x rank`), from the SVD; `tol` as in [`null_space`].",
        "Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse with a relative singular-value threshold. # Errors Returns non-finite input for invalid relative tolerance, or SVD errors.",
        "Real Schur decomposition `A = Q T Q^T` with orthogonal `Q` and quasi-upper-triangular `T`.",
        "Solves the square system `A x = b` by LU. # Errors Propagates the errors of [`lu_factor`] and [`Lu::solve_into`].",
        "Solves a symmetric positive-definite tridiagonal linear system in `O(n)` time via `LDL^T` factorisation.",
        "Solves a general tridiagonal linear system in `O(n)` time via the Thomas algorithm: `sub[i - 1] * x[i - 1] + diag[i] * x[i] + sup[i] * x[i + 1] = rhs[i]`.",
        "The Pearson correlation coefficient of two equally long samples. Population and sample normalization give the same coefficient. # Errors Returns [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`] for different lengths, [`NumericError::Empty`] for empty samples, and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values or a sample with no spread.",
        "The sample covariance of two equally long samples. Use `population` to divide by the sample length. Otherwise, divide by one less than the length for samples with two or more observations. The covariance of a pair of singleton samples is zero. # Errors Returns [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`] for different lengths, [`NumericError::Empty`] for empty samples, and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values or a result outside the finite range.",
        "The covariance of two exact samples. # Errors [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`] when the lengths differ, and [`NumericError::Empty`] when there are no elements.",
        "A uniform histogram with `bins` buckets. Returns `(counts, edges)`. For a nonconstant sample, the `bins + 1` edges span the sample range. An interior edge belongs to the bucket above it, and the highest edge belongs to the last bucket. Edges can coincide when a bucket is narrower than the representable spacing. Constant samples use unit-width buckets starting at the value minus 0.5, with all observations in the first bucket. Large samples with a bounded number of buckets can be counted in parallel. The counts and edges are independent of the active Rayon pool size. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values, a zero bucket count, or buckets that cannot be allocated.",
        "Histogram counts for caller-supplied, possibly nonuniform bucket edges. Returns one count per adjacent pair of edges. Every bucket includes its lower edge, and the last bucket also includes its upper edge. Every sample appears in one bucket, including observations exactly on the outer edges. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample. [`NumericError::Domain`] if the edges contain fewer than two values, fail to increase strictly, or would require unallocatable counter storage. Non-finite observations or edges and observations outside the supplied range also give a domain error.",
        "The excess kurtosis `m4 / m2^2 - 3`. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample, and [`NumericError::Domain`] for a constant sample or non-finite values.",
        "The arithmetic mean of an exact sample. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample.",
        "Median with type-7 interpolation; sorting occurs in adapter-owned storage, preserving the caller's vector. The median of a float sample (runs in `O(n)` average time via selection). # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values.",
        "Exact rational median; sorting occurs in local storage and no float conversion is performed. The median of an exact sample. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample.",
        "The most frequent value of a float sample; ties go to the smallest. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values.",
        "The most frequent value of an exact sample. Ties go to the smallest value, so the answer does not depend on the order of the input. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample.",
        "The `q`-quantile of a float sample, `0 \u2264 q \u2264 1`. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite values or a `q` outside `[0, 1]`.",
        "The `q`-quantile of a sorted exact sample, `0 \u2264 q \u2264 1`. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] when `q` is outside `[0, 1]`.",
        "Type-7 quantiles of a finite sample, in the supplied probability order. A single query selects the two adjacent ranks in linear time. Multiple queries share one ordered copy of the sample. # Errors Returns [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample and [`NumericError::Domain`] for non-finite observations or a probability outside `[0, 1]`.",
        "The skewness `m3 / m2^{3/2}`. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample, and [`NumericError::Domain`] for a constant sample or non-finite values.",
        "The variance of an exact sample. # Errors [`NumericError::Empty`] for an empty sample.",
