        "Numerical digamma function. Computes the digamma function, \u03c8(x) = d/dx ln(\u0393(x)) = \u0393'(x)/\u0393(x).",
        "Maps a dimensionless complex point in the open Poincare unit disk to the open upper half-plane. An input on or outside the unit circle or a nonfinite result produces a domain error. Inverse Cayley transform mapping the unit disk `{ |w| < 1 }` to the upper half-plane: `z = i * (1 + w) / (1 - w)`. Returns `None` at `w = 1`.",
        "Euclidean distance between vectors. Computes the Euclidean distance between two vectors without allocating an intermediate vector. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "Dirichlet log density on the unit simplex, with positive finite concentrations.",
        "Dirichlet density on the unit simplex, with positive finite concentrations.",
        "One Dirichlet sample from a deterministic seed and positive finite concentrations.",
        "Dimensionless multivariate normal log density with a row-major covariance matrix. Rejects non-finite data, mismatched dimensions and non-positive-definite covariance.",
        "Dimensionless multivariate normal log densities in row-major query order. A single covariance factor is reused by every query.",
        "Dimensionless multivariate normal density; covariance dimensions must match the mean.",
        "One dimensionless multivariate normal sample from a deterministic seed. Rejects mismatched dimensions and invalid covariance matrices.",
        "Inverts the Weibull survival probability `P(X > x)` directly. A survival probability of zero gives positive infinity, and a probability of one gives zero. Tiny positive probabilities retain the upper tail. # Errors Returns [`DistError::Domain`] for invalid parameters or survival probability.",
        "Evaluates Weibull lower-tail quantiles using one validated shape and scale. Probabilities are in `[0, 1]`. Order and repetitions are preserved, and an empty probability slice yields an empty vector. The endpoints are `0` and positive infinity, as in [`Distribution::quantile`]. # Errors Returns [`DistError::Domain`] for invalid parameters or probabilities.",
        "Dimensionless nonlinear system solver: initial vector,positive tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,residual callback. Callback input and output retain the initial vector length. Returns x,evaluations; callback failures and iteration exhaustion are explicit errors. Powell's trust-region dogleg solver for nonlinear systems `f(x) = 0`. # Errors As [`newton_system`].",
        "Measured nonlinear problem with explicit coordinate_scale and value_scale, dimensionless tolerance, max_iterations and max_evaluations controls. Solves in normalized coordinates/residuals. Arguments: initial, controls, optional lower/upper, objective/residual, optional gradient. Gradient units are objective/coordinate. Homogeneous coordinate and residual vectors; callback failures propagate and budgets are shared. Powell's trust-region dogleg solver for nonlinear systems `f(x) = 0`. # Errors As [`newton_system`].",
        "Dot product of equal-length vectors. Computes the dot product of two vectors. Formula: $\\\\mathbf{a} \\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{b} = \\\\sum_{i=1}^n `a_i` `b_i`$ # Arguments * `v1` - The first vector. * `v2` - The second vector. # Returns * `Ok(f64)` - The dot product. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Absolute value `|self|`.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Inverse cosine `acos(self)`.",
        "Native normalized dual arithmetic; tangent propagation stays in the RSSN Dual implementation.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Inverse sine `asin(self)`.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Inverse tangent `atan(self)`.",
        "A dimensionless normalized dual constant; used inside exact forward-AD callbacks. Creates a constant dual number with derivative `0`.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Cosine of `self`.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Hyperbolic cosine `cosh(self)`.",
        "Native normalized dual arithmetic; tangent propagation stays in the RSSN Dual implementation.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Natural exponential `exp(self)`.",
        "Exact native AD with normalized callback variables and independent physical references: point,positive coordinate_scale,positive value_scale,max_evaluations,callback. Forward callback scalar duals are {val,deriv}; vector duals are {val:vector,deriv:vector}; all dual arithmetic is dimensionless and uses native num_dual_* operators. Reverse callbacks use checked NativeAdVar handles on an ephemeral session tape; native operations enforce same-tape identity and a65536-node limit. Output derivatives restore E/D; no numerical differencing or opaque-callback finite-difference fallback. Exact first derivative `(f(x), f'(x))` of `f(Dual) -> Dual` via forward-mode AD.",
        "Exact native AD with normalized callback variables and independent physical references: point,positive coordinate_scale,positive value_scale,max_evaluations,callback. Forward callback scalar duals are {val,deriv}; vector duals are {val:vector,deriv:vector}; all dual arithmetic is dimensionless and uses native num_dual_* operators. Reverse callbacks use checked NativeAdVar handles on an ephemeral session tape; native operations enforce same-tape identity and a65536-node limit. Output derivatives restore E/D; no numerical differencing or opaque-callback finite-difference fallback. Exact gradient of `f(&[Dual]) -> Dual` at `x` via forward-mode AD. # Errors [`AdError::Empty`] when `x` is empty.",
        "Exact native AD with normalized callback variables and independent physical references: point,positive coordinate_scale,positive value_scale,max_evaluations,callback. Forward callback scalar duals are {val,deriv}; vector duals are {val:vector,deriv:vector}; all dual arithmetic is dimensionless and uses native num_dual_* operators. Reverse callbacks use checked NativeAdVar handles on an ephemeral session tape; native operations enforce same-tape identity and a65536-node limit. Output derivatives restore E/D; no numerical differencing or opaque-callback finite-difference fallback. Exact Jacobian of `f(&[Dual]) -> Vec<Dual>` at `x` via forward-mode AD. Returns a row-major `m \u00d7 n` Jacobian. # Errors [`AdError::Empty`] when `x` is empty or `f` returns an empty vector, and [`AdError::ShapeMismatch`] if the output length varies across passes.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Natural logarithm `ln(self)`.",
        "Native normalized dual arithmetic; tangent propagation stays in the RSSN Dual implementation.",
        "Construct a normalized dual from finite primal and tangent values. Creates a dual number with explicit value and derivative.",
        "Native normalized dual floating power with a dimensionless exponent. Floating-point power `self^p`.",
        "Native normalized dual integer power. Integer power `self^n`. For a finite nonzero base and finite tangent, a representable tangent is retained even when the primal or an unscaled power overflows or underflows.",
        "Evaluate the normalized integer power along val + t * deriv at t = 0. Returns the primal, first derivative and second directional derivative. The exponent is an Int in the signed 32-bit range. Nonfinite results are rejected. Evaluates `self^n` and both derivatives along `self.deriv`. Treats the input as `self.val + t * self.deriv` and differentiates at `t = 0`. The returned dual holds the value and first derivative; the second component is the second derivative with respect to `t`. The powers zero and one have zero second derivative.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Sine of `self`.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Hyperbolic sine `sinh(self)`.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Square root `sqrt(self)`.",
        "Native normalized dual arithmetic; tangent propagation stays in the RSSN Dual implementation.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Tangent of `self`.",
        "Native normalized dual elementary function; rejects nonfinite primal/tangent results. Hyperbolic tangent `tanh(self)`.",
        "A normalized dual variable with tangent one. Creates an independent variable dual number with derivative `1`.",
        "Real eigenvalues and orthonormal eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix. Eigen-decomposition of a symmetric matrix by cyclic Jacobi rotations. Returns eigenvalues in ascending order and the matrix whose columns are the corresponding orthonormal eigenvectors. # Errors [`DenseError::Shape`] for non-square input, [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] after 100 sweeps.",
        "Root annulus for at least two positive, finite real coefficients in ascending order. Coefficients may share ordinate units. The lower and upper root radii are dimensionless. Enestr\u00f6m\u2013Kakeya annulus bounds $(r_{\\\\min}, r_{\\\\max})$ for a polynomial with strictly positive real ascending coefficients $`a_0`, \\\\dots, `a_n` > 0$.",
        "Shannon entropy of the supplied probabilities. Computes the Shannon entropy of a discrete probability distribution. H(X) = -\u03a3 p(x) * log2(p(x))",
        "Numerical error function. Computes the error function, erf(x) = (2/\u221a\u03c0) \u222b\u2080\u02e3 e^(-t\u00b2) dt.",
        "Numerical complementary error function. Computes the complementary error function, erfc(x) = 1 - erf(x).",
        "The imaginary error function `erfi(x) = -i erf(ix) = (2/\u221a\u03c0) \u222b_0^x e^{t\u00b2} dt`, from its everywhere-convergent power series (all terms of one sign, so no cancellation).",
        "Decodes a codeword produced by [`bch_encode`], correcting up to `t` bit errors.",
        "Encodes data bits with a binary BCH code correcting `t` errors. The code is the shortened narrow-sense primitive BCH code of the smallest block length `2^m - 1` that can carry `data.len()` bits (see [`Bch`]); the result is the systematic codeword `data ++ parity`. Use [`Bch::encode`] for explicit control of `(n, k)` and for error details. # Arguments * `data` - The data bits (`0` or `1`). * `t` - The error correction capability (corrects up to `t` bit errors). # Returns The encoded codeword, or an empty vector if the arguments are invalid (empty or non-binary data, `t == 0`, or no supported code is large enough).",
        "Calculates the syndromes of a received codeword. The syndrome `S_i` is the polynomial evaluated at \u03b1^i. Requests beyond 255 return the 255 distinct evaluations, since subsequent roots repeat. Use [`reed_solomon_check`] to validate the block shape and syndromes.",
        "Finds the roots of the error locator polynomial to determine error locations. Uses Chien search to efficiently evaluate the polynomial at all field elements. # Errors Returns an error if the field element inversion fails.",
        "Computes the code rate (number of information bits per coded bit). # Arguments * `k` - Number of information bits. * `n` - Total codeword length. # Returns The code rate as a floating-point number.",
        "Encodes data using a simple rate-1/2 convolutional code. Uses generators G1 = 1+D^2 (0b101) and G2 = 1+D+D^2 (0b111). # Arguments * `data` - The binary data to encode (each byte is 0 or 1). # Returns The encoded data (twice the length of input).",
        "Computes the CRC-16 checksum of the given data. Uses the IBM/ANSI polynomial (0xA001 in reflected form). # Arguments * `data` - The data to compute the checksum for. # Returns The 16-bit CRC checksum. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::crc16_compute; let data = b\"123456789\"; let checksum = crc16_compute(data); assert_eq!(checksum, 0xBB3D); ```",
        "Computes the CRC-32 checksum of the given data. Uses the standard IEEE 802.3 polynomial (0xEDB88320 in reflected form). # Arguments * `data` - The data to compute the checksum for. # Returns The 32-bit CRC checksum. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::crc32_compute_numerical; let data = b\"Hello, World!\"; let checksum = crc32_compute_numerical(data); assert_eq!(checksum, 0xEC4AC3D0); ```",
        "Finalizes a CRC-32 computation started with `crc32_update_numerical`. # Arguments * `crc` - The running CRC value. # Returns The final CRC-32 checksum.",
        "Updates an existing CRC-32 with additional data (for streaming). # Arguments * `crc` - Current CRC value (use 0xFFFFFFFF for initial call). * `data` - Additional data to process. # Returns Updated CRC value (call `crc32_finalize_numerical` to get final CRC).",
        "Verifies the CRC-32 checksum of data. # Arguments * `data` - The data to verify. * `expected_crc` - The expected CRC-32 checksum. # Returns `true` if the computed CRC matches the expected value.",
        "Computes the CRC-8 checksum of the given data. Uses the ITU polynomial (0x07). # Arguments * `data` - The data to compute the checksum for. # Returns The 8-bit CRC checksum.",
        "De-interleaves data (reverses the interleave operation). # Arguments * `data` - The interleaved data. * `depth` - The interleaving depth used during interleaving. # Returns The de-interleaved data.",
        "Estimates the error correction capability from the minimum distance. A code with minimum distance d can correct up to floor((d-1)/2) errors. # Arguments * `min_distance` - The minimum distance of the code. # Returns The number of errors that can be corrected.",
        "Estimates the error detection capability from the minimum distance. A code with minimum distance d can detect up to d-1 errors. # Arguments * `min_distance` - The minimum distance of the code. # Returns The number of errors that can be detected.",
        "Computes error magnitudes using Forney's algorithm. # Arguments * `omega` - The error evaluator polynomial. * `sigma` - The error locator polynomial. * `error_locs` - The positions of errors in the codeword. # Returns A vector of error magnitudes for each error location. # Errors Returns an error if division by zero occurs during magnitude calculation.",
        "Checks if a Hamming(7,4) codeword is valid. # Arguments * `codeword` - A 7-bit codeword # Returns `true` if the codeword is valid (no errors), `false` otherwise.",
        "Decodes a 7-bit Hamming(7,4) codeword, correcting a single-bit error if found. # Arguments * `codeword` - A slice of 7 bytes, each representing a bit (0 or 1). # Returns A `Result` containing: - `Ok((data, error_pos))` where `data` is the 4-bit corrected data and `error_pos` is `Some(index)` if an error was corrected at the given 1-based index, or `None` if no error was found. - `Err(String)` if the input length is not 7. # Errors Returns an error if the input codeword length is not exactly 7. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_decode_numerical; use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_encode_numerical; let data = vec![1, 0, 1, 1]; let mut codeword = hamming_encode_numerical(&data).unwrap(); codeword[2] ^= 1; // Introduce error at position 3 (1-indexed) let (decoded, error_pos) = hamming_decode_numerical(&codeword).unwrap(); assert_eq!(decoded, data); assert_eq!(error_pos, Some(3)); ```",
        "Computes the Hamming distance between two byte slices. The Hamming distance is the number of positions at which the corresponding bits differ. # Arguments * `a` - First byte slice * `b` - Second byte slice # Returns The Hamming distance, or `None` if slices have different lengths. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_distance_numerical; let a = vec![0, 1, 1, 0]; let b = vec![1, 1, 0, 0]; assert_eq!(hamming_distance_numerical(&a, &b), Some(2)); ```",
        "Encodes a 4-bit data block into a 7-bit Hamming(7,4) codeword. Hamming(7,4) is a single-error correcting code. It takes 4 data bits and adds 3 parity bits to create a 7-bit codeword. # Arguments * `data` - A slice of 4 bytes, each representing a bit (0 or 1). # Returns A `Some(Vec<u8>)` of 7 bits representing the codeword, or `None` if the input length is not 4. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_encode_numerical; let data = vec![1, 0, 1, 1]; let codeword = hamming_encode_numerical(&data).unwrap(); assert_eq!(codeword.len(), 7); ```",
        "Computes the Hamming weight (number of 1s) of a byte slice. For binary data where each byte is 0 or 1, this counts the number of 1s. # Arguments * `data` - Byte slice where each byte represents a bit (0 or 1) # Returns The count of non-zero bytes. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_weight_numerical; let data = vec![1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1]; assert_eq!(hamming_weight_numerical(&data), 4); ```",
        "Interleaves data to spread burst errors across multiple codewords. This technique helps in correcting burst errors by distributing consecutive symbols across different codewords. # Arguments * `data` - The data to interleave. * `depth` - The interleaving depth (number of codewords to spread across). # Returns The interleaved data.",
        "Computes the minimum distance of a code given a set of codewords. # Arguments * `codewords` - A slice of codewords. # Returns The minimum Hamming distance between any two distinct codewords.",
        "Checks if a Reed-Solomon codeword is valid. # Arguments * `codeword` - The codeword to check. * `n_parity` - The number of parity symbols. # Returns `true` if the block has at most 255 symbols, contains at least the requested parity count, and has all-zero syndromes.",
        "Decode/correct a finite GF256 Reed-Solomon block, returning a new corrected block. The source's mutable buffer is local to this adapter; inputs are unchanged. Decodes a Reed-Solomon codeword, correcting errors. This implementation uses the Berlekamp-Massey algorithm to find the error locator polynomial, Chien search to find error locations, and Forney's algorithm to find error magnitudes. It corrects up to `n_parity / 2` altered symbols in place. Beyond that correction radius, the received block may decode to another codeword. An error leaves `codeword` unchanged. # Arguments * `codeword` - A mutable slice of bytes representing the received codeword. * `n_parity` - The number of parity symbols in the original encoding. # Returns A `Result` indicating success or an error if decoding fails (e.g., too many errors). # Errors Returns an error if: - The block exceeds 255 symbols or contains fewer symbols than its parity count. - The decoder cannot verify a correction within its error budget. - No valid error locations can be found. - The Berlekamp-Massey or Forney algorithms fail to converge. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::reed_solomon_decode; use rssn::kernels::error_correction::reed_solomon_encode; let message = vec![0x01, 0x02, 0x03]; let mut codeword = reed_solomon_encode(&message, 4).unwrap(); codeword[0] ^= 0xFF; // Introduce error reed_solomon_decode(&mut codeword, 4).unwrap(); assert_eq!(&codeword[..3], &message); ```",
        "Encodes a message using Reed-Solomon codes over GF(2^8). This function implements a systematic encoding scheme for Reed-Solomon codes. It appends `n_parity` parity symbols to the message. The parity symbols are computed by dividing the message polynomial (shifted by `n_parity` positions) by the generator polynomial and taking the remainder. Use [`ReedSolomonEncoder`] to encode multiple messages with the same parity. # Arguments * `message` - A slice of bytes representing the message. * `n_parity` - The number of parity symbols to add. # Returns A `Result` containing the full codeword (message + parity symbols), or an error if the total length exceeds the field size. # Errors Returns an error if: - The total length (message + parity) exceeds 255. - The number of parity symbols is invalid. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::error_correction::reed_solomon_encode; let message = vec![0x01, 0x02, 0x03]; let codeword = reed_solomon_encode(&message, 4).unwrap(); assert_eq!(codeword.len(), 7); // 3 data + 4 parity ```",
        "`E1(x) = \u222b_x^\u221e e^(-t)/t dt` for `x > 0`: the power series for `x <= 1`, Lentz's continued fraction beyond.",
        "The exponential integral `Ei(x) = -PV \u222b_(-x)^\u221e e^(-t)/t dt`.",
        "Floating-point factorial of a nonnegative u64 integer. Computes the factorial n!",
        "The unnormalised forward transform.",
        "Forward two-dimensional FFT of a row-major matrix. # Errors Returns an error if the shape overflows or does not match the input length.",
        "The sample frequencies of a length-`n` transform with spacing `d`.",
        "Forward FFT over every tensor axis. # Errors Returns an error if the shape overflows or does not match the input length.",
        "Center the zero frequency in a one-dimensional spectrum.",
        "The inverse transform, scaled by `1/N`.",
        "Inverse two-dimensional FFT of a row-major matrix. # Errors Returns an error if the shape overflows or does not match the input length.",
        "Undo a centered one-dimensional spectrum.",
        "Rebuilds a real signal of length `n` from the half spectrum.",
        "Rebuilds a real tensor from the half spectrum of [`rfft_axes`]. `shape` is the half-spectrum shape; `n_last` the length of the last listed axis in the result (`2 * (m - 1)` when the original was even).",
        "The transform of a real signal: the non-redundant half of the spectrum, `n/2 + 1` bins.",
        "The half-spectrum transform of a real tensor over `axes`: the last listed axis keeps `n/2 + 1` bins. Returns the data and its shape.",
        "The non-negative sample frequencies of a length-`n` real transform, `n/2 + 1` of them.",
        "Where each element goes under `fftshift` (`inverse = false`) or `ifftshift` of a row-major tensor over `axes`: result element `i` is input element `permutation[i]`.",
        "Linear autocorrelation at nonnegative lags, from zero through `samples.len() - 1`.",
        "Circular convolution of two real sequences of period `max(a.len(), b.len())`.",
        "Linear convolution of two real sequences (`len = a.len() + b.len() - 1`).",
        "Full linear convolution using block FFTs and a reused filter spectrum. Uses zero extension and independent overlap-save blocks. The result has `a.len() + taps.len() - 1` samples, or zero samples if either input is empty. This is especially useful for a recording much longer than its filter. Use [`OverlapSavePlan`] when applying the same filter to several recordings. # Errors Errors on non-finite inputs or output, or an unallocatable extent.",
        "Cross-correlation `c[k] = sum_i a[i + k - (len(b) - 1)] * b[i]` (full mode, length `a.len() + b.len() - 1`).",
        "Multiplies two integer polynomials (ascending coefficients) exactly. Each output is the corresponding integer coefficient modulo `2^64`, interpreted as an `i64`. Use [`poly_mul_i64_checked`] to detect overflow.",
        "Multiplies two integer polynomials (ascending coefficients) exactly. Returns `None` if any output coefficient is outside the range of `i64`.",
        "`num_finite_field_batch_inverse_mod(values, modulus)` returns every modular inverse using a single inversion and pooled storage. The modulus must be at least 2 and every element must be invertible. Computes the modular inverses of all elements in `values` modulo `modulus` using Montgomery's batch-inversion trick. Returns `None` if `modulus < 2` or any element is not coprime to `modulus`.",
        "`num_finite_field_batch_inverse_skip_zeros(values, modulus)` returns each inverse, with 0 in the position of any noninvertible element. The modulus must be at least 2.",
        "`num_finite_field_dot_mod(a, b, modulus)` returns the exact modular dot product of two equal-length vectors. The modulus must be at least 2.",
        "Performs addition in GF(2^8). In GF(2^8), addition is equivalent to a bitwise XOR operation.",
        "Performs division in GF(2^8).", "Computes the multiplicative inverse in GF(2^8).",
        "Performs multiplication in GF(2^8) using lookup tables.",
        "Returns destination XOR coefficient times source over GF(256) with polynomial 0x11d. Supply equal-length byte vectors and an exact byte coefficient. Adds the scaled source into the destination using field addition (XOR). # Errors Returns an error and leaves the destination unchanged when the lengths differ.",
        "Performs exponentiation in GF(2^8).",
        "Multiplies every byte by the supplied coefficient in GF(256) with polynomial 0x11d. Inputs and results are exact integers from 0 through 255. Returns the products in input order. Multiplies each byte of the slice by the coefficient in place.",
        "Exact coefficient convolution modulo an odd prime. Inputs use the same coefficient order. For a nonempty product the next power of two of the output length must divide p-1. An empty input gives an empty product. Output uses the native tensor size limit.",
        "Smallest positive primitive root modulo a prime. Accepts 2 and the odd primes representable in u64. Finds the smallest positive primitive root modulo a prime `modulus`. The root and Montgomery context for an odd prime are cached by modulus. Returns `None` if the modulus is composite, zero or one.",
        "`num_finite_field_slice_add_mod(a, b, modulus)` adds two equal-length vectors of exact u64 integers, returning residues in [0, modulus). The modulus must be at least 2.",
        "`num_finite_field_slice_axpy_mod(alpha, x, y, modulus)` returns (alpha*x + y) reduced elementwise. The vectors must have equal lengths and the modulus must be at least 2.",
        "`num_finite_field_slice_scale_mod(values, scalar, modulus)` multiplies an exact u64 vector by a scalar modulo an integer of at least 2.",
        "`num_finite_field_slice_sub_mod(a, b, modulus)` subtracts two equal-length vectors of exact u64 integers, returning residues in [0, modulus). The modulus must be at least 2.",
        "Actual native unit-height inflow profile evaluated at normalized y; amplitude is normalized by the mechanical L/T reference and result retains velocity units. Axial velocity at height `y` in `[0, 1]`.",
        "Native square unit-domain channel with original free-slip walls and uniform unit inflow; normalized dt uses the explicit Time reference, output velocities and pressure have mechanical units. The native rectangular rejection is preserved. Solves the 2D Navier-Stokes equations for channel flow with an obstacle. Free-slip walls and a uniform unit inflow; see [`run_channel_flow_with`] for no-slip walls and a Poiseuille inflow, and for the boundary treatment. # Arguments * `nx` - Grid width. * `ny` - Grid height. * `re` - Reynolds number. * `dt` - Time step. * `n_iter` - Number of iterations. * `obstacle_mask` - A boolean mask where true indicates an obstacle (u=0). # Returns Tuple of (u, v, p) arrays. The solver works on a square `nx` x `nx` grid (`ny` must equal `nx`). # Errors Returns an error if the grid is smaller than 3 points, not square, or if the obstacle mask does not have shape `(ny, nx)`; also if the pressure solver fails.",
        "Actual native configurable channel model on a square unit domain; inflow, no-slip/slip wall, obstacle mask, Reynolds number and normalized time step are passed unchanged. Physical outputs retain independent velocity/pressure references. Solves the 2D incompressible Navier-Stokes equations in the unit square with an optional no-slip obstacle, by Chorin's projection method. Each step 1. advances an intermediate velocity explicitly (first-order upwind advection, central diffusion) at every fluid point; solid points and boundary points are then set by the boundary conditions, 2. imposes the boundary conditions: the inflow profile on the left, zero-gradient outflow on the right, the [`WallCondition`] on top and bottom, and *no slip* (`u = v = 0`) on every point of the obstacle mask (so fluid points next to the obstacle see zero velocity in their diffusion and advection stencils), 3. solves the pressure Poisson equation `lap(p) = div(u*) / dt` over the fluid points only, with `dp/dn = 0` on the inflow, the walls and all obstacle faces and `p = 0` at the outflow, by preconditioned conjugate gradients, and 4. corrects `u = u* - dt grad(p)` at fluid points, where a gradient across an obstacle face uses the Neumann (mirrored) pressure. The explicit scheme needs `dt <= h^2 re / 4` and `dt <= h / |u|`. # Errors Returns an error for invalid grid size, mask shape, time step, Reynolds number or inflow, non-finite fields, incompatible flux in a closed fluid region, or an unconverged pressure solve.",
        "Actual native legacy staggered cavity on a square unit domain. Lid velocity and time are normalized by explicit mechanical references. Native pressure/multigrid behavior is retained and work is bounded before invocation. Main solver for the 2D lid-driven cavity problem. Chorin's projection method on a staggered (MAC) grid: `p[j][i]` lives at cell centres, `u[j][i]` on the left face of cell `(j, i)` and `v[j][i]` on its bottom face. Each step 1. advances the intermediate velocity explicitly with first-order upwind advection and central diffusion with `nu = 1 / re`, 2. solves `lap(phi) = div(u*) / dt` with the multigrid solver (the same solver and zero-Dirichlet closure as [`run_channel_flow`]), and 3. corrects `u = u* - dt grad(phi)` and sets `p = phi`. The top row of `u` is the moving lid (`lid_velocity`), the bottom row and the side faces are at rest. The explicit scheme needs `dt <= h^2 re / 4` (diffusion) and `dt <= h / |u|` (advection) to stay stable. # Errors Returns an error if the grid is smaller than 3 points, if the cells are not square (`nx != ny`), or if the multigrid solver fails.",
        "Runs the actual native fixed 65x65, 200-step cavity scenario and writes its three native NPY files to the explicitly provided output directory. This legacy routine reports failures itself; the result only acknowledges return from the routine, not successful file creation. An example scenario for the lid-driven cavity simulation. The fields are written as `cavity_u_velocity.npy`, `cavity_v_velocity.npy` and `cavity_pressure.npy` inside `output_dir`, which is created if missing.",
        "Creates the Barnsley fern IFS.",
        "Box-counting dimension of a finite N x 2 point tensor; both coordinate axes share units. Scale/work counts are bounded before native execution. Estimates the box-counting (Minkowski) dimension from a set of 2D points. The box-counting dimension is estimated by: D = lim(\u03b5\u21920) [log(N(\u03b5)) / log(1/\u03b5)] where N(\u03b5) is the number of boxes of side \u03b5 needed to cover the set. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of 2D points. * `num_scales` - Number of different box sizes to use. # Returns An estimate of the box-counting dimension.",
        "Correlation dimension of a finite N x 2 coordinate tensor. Pair allocation and radius-work counts are bounded. Estimates the correlation dimension from the log-log slope of the fraction of pairs closer than each of `num_radii` logarithmically spaced radii. Returns zero when fewer than two scales can determine a slope, and NaN for non-finite coordinates or distances outside the range of `f64`.",
        "Correlation dimension from an N x 2 point tensor and explicit positive finite radii. Radii have the same units as the coordinates. Returns None when fewer than two radii determine a slope. Estimates correlation dimension from caller-supplied positive, finite radii. Radii can be supplied in any order. At radius `r`, only pairs strictly closer than `r` contribute. At least two distinct radii with close pairs are needed for a fit. Returns `None` for non-finite coordinates or when the radii cannot determine a slope.",
        "Generates the Burning Ship fractal. Similar to the Mandelbrot set but uses absolute values: `z = (|Re(z)| + i|Im(z)|)^2 + c` The resulting fractal resembles a burning ship. # Arguments * `width`, `height` - The dimensions of the output image. * `x_range`, `y_range` - The region in the complex plane to plot. * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations per point. # Returns A 2D vector where each element is the escape time.",
        "Native Henon map in normalized dimensionless coordinates. Returns an N x 2 point tensor with a checked finite allocation budget. Generates the Henon map trajectory. The Henon map is a discrete-time dynamical system that exhibits chaotic behavior. The map is: - x_{n+1} = 1 - a*`x_n^2` + `y_n` - y_{n+1} = b*`x_n` Classic parameter values: a = 1.4, b = 0.3 # Arguments * `start_point` - The initial `(x, y)` coordinates. * `num_steps` - The number of iterations. * `a`, `b` - The Henon map parameters. # Returns A `Vec` of `(f64, f64)` tuples representing the trajectory.",
        "Runs the affine chaos game in dimensionless coordinates with historical seed 12345. Provide N x 6 rows (a,b,c,d,e,f), N finite nonnegative weights including a positive weight, and the starting coordinates. Returns one two-coordinate row per retained step after burn-in. Generates an IFS fractal using the chaos game algorithm. # Arguments * `transforms` - The affine transformations. * `probabilities` - Probabilities for selecting each transformation. * `start_point` - Initial point. * `num_points` - Number of points to generate. * `skip` - Number of initial points to skip (for convergence). # Returns A vector of 2D points on the fractal. Invalid input or a nonfinite orbit yields an empty vector. Use [`AffineIfs`] to reuse preparation or choose a seed.",
        "Generates the data for a Julia set image. The Julia set is defined by iterating `z = z*z + c` for a fixed c parameter. Unlike the Mandelbrot set, c is constant and z varies. # Arguments * `width`, `height` - The dimensions of the output image. * `x_range`, `y_range` - The region in the complex plane to plot. * `c` - The constant c parameter `(real, imaginary)`. * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations per point. # Returns A 2D vector where each element is the escape time. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::fractal_geometry_and_chaos::generate_julia_set; // Julia set for c = -0.4 + 0.6i let data = generate_julia_set(100, 100, (-2.0, 2.0), (-2.0, 2.0), (-0.4, 0.6), 50); assert_eq!(data.len(), 100); ```",
        "Generates the points for a Lorenz attractor simulation. The Lorenz attractor is a set of chaotic solutions for a simplified model of atmospheric convection. This function numerically integrates the Lorenz system of differential equations to produce a sequence of points that trace out the attractor. The system is defined by: - dx/dt = \u03c3(y - x) - dy/dt = x(\u03c1 - z) - y - dz/dt = xy - \u03b2z Uses standard parameters: \u03c3 = 10, \u03c1 = 28, \u03b2 = 8/3 # Arguments * `start_point` - The initial `(x, y, z)` coordinates. * `dt` - The time step for the integration. * `num_steps` - The number of integration steps to perform. # Returns A `Vec` of `(f64, f64, f64)` tuples representing the trajectory of the attractor. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::fractal_geometry_and_chaos::generate_lorenz_attractor; let points = generate_lorenz_attractor((0.1, 0.0, 0.0), 0.01, 1000); assert_eq!(points.len(), 1000); ```",
        "Generates a Lorenz attractor with custom parameters. # Arguments * `start_point` - The initial `(x, y, z)` coordinates. * `dt` - The time step for the integration. * `num_steps` - The number of integration steps. * `sigma`, `rho`, `beta` - The Lorenz system parameters. # Returns A `Vec` of `(f64, f64, f64)` tuples representing the trajectory.",
        "Generates the data for a Mandelbrot set image. The Mandelbrot set is a fractal defined by the iteration `z = z*z + c`. Points `c` for which the iteration remains bounded form the set. This function computes the escape time for each point in a given region. # Arguments * `width`, `height` - The dimensions of the output image. * `x_range`, `y_range` - The region in the complex plane to plot. * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations per point. # Returns A 2D vector where each element is the number of iterations it took for that point to escape. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::fractal_geometry_and_chaos::generate_mandelbrot_set; let data = generate_mandelbrot_set(100, 100, (-2.5, 1.0), (-1.5, 1.5), 50); assert_eq!(data.len(), 100); ```",
        "Generates the Multibrot set with exponent d. The Multibrot set is a generalization where `z = z^d + c`. d=2 gives the standard Mandelbrot set. # Arguments * `width`, `height` - The dimensions of the output image. * `x_range`, `y_range` - The region in the complex plane to plot. * `d` - The exponent (power). * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations per point. # Returns A 2D vector where each element is the escape time.",
        "Generates a Newton fractal for `z^3 - 1 = 0`. Newton's method applied to finding roots of `f(z) = z^3 - 1` produces a fractal pattern. Each pixel is colored based on which root it converges to. # Arguments * `width`, `height` - The dimensions of the output image. * `x_range`, `y_range` - The region in the complex plane to plot. * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations per point. * `tolerance` - The convergence tolerance. # Returns A 2D vector where each element indicates which root (0, 1, or 2) was reached, or 3 if no convergence.",
        "Generates the Rossler attractor. The Rossler attractor is a system of three non-linear ordinary differential equations that exhibits chaotic dynamics. It is simpler than the Lorenz system. The system is defined by: - dx/dt = -y - z - dy/dt = x + ay - dz/dt = b + z(x - c) Common parameter values: a = 0.2, b = 0.2, c = 5.7 # Arguments * `start_point` - The initial `(x, y, z)` coordinates. * `dt` - The time step for the integration. * `num_steps` - The number of integration steps. * `a`, `b`, `c` - The Rossler system parameters. # Returns A `Vec` of `(f64, f64, f64)` tuples representing the trajectory.",
        "Native Tinkerbell map in normalized dimensionless coordinates. Returns an N x 2 tensor under a finite path budget. Generates the Tinkerbell map trajectory. The Tinkerbell map is a two-dimensional iterated map: - x_{n+1} = `x_n^2` - `y_n^2` + `ax_n` + `by_n` - y_{n+1} = `2x_n`*`y_n` + `cx_n` + `dy_n` Classic parameter values: a = 0.9, b = -0.6013, c = 2.0, d = 0.5 # Arguments * `start_point` - The initial `(x, y)` coordinates. * `num_steps` - The number of iterations. * `a`, `b`, `c`, `d` - The map parameters. # Returns A `Vec` of `(f64, f64)` tuples representing the trajectory.",
        "Computes the escape time for a single point in a Julia set.",
        "Logistic bifurcation samples in normalized coordinates; transient and retained iterations share a finite work budget. Returns columns r and x. Generates data for a bifurcation diagram of the logistic map. For each value of r, the map is iterated and the final values after discarding transients are recorded. # Arguments * `r_range` - The range of r values `(r_min, r_max)`. * `num_r_values` - Number of r values to sample. * `transient` - Number of initial iterations to discard. * `num_points` - Number of points to record after transient. * `x0` - Initial x value. # Returns A `Vec` of `(r, x)` pairs for plotting the bifurcation diagram.",
        "Iterates the logistic map. The logistic map is defined by: x_{n+1} = r * `x_n` * (1 - `x_n`) It is a classic example of how complex behavior can arise from simple nonlinear dynamical equations. For r between 0 and 4, the dynamics range from stable fixed points to period doubling to chaos. # Arguments * `x0` - Initial value (should be between 0 and 1). * `r` - The growth rate parameter. * `num_steps` - The number of iterations. # Returns A `Vec` of x values representing the orbit.",
        "Computes the Lyapunov exponent for the logistic map. The Lyapunov exponent \u03bb measures the rate of separation of infinitesimally close trajectories. For the logistic map: \u03bb = lim (1/n) * sum(ln|r(1-2x_i)|) Positive \u03bb indicates chaos. # Arguments * `r` - The growth rate parameter. * `x0` - Initial value. * `transient` - Number of iterations to discard. * `num_iterations` - Number of iterations for averaging. # Returns The estimated Lyapunov exponent.",
        "Computes the largest Lyapunov exponent for a 3D flow (e.g., Lorenz system). Uses the method of tracking the evolution of a small perturbation. # Arguments * `start_point` - Initial point `(x, y, z)`. * `dt` - Time step. * `num_steps` - Number of integration steps. * `sigma`, `rho`, `beta` - Lorenz system parameters. # Returns The estimated largest Lyapunov exponent.",
        "Computes the escape time for a single point in the Mandelbrot set. # Arguments * `c_real` - Real part of the complex number c * `c_imag` - Imaginary part of the complex number c * `max_iter` - Maximum iterations # Returns The number of iterations before escape, or `max_iter` if the point is in the set.",
        "Normalized phase-space point histogram. N x 2 input points and bounded bin counts produce CSR integer rows (offsets/indices); entries are counts, preserving empty rows without padding. Computes the density distribution of orbit points in a 2D histogram. # Arguments * `points` - The orbit trajectory. * `x_bins` - Number of bins in x direction. * `y_bins` - Number of bins in y direction. * `x_range` - Range for x axis. * `y_range` - Range for y axis. # Returns A 2D vector of counts for each bin.",
        "Computes the entropy of an orbit from its density distribution. Uses Shannon entropy: H = -`sum(p_i` * `log(p_i)`) # Arguments * `density` - A 2D density histogram from `orbit_density`. # Returns The Shannon entropy of the distribution.",
        "Creates the Sierpinski triangle IFS.",
        "Fresnel cosine integral `C(x) = \u222b_0^x cos(\u03c0 t\u00b2 / 2) dt`.",
        "Error function of a complex argument. Maclaurin series for `|z| < 1.5`, otherwise `1 - exp(-z\u00b2) w(i z)` after folding to `Re z >= 0` with `erf(-z) = -erf(z)`. The exponential is scaled with the Faddeeva factor near the floating-point range limits.",
        "The Faddeeva function `w(z) = exp(-z\u00b2) erfc(-i z)`.",
        "Auxiliary function `f(x)` of DLMF 7.2.10 (`\u2248 1/(\u03c0 x)` for large `x`).",
        "Auxiliary functions `(f(x), g(x))` of DLMF 7.2.10: `f = (1/2 - S) cos \u03b8 - (1/2 - C) sin \u03b8`, `g = (1/2 - C) cos \u03b8 + (1/2 - S) sin \u03b8`, `\u03b8 = \u03c0 x\u00b2 / 2`.",
        "Auxiliary function `g(x)` of DLMF 7.2.10 (`\u2248 1/(\u03c0\u00b2 x\u00b3)` for large `x`).",
        "Fresnel integrals `(S(x), C(x))` together.",
        "`(S(z), C(z))` for a complex argument `z = re + i\u00b7im`, returned as `((S.re, S.im), (C.re, C.im))`.",
        "Fresnel sine integral `S(x) = \u222b_0^x sin(\u03c0 t\u00b2 / 2) dt`.",
        "Returns the dimensionless Fresnel sine and cosine integrals from a to b as item0 and item1. Narrow intervals and far-field tails use direct differences. Infinite bounds give improper integrals. Reversing the bounds negates both results. Normalised Fresnel sine and cosine integrals `(S, C)` over `[a, b]`. Both integrands use the phase `\u03c0 t\u00b2 / 2`. Narrow intervals are integrated directly, and far from the origin the common endpoint limit cancels before rounding, so differences can survive even when the values from [`fresnel_sc`] round to the same number. Reversing the limits negates both results. Infinite limits are interpreted as improper integrals. Equal limits give zero, including equal infinities. A NaN limit gives two NaNs. ``` use rssn::kernels::fresnel::fresnel_sc_interval; let (s, c) = fresnel_sc_interval(1.0, 1.0_f64.next_up()); assert_eq!(s, f64::EPSILON); assert!(c < 0.0); ```",
        "`(sin(\u03c0 x\u00b2 / 2), cos(\u03c0 x\u00b2 / 2))` with the phase reduced exactly.",
        "Fujiwara upper bound from ascending complex coefficients with a common ordinate dimension. The polynomial coordinate and the result are dimensionless. Fujiwara upper bound on the modulus of all polynomial roots: $R = 2 \\\\max_{1 \\\\le k \\\\le n} \\\\left|\\\\frac{c_{n-k}}{c_n}\\\\right|^{1/k}$ with factor $1/2$ on $k=n$.",
        "Performs the Gram-Schmidt process to orthogonalize a set of functions. All functions must have the same sampling points.",
        "Performs the Gram-Schmidt process to orthonormalize a set of functions. # Errors Returns an error if any pair of input functions has mismatched sample points.",
        "Calculates the L-infinity norm of a function represented by discrete points. The L-infinity norm (or Chebyshev norm) is defined as `max(|f(x)|)`. For discrete points, it is simply the maximum absolute value among the sampled points. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples representing the function's samples. # Returns The numerical value of the L-infinity norm.",
        "Calculates the inner product of two functions, `<f, g> = \u222bf(x)g(x)dx`. Both functions must be sampled at the same x-coordinates. For discrete points, it is approximated as `\u03a3 f(x_i)g(x_i)\u0394x_i`. # Arguments * `f_points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples representing the first function's samples. * `g_points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples representing the second function's samples. # Returns A `Result` containing the numerical value of the inner product. # Errors Returns an error if the input functions have different numbers of sample points.",
        "Calculates the L1 norm of a function represented by discrete points. The L1 norm (or Manhattan norm) is defined as `\u222b|f(x)|dx`. For discrete points, it is approximated as `\u03a3|y_i|\u0394x_i`. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples representing the function's samples. # Returns The numerical value of the L1 norm.",
        "Prepares trapezoidal L2 weights for finite nondecreasing coordinates. The result can be reused for independent sampled fields on the same axis. Repeated coordinates contribute zero panel width. Prepares the weights for finite, nondecreasing coordinates. # Errors Returns an error for a nonfinite coordinate or a decrease.",
        "Returns the sampled L2 norm, with dimension equal to the ordinates' dimension times the square root of the axis dimension. Returns a domain error if the norm overflows Float. Computes the sampled L2 norm using the prepared weights. # Errors Returns an error for the wrong number of ordinates or a nonfinite one.",
        "Returns {norm, normalized} from one reduction. The measured norm is None if it overflows Float. Normalized ordinates carry the inverse square root of the axis dimension and are None for zero measure. Normalization uses the unrounded scale, including when the norm underflows or overflows. Returns the L2 norm and normalized ordinates from one reduction. Zero measure leaves the ordinates as supplied. If the rounded norm overflows or underflows, normalization still uses its internal scale. # Errors Returns an error for the wrong number of ordinates or a nonfinite one.",
        "Calculates the L2 norm of a function represented by discrete points. The L2 norm is `sqrt(\u222b|f(x)|\u00b2dx)`. For samples, the integral is the trapezoidal rule applied to the squared ordinates. On a finite nondecreasing grid, repeated coordinates give zero-width panels. # Arguments * `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples representing the function's samples. # Returns The numerical value of the L2 norm.",
        "Normalizes a function relative to its L2 norm. Zero measure leaves the samples as supplied. Normalization uses the norm's internal scale even if the rounded norm is zero or infinite.",
        "Computes the projection of function `f` onto function `g`. Both functions must be sampled at the same x-coordinates.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Number of cells per level.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. `L1` norm of `component(prim) - exact(x)` over the composite grid (every cell counted at its finest level).",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Default AMR parameters around a scheme: two levels, regrid every 4 steps, threshold 0.05, refluxing on.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Primitive variables on the uniform grid of level `level` (coarser data are injected piecewise constantly).",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Integrals of the conserved variables over the composite grid (every cell counted at its finest level).",
        "Creates a ideal-gas Euler configuration with the dimensionless adiabatic exponent. The options record contains cfl, reconstruction and riemann. Riemann solver names are Hllc, Hlle, Roe and Rusanov. Hlle uses two Einfeldt wave speeds. Euler solver with all faces transmissive, HLLC + WENO5, CFL 0.4.",
        "Creates a shallow-water configuration with gravity in the frame\u2019s acceleration reference. The options record contains cfl, reconstruction and riemann. Riemann solver names are Hllc, Hlle, Roe and Rusanov. Hlle uses two Einfeldt wave speeds. Shallow-water solver with all faces transmissive, HLLC + MUSCL(minmod).",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Cell volume.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Cell centre.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Cell width along `axis`.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Cell multi-index.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Flat index of cell `(i, j, k)`.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Whether there are no cells.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Number of cells.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Creates a grid. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid dimension, extent, spacing or storage size.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Discrete L1 norm of `component(prim) - exact(x)`.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Primitive variables of cell `p`.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Runs the solver from primitive initial data to `t_end`. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid grid, an invalid time or CFL number, inconsistent periodic faces or inadmissible initial data.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Runs the AMR solver from primitive initial data (evaluated at the cell centres of every level) to `t_end`. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions, a CFL number outside `(0, 1)`, an invalid grid or refinement extent, a non-finite or negative final time, non-admissible base initial data, or a step that cannot advance.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Runs the solver with the source terms of [`Sources`]. Gravity (Euler) uses the well-balanced interface pressure described in the module documentation; a bed (shallow water) uses the hydrostatic reconstruction, which is well-balanced for the lake at rest. # Errors As [`solve`], and for gravity with shallow water, a bed or Manning friction with Euler, or a bed or Manning friction with shallow water in 3D.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Integrals of the conserved variables over the domain.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Sets both faces of an axis.",
        "Actual native finite-volume/AMR method in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Primitive slots are density or depth, three velocities, pressure or unused zero; conserved and integrated outputs use their distinct physical units. Native reconstruction, Riemann solver, sources, boundary identity and scoped step/work limits are retained. Sets one face (`2 * axis + is_max`).",
        "Numerical gamma function. Computes the gamma function, \u0393(x). \u0393(n) = (n-1)! for positive integers.",
        "Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget. Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh: a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules: finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule: tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations. Callback failures propagate unchanged. `n`-point Gauss-Legendre quadrature of `f` on `[a, b]`.",
        "Geometric mean of a sample. Computes the geometric mean of a slice of positive data. GM = (x1 * x2 * ... * xn)^(1/n)",
        "The 2D convex hull of `points` in counter-clockwise order, via Andrew's monotone chain algorithm.",
        "The 3D convex hull of `points`, returned as outward-oriented triangular faces (indices into `points`).",
        "2D Delaunay triangulation of `points`, returning triangles as `[usize; 3]` vertex indices in counter-clockwise order, via the Bowyer\u2013Watson algorithm.",
        "The Euclidean distance between two points of the same dimension. # Errors [`GeometryError::ShapeMismatch`] when the dimensions differ.",
        "Returns `true` if `point` lies inside or on the boundary of `polygon`.",
        "The area of a polygon given its vertices, by the shoelace formula. `points` is `[x0, y0, x1, y1, \u2026]`; the result is non-negative and does not depend on the winding direction. Fewer than three vertices give zero. Ill-conditioned and extreme-scale inputs use the exact represented coordinates.",
        "Intersection of a 2D ray `origin + t * dir` (`t >= 0`) with segment `[b1, b2]`. Returns `Some((t, point))` if they intersect.",
        "Intersection of a 3D ray `origin + t * dir` (`t >= 0`) with triangle `(v0, v1, v2)`, via the M\u00f6ller\u2013Trumbore algorithm.",
        "Intersection point of two 2D line segments `[a1, a2]` and `[b1, b2]`, if any.",
        "Intersection of a 3D line segment `[p0, p1]` with triangle `(v0, v1, v2)`.",
        "The non-negative volume of a tetrahedron, from four vertices in `R^3`. Ill-conditioned and extreme-scale inputs use an exact determinant of the represented coordinates. Fewer than twelve coordinates give zero. Only the first twelve coordinates are used.",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Rigid-wall configuration with unit density. # Errors For a bad dimension or sizes.",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Acoustic energy `\u00bd \u03a3 (p\u00b2/K + \u03c1|v|\u00b2) \u0394V`, the invariant of the conservative scheme (`K = \u03c1c\u00b2` in the cells, `\u03c1` the face density).",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle.",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Flat cell index.",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Cells per axis.",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Creates the solver. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions or a PML that is too thick.",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Initialises `v^{-1/2}` from `p^0` for a start from rest (velocity zero at `t = 0`); call once after [`Acoustics::set_pressure`]. Not for PML runs.",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Runs `steps` steps.",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Sets a spatially varying medium from `x \u21a6 (c, \u03c1)` sampled at the cell centres, and re-derives [`Acoustics::dt`] and the PML from the result. The density on a face is the harmonic mean of the two cells it separates and the bulk modulus of a cell is `\u03c1c\u00b2`, which is what keeps the scheme second order across an interface (an interface is exact when it lies on a face). Call this before stepping: the fields, the clock and the sources are left alone. # Errors A non-positive or non-finite `c` or `\u03c1` at a cell centre.",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Sets the pressure from a function of the cell-centre position (velocity zero).",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. Adds a soft source `s(t)` to the pressure equation of a cell.",
        "Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle. One leapfrog step (velocity, then pressure).",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Applies boundary conditions to a global stiffness matrix and force vector. Uses the penalty method for prescribed DOFs.",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. 1D global stiffness assembly (one DOF per node); alias of [`assemble_global_stiffness_matrix`] named like its 2D/3D siblings.",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Assembles a 2D global stiffness matrix from triangular elements.",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Assembles a 3D global stiffness matrix from tetrahedral elements (12 DOFs each, ordered `(u, v, w)` per node).",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Assembles the global stiffness matrix for a 1D structure composed of linear elements. This function takes a list of local stiffness matrices and their connectivity to construct the global stiffness matrix for the entire structure. # Arguments * `num_nodes` - The total number of nodes in the structure. * `elements` - A vector of tuples `(element_matrix, node1_idx, node2_idx)`, where `element_matrix` is the local stiffness matrix and `node1_idx`, `node2_idx` are the global indices of the nodes connected by this element. # Returns A `Matrix<f64>` representing the global stiffness matrix.",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Assembles a global stiffness matrix from local matrices and DOF maps. `dof_map[i]` is the global DOF of local DOF `i`): the dimension-generic form of [`assemble_global_stiffness_matrix`] (1D bars, 2 DOFs), [`assemble_2d_stiffness_matrix`] (triangles, 6 DOFs) and [`assemble_3d_stiffness_matrix`] (tetrahedra, 12 DOFs). # Panics Panics if a local matrix is smaller than its DOF map or a DOF is out of range.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the global stiffness matrix.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the local stiffness matrix in local coordinates (6x6). DOFs: [u1, v1, \u03b81, u2, v2, \u03b82]",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the consistent mass matrix.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a new beam element.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the transformation matrix from global to local coordinates.",
        "Solves the interior Laplace problem on a closed mesh using ACA-compressed constant panels and GMRES. Supply one Potential or Flux coefficient per triangle, normalized by the frame's field or field-per-length reference. A pure-flux interior problem requires zero net outward flux and returns a zero area-weighted mean boundary potential. The mesh cap is 512 panels. Restart and iteration lengths are positive and capped at 128 and 4096. The positive tolerance, at most one, controls both ACA and GMRES. Invalid controls, incompatible fluxes and nonconvergence produce errors.",
        "Returns the fraction of matrix entries stored by ACA relative to dense storage. One means that storing compressed blocks saved no entries. Requires a retained ACA solution. Stored fraction relative to dense storage.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Scenario for 2D BEM: Simulates potential flow around a cylinder. This function sets up a circular boundary and applies boundary conditions corresponding to a uniform flow in the x-direction. It then uses the BEM solver to calculate the potential and flux on the cylinder's surface. # Returns A `Result` containing a tuple `(u, q)` of potentials and fluxes on the cylinder surface, or an error string if the BEM system cannot be solved. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_laplace_bem_2d` function encounters an error.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Creates a new 2D boundary element.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Evaluates the potential at an internal point in the domain after solving the boundary. # Arguments * `point` - The `(x, y)` coordinate of the internal point. * `elements` - The boundary elements. * `u` - The solved boundary potentials. * `q` - The solved boundary fluxes.",
        "Evaluates a retained interior constant-panel Laplace solution. Supply an interior point as three coordinates normalized by the frame's Length reference. The result is a measured potential in the field dimension. Accuracy decreases near the boundary. Exterior and vertex-coefficient solutions require different evaluators and are rejected.",
        "Evaluates a retained exterior constant-panel Laplace solution. Supply a point outside the closed body as three coordinates normalized by the frame's Length reference. The result is a measured potential in the field dimension. Accuracy decreases near the boundary. Interior and vertex-coefficient solutions require different evaluators and are rejected.",
        "Solves the exterior Laplace problem with decay at infinity using ACA-compressed constant panels and GMRES. Supply one Potential or Flux coefficient per triangle, normalized by the frame's field or field-per-length reference. Flux follows the body's outward normal into the exterior. Dirichlet, Neumann and mixed data are accepted. The mesh cap is 512 panels. Restart and iteration lengths are positive and capped at 128 and 4096. The positive tolerance, at most one, controls both ACA and GMRES. Invalid controls and nonconvergence produce errors.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Area of triangle `t`, if it exists.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Centroid of triangle `t` (the collocation point), if it exists.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. The three corners of triangle `t`, or `None` if out of range.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. An axis-aligned cube of edge length `side` centred at `center`, each face split into `n x n` squares of two triangles (`12 n^2` triangles in total), with outward orientation.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. A geodesic sphere: the icosahedron with every triangle split into four `subdivisions` times (`20 * 4^subdivisions` triangles), vertices projected onto the sphere.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Checks that the mesh is a closed, consistently oriented surface with non-degenerate triangles and positive enclosed volume. # Errors Describes the first defect found.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Outward unit normal of triangle `t`, if it exists and is not degenerate.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Total surface area.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Checks that the mesh is a closed, consistently oriented surface with non-degenerate triangles and positive enclosed volume. # Errors Describes the first defect found.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Enclosed volume (divergence theorem; positive for outward orientation).",
        "Solves the interior or exterior Laplace problem with linear elements. Each vertex has one Potential or Flux coefficient in the supplied frame's field or field-per-length reference. Use the boundary-field accessor for the resulting vertex coefficients. Solves the interior Laplace problem with **continuous piecewise-linear** potential and flux on the triangulated surface, by collocation at the vertices (dense LU). `bcs` gives the data per **vertex**: the potential or the derivative along the outward normal. Single-layer integrals use adaptive subdivision away from the collocation point and a Duffy transformation for the elements that contain it; the double-layer kernel vanishes on those (planar) elements, and the free-term/diagonal is fixed by the rigid-body identity `\u03a3_j H_ij = 0`. Exterior problems: pass `exterior = true` (orientation reversed and the flux sign convention of [`solve_laplace_bem_3d_exterior`]). # Errors For an invalid mesh, wrong data length, or a singular system (pure Neumann interior problems are singular).",
        "Solves the interior or exterior Laplace problem with ACA-compressed linear elements and GMRES. Each vertex has one Potential or Flux coefficient in the supplied frame's field or field-per-length reference. The positive tolerance, at most one, controls both ACA and GMRES. Solves the Laplace problem with **linear (P1) elements** like [`solve_laplace_bem_3d_p1`], but with hierarchical-matrix compression. The vertex collocation matrices `G` and `H` are assembled lazily from the element integrals (adaptive subdivision away from the collocation point, Duffy transformation on elements containing it), far blocks of the vertex cluster tree are compressed by adaptive cross approximation, the rigid-body diagonal of `H` follows from one compressed matrix-vector product with the vector of ones, and the system is solved by Jacobi-preconditioned GMRES. # Errors For an invalid mesh, wrong data length, non-finite data, a pure Neumann interior problem (singular) or GMRES failure.",
        "Returns the number of boundary values required by a prepared three-dimensional Laplace BEM plan. Number of panels and prescribed values in each solve.",
        "Prepares an interior three-dimensional Laplace boundary-element solve and stores the constant-panel collocation operator and LU factors. Supply Potential or Flux for each triangle in mesh order. The plan retains the mesh's reference frame. Dense storage is quadratic in the panel count. Factors the system for the supplied trianglewise boundary kinds. # Errors Rejects invalid meshes, a mismatched number of kinds, singular systems and non-finite boundary operators. Reports cancellation.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Self-check of the 3D BEM: solves the Dirichlet problem `u = x` on a 320-triangle unit icosphere with [`solve_laplace_bem_3d_mesh`] and verifies the recovered flux against the exact `q = n_x`. This is the former argument-less entry point; the general solver is [`solve_laplace_bem_3d_mesh`]. # Errors Returns an error if the solve fails or the flux error exceeds 5 %.",
        "Returns normalized potential and flux arrays in panel order for constant elements or vertex order for linear elements. The record carries a measured field reference for potential and a field-per-length reference for flux.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Solves a 2D Laplace problem (e.g., potential flow, steady-state heat conduction) using the Boundary Element Method (BEM) with constant elements. This function discretizes the boundary of the domain into elements and applies boundary conditions to solve for unknown potentials or fluxes on the boundary. # Arguments * `points` - A `Vec` of `(x, y)` tuples defining the vertices of the boundary polygon. * `bcs` - A `Vec` of `BoundaryCondition` for each element, specifying known potential or flux. # Returns A `Result` containing a tuple `(u, q)`, where `u` is a `Vec<f64>` of potentials and `q` is a `Vec<f64>` of normal fluxes on each element. Returns an `Err` string if the system is ill-posed or has no unique solution. # Errors This function will return an error if the number of points and boundary conditions do not match, or if the BEM system has no unique solution.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Solves the 3D Laplace equation inside a closed triangulated surface by the collocation Boundary Element Method with constant elements. The potential `u` and flux `q = du/dn` (outward normal) are piecewise constant on the triangles and collocated at their centroids. Per element, `bcs` prescribes either the potential (Dirichlet) or the flux (Neumann); the other quantity is solved for. The discretised boundary integral equation is `sum_j Hhat_ij u_j = sum_j G_ij q_j`, with the single-layer kernel `G = 1 / (4 pi r)` integrated over each triangle by adaptive subdivision with a 7-point Gauss rule (the singular self term analytically), the double-layer coefficients `H_ij = -Omega_ij / (4 pi)` given exactly by the solid angle subtended by element `j` at the collocation point `i`, and the free-term diagonal fixed by the rigid-body-motion identity `sum_j Hhat_ij = 0`. With Neumann data on every element the potential is determined only up to an additive constant; it is then fixed by the condition that the area-weighted mean of `u` vanishes, and the fluxes must satisfy the compatibility condition `sum_j q_j A_j = 0` to within `1e-6` of the flux scale. For repeated values with the same mesh and boundary kinds, use [`PreparedLaplaceBem3d`]. # Errors Fails for an invalid mesh (see [`SurfaceMesh3D::validate`]), a number of boundary conditions different from the number of triangles, non-finite data, an incompatible pure-Neumann problem, or a singular system. Reports cancellation.",
        "Solves for new per-triangle boundary values using prepared Laplace operators. Potentials are expressed in the mesh's field reference and fluxes in field per length. A pure-flux solve requires zero net outward flux and uses a zero area-weighted mean boundary potential. The returned solution works with the ordinary field and interior evaluation operators. Solves for new values in mesh triangle order, using the fixed boundary kinds. # Errors Rejects a mismatched number of values, non-finite data or an incompatible pure-Neumann problem. Reports cancellation or a non-finite solution.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Creates a new 2D vector.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Calculates the norm of the vector.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Creates a new 3D vector.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Calculates the norm of the vector.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Whether this is a Dirichlet condition.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Whether this is periodic.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Chebyshev differentiation matrix on `x_i = -cos(\u03c0 i / n)` scaled to an interval of length `len`.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Coordinates of flat index `p`.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Integrates `u_t = \u03b1 \u0394u + s(t, x)` with the \u03b8-method (`\u03b8` in `[1/2, 1]`). # Errors For unsupported boundary types or a singular system.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Largest error against an exact function.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Creates a grid with the given degrees and bounds. # Errors For bad dimensions or degrees below 2.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Node coordinate along an axis.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Samples a function at all nodes.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Number of unknowns.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Solves `-\u0394u + \u03c3 u = f` with the given boundary conditions. # Errors For unsupported boundary types or a singular system.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Crank\u2013Nicolson (\u03b8 = 1/2) heat equation on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid. Any face conditions of [`crate::sim::boundary`] are allowed (Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin, periodic, time-dependent data); data are stored with `x` fastest. # Errors For inconsistent sizes or boundary descriptions.",
        "Native nondimensional Crank-Nicolson Schrodinger equation i*psi_t=-Delta(psi)/2+V*psi. The explicit frame restores kinetic coefficient L\u00b2/(2T), potential frequency 1/T and complex amplitude units; it does not silently choose SI hbar or mass. Native spacing, method, layout and work bounds are retained. Solves the 1D Schr\u00f6dinger equation i * `psi_t` = -0.5 * `psi_xx` + V * psi using the Crank-Nicolson method. # Arguments * `psi_initial` - The initial wave function (complex). * `v` - The potential energy distribution (real). * `dx` - The spatial step size. * `dt` - The time step. * `steps` - The number of time steps.",
        "Native nondimensional Crank-Nicolson Schrodinger equation i*psi_t=-Delta(psi)/2+V*psi. The explicit frame restores kinetic coefficient L\u00b2/(2T), potential frequency 1/T and complex amplitude units; it does not silently choose SI hbar or mass. Native spacing, method, layout and work bounds are retained. 2D Schrodinger equation `i psi_t = -1/2 Laplacian psi + V psi`. Zero Dirichlet, by the Cayley/Crank-Nicolson split of [`solve_schrodinger_cn_nd`]; `x` fastest. 2D counterpart of [`solve_schrodinger_1d_cn`]; conserves the norm. # Errors For inconsistent lengths or a non-positive step.",
        "Native nondimensional Crank-Nicolson Schrodinger equation i*psi_t=-Delta(psi)/2+V*psi. The explicit frame restores kinetic coefficient L\u00b2/(2T), potential frequency 1/T and complex amplitude units; it does not silently choose SI hbar or mass. Native spacing, method, layout and work bounds are retained. 3D Schrodinger equation by the Cayley/Crank-Nicolson split; see [`solve_schrodinger_2d_cn`]. # Errors As [`solve_schrodinger_2d_cn`].",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Solves the 2D heat equation `u_t` = D * (`u_xx` + `u_yy`) using the ADI method. Uses homogeneous Dirichlet boundaries from the first half-step. With zero steps, returns the initial values verbatim. Grid data is x-fastest. # Panics Panics for a positive-step nonempty grid whose extents overflow or exceed the supplied data length.",
        "Advances a 2D heat field with Crank\u2013Nicolson ADI. The x-fastest initial field provides all four fixed Dirichlet edges, which retain their values throughout the solve. Supply spacing in units of length_reference, diffusivity in length_reference^2/time_reference, the time step in time_reference, and temperature in value_reference. The returned field keeps its grid references. Evolves a two-dimensional heat field with fixed Dirichlet boundaries. The x-fastest initial field supplies the four boundary edges. They remain fixed during both ADI half-steps. The grid has `nx * ny` nodes, including the boundaries. Spacings must be positive, and diffusivity and the time step must be nonnegative. A grid without interior nodes is stationary. # Errors Returns an error for shape mismatches, non-finite data or results, invalid physical parameters, unrepresentable coefficients, or cancellation.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. 3D heat equation `u_t = D (u_xx + u_yy + u_zz)` by Crank-Nicolson. Uses approximate factorisation (ADI): three tridiagonal sweeps per step, unconditionally stable, second order, zero Dirichlet boundaries. `x` is the fastest index. 3D counterpart of [`solve_heat_equation_2d_cn_adi`]. # Errors For a wrong array length or non-positive step sizes.",
        "Native normalized strain-coordinate contraction; B and displacement columns use one explicitly normalized coordinate basis, returning dimensionless strain coordinates. Shapes and work are bounded. Calculates element strains from nodal displacements.",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Creates a simple rectangular mesh of triangular elements. Returns (nodes, elements) where elements are node indices.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Constant datum.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Evaluates the datum.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Space-dependent datum `g(x)`.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Space-time datum `g(t, x)`.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Time-dependent datum `g(t)`.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Like [`solve_transport_nd`] but returns only the final state (no snapshot history, so memory stays `O(n)`). # Errors As [`solve_transport_nd`].",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Crank-Nicolson heat equation `u_t = D \u0394u` with zero Dirichlet boundaries on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid, advanced `steps` steps of size `dt`. The step is the factorised (Beam-Warming) approximate-factorisation form `\u03a0_a (I - \u03c1_a \u03b4\u00b2_a) u\u207f\u207a\u00b9 = \u03a0_a (I + \u03c1_a \u03b4\u00b2_a) u\u207f` with `\u03c1_a = D dt / (2 h_a\u00b2)`: unconditionally stable, second order in space and time, one tridiagonal solve per line and axis. For data that is a product of functions of the single axes it equals the product of the 1D Crank-Nicolson solutions. Boundary entries of `u0` are ignored (treated as zero). # Errors For inconsistent shapes, non-positive `dt`, negative diffusivity, or an unrepresentable diffusion coefficient at the given spacing and time step. A cancelled computation also returns an error.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Solves `\u2207\u00b2u = f` on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid by a stationary iteration. `u0` is the initial guess; its boundary nodes are the (fixed) Dirichlet data. Iteration stops when the largest update is below `tolerance` or after `max_iter` sweeps. With a 1D grid this is the scalar tridiagonal iteration, with `dims = [nx, ny]` it reproduces [`crate::sim::physics_cfd::solve_poisson_2d_jacobi`] and friends. # Errors For inconsistent shapes, spacings or lengths.",
        "Native nondimensional Crank-Nicolson Schrodinger equation i*psi_t=-Delta(psi)/2+V*psi. The explicit frame restores kinetic coefficient L\u00b2/(2T), potential frequency 1/T and complex amplitude units; it does not silently choose SI hbar or mass. Native spacing, method, layout and work bounds are retained. Schrodinger equation `i \u03c8_t = -\u00bd \u0394\u03c8 + V \u03c8` (\u0127 = m = 1) with zero Dirichlet boundaries on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid. One step is the symmetric splitting `e^{-iV dt/2} \u00b7 \u03a0_a C_a \u00b7 e^{-iV dt/2}` where `C_a = (I + i dt/2 K_a)\u207b\u00b9 (I - i dt/2 K_a)` is the Crank-Nicolson (Cayley) step of the one-dimensional kinetic operator `K_a = -\u00bd \u03b4\u00b2_a`. The kinetic factors commute, every factor is unitary, so the scheme is second order in space and time and conserves the norm to round-off. For `V = 0` and separable data it equals the product of 1D Crank-Nicolson solutions. # Errors For inconsistent shapes, non-positive `dt`, or cancellation.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Advances a [`Transport`] equation `steps` explicit Euler steps on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid and returns all `steps + 1` snapshots (the first is `u0`). The scheme is only conditionally stable: keep `\u03a3|c_a| dt/h_a <= 1` and `2 \u03b1 dt \u03a3 1/h_a\u00b2 <= 1`. # Errors For inconsistent shapes, a velocity list of the wrong length, invalid time controls, too many snapshots, or cancellation.",
        "Advance the retained transport equation and record step 0, positive multiples of save_every, and the final step. Data, spacings, and time steps are normalized in the equation's Length/Time/field frame. Returns x-fastest fields as rows with their physical reference. Work and retained history are bounded independently. Advances a [`Transport`] equation and retains snapshots at a fixed step cadence. The result holds step 0, every positive multiple of `save_every` through `steps`, and the final step if it is off cadence. The initial and final state share one snapshot when `steps` is zero. Boundary and stability conventions are those of [`solve_transport_nd`]. # Errors For an inconsistent grid or equation, zero cadence, or an unrepresentable snapshot count, or cancellation.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Explicit leapfrog for `u_tt = c\u00b2 \u0394u` with zero Dirichlet boundaries on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid. Returns the `steps + 1` snapshots (`u0`, `u1`, ...), the first step using the initial velocity `ut0` (Taylor start). # Errors For inconsistent shapes, an unrepresentable snapshot count, a nonfinite speed or invalid time step, cancellation, or a violated CFL condition `|c| dt sqrt(\u03a3 1/h_a\u00b2) <= 1` (with a `10\u207b\u00b9\u00b2` rounding allowance).",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Homogeneous or constant Dirichlet condition.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Fully clamped boundary.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Elasticity matrix in Voigt notation.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Displacement vector at dof `d`.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Displacement at a point (point location by search).",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Constant normal-pressure traction `\u03c3 n = -p n` is built by the caller from `n`; this helper fixes only one displacement component to zero (a roller).",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Solves the static problem. # Errors If the mesh dimension does not match the model, a displacement-free body (singular system) or an unsupported boundary type is met.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Solves the static problem with a spatially varying material. `E(x)` and `\u03bd(x)` are evaluated at every quadrature point; the per-cell stress recovery ([`ElasticSolution::stress_at_cell`]) uses the constitutive matrix at the cell centroid. A constant material reproduces [`solve`] to round-off. # Errors As [`solve`].",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Stress (Voigt) at the centroid of a cell, using that cell's constitutive matrix (heterogeneous material) or the homogeneous one.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Constant traction.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Von Mises equivalent of a Voigt stress (2D: plane-stress form with `\u03c3_zz = 0`).",
        "Native ETDRK4 with a genuinely coupled compiled nonlinear map: two scalar programs per complex mode (real then imaginary), each taking coherent [time,re0,im0,...]. At most31 modes fit the compiled ABI's63 inputs. Linear symbols are normalized inverse-time coefficients; nonlinear outputs must have field/time units. Native x-fastest spectral extent ordering and exact ETDRK4 stepping are preserved. Fourth-order ETDRK4 time stepping of `u_t = L u + N(u, t)` in the spectral domain. Integrates `u_t = L u + N(u, t)` in the spectral domain with the fourth-order exponential time differencing Runge\u2013Kutta scheme of Cox and Matthews (contour-integral coefficients of Kassam and Trefethen). `lin[p]` is the (diagonal) linear symbol; `nonlinear(t, uh)` returns the spectral transform of `N`.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Axis index 0, 1 or 2.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Storage index `2 * axis + is_max`.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Whether this is the upper face of its axis.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Face of `axis` (lower or upper).",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Whether `axis` is periodic (both faces must agree). # Errors If `axis` is out of range or only one face of the axis is periodic.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Homogeneous Dirichlet on every face.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Condition on a face.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. The same description with all boundary data set to zero (used for error / residual equations in multigrid).",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. The same condition on every face (periodic is allowed).",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Sets the condition on one face.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Sets the condition on both faces of an axis.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Periodic boundary conditions on `[0, L)`.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Uniform boundary condition on both ends.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. True if `nx == 0`.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Number of grid points.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Doubly periodic boundary conditions.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Same boundary condition on all four faces.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. True when the grid has no points.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. The number of points.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Triply periodic boundary conditions.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Same boundary condition on all six faces.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Returns the value at grid node `(x, y, z)`.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Flat index `(z * ny + y) * nx + x`.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. True if empty.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Total number of grid points `nx * ny * nz`.",
        "Actual generic native adaptive controller over caller-provided compiled step/stability/event functions. Each scalar step output takes coherent [time,step,state...] and has field units; stability takes [time,state...] and returns Time, optional event returns dimensionless. Controls/state arrays are normalized in the explicit frame; order is the supplied stepper's actual order. Native step doubling, rejection/work budgets and terminal-event localization are preserved. Adapt any restartable PDE step kernel with a step-doubling error estimate. `order` is the selected method's temporal order. `absolute_tolerances` has one positive entry per state coefficient. `stability` returns the maximum stable step for the current state, or positive infinity for an implicit scheme. Temporal control cannot certify spatial discretization error. An accepted endpoint on the event uses its existing validated state. # Errors Invalid controls or state shape, exhausted budgets, underflow, a failed step kernel, or a non-finite event. Tolerance violations are never force-accepted.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.",
        "Native per-face constant/profile FDM boundary construction from exactly2/4/6 scalar condition capabilities. Every condition must use the same physical reference frame; solver calls validate profile face lengths and native paired periodicity.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets.",
        "Original native solve_advection: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves advection with the first-order upwind scheme. # Errors [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] when the field does not fit, and [`FdmError::Domain`] when the CFL condition fails.",
        "Original native solve_advection_1d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves 1D linear advection `u_t + v u_x = 0` on `grid` with boundary conditions `bc`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if `|v| dt / h > 1`, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial.len() != grid.nx`.",
        "Original native solve_advection_3d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves 3D linear advection `u_t + v\u00b7\u2207u = 0` on `grid` with velocity `(vx, vy, vz)` and boundary conditions `bc`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if `(|vx| + |vy| + |vz|) dt / h > 1`, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial.len() != grid.len()`.",
        "Original native solve_advection_with_bc: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves 2D linear advection `u_t + v\u00b7\u2207u = 0` with general boundary conditions `bc` (including `Periodic`, `Dirichlet`, and outflow `Neumann`). # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if `(|vx| + |vy|) dt / h > 1`, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `spec.initial.len() != spec.grid.len()`.",
        "Original native solve_heat: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the heat equation with an explicit scheme, returning every snapshot including the initial one. # Errors [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] when the initial field does not fit, and [`FdmError::Domain`] when `\u03b1 dt / h\u00b2` exceeds the stability limit.",
        "Original native solve_heat_1d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 1D heat equation `u_t = \\\\alpha u_{xx}` on `grid` with boundary conditions `bc` and time-stepping `stepping` (`Explicit`, `BackwardEuler`, `CrankNicolson`, or `Adi`). Implicit schemes (`BackwardEuler`, `CrankNicolson`, `Adi`) are unconditionally stable for any `\\\\Delta t > 0`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid parameters or unstable explicit step, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial.len() != grid.nx`.",
        "Original native solve_heat_3d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solve the three-dimensional heat equation with prescribed face data. Solves the 3D heat equation `u_t = \\\\alpha \u2207\u00b2u` on `grid` with boundary conditions `bc` and time-stepping `stepping` (`Explicit`, `BackwardEuler`, `CrankNicolson`, or `Adi` Douglas\u2013Gunn 3D Alternating Direction Implicit). # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid parameters or unstable explicit step (`r > 1/6`), [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial.len() != grid.len()`.",
        "Original native solve_heat_with_bc: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solve the two-dimensional heat equation with prescribed face data. Solves the 2D heat equation `u_t = \\\\alpha \u2207\u00b2u` with general boundary conditions `bc` and time-stepping `stepping` (`Explicit`, `BackwardEuler`, `CrankNicolson`, or `Adi` Peaceman\u2013Rachford Alternating Direction Implicit). `BackwardEuler`, `CrankNicolson`, and `Adi` are unconditionally stable for arbitrarily large diffusion Courant numbers `\\\\alpha \\\\Delta t / h^2`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid, BCs, or unstable explicit CFL, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `spec.initial.len() != spec.grid.len()`.",
        "Original native solve_poisson: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves `\u2207\u00b2u = f` by successive over-relaxation. # Errors [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] when the source does not fit the grid, and [`FdmError::NotConverged`] when the sweep limit is reached.",
        "Original native solve_poisson_1d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 1D Poisson equation `u_{xx} = source` on `grid` with boundary conditions `bc` using direct `O(N)` tridiagonal / cyclic-tridiagonal elimination. Note: Pure Neumann or Periodic problems with zero reaction have a constant nullspace; in that case the first node is pinned to zero mean. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid or boundary conditions, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `source.len() != grid.nx`.",
        "Original native solve_poisson_3d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 3D Poisson equation `\u2207\u00b2u = source` on `grid` with boundary conditions `bc` using Successive Over-Relaxation (SOR). # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid or BCs, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `source.len() != grid.len()`, and [`FdmError::NotConverged`] if `iterations` is exceeded.",
        "Original native solve_poisson_with_bc: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 2D Poisson equation `\u2207\u00b2u = spec.source` with general boundary conditions `bc` (Dirichlet, non-zero Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin, or Periodic). # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid or BCs, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `spec.source.len() != spec.grid.len()`, and [`FdmError::NotConverged`] if SOR does not converge within `spec.iterations`.",
        "Original native solve_wave: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the wave equation by leapfrog with zero Dirichlet boundaries. Returns the initial field with zeroed boundaries and each of the `steps` subsequent fields. Initial velocities on the boundary are ignored. # Errors [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] when a field does not fit, and [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid or evolution parameters, including a CFL violation or an unrepresentable snapshot count.",
        "Original native solve_wave_1d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 1D wave equation `u_{tt} = c^2 u_{xx}` using the leapfrog scheme with boundary conditions `bc`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if CFL `|c| dt / h > 1`, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial` or `velocity` does not match `grid.nx`.",
        "Original native solve_wave_3d: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 3D wave equation `u_{tt} = c^2 \u2207\u00b2u` on `grid` using the leapfrog scheme. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if CFL `|c| dt / h > 1/\\\\sqrt{3}`, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial` or `velocity` does not match `grid.len()`.",
        "Original native solve_wave_3d_with_bc: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Three-dimensional leapfrog wave solver with Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin, or periodic faces. # Errors Invalid finite inputs, incompatible field dimensions, or unstable Courant number.",
        "Original native solve_wave_with_bc: only its actual controls are exposed. The plain 2D/3D zero-boundary entry points preserve zero boundary semantics; *_with_bc variants use explicit boundary objects. All numeric data use the retained Length/Time/field references, native x-fastest layout and selected method; work/history bounds are enforced. Solves the 2D wave equation `u_{tt} = c^2 \u2207\u00b2u` with general boundary conditions `bc`. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for an invalid time step, CFL `|c| dt / h > 1/\\\\sqrt{2}`, or too many snapshots. [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial` or `velocity` does not match `spec.grid.len()`.",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Homogeneous Dirichlet boundary condition (`u = 0`).",
        "Original native low-level FDM method with explicit Length/Time/field references. Coordinates, steps and coefficients are normalized; output fields/histories retain their physical field reference. Native x-fastest layout, selected integrator and stability/domain checks are preserved with allocation/history budgets. Homogeneous Neumann (insulating / reflective) boundary condition (`\\\\partial u / \\\\partial n = 0`).",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention.",
        "Returns the nodal shape, including the boundary, in x, y and optional z order. Nodal shape in x, y and optional z order, including the boundary.",
        "Prepares the tensor-product Q1 finite-element Poisson operator on a two- or three-dimensional unit grid. Element counts may differ by axis. The physical domain uses the frame's length reference and all boundary values are zero. Prepares a grid with two or three positive element counts. Coordinates and output storage use x-fast order on the unit domain. # Errors Rejects invalid extents and grids too large for addressable storage.",
        "Solves the prepared Poisson equation for a new compiled source. Each quadrature point needs one evaluation from measured Length coordinates to the field divided by Length squared. Result data are normalized by value_reference and use x-fast order. Solves for a new source. The callback runs once per element quadrature point. # Errors Reports non-finite source values, cancellation and unrepresentable results.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Residual error indicators `\u03b7_T`, one per cell, of a P1 or P2 solution. `\u03b7_T\u00b2 = h_T\u00b2 \u222b_T (f(t,\u00b7) - c u_h + \u2207\u00b7(k \u2207u_h))\u00b2 dx + \u03a3_{e \u2282 \u2202T} \u00bd h_e \u222b_e [k \u2202_n u_h]\u00b2 ds`, with `h_T` and `h_e` the diameters of the cell and of its faces. The face sum runs over interior faces only, so the estimator measures the discretisation error of the differential equation, not the approximation of non-homogeneous boundary data. For P1 the element term is `f - c u_h` (`\u2207\u00b7(k \u2207u_h) = 0`); for P2 the constant `k \u0394u_h` is included. In 1D the face term is empty (the jump lives on a set of measure zero).",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. [`error_indicators`] for spatially varying `k(x)`, `c(x)`: `\u03b7_T\u00b2 = h_T\u00b2 \u222b_T (f - c(x) u_h + \u2207\u00b7(k \u2207u_h))\u00b2 + \u03a3_e \u00bd h_e \u222b_e [k(x) \u2202_n u_h]\u00b2`, with `\u2207\u00b7(k \u2207u_h) = k \u0394u_h + \u2207k \u00b7 \u2207u_h` (the gradient of `k` by central differences of the closure at the quadrature points, nominal step `10\u207b\u2076 h_T`) and `k` evaluated at the quadrature points of the face in the jump. At floating- point limits the difference uses distinct finite representable coordinates and their actual spacing.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Evaluates a finite element function at `x` (brute-force point location).",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Advance nodal finite-element heat data by one theta-method step. Uses the existing consistent mass/stiffness assembly and boundary load machinery at both physical times; no reinterpolation of the state occurs. # Errors Invalid state/control data, incompatible boundary conditions, or failed linear solve.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Advance FEM heat data with additional nodal source values at both times. The source is interpolated in the existing finite-element basis and mapped to a load by the consistent mass matrix, preserving the theta method. # Errors Invalid nodal source/state data, controls, boundary data, or linear solve.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Interpolates a function at the dofs.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. `L2` error against an exact function.",
        "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Example scenario for the 1D FEM Poisson solver. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_poisson_1d` function encounters an error.",
        "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Example scenario for the 2D FEM Poisson solver. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_poisson_2d` function encounters an error.",
        "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Example scenario for the 3D FEM Poisson solver. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_poisson_3d` function encounters an error.",
        "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Poisson/reaction\u2013diffusion `-\u0394u + c u = f` on interval, triangle or tetrahedron meshes with P1/P2 elements and marker boundary conditions. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions or a singular system.",
        "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Solves the 1D Poisson equation: -d^2u/dx^2 = f(x) with Dirichlet boundary conditions u(0) = 0, u(L) = 0. # Arguments * `n_elements` - Number of linear elements. * `domain_length` - The length L of the domain. * `force_fn` - The forcing function f(x). # Returns A vector representing the solution u at each node. # Errors This function will return an error if `n_elements` is 0, or if the underlying linear system solver fails to converge or encounters numerical instability.",
        "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Solves the 2D Poisson equation on a unit square with zero Dirichlet boundaries. Uses the same tensor-product Q1 stiffness assembled from bilinear or trilinear elements, diagonalized by the discrete sine basis. # Errors Reports invalid extents, non-finite source values, cancellation or an unrepresentable result.",
        "Native Poisson finite elements with explicit PDE reference frame. Numeric domain lengths and field arrays are normalized by Length and solution reference U; source callbacks accept measured coordinates (and measured Time for simplex) and return U/Length^2. Allocation/DOF and callback evaluation budgets are checked. Solves the 3D Poisson equation on a unit cube with zero Dirichlet boundaries. Uses the same tensor-product Q1 stiffness assembled from bilinear or trilinear elements, diagonalized by the discrete sine basis. # Errors Reports invalid extents, non-finite source values, cancellation or an unrepresentable result.",
        "Evaluates normalized cell residual indicators using prepared geometry and a constant-coefficient FEM problem in the same reference frame. Time and solution values are normalized; the indicator reference is U L^((d+2)/2) / T for mesh dimension d. Values follow the source space DOF order. Computes the cell indicators for constant coefficients. # Errors Returns an error when the solution length differs from the space size.",
        "Evaluates normalized cell residual indicators using prepared geometry and a variable-coefficient FEM problem in the same reference frame. Diffusivity must be positive at every sampled coordinate. The indicator reference is U L^((d+2)/2) / T for mesh dimension d. Computes the cell indicators for spatially varying coefficients. # Errors Returns an error when the solution length differs from the space size.",
        "Prepares residual error estimation on a fixed P1 or P2 simplex space. The immutable estimator retains its own cell geometry, interior face adjacency and reference quadrature. The source space can be released after preparation. Prepares cell geometry and interior face adjacency. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid space, degenerate cells, or geometry whose edge differences, diameters or barycentric gradients cannot be represented in f64.",
        "Actual native spatial D\u00f6rfler refinement, independently from temporal adaptivity. Positive Length/Time/field references determine coefficient/source units and residual-indicator reference. A conservative worst-case refinement/DOF bound is checked before native allocation; actual returned space, field and estimator history are retained. Adaptive solution of the static problem: solve, estimate, mark and refine. Starting from `mesh`, the loop solves `-\u2207\u00b7(k \u2207u) + c u = f(t, x)` with [`solve_static`], estimates the error with [`error_indicators`], marks the smallest set of cells whose indicators carry the fraction `theta` of `\u03a3 \u03b7_T\u00b2` (D\u00f6rfler marking), refines them with [`SimplexMesh::refine_marked`] and repeats. It stops when the total estimate is at most `tolerance` or after `max_steps` refinements (so at most `max_steps + 1` solves). `degree` selects P1 or P2. # Errors If `degree` is not 1 or 2, `theta` is outside `(0, 1]`, `max_steps` is zero, or one of the solves fails.",
        "Actual native spatial D\u00f6rfler refinement, independently from temporal adaptivity. Positive Length/Time/field references determine coefficient/source units and residual-indicator reference. A conservative worst-case refinement/DOF bound is checked before native allocation; actual returned space, field and estimator history are retained. [`solve_adaptive`] with spatially varying `k(x)`, `c(x)` ([`error_indicators_var`]). # Errors As [`solve_adaptive`].",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Integrates `u_t - \u2207\u00b7(k \u2207u) + c u = f` with the \u03b8-method (`\u03b8` in `[1/2, 1]`). # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions, a singular system or an invalid `theta`.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. [`solve_heat`] with spatially varying `k(x)` and `c(x)`. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions, a singular system or an invalid `theta`.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Solves the static problem at time `t` (the source and boundary data are evaluated at `t`). # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions or a singular system.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. [`solve_static`] with spatially varying `k(x)` and `c(x)`. # Errors For unsupported boundary conditions or a singular system.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Builds the space. # Errors For a degree other than 1 or 2.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Number of degrees of freedom.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. In-place N-dimensional FFT of row-major (x fastest) data with extents `n` (each a power of two). The inverse includes the `1/N` normalisation. # Errors If an extent is not a power of two or the length does not match.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Condition of a face.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. The same condition on every face.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Sets one face.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Sets both faces of an axis.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Inflow with constant velocity.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Wall moving with a constant velocity.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Outflow at zero pressure.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Fixed no-slip wall.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Point coordinates (`x = i h`, origin at the corner).",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. 2/3-rule dealiasing mask (`true` = keep).",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Spectral derivative `\u2202/\u2202x_axis` (the Nyquist mode is dropped).",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Grid spacing along an axis.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Solves `-\u0394u + \u03c3 u = f` spectrally with periodic boundaries. For `\u03c3 = 0` the forcing must have zero mean up to transform roundoff, and the solution has zero mean. For repeated or batched sources use [`Self::helmholtz_plan`]. # Errors Invalid grid, shift or forcing, incompatible forcing in a singular mode, or a nonrepresentable symbol, transform or solution.",
        "Solves (-Laplace + sigma) u = f on a periodic Fourier grid for one or more forcing rows in input order. Give measured forcing values in field/length^2 and sigma in inverse length squared. Each row has exactly grid.size points with the x axis fastest. The result reports the batch size followed by the Fourier grid extents, flattened solutions divided by value_reference, and the grid length, time, and field references. Singular compatible Fourier modes use zero amplitude. Solves several real periodic forcings in input order. Larger batches use independent workers under the active execution policy. # Errors The first failing source in input order, as for [`Self::solve`].",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. `|k|\u00b2` of flat mode index `p`.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Wavenumber vector of flat mode index `p`.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Creates a grid. # Errors For bad sizes.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Total number of points.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Real part of the inverse transform.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Complex inverse transform.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Transform of real data to the spectral domain.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Transform of complex data to the spectral domain.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked. Wavenumber of mode index `j` along `axis` (negative frequencies wrapped).",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked.",
        "Native ideal-gas method in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric primitives are normalized density Mass/Length^3, velocity Length/Time and pressure Mass/(Length Time^2); numeric times and coordinates use the frame references. Distinct units remain separate in returned records. Native timesteps and callback evaluations have explicit budgets. The exact solution of the Riemann problem `l | r` at `\u03be = x/t`. Use [`ExactRiemann`] to sample the same initial states at several points.",
        "Native ideal-gas method in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric primitives are normalized density Mass/Length^3, velocity Length/Time and pressure Mass/(Length Time^2); numeric times and coordinates use the frame references. Distinct units remain separate in returned records. Native timesteps and callback evaluations have explicit budgets. The HLLC numerical flux between the states `l` and `r`.",
        "Prepares the exact ideal-gas Riemann solution for repeated sampling. Each normalized input vector is [density, velocity, pressure], and gas density and pressure must be positive. The mechanical frame fixes the physical references of all three quantities. Solves the wave pattern for initial states separated by a discontinuity.",
        "Samples a prepared exact Riemann solution at a measured self-similar velocity x/t. Returns the measured density, velocity and pressure. Within a vacuum, density and pressure are zero and velocity is x/t. Samples at the self-similar velocity `xi = x/t`. At a contact this returns the left star state, and at a shock it returns the undisturbed state. In a vacuum, including its fan tails, density and pressure are zero and velocity is continued as `xi`.",
        "Samples up to 65536 measured self-similar velocities x/t. The record contains measured density, velocity and pressure vectors in the input order. Independent samples can use the active CPU pool. Samples a series of self-similar velocities in their input order. Large queries share the active CPU pool without altering any state value.",
        "Native ideal-gas method in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric primitives are normalized density Mass/Length^3, velocity Length/Time and pressure Mass/(Length Time^2); numeric times and coordinates use the frame references. Distinct units remain separate in returned records. Native timesteps and callback evaluations have explicit budgets. Sod's shock tube on `[0, 1]` at `t`: numerical and exact densities at the cell centres.",
        "Native ideal-gas method in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric primitives are normalized density Mass/Length^3, velocity Length/Time and pressure Mass/(Length Time^2); numeric times and coordinates use the frame references. Distinct units remain separate in returned records. Native timesteps and callback evaluations have explicit budgets. Solves the 1D Euler equations (HLLC, MUSCL, SSP-RK3). The domain is `[x0, x1]` with `cells` cells, the initial data `initial(x)`, the final time `t_end`, the CFL number `cfl` and the reconstruction `limiter`. Returns the cell centres and the primitive states. This entry point delegates to the dimension-generic engine [`crate::sim::euler_nd`] (1D case); grids with fewer than six cells or a CFL number outside `(0, 1)` use the compact reference implementation.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Crank\u2013Nicolson heat integration with step-doubling error control. # Errors On solver failure or when `max_steps` is exceeded.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Integrates the heat equation with the \u03b8-method (`\u03b8 = 1` backward Euler, `\u03b8 = 1/2` Crank\u2013Nicolson) using `steps` equal steps. # Errors On solver failure or an invalid `theta` / absorbing boundary.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Crank\u2013Nicolson integration of the variable-coefficient heat equation with step-doubling error control. # Errors On solver failure, a bad `alpha` or when `max_steps` is exceeded.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Integrates the variable-coefficient heat equation with the \u03b8-method (`\u03b8 = 1` backward Euler, `\u03b8 = 1/2` Crank\u2013Nicolson) using `steps` equal steps. # Errors On solver failure, a bad `alpha` or an invalid `theta` / absorbing boundary.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Incident field at a point.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses. Scattered pressure at a point away from the surface.",
        "Native exterior Helmholtz solver. Wave number is normalized by inverse frame length, coupling by frame length, direction dimensionless. Unit incident amplitude corresponds to the frame potential reference; returned fields retain it. Mesh, dense work, ACA iterations and storage are bounded. Solves the exterior scattering problem. `coupling` is the Burton\u2013Miller parameter `\u03b1` (`None` for the recommended `i / k`, `Some(0)` for the conventional equation, which is singular at the irregular frequencies). # Errors For an invalid mesh, `k <= 0` or a singular system.",
        "Native exterior Helmholtz solver. Wave number is normalized by inverse frame length, coupling by frame length, direction dimensionless. Unit incident amplitude corresponds to the frame potential reference; returned fields retain it. Mesh, dense work, ACA iterations and storage are bounded. [`solve_helmholtz_exterior`] with a hierarchical matrix: complex ACA for the admissible blocks and complex GMRES; no dense `n x n` matrix is formed. Returns the solution and storage statistics. # Errors As [`solve_helmholtz_exterior`]; also when GMRES does not converge.",
        "Native BEM call. Numeric coordinates are normalized by the supplied PDE frame length, potentials by its field reference, and fluxes by field/length. Outputs retain explicit physical reference witnesses.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Integrates `M a + f_int(u) = f_ext(t)` from the given initial state with fixed step `dt`. The external load is the body force and the nominal tractions of `prob` (constant in time, applied suddenly at `t = 0`); displacement conditions are evaluated at the new time. `observer` is called after every step with the new state. # Errors If an element inverts, Newton fails or the effective matrix is singular.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Kinetic energy `\u00bd v\u1d40 M v` with the consistent mass matrix.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Lam\u00e9 parameters `(\u03bb, \u03bc)`.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. First Piola\u2013Kirchhoff stress `P(F)`. # Errors If the deformation or stress is invalid. See [`HyperelasticPoint::new`].",
        "Returns the strain energy per reference volume of a prepared material point. The measured result has pressure units. Stored strain energy per unit reference volume. # Errors Returns an error if the energy density is outside the finite range.",
        "Returns the measured 3 by 3 first Piola stress of a prepared material point. First Piola\u2013Kirchhoff stress `P(F)`. # Errors Returns an error if a stress component is outside the finite range.",
        "Returns the measured 3 by 3 by 3 by 3 material tangent with indices (i, J, k, L) for the derivative of P[i, J] with respect to F[k, L]. Material tangent `\u2202P[i][J] / \u2202F[k][L]`, indexed as `a[i][J][k][L]`. # Errors Returns an error if a tangent component is outside the finite range.",
        "Applies the prepared material tangent to a dimensionless gradient increment. Returns the 3 by 3 measured stress increment without constructing a rank-four tensor. Applies the material tangent to a dimensionless gradient increment. Returns the stress increment `dP(F)[h]` without forming the fourth-order tangent. The result has the same units as the stress. # Errors Returns an error for a non-finite direction or stress increment.",
        "Prepares one hyperelastic material point at a dimensionless 3 by 3 deformation gradient. Stress, stiffness and energy density share its constitutive state and retain the material pressure units. Prepares a material with a dimensionless deformation gradient `f`. # Errors Returns an error for invalid Lam\u00e9 parameters, non-finite gradients, `det F <= 1e-12`, or an unrepresentable inverse needed by neo-Hooke.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Initial state at rest with the consistent initial acceleration `a = M\u207b\u00b9 (f_ext - f_int(u))`. # Errors If the mass matrix is singular or an element is inverted.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Static solution by load stepping and Newton iterations. The load is applied in `steps` equal increments (the displacement data, tractions and body force are scaled by `s / steps`) with Newton iterations (tolerance on the free-dof residual relative to the largest external or internal force norm). # Errors If an element inverts, Newton fails to converge or the system is singular.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Strain energy `\u222b W dV` of the displacement field `u`. # Errors If the field length is wrong or the deformation or energy is invalid.",
        "Native elasticity call in an explicit mechanical reference frame. Numeric displacements/coordinates use Length, body forces use Mass/(Length^2 Time^2), stress/Young modulus use Mass/(Length Time^2), and density uses Mass/Length^3. Reduced 2D hyperelastic energies are per unit out-of-plane thickness. Outputs retain distinct dimensional reference witnesses. Native DOF, nonlinear work, callbacks and allocation budgets are checked. Material tangent `A_{iJkL} = \u2202P_{iJ}/\u2202F_{kL}` stored as `a[i][J][k][L]`. # Errors If the deformation or tangent is invalid. See [`HyperelasticPoint::new`].",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. `out = L u` at free nodes, `0` at Dirichlet nodes.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Number of Dirichlet nodes.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Boundary-datum contribution to `\u0394u` for Neumann and Robin faces at time `t`.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Whether any face carries an absorbing (wave-only) condition.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Whether node `p` is a Dirichlet node.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Assembles the operator for the given boundary conditions. # Errors If a Robin condition is nonfinite or degenerate (`alpha = beta = 0`), or the boundary periodicity differs from the grid's.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Overwrites Dirichlet nodes of `u` with `g(t, x)`.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Matrix `shift I + scale L` with identity rows at Dirichlet nodes.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Applies Dirichlet boundary conditions to a 2D field.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Applies Neumann boundary conditions (zero gradient) to a 2D field.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Solves the 1D viscous Burgers' equation: `\u2202u/\u2202t + u\u00b7\u2202u/\u2202x = \u03bd\u00b7\u2202\u00b2u/\u2202x\u00b2` This nonlinear equation models shock formation and viscous dissipation.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. 2D viscous Burgers `u_t + u (u_x + u_y) = \u03bd \u0394u` (upwinded); 2D counterpart of [`solve_burgers_1d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. 3D viscous Burgers `u_t + u (u_x + u_y + u_z) = \u03bd \u0394u`; see [`solve_burgers_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Second-order MAC projection solver for the lid-driven cavity. 2D, or 3D with `dim = 3`, at Reynolds number `re`, run to `t_end`; see [`crate::sim::ns_mac`] for obstacles, inflow/outflow and periodic faces. # Errors For invalid sizes.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Simplified lid-driven cavity flow solver using stream function-vorticity formulation. # Arguments * `nx`, `ny` - Grid dimensions * `re` - Reynolds number * `lid_velocity` - Top lid velocity * `num_steps` - Number of time steps * `dt` - Time step # Returns (stream function, vorticity)",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the divergence of a 2D velocity field. div(V) = \u2202u/\u2202x + \u2202v/\u2202y",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the gradient of a scalar field. Returns (\u2202p/\u2202x, \u2202p/\u2202y)",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the Laplacian of a scalar field with centered second differences. The row axis uses `dx`, the column axis uses `dy`, and the outer layer is zero. Grids without interior cells therefore return an all-zero result. Spacing signs have no effect. Zero or non-finite spacing uses IEEE division.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the stream function from vorticity using Poisson equation. \u2207\u00b2\u03c8 = -\u03c9",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the vorticity \u03c9 = \u2202v/\u2202x - \u2202u/\u2202y from velocity field. # Arguments * `u` - x-component of velocity (Matrix) * `v` - y-component of velocity (Matrix) * `dx`, `dy` - Grid spacing",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Returns a mutable slice to the underlying data.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Returns a slice to the underlying data.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Returns the dimensions of the grid.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Creates a new grid from existing data and dimensions.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Heat equation `u_t = \u03b1 \u0394u` on the grid of `initial` (1D, 2D or 3D). Face conditions `bcs` may be Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin, periodic or time dependent; the Crank\u2013Nicolson method of [`crate::sim::structured`] is used. # Errors For inconsistent sizes or boundary descriptions.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Is the grid empty.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Size of the grid.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Creates a new grid with the given dimensions, initialized with a default value.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Solves Poisson equation `\u2207\u00b2u = f` using Successive Over-Relaxation (SOR). # Panics Panics if the `source` grid's dimensions do not match the `width` and `height` provided. Panics if internal indexing operations go out of bounds due to incorrect `FdmGrid` usage, though typical usage within this function should prevent this.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Poisson/Helmholtz equation `-\u0394u + \u03c3 u = f` on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid with face conditions `bcs`, solved by geometric multigrid. # Errors For inconsistent sizes or boundary descriptions.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Solves `\u0394u = f` with zero Dirichlet boundaries on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid by SOR. # Errors For inconsistent shapes or lengths.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Example scenario: Simulates heat conduction on a 100x100 plate.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Example scenario: Simulates wave propagation on a 120x120 grid.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Solves a 2D heat equation `u_t = alpha * \u2207\u00b2u` using the finite difference method.",
        "Advances a 2D or 3D homogeneous Dirichlet wave from a measured displacement grid and a flat rate tensor in displacement/time. Give measured length spacings, a speed, a signed time step, a step count and a positive save cadence. Samples contain step zero, cadence multiples and the final step. Each row of data is a measured displacement field with x fastest. grid_shape gives the field extents and steps gives the completed step indices. Evolves a two- or three-dimensional Dirichlet wave and retains step samples. Initial displacement and velocity share a grid. Faces are zero from the initial sample onward. Retains step zero, every multiple of `save_every`, and the last step. Time advances by the signed step `dt`. # Errors Invalid shapes, spacing, speed, time step or cadence, a violated leapfrog CFL bound, non-finite data or states, allocation limits, or cancellation.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Solves the two-dimensional wave equation with zero initial velocity. Positive step counts impose homogeneous Dirichlet values from the initial time. With zero steps the function returns the raw initial samples. Use time steps satisfying `(c dt / dx)^2 + (c dt / dy)^2 <= 1`.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Solves the three-dimensional wave equation with zero initial velocity. Uses a second-order start and homogeneous Dirichlet faces for positive step counts. With zero steps the function returns the raw initial samples. Use time steps satisfying `\u03a3_a (c dt / h_a)^2 <= 1`.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Wave equation `u_tt = c\u00b2 \u0394u` on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid with face conditions `bcs` (including first-order absorbing faces) by leapfrog. # Errors If the CFL condition is violated or the description is inconsistent.",
        "Explicit native Float FDM-grid and sampled-initializer specialization. Measured field/coefficient/time/spacing inputs are normalized by retained physical references. Grid, CFL, work and data bounds precede native work; BC callbacks retain native errors. Creates a new grid with the given dimensions, initialized with a specified value.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Air at 20\u00b0C and 1 atm.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Kinematic viscosity \u03bd = \u03bc/\u03c1 (m\u00b2/s)",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Creates new fluid properties.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Prandtl number Pr = \u03bd/\u03b1 = \u03bc\u00b7Cp/k",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Thermal diffusivity \u03b1 = k/(\u03c1\u00b7Cp) (m\u00b2/s)",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Water at 20\u00b0C.",
        "Advances a retained 1D finite-volume mesh with an upwind flux. Supply a one-entry measured Length/Time velocity, a positive measured Time step, an integer step count, and the left and right ghost-cell values in the mesh field dimension. Both ghost values are validated. CFL must be at most one. The upwind face uses the ghost value on the side from which the flow enters. Step count and cell-step work are bounded. The measured field is returned in increasing cell order and replaces the retained field on success. An invalid input or unrepresentable output leaves the previous field intact.",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Solves the 2D linear advection equation using the Finite Volume Method.",
        "Advances the retained 3D finite-volume mesh using measured velocity and timestep. The Boolean mask is flat in x-fastest order or shaped [depth, height, width]. True cells are set to zero on each step. Unmarked exterior cells use zero inflow and free outflow. Returns a flat measured field in x-fastest order. CFL and work bounds are checked. Advances the 3D linear advection equation with first-order upwind fluxes. The callback marks cells to set to zero on each time step. All other cells use a zero exterior state at an inflow face and their own state at an outflow face. The returned values use the mesh's x-fastest storage order. For stability, choose `dt` so that the sum of the three directional Courant numbers `dt * |velocity| / spacing` is at most one. # Panics Panics if a mesh advanced for at least one step has inconsistent extents and cell storage.",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Solves the 1D Burgers' equation `u_t + (u^2/2)_x = 0` using FVM and Lax-Friedrichs flux.",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Lax-Friedrichs numerical flux for a general conservation law `u_t` + f(u)_x = 0.",
        "Creates a retained one-dimensional finite-volume mesh directly from sampled cell averages. Supply a one-entry cell-count vector with at least three cells and a one-entry measured Length vector for the total domain extent. Supply one value in the frame's field dimension per cell in increasing coordinate order. The adapter uses those samples directly and stores a uniform cell spacing from the total extent and cell count. Invalid shapes, dimensions, samples and nonpositive or unrepresentable spacing produce errors. The mesh handle can be reused for advection and data retrieval.",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Creates a new 2D mesh.",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Creates a new 3D mesh.",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked.",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Returns the number of cells in the mesh.",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Example scenario: Simulates the advection of a square wave (top-hat profile).",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Example scenario: Simulates the advection of a 2D Gaussian blob.",
        "Runs the built-in 3D Gaussian advection example on a 30-by-30-by-30 unit cube. The outer layer is set to zero each step. The returned dimensionless field is flattened in x-fastest order. Example scenario: Simulates the advection of a 3D Gaussian blob.",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. Solves 1D Shallow Water Equations using FVM and Lax-Friedrichs flux.",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. 2D shallow water equations `h_t + (hu)_x + (hv)_y = 0`, ... On a grid of `dims = [nx, ny]` cells (flat, `x` fastest) with the Lax-Friedrichs flux and zero-gradient boundaries. 2D counterpart of [`solve_shallow_water_1d`]; the numerical viscosity is shared between the two axes (unsplit scheme), so it smears slightly more than the 1D routine on y-independent data. Stable for `(|u| + sqrt(g h)) dt (1/dx + 1/dy) <= 1` (roughly). # Errors For a length mismatch or non-positive step sizes.",
        "Actual native finite-volume specialization. Measured field, velocity, length and time retain explicit reference units. Sampled initializer arrays and finite boundary masks instantiate generic native functions; masks must cover exterior cells. Returned values update the retained mesh. Native CFL, work and allocation bounds are checked. 3D barotropic shallow-water system `h_t + \u2207\u00b7(h u) = 0`, `(h u)_t + \u2207\u00b7(h u \u2297 u) + \u2207(g h\u00b2/2) = 0`. Euler with `\u03b3 = 2`, `p = g h\u00b2/2` and no energy equation: the 3D analogue of the planar shallow-water equations. On a grid of `dims = [nx, ny, nz]` cells (flat, `x` fastest) with the Lax-Friedrichs flux and zero-gradient boundaries. 3D counterpart of [`solve_shallow_water_2d`]; the numerical viscosity is shared between the three axes (unsplit scheme). Stable for `(|u| + sqrt(g h)) dt (1/dx + 1/dy + 1/dz) <= 1` (roughly). # Errors For a length mismatch or non-positive step sizes.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the L2 norm of a field for convergence checking.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the maximum absolute value in a field.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes the maximum velocity magnitude from a 2D velocity field.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Solves the 2D Poisson equation using Gauss-Seidel iteration. Generally converges faster than Jacobi.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Solves the 2D Poisson equation `\u2207\u00b2u = f` using Jacobi iteration. This function implements the Jacobi iterative method to solve the Poisson equation on a 2D grid with Dirichlet boundary conditions (implicitly handled by the iteration not updating boundary points). It is suitable for steady-state problems. # Arguments * `f` - Source term (2D grid) as a `Matrix<f64>`. * `u0` - Initial guess for `u` (2D grid) as a `Matrix<f64>`. * `dx`, `dy` - Grid spacing in x and y directions. * `max_iter` - Maximum number of iterations. * `tolerance` - Convergence tolerance for the maximum difference between successive iterations. # Returns A `Matrix<f64>` representing the solution `u`.",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Solves the 2D Poisson equation using Successive Over-Relaxation (SOR). # Arguments * `omega` - Relaxation parameter (1 < \u03c9 < 2 for over-relaxation)",
        "Measured native continuum adapter. Float matrices and mutable borrows use explicit checked tensor inputs and returned values; SI dimensions, grid shape, work/storage bounds and original native methods are preserved. Computes velocity field from stream function. u = \u2202\u03c8/\u2202y, v = -\u2202\u03c8/\u2202x",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the local stiffness matrix for a 1D linear element. The local stiffness matrix relates the forces and displacements at the nodes of a single element. For a 1D linear element, it is given by: `[EA/L -EA/L]` `[-EA/L EA/L]` # Returns A `Matrix<f64>` representing the local stiffness matrix.",
        "Native PDE in an explicit reference frame. Callback coordinates and outputs retain their physical domain; normalized numeric coefficients are checked. Native step/factor budgets, boundary errors and callback errors propagate. Prepares `a` for repeated solves. # Errors Invalid/non-finite CSR storage or tolerance, or a singular direct system.",
        "Native PDE in an explicit reference frame. Callback coordinates and outputs retain their physical domain; normalized numeric coefficients are checked. Native step/factor budgets, boundary errors and callback errors propagate. Solves `A x = b` (`x0` is the Krylov initial guess). # Errors Invalid/non-finite RHS or initial data, unrepresentable output, or an iterative solve that misses its tolerance.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Condition of a marker, or the default.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Empty assignment without default.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Assigns a condition to a marker.",
        "Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked. Assignment where unlisted markers use `default`.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates aluminum material with typical properties.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Bulk modulus K = E / (3(1-2\u03bd))",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates copper material with typical properties.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a new material with specified properties.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Shear modulus G = E / (2(1+\u03bd))",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates steel material with typical properties.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Computes the maximum shear stress from principal stresses.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference.",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention.",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention.",
        "Native residual reduction factor, without replacing the kernel's stopping report. Geometric-mean residual reduction per cycle. Returns zero if there was no cycle or the initial residual was zero.",
        "Actual native structured multigrid hierarchy with explicit Length/Time/field references and original V/W/Fmg controls. Nonzero/mixed/periodic native boundary conditions are retained; sources are normalized by field/length\u00b2. Builds the hierarchy for `-\u0394u + \u03c3 u = f`. # Errors On invalid coefficients, boundary data or a singular coarsest problem.",
        "Actual number of native continuum multigrid levels. Number of levels.",
        "Native continuum multigrid solution with explicit physical field reference and original normalized residual history/status.",
        "Native continuum multigrid solve, preserving normalized residual history and convergence status. Source arrays use field/length\u00b2 reference and time is normalized by the retained Time reference; output field retains its physical amplitude. Solves with source `f` at the nodes and boundary data evaluated at time `t`. # Errors If the source length is wrong, the time or active data are nonfinite, or the residual, solution or workspace cannot be represented.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Homogeneous or constant Neumann condition.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Distance to another node.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a new 2D node.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Creates a new 3D node.",
        "Native PDE in an explicit reference frame. Callback coordinates and outputs retain their physical domain; normalized numeric coefficients are checked. Native step/factor budgets, boundary errors and callback errors propagate. Fixed-step implicit integration (\u03b8-method) of nonlinear diffusion by Newton\u2013Krylov. # Errors If Newton fails to converge or `theta` is outside `[1/2, 1]`.",
        "Native PDE in an explicit reference frame. Callback coordinates and outputs retain their physical domain; normalized numeric coefficients are checked. Native step/factor budgets, boundary errors and callback errors propagate. Adaptive (step-doubling, Crank\u2013Nicolson) Newton\u2013Krylov integration of nonlinear diffusion. # Errors If Newton fails to converge or `max_steps` is exceeded.",
        "Returns the dimensionless open fraction along one, two, or three distinct zero-based axes. Normalize the three origin and length slots by the frame's Length reference and supply positive lengths on the active axes. The compiled field takes three coherent Length coordinates and returns Length. Positive signed distances are open. One active axis uses segment root refinement. Two or three active axes use adaptive subdivision and simplex clipping. The depth cap is 12 and depth times the number of axes is at most 18. The independent callback budget accepts 1 through 8,388,608 evaluations. Invalid geometry, callback units or arity, nonfinite samples and budget exhaustion produce errors.",
        "Returns the dimensionless open fraction of an axis-aligned segment. Axis indices are zero based. Normalize the three origin coordinates and positive segment length by the frame's Length reference. The compiled field takes three coherent Length coordinates and returns Length. Positive distances are open. The kernel samples the segment, then bisects intervals where its sampled sign changes. The callback budget accepts 1 through 8,388,608 evaluations, including refinement. Invalid geometry, callback units or arity, nonfinite samples, and exhausted evaluation budgets produce errors.",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves the 1D advection equation `du/dt + c * du/dx = 0` using an explicit finite difference scheme. This function implements a simple first-order upwind scheme for stability. # Arguments * `u0` - Initial condition (vector of `u` values at `t=0`). * `c` - Advection speed. * `dx` - Spatial step size. * `dt` - Time step size. * `num_steps` - Number of time steps to simulate. # Returns A `Vec<Vec<f64>>` where each inner `Vec` is the solution `u` at a given time step.",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D upwind advection `u_t + c\u00b7\u2207u = 0` (periodic with period `n - 1` per axis); 2D counterpart of [`solve_advection_1d`]. Returns all snapshots. # Errors For inconsistent shapes or a non-positive `dt`.",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D upwind advection; see [`solve_advection_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves the 1D advection-diffusion equation: `\u2202u/\u2202t + c\u00b7\u2202u/\u2202x = \u03b1\u00b7\u2202\u00b2u/\u2202x\u00b2`",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D advection-diffusion `u_t + c\u00b7\u2207u = \u03b1 \u0394u`; 2D counterpart of [`solve_advection_diffusion_1d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D advection-diffusion; see [`solve_advection_diffusion_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves the 1D diffusion equation `du/dt = alpha * d2u/dx2` using an explicit finite difference scheme. # Arguments * `u0` - Initial condition. * `alpha` - Diffusion coefficient. * `dx` - Spatial step size. * `dt` - Time step size. * `num_steps` - Number of time steps. # Returns A `Vec<Vec<f64>>` where each inner `Vec` is the solution `u` at a given time step.",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D explicit diffusion `u_t = \u03b1 \u0394u` with frozen boundary values; 2D counterpart of [`solve_diffusion_1d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D explicit diffusion; see [`solve_diffusion_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_advection_2d`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 1D Poisson equation by Gauss-Seidel iteration; see [`solve_poisson_1d_jacobi`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_1d_jacobi`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 1D Poisson equation `u'' = f` by Jacobi iteration (Dirichlet values are the end values of `u0`). 1D counterpart of [`solve_poisson_2d_jacobi`]. # Errors For inconsistent lengths or a non-positive spacing.",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 1D Poisson equation by SOR with relaxation factor `omega`; see [`solve_poisson_1d_jacobi`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_1d_jacobi`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Poisson equation by Gauss-Seidel iteration; see [`solve_poisson_3d_jacobi`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_3d_jacobi`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Poisson equation `\u0394u = f` by Jacobi iteration on `dims = [nx, ny, nz]` (flat, `x` fastest). 3D counterpart of [`solve_poisson_2d_jacobi`]. # Errors For inconsistent lengths or non-positive spacings.",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Poisson equation by SOR; see [`solve_poisson_3d_jacobi`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_3d_jacobi`].",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Scenario for the 1D Crank-Nicolson solver.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Scenario for the 2D Crank-Nicolson ADI solver.",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves the 1D heat equation `u_t` = D * `u_xx` using the Crank-Nicolson method. # Arguments * `initial_condition` - The initial temperature distribution. * `dx` - The spatial step size. * `dt` - The time step. * `d_coeff` - The diffusion coefficient D. * `steps` - The number of time steps to simulate. # Returns The final temperature distribution.",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Direct tridiagonal Crank\u2013Nicolson reference (zero Dirichlet ends).",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves a 1D advection-diffusion equation `u_t + c*u_x = d*u_xx` using FDM.",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D advection-diffusion `u_t + c\u00b7\u2207u = d \u0394u` (upwind + central, frozen boundaries); returns the final state. 2D counterpart of [`solve_advection_diffusion_1d`]. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_2d`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D advection-diffusion; see [`solve_advection_diffusion_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_2d`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Solves 1D Burgers' equation `u_t + u*u_x = nu*u_xx`.",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D Burgers' equation `u_t + u (u_x + u_y) = nu \u0394u` with central convection differences and frozen boundaries; returns the state after `steps` steps. 2D counterpart of [`solve_burgers_1d`]. # Errors For inconsistent shapes or a non-positive `dt`.",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Burgers' equation; see [`solve_burgers_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_2d`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 1D Poisson equation `u'' = f`, zero Dirichlet ends, SOR. 1D counterpart of [`solve_poisson_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_nd`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Poisson equation `\u0394u = f`, zero Dirichlet boundaries, SOR. 3D counterpart of [`solve_poisson_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_poisson_nd`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 2D Burgers' equation; 2D counterpart of [`solve_burgers_1d`]. See [`solve_burgers_nd`]. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_nd`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. 3D Burgers' equation; see [`solve_burgers_nd`]. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_nd`].",
        "Native transport calculation in the supplied reference frame. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivities and timesteps are explicitly normalized by Length, Time and field references; no measured numeric input is silently erased. Solver work and storage are bounded. Burgers' equation `u_t + \u03a3_a (u\u00b2/2)_{x_a} = 0` on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid. The cells are stored flat, with x fastest. Uses the multi-dimensional Lax\u2013Friedrichs flux with equal numerical-viscosity shares per axis and zero-gradient outflow boundaries. The one-dimensional stencil agrees with [`solve_burgers_1d`] up to arithmetic roundoff. See [`solve_burgers_nd_with_boundaries`] for the stability condition. # Errors As [`solve_burgers_nd_with_boundaries`].",
        "Solves the multidimensional Burgers equation by conservative Lax\u2013Friedrichs fluxes. Use a velocity-valued frame. The flat initial field is in units of value_reference with x fastest, spacing in units of length_reference, and dt in units of time_reference. Supply Periodic or Outflow for both faces of each axis. The CFL bound holds separately on each axis. The returned field keeps all three references. Advances Burgers' equation with a boundary choice for each active axis. Uses the same Lax\u2013Friedrichs flux on both sides of an interface. A fully periodic grid conserves the sum of cell averages up to roundoff. Zero-gradient faces allow the boundary physical flux to leave or enter the grid. For the equal viscosity shares used here, a sufficient monotonicity condition is `D * dt * max|u| <= h[a]` on each active axis, where `D` is the number of axes. At fixed grid spacing, the one-step limit as `dt` tends to zero retains Lax\u2013Friedrichs averaging. # Errors An invalid grid, boundary count, initial field, or step size, or a flux or state that cannot be represented.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Example scenario for the 1D spectral solver.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Example scenario for the 2D spectrclsal solver.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Example scenario for the 3D spectral solver.",
        "Native finite-element model in an explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature frame. All numeric coefficients/coordinates/displacements are normalized by the corresponding derived references. Results retain physical reference metadata; beam matrices expose heterogeneous translation/rotation DOF references and their quadratic-form reference. Converts stress to principal stresses. Returns (sigma1, sigma2, angle) where sigma1 >= sigma2. # Panics Panics if the stress vector length is not 3.",
        "Execute the actual fixed native cantilever scenario (105 nodes,80 elements, CG maximum5000 iterations), writing beam_original.csv and beam_deformed.csv to the requested directory. Native educational coefficients are explicitly normalized; no measured inputs are accepted. An example scenario for a cantilever beam under a point load. This function sets up a mesh for a 2D cantilever beam, defines fixed boundary conditions at one end and applies a point load at the free end. It then runs the elasticity simulation and saves the original and deformed node positions to CSV files for visualization. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `run_elasticity_simulation` fails or if it cannot create or write to the output CSV files. `beam_original.csv` and `beam_deformed.csv` are written into `output_dir`, which is created if it does not exist.",
        "Computes the plane-stress stiffness matrix of one bilinear quadrilateral. Supply four counterclockwise [x,y] rows scaled by the mechanical Length reference, Young's modulus scaled by the stress reference, and a dimensionless Poisson ratio. The record contains rows, cols, the 64 normalized entries in row-major order, and an entry_reference in Force per Length. Model thickness is one Length reference. Invalid material, malformed vertices, and nonpositive or nonfinite quadrature Jacobians produce errors.",
        "Solves a bilinear quadrilateral plane-stress mesh. Supply [x,y] node rows and four zero-based node indices per counterclockwise element, followed by Young's modulus, the dimensionless Poisson ratio, fixed node indices and [node,fx,fy] load rows. Normalize coordinates, Young's modulus and loads by the mechanical Length, stress and Force references. Thickness is one Length reference. Both displacement components of a fixed node are zero. The return record contains interleaved normalized x/y displacements and their measured Length reference. The adapter bounds node count, load rows and estimated solve work. It rejects invalid connectivity, geometry, material and nonconvergent solves.",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Refines a triangular mesh by subdividing each triangle into 4 smaller triangles.",
        "Native continuum model in an explicit positive Length/Time/field reference frame. Numeric coordinates, boundary coefficients and field arrays are normalized in that frame. Session capabilities, model frames, physical callback domains and work/allocation budgets are checked. Robin condition with constant data.",
        "Native dimensionless safety factor from three normalized plane stresses and yield strength in the same stress reference; zero equivalent stress preserves the native positive-infinite result. Safety factor based on von Mises criterion.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Constant gradients of the barycentric coordinates of a cell.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Tetrahedral mesh of a box (six tetrahedra per cube, Kuhn subdivision), markers 0-5.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Uniform interval mesh with `n` cells on `[lo, hi]` (markers 0 and 1).",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Cell measure (length, area or volume).",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked.",
        "Native positive fraction of a segment/triangle/tetrahedron in dimensionless level-set coordinates; common positive rescaling does not change the fraction. Fraction of a point, segment, triangle or tetrahedron where the linearly interpolated vertex values are positive. Returns NaN for any other vertex count or a nonfinite value.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Triangulated rectangle (two triangles per quad), markers 0-3.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Refines the cells flagged in `marked` by conforming edge bisection. Every marked tetrahedron is split at the midpoints of its six edges into eight tetrahedra. The split is carried out as recursive bisection: a cell is cut along its longest split edge (ties broken by vertex indices) and the two halves are cut again until no split edge is left. Only midpoints of edges of marked cells are inserted, so an unmarked neighbour is cut along exactly the edges it shares with marked cells (**red\u2013green closure**) and a cell without a split edge is kept. Both cells sharing a face cut that face from the same edge midpoints to the opposite vertex of the face, hence there are no hanging nodes. Boundary facets inherit the marker of the parent facet containing them. The refinement level of every produced cell is one more than the level of its parent; cells that are not touched keep their level. In 1D and 2D the same scheme splits a marked cell into 2 or 4 cells. Entries of `marked` past the last cell are ignored.",
        "Native simplex finite-element method with explicit geometry/time/field references. Diffusivity is normalized by L\u00b2/T, reaction by1/T, source by U/T; compiled callbacks use coherent coordinates and retained physical output domains. Mesh/DOF/refinement/callback work bounds are checked. Grundmann-Moeller quadrature on the `n`-simplex. Grundmann\u2013M\u00f6ller quadrature on the `n`-simplex, exact for polynomials of degree `2s + 1`. Returns barycentric points (`n + 1` coordinates) and weights that sum to one (multiply by the simplex measure).",
        "Advance existing unnormalized-forward 3D FFT coefficients with spectral Forward Euler. Data uses x-fastest order and the supplied frame's field reference. Spacing, velocity, diffusivity and time are normalized in the supplied frame. Zero steps or a zero time step preserves the coefficients. Advances an existing complex Fourier spectrum by the configured Euler steps. The spectrum uses the unnormalized forward transform of [`fft3d`] and the same axis order, with x varying fastest, then y, then z. [`ifft3d`] divides by the grid size on returning to physical space. A zero-step or zero-time-step call keeps all validated coefficients unchanged. # Errors Rejects zero or overflowing extents, mismatched shape, nonfinite data, invalid spacing or periods, invalid diffusivity or time step, and nonfinite computed output. When a call fails, the spectrum stays unchanged.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved. Exact-in-time Fourier solution of `u_t + c\u00b7\u2207u = D \u0394u` on a periodic box of 1D, 2D or 3D (power-of-two points per axis, data with `x` fastest): every mode is multiplied by `exp((-i c\u00b7k - D |k|\u00b2) t)`. # Errors For sizes that are not powers of two or inconsistent data.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved.",
        "Actual native grid/transform method in explicit Length/Time/field references; arrays and coefficients are normalized in that frame and native x-fastest storage is retained. Method identity, FFT normalization, boundary semantics and bounded work/history are preserved.",
        "Original Fourier spectral Forward Euler stepping, preserving conditional stability and the native method; this is distinct from sm_advection_diffusion_exact_nd. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivity and steps use explicit L/T/field reference normalization. Odd/even FFT sizes, native x-fastest layout and work bounds are checked. Solves the 1D advection-diffusion equation (`u_t` + c*`u_x` = D*`u_xx`) using a Fourier spectral method.",
        "Original Fourier spectral Forward Euler stepping, preserving conditional stability and the native method; this is distinct from sm_advection_diffusion_exact_nd. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivity and steps use explicit L/T/field reference normalization. Odd/even FFT sizes, native x-fastest layout and work bounds are checked. Solves the 2D advection-diffusion equation using a Fourier spectral method.",
        "Original Fourier spectral Forward Euler stepping, preserving conditional stability and the native method; this is distinct from sm_advection_diffusion_exact_nd. Coordinates, velocities, diffusivity and steps use explicit L/T/field reference normalization. Odd/even FFT sizes, native x-fastest layout and work bounds are checked. Solves the 3D advection-diffusion equation.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Solves `-\u0394u + \u03c3 u = f` on the grid with the given boundary conditions (`\u03c3` may be negative for the indefinite Helmholtz equation). # Errors On a singular problem (e.g. pure Neumann with `\u03c3 = 0`), a solver failure or an absorbing boundary.",
        "Actual native continuum multigrid free solver with coherent compiled spatial source of field/length\u00b2 units, retained physical boundary data, and original cycle controls. Convenience wrapper: solves `-\u0394u + \u03c3u = f(x)` with multigrid. # Errors See [`MultigridSolver::new`].",
        "Actual native ND multigrid wrapper on a zero-origin rectangular grid. Extents and positive spacings are normalized by the retained Length reference; source uses field/length\u00b2. Original V/W/Fmg controls, boundary conditions, residuals and convergence status are preserved. Geometric multigrid for `-\u0394u + \u03c3u = f` on a 1D, 2D or 3D grid. Face conditions may be Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin or periodic; V, W or full multigrid cycles. Data are stored with `x` fastest. # Errors For inconsistent sizes or boundary descriptions.",
        "Actual native finite-element assembly/structural method in an explicit mechanical frame. Matrices and loads are normalized in the declared DOF references; the energy reference and heterogeneous DOF references determine every physical coefficient. Assembly, geometry, bounds and mutation results are checked before retention. Solves a static structural problem for displacements. This function takes the global stiffness matrix, applied forces, and boundary conditions to solve for the nodal displacements. It modifies the global stiffness matrix and force vector to incorporate Dirichlet boundary conditions. # Arguments * `global_k` - The global stiffness matrix. * `forces` - The global force vector. * `fixed_dofs` - A vector of tuples `(node_idx, prescribed_displacement)` for fixed degrees of freedom. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Vec<f64>` of nodal displacements, or an error string if the system is singular. # Errors Returns an error if the force vector dimension mismatches the stiffness matrix, or if the resulting system is singular.",
        "Native structured PDE method in the grid reference frame; normalized numeric inputs are converted to coherent callback coordinates and checked physical callback output domains. Callback and discretization work are bounded; native boundary errors propagate. Solves `-\u2207\u00b7(\u03b1(x)\u2207u) + \u03c3 u = f` with a node-sampled diffusivity and the given boundary conditions (`\u03c3` may be negative for the indefinite Helmholtz equation). The face values of `\u03b1` use `averaging`. # Errors On a singular problem (e.g. pure Neumann with `\u03c3 = 0`), a solver failure, an absorbing boundary or a bad `alpha`.",
        "Native spectral discretization with explicit coordinate/time/field references. Transforms retain the field domain; wave numbers and derivatives retain inverse-length powers. Native sizes, callback domains and solve work are checked.",
