        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. # Block Matrix Determinant Computes the determinant using block matrix decomposition (Schur complement). This is efficient for large matrices. # Errors This function will return an error if: - The matrix is not square. - The matrix is singular or reduction fails.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the determinant using LU decomposition. # Errors This function will return an error if: - The matrix is not square. - The matrix is singular or reduction fails.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the Frobenius norm of the matrix.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Returns an immutable reference to the element at the specified row and column. # Arguments * `row` - The row index. * `col` - The column index. # Returns An immutable reference to the element. # Panics Panics if the `row` or `col` indices are out of bounds.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Returns a `Vec` of `Vec<T>` where each inner `Vec` represents a column of the matrix. This method effectively transposes the matrix data into a column-major representation. # Returns A `Vec<Vec<T>>` where each inner vector is a column.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Returns a mutable reference to the element at the specified row and column. # Arguments * `row` - The row index. * `col` - The column index. # Returns A mutable reference to the element. # Panics Panics if the `row` or `col` indices are out of bounds.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Creates an identity matrix of a given size.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Consumes the matrix and returns its internal data vector.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the inverse of a square matrix. This method uses Gaussian elimination on an augmented matrix `[A | I]` to transform `A` into the identity matrix `I`, resulting in `[I | A^-1]`. # Returns * `Some(Matrix)` containing the inverse matrix if it exists. * `None` if the matrix is not square or is singular (not invertible).",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Checks if the matrix is diagonal.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Checks if the matrix is identity within a given tolerance.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Checks if the matrix is orthogonal ($A^T A = I$).",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Checks if the matrix is symmetric ($A = A^T$).",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the L1 norm of the matrix (max column sum).",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the Linf norm of the matrix (max row sum).",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the LU decomposition of a square matrix. # Returns A tuple containing the LU matrix and the number of row swaps. # Errors Returns an error if: - The matrix is not square. - The matrix is singular.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. # Strassen's Matrix Multiplication This function multiplies two matrices using Strassen's algorithm, which is more efficient for large matrices than the naive O(n^3) algorithm. ## Arguments * `other` - The matrix to multiply with. ## Returns A new `Matrix` representing the product of the two matrices. ## Errors Returns an error if: - Matrix multiplication is not possible due to incompatible dimensions. - The matrices are not square (for the internal recursive calls).",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Creates a new `Matrix` from dimensions and a flat `Vec` of data. # Arguments * `rows` - The number of rows. * `cols` - The number of columns. * `data` - A `Vec` containing the matrix elements in row-major order. # Panics Panics if the product of the dimensions overflows or differs from `data.len()`.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes a basis for the null space (kernel) of the matrix. The null space of a matrix `A` is the set of all vectors `x` such that `Ax = 0`. This method finds the null space by first computing the RREF of the matrix, identifying pivot and free variables, and then constructing basis vectors. # Returns A `Matrix` whose columns form a basis for the null space. # Errors Returns an error if the RREF calculation fails.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the rank of the matrix. # Errors Returns an error if the RREF computation fails.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Returns the number of rows in the matrix.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the reduced row echelon form (RREF) of the matrix in-place. This method applies Gaussian elimination to transform the matrix into its RREF. It is used for solving linear systems, finding matrix inverses, and determining rank. # Returns The rank of the matrix (number of non-zero rows in RREF). # Errors Returns an error if a division by zero occurs during the elimination process, which can happen if the matrix elements are not from a proper field.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Updates the backend for the matrix in-place.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the trace of the matrix (sum of diagonal elements). # Errors Returns an error if the matrix is not square.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Computes the transpose of the matrix. The transpose of a matrix `A` (denoted `A^T`) is obtained by flipping the matrix over its diagonal; that is, it switches the row and column indices of the matrix. # Returns A new `Matrix` representing the transpose.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Sets the backend for the matrix.",
        "Validated portable Matrix specialization. Format/version, dense rank/shape, indices, finite values and allocation limits are checked. Real specializes exact rational arithmetic and permits only Native backend. Backend changes are immutable. Determinants return normalized magnitude, entry_unit and order, representing normalized*entry_unit^order; packed LU similarly operates on entries normalized by one canonical input unit. RREF/null-space vectors are dimensionless. No pointer or process-local handle is serialized. Creates a new `Matrix` filled with the zero element of type `T`. # Arguments * `rows` - The number of rows. * `cols` - The number of columns. # Returns A new `Matrix` of the specified dimensions, with all elements initialized to `T::zero()`. # Panics Panics if the product of the dimensions overflows.",
        "Computes the maximum value of a slice of data.",
        "Arithmetic mean; zero for an empty sample. Computes the mean of a slice of data. # Arguments * `data` - A unmutable slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The mean of the data as an `f64`.",
        "Computes the median of a slice of data. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The median of the data as an `f64`.",
        "`num_meshgen_boundary_faces(tetrahedra)` returns the oriented triangular faces incident to exactly one tetrahedron, in first occurrence order. Tetrahedron rows have four distinct zero-based vertex indices. Positively oriented tetrahedra give outward face winding. The faces of `tets` that belong to exactly one tetrahedron. Each face is returned once, with the winding it has in the tetrahedron that owns it, so the faces of a closed mesh point outwards.",
        "`num_meshgen_delaunay_2d_incremental(points)` inserts between 3 and 4096 distinct planar points in input order and returns counter-clockwise triangles of zero-based vertex indices. Points may carry coordinate units. The triangulation contains indices only. Incremental Delaunay triangulation of `points`. This is the incremental entry point of the same Bowyer\u2013Watson insertion used by [`delaunay_2d`]: points are inserted one at a time into the growing triangulation, and each insertion only re-triangulates the cavity of triangles whose circumcircle contains the new point. # Errors Returns `Err` under the same conditions as [`delaunay_2d`].",
        "`num_meshgen_min_angle_deg(points, triangles)` returns the smallest planar triangle angle as a scalar in degrees. Points have two columns and triangles have three zero-based vertex indices. An empty mesh or a degenerate triangle gives zero. Out-of-range indices are errors. The smallest angle in degrees over all `triangles`. Returns `0.0` for an empty mesh or a degenerate triangle.",
        "`num_meshgen_tetra_volumes(points, tetrahedra)` returns nonnegative volumes in input order, with cubed coordinate units. Points have three columns and tetrahedra have four zero-based vertex indices. Degenerate tetrahedra have zero volume. Out-of-range indices are errors. The volume of every tetrahedron in `tets`, always non-negative. Tetrahedra whose indices do not address `points` are reported as `0.0`.",
        "`num_meshgen_triangle_areas(points, triangles)` returns an area for each triangle in input order, with squared coordinate units. Points have two columns and triangles have three zero-based vertex indices. Degenerate triangles have zero area. Out-of-range indices are errors. The area of every triangle in `triangles`. Triangles whose indices do not address `points` are reported as `0.0`. Large batches use the current execution pool and preserve input order.",
        "Computes the minimum value of a slice of data.",
        "Returns a dyadic enclosure of the absolute values in the closed interval at the requested precision. The absolute value, as the hull of the endpoints' absolute values. # Errors Fails when an exponent leaves the range.",
        "Tests whether the closed interval contains zero, including either endpoint. Whether the interval contains zero.",
        "Encloses all quotients of two closed dimensionless intervals. Both arguments and the result have canonical dyadic midpoint and radius fields. The radius is nonnegative and can have a different bit precision from the midpoint. The divisor interval must exclude zero, including at its endpoints. An out-of-range exponent is a domain error. Encloses the quotients of the two intervals when the divisor excludes zero. # Errors Fails when the divisor contains zero or an exponent leaves the range.",
        "Returns the lower endpoint rounded down and upper endpoint rounded up at the requested precision. The two endpoints, `mid - rad` and `mid + rad`. # Errors Fails when an exponent leaves the range.",
        "Reflects a closed interval about zero and retains its radius. The value negated.",
        "Creates a closed interval from canonical dyadic midpoint and radius. The radius must be nonnegative. The ball of radius `rad` around `mid`. # Errors Fails for a negative radius or when an exponent leaves the range.",
        "Creates a closed interval containing exactly the given dyadic. The degenerate ball holding exactly `mid`.",
        "Returns twice the radius as a dyadic. An out-of-range exponent is a domain error. The width `2 * rad`. # Errors Fails when an exponent leaves the range.",
        "Returns the absolute value of an exact dyadic without rounding. The absolute value.",
        "Adds two canonical dyadic records and rounds the exact sum once to the requested bit precision and mode. The sum, rounded once to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails when the exponent would leave the representable range.",
        "Compares canonical dyadic records exactly. Returns -1, 0, or 1 and allows different operand precisions. Total order: `-1`, `0` or `1` as `self` is below, equal to or above `other`.",
        "Divides two canonical dyadic records and rounds the exact quotient once to the requested bit precision and mode. A zero divisor or an out-of-range normalized exponent is a domain error. The quotient, rounded once to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails on division by zero or when the exponent leaves the range.",
        "Returns floor(log2(abs(value))) or None if value is zero. `floor(log2(|x|))`, or `None` for zero.",
        "Tests whether a dyadic has no fractional binary digits. Whether the value is an exact integer.",
        "Tests whether a canonical dyadic is zero. Whether the value is zero.",
        "Multiplies two canonical dyadic records and rounds the exact product once to the requested bit precision and mode. The product, rounded once to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails when the exponent would leave the representable range.",
        "Negates an exact dyadic at its existing precision. The value negated.",
        "Rounds mantissa times 2^exponent once to the requested bit precision. Returns the integer fields mantissa, exponent and precision. Nonzero mantissas have exactly precision bits and zero has exponent zero. Precision is 1 to 32768 bits, input mantissas may have up to 65536 bits and exponents must lie within +/-262144. Accepts nearest (ties to even), zero, away, up, or down. `mant * 2^exp` rounded to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails when the exponent would leave the representable range.",
        "Multiplies a dyadic by an integer power of two exactly. Zero keeps its canonical exponent. A requested exponent outside the representable range is a domain error. `self * 2^k`, exact. # Errors Fails when the exponent leaves the representable range.",
        "Rounds the square root of a nonnegative dyadic at the requested bit precision. A negative input is a domain error. The square root, rounded once to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails for a negative operand or an exponent outside the range.",
        "Subtracts two canonical dyadic records and rounds the exact difference once to the requested bit precision and mode. The difference, rounded once to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails when the exponent would leave the representable range.",
        "Truncates a dyadic toward zero and returns an exact integer. The dyadic representation bounds the result to 294912 bits. The value truncated toward zero to an integer. # Errors Fails when the exponent leaves the representable range.",
        "Rounds an existing canonical dyadic to a new bit precision. The rounding modes are nearest, zero, away, up and down. The same value rounded to `prec` bits. # Errors Fails when the exponent would leave the representable range.",
        "`num_multinomial(n, parts)` returns the floating-point multinomial coefficient. Parts are nonnegative integers and must sum exactly to n. The empty partition of zero gives one. A coefficient that exceeds binary64 produces a domain error. Computes a floating-point multinomial coefficient as a product of binomials. Returns `None` if the parts do not sum to `n`, and `Some(infinity)` when the coefficient overflows. Zero parts and the empty partition of zero have no effect on the product.",
        "Multivariate log density relative to coordinate_scale^n, where n is the vector length. An explicit positive scale makes the logarithm dimensionless and defines the reference measure. Dimensionless multivariate normal log density with a row-major covariance matrix. Rejects non-finite data, mismatched dimensions and non-positive-definite covariance.",
        "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. AAA algorithm (Nakatsukasa, Sete, Trefethen) on real samples `(z_i, f_i)`: greedy support-point selection and a least-squares weight vector from the SVD of the Loewner matrix. # Errors [`ApproxError::Invalid`] for fewer than 3 samples.",
        "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Degree (`numerator = denominator = m - 1`).",
        "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Evaluates the rational function at `x`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Applies the transformation to a point.",
        "Construct an affine map in explicitly nondimensionalized coordinate space; coefficients represent a,b,c,d,e,f in the native map. Creates a new affine transformation.",
        "Generates a dimensionless two-column point tensor after the requested burn-in, using an exact unsigned 64-bit seed. The starting point is excluded. A zero point count returns shape [0, 2]. Nonfinite trajectories and excessive work return errors. Generates `num_points` transformed points after `skip` burn-in steps. The seed selects the same draw stream on every call. The starting point itself is excluded. A request for zero points skips the burn-in. # Errors A nonfinite starting point or step, work count overflow, or allocation failure.",
        "Prepares a session-owned affine iterated function system from up to 4096 dimensionless rows (a,b,c,d,e,f) and matching finite nonnegative relative weights. A positive weight is required. Reuse the plan across independent starting points and seeds. Prepares an affine IFS from finite coefficients and relative weights. Zero-weight transformations are excluded. Bounds are inclusive at the upper end of each draw interval. # Errors Invalid shape, weights, coefficients or a failed allocation.",
        "Native scaled global/constrained optimization with shared actual host evaluation limit. Nelder: x,xscale,yscale,step,tol,iterations,budget,f. Differential evolution: lo,hi,xscale,yscale,population,generations,budget,seed,f. Annealing: x,xscale,yscale,step,temperature,cooling,iterations,budget,seed,f. Constrained: x,xscale,yscale,equality_scale,inequality_scale,tol,iterations,budget,f,eq,ineq. Constraints independently carry C/H units; SQP returns physical multipliers E/C and E/H, native status and second-order verdict. Second-order: x,lambda,mu,xscale,yscale,eqscale,ineqscale,budget,f,eq,ineq. No global-optimality claim; native iteration/status semantics retained. Augmented Lagrangian for `min f(x)` subject to `h(x) = 0` and `g(x) <= 0`; the inner problems are solved by BFGS.",
        "Compile multiple elementary expressions with physical coordinate, parameter and output references. Uses the actual selected native backend and bounded source work.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The Jacobian of a real system over a box.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The complex Jacobian `\u2202f_i/\u2202z_j` over the box. Each entry is an enclosure. A missing or zero exponent gives no contribution to that column.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. `f_i(X)` of a real system over a box.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. `f_i(X)` for every equation of the system (an enclosure of the range over the box).",
        "Evaluates a polynomial system and its Jacobian over one complex box, sharing term products. Returns {values, jacobian} as enclosure handles. Value units equal the residual unit. Jacobian units are residual divided by the coordinate unit. Encloses both the values and the complex Jacobian of a system over a box. The evaluator can reuse powers across rows and shares each term's products across the value and partial derivatives. Missing exponents are zero, and exponents beyond the number of coordinates are ignored.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The Jacobian of a real system at a point.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The complex Jacobian at a point.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Lifts a real system to complex terms.",
        "Evaluates a polynomial system and its Jacobian at one complex point. Returns {values, jacobian} with residual units and residual-per-coordinate units respectively. The Jacobian is a rank-two tensor with one row per equation. Evaluates the values and complex Jacobian at a point using one power cache. The arithmetic is ordinary complex `f64` arithmetic. The exponent convention matches [`eval_value_and_jacobian_terms`].",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The values of a real system at a point.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The values `f_i(x)` at a point, in plain `f64` arithmetic.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Corrects up to `t` bit errors in a (possibly shortened) codeword. Returns the corrected codeword and the number of corrected bits. # Errors Fails if the word is empty, longer than `n`, not binary, or if the error pattern is uncorrectable (more than `t` errors that are detected; patterns of more than `t` errors may also be mis-decoded to a different codeword, as for every bounded-distance decoder).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Decodes a (possibly shortened) codeword to its message bits, correcting up to `t` errors. # Errors See [`Bch::correct`].",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Encodes `data` (`1..=k` bits) into a systematic codeword `data ++ parity` of `data.len() + (n - k)` bits. # Errors Fails for an empty message, a message longer than `k`, or entries other than `0` and `1`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Builds the smallest-length code of degree `m <= 16` correcting `t` errors that carries at least `data_bits` information bits. # Errors Fails if no supported code is large enough.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Generator polynomial coefficients over GF(2), lowest power first.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether `word` is a codeword (all syndromes vanish).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Dimension `k = n - deg g` (message bits per block).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Field degree `m` (the block length is `2^m - 1`).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Block length `n = 2^m - 1`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Builds the BCH code of length `2^m - 1` correcting `t` errors. # Errors Fails if `m` is outside `2..=16`, `t == 0`, or `t` is so large that the code has no information bits (`k < 1`).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Number of parity bits `n - k`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Designed error-correction capability `t`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the cumulative distribution function (CDF) value at `k`.",
        "Checked native legacy distribution. Continuous parameters and queries retain coherent physical units; inverse CDF restores sample units and PDF has inverse sample units. Creates a new `BinomialDist` instance. # Errors Returns an error if `p < 0.0` or `p > 1.0`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the probability mass function (PMF) value at `k`.",
        "Return the original weight count, including zero-weight categories. Returns the length of the original weight slice.",
        "Return an integer tensor of zero-based draws in stream order. It advances the supplied session random stream exactly as repeated scalar sampling would. Rust callers can fill existing storage. Fills an existing slice with the same draws as repeated calls to [`Self::sample`], including the advancement of the random stream.",
        "Prepare repeated sampling of up to 32768 categories. Provide finite nonnegative dimensionless relative weights with at least one positive entry. The same table can be used with different random streams in the session. Prepares a sampler from finite, nonnegative relative weights. Returns indices in the original weight order and never selects a zero-weight entry. At least one weight must be positive. The total need not be finite before scaling. Conditional alias probabilities are rounded down to multiples of `2^-64`. # Errors Returns an error for an empty slice, an invalid weight, an all-zero slice or an allocation failure.",
        "Draw one zero-based index and advance the supplied session random stream as needed. Zero-weight categories are excluded. A single supported category does not consume random values. Draws one zero-based category index from the supplied random stream. A single possible category consumes no random values. A uniform distribution chooses an unbiased column. A weighted distribution also draws an independent raw value for that column's cutoff.",
