        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The square `|z - (re + i im)|_inf <= radius` (one radius for both parts), widened outward.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. `0`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Builds a value from two `f64` components.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Fast `f64` modulus of the leading components.",
        "Computes the modulus of a complex double-double value and keeps both components of the real double-double result. The result preserves the input's physical dimension. Modulus computed from both parts of each component.",
        "Computes the reciprocal of a complex double-double value. The retained result has the inverse physical dimension. Zero inputs and nonfinite results are rejected. Multiplicative inverse, using the same scale handling as division. The inverse of zero has NaN components.",
        "Construct a portable native Chebyshev series from a checked serial callback with independent physical coordinate and ordinate units. Bounds, degree, finite callback values, original callback failures and evaluation budgets are checked. Adaptive max_degree is a power of two from 8 through 64. Returns the same coefficient model accepted by native evaluation, derivative, integral and root methods. Adaptive construction: doubles the degree (up to `max_degree`) until the trailing coefficients fall below `tol * max|c|`, then chops the negligible tail. Values at shared nodes are reused when refining the grid. The same coordinate should always give the same value when supplied to `f`.",
        "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references.",
        "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Definite integral over `[a, b]`.",
        "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Degree of the series.",
        "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Derivative series.",
        "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Value at `x in [a, b]`.",
        "Construct a portable native Chebyshev series from a checked serial callback with independent physical coordinate and ordinate units. Bounds, degree, finite callback values, original callback failures and evaluation budgets are checked. Adaptive max_degree is a power of two from 8 through 64. Returns the same coefficient model accepted by native evaluation, derivative, integral and root methods. Interpolates `f` at the Chebyshev extrema in [`chebyshev_points`] order. Degree zero and equal endpoints use one function evaluation.",
        "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Interpolates already sampled values in [`chebyshev_points`] order. `values[0]` is the sample at `b` for two or more values. One value represents a constant on the whole interval. An empty slice represents zero. With equal endpoints the first value is used.",
        "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Antiderivative series vanishing at `a`.",
        "Portable native approximation model with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Every query checks kind/version, defining data lengths, finite data, monotone grids where required, interval and allocation budgets. Derivatives restore ordinate/coordinate powers; integrals restore their product. Model values contain only defining data and no process-local references. Real roots in `[a, b]` from the eigenvalues of the colleague matrix. # Errors [`ApproxError::Singular`] if the eigenvalue iteration fails.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Calculates the total gravitational potential energy of a system of particles.",
        "Returns the measured mass, position and velocity of a retained classical particle. The position and velocity vectors each have three components in x, y, z order.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Kinetic energy of the particle.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Momentum magnitude.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Creates a new particle.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Simulates projectile motion with air resistance (drag). Uses Euler integration to solve equations of motion: `F_drag` = -0.5 * \u03c1 * A * Cd * v\u00b2 * `v_hat` # Arguments * `params` - Simulation parameters # Returns Vector of (time, x, y, vx, vy) tuples",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Simulates a 2D Ising model using the Metropolis-Hastings algorithm. The Ising model is a mathematical model of ferromagnetism in statistical mechanics. This function simulates the evolution of a 2D lattice of spins using the Metropolis-Hastings algorithm to reach thermal equilibrium at a given temperature. # Arguments * `size` - The dimension of the square lattice (e.g., `size x size`). * `temperature` - The temperature of the system. * `steps` - The number of Monte Carlo steps to perform. # Returns A `Vec<Vec<i8>>` representing the final spin configuration of the lattice.",
        "Simulates classical gravitational motion from 1 to 512 retained particle handles. Supply a positive measured Time step, a nonnegative step count and a gravitational constant in Length cubed per Mass per Time squared. The integrator applies a gravitational velocity update followed by a position update. The outer result contains the initial snapshot followed by one snapshot per step. Each snapshot preserves input particle order and contains records with measured mass and three-component position and velocity. The input handles are unchanged. The adapter checks the pair-step work and encoded history budgets before integration. If evolution produces a nonfinite state, the call returns an error.",
        "Actual native RK4 particle motion. Force receives seconds plus a record with separately measured position and velocity; force results are Newtons. Positive mass/ordered finite times and step/callback budgets are checked, and first callback failure aborts immediately. Simulates the 3D motion of a particle under a force field. This function integrates Newton's second law (`F=ma`) for a single particle in a given force field using the fourth-order Runge-Kutta method. # Arguments * `force` - The force `(Fx, Fy, Fz)` as a function of time and the state `[x, y, z, vx, vy, vz]`. * `mass` - The mass of the particle. * `initial_pos` - The initial position `(x, y, z)`. * `initial_vel` - The initial velocity `(vx, vy, vz)`. * `t_range` - The time interval `(t_start, t_end)` for the simulation. * `num_steps` - The number of time steps. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Vec<Vec<f64>>` representing the trajectory over time, or an error string if evaluation fails. # Errors Returns an error if the integration produces a non-finite state.",
        "Native normalized Schrodinger operator -onehalfLaplacian+V, mapped to physical SI using positive mass and coordinate reference with energy=hbar^2/(mass*length^2). Potential callbacks receive physical coordinates and return energy. Eigenvectors are dimensionless discrete coefficients; no continuum wavefunction normalization is fabricated. Dense eigensystem limited256nodes. Solves the 1D time-independent Schr\u00f6dinger equation `H\u03c8 = E\u03c8` using the finite difference method. This function discretizes the 1D Schr\u00f6dinger equation on a grid, transforming it into an eigenvalue problem for the Hamiltonian matrix. The eigenvalues correspond to energy levels, and the eigenvectors correspond to the wave functions. All supplied grid points are unknowns, including the range endpoints. The stencil assumes zero values one grid spacing beyond each endpoint. Assumes `hbar = 1` and `m = 1`. # Arguments * `potential` - The potential `V(x)` as a function of the coordinates. * `range` - The spatial range `(x_min, x_max)`. * `num_points` - The number of grid points. # Returns A `Result` containing a tuple `(eigenvalues, eigenvectors_matrix)`, or an error string if the number of points is too small or evaluation fails. # Errors Returns an error if `num_points` is less than 3, if symbolic evaluation fails, or if the decomposition fails to converge.",
        "Native normalized Schrodinger operator -onehalfLaplacian+V, mapped to physical SI using positive mass and coordinate reference with energy=hbar^2/(mass*length^2). Potential callbacks receive physical coordinates and return energy. Eigenvectors are dimensionless discrete coefficients; no continuum wavefunction normalization is fabricated. Dense eigensystem limited256nodes. Solves the 2D time-independent Schr\u00f6dinger equation `H\u03c8 = E\u03c8` on a rectangular grid. This function discretizes the 2D Schr\u00f6dinger equation using a 5-point Laplacian finite-difference stencil, converting it into a large eigenvalue problem. Homogeneous Dirichlet conditions are imposed at the supplied grid boundary. Interior modes have zero boundary coefficients. The full grid shape is retained with separate boundary modes at the diagonal values `1e12 + V`. Assumes `hbar = 1` and `m = 1`. Warning: For large grids, this dense matrix approach can be very slow and memory-intensive. # Arguments * `potential_fn` - The potential `V(x,y)` as a function of the coordinates. * `ranges` - The spatial ranges `(x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max)`. * `grid` - The number of grid points `(nx, ny)` in each direction (including boundaries). Eigenvectors are columns. Grid rows vary `y` fastest. # Returns A `Result` containing a tuple `(eigenvalues, eigenvectors_matrix)`, or an error string if grid dimensions are too small or evaluation fails. # Errors Returns an error if grid dimensions are less than 3, if symbolic evaluation fails, or if the decomposition fails to converge.",
        "Native normalized Schrodinger operator -onehalfLaplacian+V, mapped to physical SI using positive mass and coordinate reference with energy=hbar^2/(mass*length^2). Potential callbacks receive physical coordinates and return energy. Eigenvectors are dimensionless discrete coefficients; no continuum wavefunction normalization is fabricated. Dense eigensystem limited256nodes. Solves the 3D time-independent Schr\u00f6dinger equation `H\u03c8 = E\u03c8` on a rectangular grid. This function discretizes the 3D Schr\u00f6dinger equation using a 7-point Laplacian finite-difference stencil, converting it into a large eigenvalue problem. Homogeneous Dirichlet conditions are imposed at the supplied grid boundary. Interior modes have zero boundary coefficients. The full grid shape is retained with separate boundary modes at the diagonal values `1e12 + V`. Assumes `hbar = 1` and `m = 1`. Warning: For large grids, this dense matrix approach can be extremely slow and memory-intensive. Consider smaller grids or sparse/iterative methods for higher performance. # Arguments * `potential_fn` - The potential `V(x,y,z)` as a function of the coordinates. * `ranges` - The spatial ranges `(x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max, z_min, z_max)`. * `grid` - The number of grid points `(nx, ny, nz)` in each direction (including boundaries). Eigenvectors are columns. Grid rows vary `z` fastest. # Returns A `Result` containing a tuple `(eigenvalues, eigenvectors_matrix)`, or an error string if grid dimensions are too small, too large, or evaluation fails. # Errors Returns an error if grid dimensions are less than 3, if the total number of points exceeds 25,000, if symbolic evaluation fails, or if the decomposition fails to converge.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Solves the 1D Heat Equation `u_t = alpha * u_xx` using the Crank-Nicolson method. The Crank-Nicolson method is a finite difference method for numerically solving the heat equation and similar partial differential equations. It is implicit, unconditionally stable, and second-order accurate in both space and time. Assumes Dirichlet boundary conditions `u(a,t)=u(b,t)=0`. # Arguments * `init_func` - A closure representing the initial condition `u(x,0)`. * `alpha` - The thermal diffusivity constant. * `range` - The spatial range `(a, b)`. * `nx` - The number of spatial grid points. * `t_range` - The time range `(t_start, t_end)`. * `nt` - The number of time steps. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Vec<Vec<f64>>` where each inner `Vec` is the solution `u` at a given time step, or an error string if input dimensions are invalid. # Errors Returns an error if `nx` < 3 or `nt` < 1.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. 2D Crank-Nicolson heat equation; 2D counterpart of [`solve_heat_equation_1d_crank_nicolson`]. `init_func(x, y)`; arrays are flat with `x` fastest. # Errors As [`solve_heat_equation_nd_crank_nicolson`].",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. 3D Crank-Nicolson heat equation; see [`solve_heat_equation_2d_crank_nicolson`]. # Errors As [`solve_heat_equation_nd_crank_nicolson`].",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Heat equation `u_t = alpha Laplacian u` on a box by Crank-Nicolson. Factorised ADI, any of 1D, 2D, 3D, with zero Dirichlet boundaries. `ranges[a]` is the extent of axis `a`, `nodes[a]` its node count (flat arrays, `x` fastest) and `init_func` receives the coordinates. Returns `nt + 1` snapshots like [`solve_heat_equation_1d_crank_nicolson`]. # Errors For a bad shape (1 to 3 axes, >= 3 nodes each), `nt < 1` or a bad range.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Solves the 1D Wave Equation `u_tt = c^2 * u_xx` using an explicit finite-difference method. This function implements a basic explicit finite-difference scheme for the 1D wave equation. It requires initial displacement and velocity profiles and applies Dirichlet boundary conditions. Stability is governed by the CFL condition (`c*dt/dx <= 1`). # Arguments * `initial_u` - Initial displacement vector (length `num_points`). * `initial_ut` - Initial velocity vector (length `num_points`). * `c` - Wave speed. * `dx` - Spatial step size (uniform). * `dt` - Time step size (must satisfy CFL condition). * `num_steps` - Number of time steps to march. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Vec<Vec<f64>>` where each inner `Vec` is the solution `u` at a given time step, or an error string if input dimensions mismatch or CFL is violated. # Errors Returns an error if initial vectors have different lengths, if `num_points` < 3, or if the CFL stability condition is violated.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. 2D wave equation `u_tt = c^2 (u_xx + u_yy)` by explicit leapfrog. Zero Dirichlet boundaries, flat arrays with `x` fastest. 2D counterpart of [`solve_wave_equation_1d`]; stable for `|c| dt sqrt(1/dx^2 + 1/dy^2) <= 1`. # Errors For inconsistent lengths or a violated CFL condition.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. 3D wave equation by explicit leapfrog; see [`solve_wave_equation_2d`]. # Errors As [`solve_wave_equation_2d`].",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Calculates the total kinetic energy of a system of particles.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The vertex colours.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The number of vertices that are joined to `v` by an arc (in either direction for an undirected graph), self-loops included once.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The label of the arc `u -> v`; `0` when there is none (or when an endpoint is out of range).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether the arc `u -> v` exists.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The in-arcs of `v` as `(tail, label)`; empty for an undirected graph.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether the arcs are directed.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The adjacency matrix, row by row.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. A graph on `n` vertices with no edges and the single colour class `0`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The number of vertices.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The out-arcs of `v` as `(head, label)`, sorted by head.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Replaces the vertex colours. Returns `false` when `colours` does not have one entry per vertex.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Sets the label of the edge `u - v` (`u -> v` for a directed graph), replacing any previous label; a label of `0` removes the edge. For an undirected graph the arc `v -> u` is set as well. Returns `false` when an endpoint is out of range.",
