        "Evaluates Euler\u2013Poisson\u2013Darboux Goursat data from two piecewise-linear sampled characteristics at [r, s] targets. Both axes and target coordinates share a physical dimension. The samples share the output dimension. The first samples define the common corner. Supports at most 128 knots per characteristic and 256 targets. Evaluates a Goursat solution at ordered target pairs `[r, s]` from two piecewise-linear sampled characteristics. The first axis supplies `t(\u03c1, s\u2080)` and the second supplies `t(r\u2080, \u03c3)`. Their first coordinates define `(r\u2080, s\u2080)`. Quadrature uses the sample knots as breakpoints. Targets on a characteristic return the interpolated boundary data. Independent interior targets can be evaluated concurrently and the output order matches the input order. # Errors Returns a shape error for missing or misaligned samples. Domain errors cover nonfinite or unordered data, inconsistent corner values, targets outside `r \u2265 r\u2080 > s \u2265 s\u2080` or the sampled coordinate ranges, and quadrature that cannot produce a finite, converged estimate.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Cardinal B-spline `M_n(u)` (support `(0, n)`).",
        "Construct the actual diagonal Ewald cell from three measured lengths; returns a measured3x3tensor. Diagonal cell matrix of an orthorhombic box.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Complete Ewald energy and forces (real space with minimum image requires `rcut <= min(L)/2`).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Complete Ewald energy and forces for a triclinic cell. The real-space sum runs over all periodic images within `rcut` (no minimum-image restriction, self-images included), the reciprocal part is [`ewald_reciprocal_cell`]. # Errors Fails for a singular cell.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Force callback adding the direct-Ewald reciprocal, self and background terms to an MD force evaluation.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Direct reciprocal-space Ewald sum of an orthorhombic box with all wave vectors `|n_a| <= kmax`, plus self and background terms. See [`ewald_reciprocal_cell`] for general cells.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Direct reciprocal-space Ewald sum for a general triclinic cell. The rows of `h` are lattice vectors. Returns energy, forces and the reciprocal virial, including self and neutralising terms in the energy. Small fixed-cell plans are retained for repeated evaluations. # Errors Invalid cell or particle data, or an unrepresentable result.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Force callback adding the P3M reciprocal (optimal influence function), self and background terms.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. P3M reciprocal-space energy, forces and virial with the **optimal (Hockney-Eastwood) influence function** for a general triclinic cell. Charges are assigned with cardinal B-splines of even `order` on a mesh in fractional coordinates; the influence function minimises the rms force error over all charge configurations for `ik` differentiation, `G(k) = sum_m U^2(k_m) (k . k_m) R(k_m) / (k^2 [sum_m U^2(k_m)]^2)` with `R(k) = 4 pi exp(-k^2/4 alpha^2)/k^2`, `U` the transform of the assignment function and the sum running over the `(2 n_alias + 1)^3` aliases `k_m = k + m K b`. The field is obtained by `ik` differentiation of the mesh potential (three inverse FFTs) and interpolated with the assignment function; the virial uses the analytic Ewald reciprocal form with the optimal `G` (exact up to the aliasing error). The self and neutralising-background terms are included. # Errors Fails for a singular cell, an odd or too small spline order, or a mesh smaller than the order.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Force callback adding the PME reciprocal, self and background terms.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Smooth PME reciprocal energy, forces and virial (plus self/background terms) of an orthorhombic box. See [`pme_reciprocal_cell`] for general cells. `grid` are the mesh sizes (any size, powers of two are fastest), `order` the (even) B-spline order.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Smooth particle-mesh Ewald for a general triclinic cell. The cell `h` has the lattice vectors as rows. Charges are spread with cardinal B-splines on a mesh in the fractional coordinates `s = r h^-1`, the convolution is done by FFT, and the forces use the analytic spline derivatives (chain rule through `h^-1`). The structure-factor correction and the reciprocal-space influence function are those of the sheared lattice (`k = m_1 b_1 + m_2 b_2 + m_3 b_3`). # Errors Fails for a singular cell, an odd or too small spline order, or a mesh smaller than the order.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Real-space screened Coulomb energy of a pair (convenience for checks).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Reciprocal lattice vectors (rows `b_i` with `b_i . a_j = 2 pi delta_ij`) of the cell `h` whose rows are the lattice vectors. # Errors Fails for a singular cell.",
        "Computes reciprocal Ewald energy, force on each particle and the virial. Includes self and background energy. Reuse the plan across systems with the same cell and reference frame. The measured coupling constant has energy times length divided by charge squared units. Computes reciprocal forces and virial, with self and background energy. The charges correspond one-to-one to the Cartesian positions. The coupling constant scales energies, forces and virial. Fixed-order reduction keeps repeated evaluations bitwise independent of worker count. # Errors Wrong charge length, non-finite input, or unrepresentable phases or results.",
        "Prepares the direct reciprocal Ewald sum in the periodic cell and physical reference frame of a 3D particle system. The measured screening parameter has inverse length units. Retains reciprocal wave pairs for the integer cutoff, which must be from 0 to 16. Prepares all nonzero reciprocal wave pairs with `|n_i| <= kmax`. # Errors Invalid cell, screening or cutoff, unrepresentable reciprocal geometry, or inability to allocate the wave list.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the cumulative distribution function (CDF) value at `x`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the quantile (inverse CDF) at probability `p`. Returns `NaN` when `p` is outside `[0, 1]`.",
        "Checked native legacy distribution. Continuous parameters and queries retain coherent physical units; inverse CDF restores sample units and PDF has inverse sample units. Creates a new `ExponentialDist` instance. # Errors Returns an error if `rate <= 0.0`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the probability density function (PDF) value at `x`.",
        "Advance the legacy two-dimensional TM model from zero fields. The length and wave-speed references define the physical time step L/(2c). The physical source frequency is sampled at that step. Returns the measured electric field after steps 1, 6, 11 and so on, and the corresponding times. The soft source precedes the ten-cell boundary damping. Runs the two-dimensional transverse-magnetic model from zero fields. The returned arrays use shape `(width, height)` and record the electric field after time steps 1, 6, 11, and so on. The soft point source is added before the ten-cell boundary belt damps the electric field. # Panics Panics if the source lies outside the grid or its dimensions overflow addressable field storage.",
        "Advance the legacy two-dimensional TM model and return its electric field at the last requested step. The field has shape (width, height), and time is steps multiplied by L/(2c). Zero steps return the initial zero field. The final-state mode admits one-cell axes and uses the same source, wave-speed and damping rules as the snapshot mode. Returns the electric field after the last requested time step. The model starts from zero fields and records only the final array in `(width, height)` order. Zero steps return the unexcited field. The half-Courant update, point source and boundary damping are shared with [`run_fdtd_simulation`]. # Errors Returns an error for an out-of-grid source, oversized dimensions or a non-finite frequency.",
        "IO: call the actual legacy1D zero-initial-field FDTD NPY writer. The explicit native-normalized contract states that the NPY numeric array has the native dimensionless representation; NPY carries no unit metadata. Grid/work/path bounds precede native allocation and file IO. An example scenario that runs an FDTD simulation and saves the final state. # Errors This function will return an error if the `ez` array cannot be reshaped or if the final state cannot be written to the file.",
        "Evaluates a prepared open-boundary two-dimensional FMM with new source strengths. The positive source reference has the same dimension as the strengths. The field has one two-component row per original position, with units of source strength divided by length. Evaluates the field in input particle order with new source strengths. # Errors Rejects a wrong source count, non-finite strengths, coincident unsoftened points and fields that are outside the finite range.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Adaptive 2-D FMM: the field sum of each source strength times `(x_j - x_i) / (|x_j - x_i|^2 + eps^2)`, with self excluded. Expansions use the unsoftened kernel `1/z`. Interactions closer than `eps / sqrt(theta^(order + 1))` are summed directly so the softening error is bounded by the same scale as the truncation error. Softening comparable to the domain extent can therefore require a quadratic sum. Use [`PreparedFmm2d`] to evaluate new source strengths at fixed positions. # Errors Rejects empty, mismatched or non-finite input, invalid options, coincident unsoftened points and fields that are outside the finite range.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Adaptive 3-D FMM for the potential `sum s_j / r` and its gradient field. The P2M, near-field, M2L and L2P passes are parallel over nodes/leaves (each owns its output, so the result is bit-identical to the serial sweep); M2M and L2L are level-sequential and stay serial. # Errors Fails for empty input, length mismatch or `theta` outside `(0, 1)`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Periodic 3-D FMM in a triclinic cell. Evaluates the potential `sum s_j / r` and field `sum s_j (x_j - x_i)/r^3` of charges in the cell `h` (rows are the lattice vectors) with tinfoil boundary conditions and a neutralising background. The central cell and its `(2w+1)^3 - 1` nearest images are summed by the adaptive tree code ([`fmm3d`], Plummer softening `opts.fmm.eps` included); the rest of the lattice enters through the Taylor expansion (degree `2 far_half_order`) of the regularised Ewald lattice potential about the cell centre, combined with the cell's multipole moments. Positions are wrapped into the cell. Self-image interactions are included, the particle's own `n = 0` image is not. # Errors Fails for empty input, mismatched lengths, a singular cell, or invalid FMM options.",
        "Returns the number of source values accepted by a prepared open-boundary two-dimensional FMM. Number of source strengths expected per evaluation.",
        "Prepares an open-boundary two-dimensional FMM on fixed measured positions. The options set expansion order, leaf size, opening angle and measured softening. The retained interaction list has a bounded capacity. Prepares the input positions and the expansion and softening options. # Errors Rejects empty or non-finite positions and invalid expansion options.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Gets the escape time at a specific pixel",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Creates a new `FractalData` with given dimensions",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Sets the escape time at a specific pixel",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the cumulative distribution function (CDF) value at `x`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the quantile (inverse CDF) at probability `p`. Returns `NaN` when `p` is outside `[0, 1]`.",
        "Checked native legacy distribution. Continuous parameters and queries retain coherent physical units; inverse CDF restores sample units and PDF has inverse sample units. Creates a new `GammaDist` instance. # Errors Returns an error if `shape <= 0.0` or `rate <= 0.0`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the probability density function (PDF) value at `x`.",
        "Evaluates the equatorial Schwarzschild effective potential for the retained mass. Supply a positive radius and specific angular momentum, both as geometric lengths in a physical frame. Returns the dimensionless potential \u2212M/r + (1/2 \u2212 M/r)(L/r)^2. The scaled calculation preserves finite ratios even when individual length powers fall outside Float. Invalid units, nonpositive radius and unrepresentable results produce errors.",
        "Prepare a future-directed equatorial timelike Kerr\u2013Newman geodesic. Spin and charge are geometric lengths in a physical frame. Set nonpositive_energy for the second branch available for compatible states inside the stationary limit and outside the horizon. Use the relativity state operator to read energy, angular momentum and the initial state. Prepares a timelike equatorial Kerr\u2013Newman geodesic in geometric units. The proper-time state is `[r, dr/d\u03c4, \u03c6, d\u03c6/d\u03c4]`. Normalization selects a root with `dt/d\u03c4 > 0` in Boyer\u2013Lindquist coordinates and converts it to the conserved energy per unit mass, angular momentum and radial momentum used by the Hamiltonian integrator. The radius must be outside the outer horizon when a horizon exists. The nonnegative branch includes the stationary-limit surface. Within the ergoregion, a compatible spatial velocity can give two future-directed roots, one for each sign of the Killing energy. The branches coincide when the discriminant vanishes. The nonpositive branch is available only where the stationary Killing vector is spacelike. # Errors Returns an error for nonfinite inputs, a radius on or inside the outer horizon, or a state without a representable future-directed root on the selected branch.",
        "IO: actual native reduced-unit Schwarzschild orbit demonstration writes CSV files in the chosen directory. The explicit shared step/RHS budget is inherited by every native Rayon orbit worker and returns a checked error on exhaustion. An example scenario that simulates several types of orbits around a black hole. This function sets up and runs simulations for: - A stable, precessing orbit. - A plunging orbit (where the particle falls into the black hole). - A fast timelike approach, saved under the historical name `photon_orbit`. The results are saved to `.csv` files for external visualization. # Errors This function will return an error if it fails to create the output directory. The files `orbit_<name>.csv` are written into `output_dir`, which is created if missing; a file that cannot be created is skipped.",
        "Prepare equatorial geodesic parameters. In a physical frame, mass and radius have length units, radial velocity has length per time, angular velocity is inverse time, and simulation times are seconds. Positive radius is checked here. The selected spacetime determines the horizon at initialization or integration.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Runs a geodesic simulation around a Schwarzschild black hole. Delegates to [`run_kerr_newman_geodesic`] (`a = Q = 0`): the symplectic implicit-midpoint engine of [`crate::sim::relativity`]. If the engine cannot start from the given state it falls back to the adaptive Dormand-Prince 5(4) integration of the reduced Schwarzschild equations. # Arguments * `params` - A reference to `GeodesicParameters` containing the black hole mass, initial state of the particle, total proper time, and initial time step. # Returns A `Vec` of `(f64, f64)` tuples, where each tuple is an `(x, y)` coordinate in Cartesian space, representing the simulated orbit.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Adaptive Dormand-Prince 5(4) integration of the reduced Schwarzschild equations (the legacy path, kept as an independent reference and as the fallback of [`run_geodesic_simulation`]). An invalid proper-time target or initial step returns only the initial Cartesian position.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Equatorial timelike Kerr-Newman geodesic on the symplectic engine. The spacetime has (mass `params.black_hole_mass`, spin parameter `spin = a`, charge `charge = Q`, geometric units) integrated for the proper time `params.proper_time_end` with the symplectic implicit-midpoint engine of [`crate::sim::relativity`] (step `params.initial_dt`, mass-shell projection). This uses the [`EquatorialEnergyBranch::Nonnegative`] branch. The initial state is `[r, dr/dtau, phi, dphi/dtau]` as in [`GeodesicParameters`]. Returns the Cartesian path `(r cos phi, r sin phi)`; the run stops at the horizon (`r <= 1.001 r_+`) or on escape. # Errors Fails when the initial data are not a valid timelike state outside the horizon or the implicit step does not converge.",
        "Return a two-column equatorial Cartesian orbit in the frame's length units. Retain the initial point, every specified positive number of steps, and the final or termination point. Sampling leaves the implicit-step budget and spacetime termination rules unchanged. nonpositive_energy requests the second future-directed branch. Runs an equatorial timelike Kerr\u2013Newman geodesic and retains fewer positions. Returns the initial Cartesian position, every `retain_every` integration steps, and the final reached position, including an event between samples. A stride of one gives the full trajectory. The stride changes output storage only. The same initial step, proper-time target, horizon and escape tests, and model step limit apply at every stride. The selected energy branch has the meaning of [`equatorial_timelike_geodesic`]. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid initial state, a nonfinite or negative proper-time target, a nonfinite or nonpositive initial step, or a failed implicit integration step.",
        "Native finite-difference differential geometry over homogeneous physical coordinates and a typed symmetric metric callback. Explicit positive coordinate/metric references normalize derivative step scales; metric/connection/curvature result dimensions are restored. Dimension<=6 with first-failure and callback budget checks. Christoffel symbols of the second kind `\u0393^k_{ij}` at `point`, computed as `\u00bd g^{km} (\u2202_j g_{mi} + \u2202_i g_{mj} - \u2202_m g_{ij})`. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid metric sample, a singular metric at `point`, or an unrepresentable stencil, inverse or result.",
        "Native finite-difference differential geometry over homogeneous physical coordinates and a typed symmetric metric callback. Explicit positive coordinate/metric references normalize derivative step scales; metric/connection/curvature result dimensions are restored. Dimension<=6 with first-failure and callback budget checks. Evaluates the metric at `point`, retaining the callback's entries. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite coordinates or a non-finite, asymmetric or incorrectly shaped metric. Symmetry is checked relative to the largest absolute metric entry.",
        "Computes the covariant Ricci tensor and scalar curvature with one finite-difference metric stencil and factorization. Coordinates use a common dimension in one to six dimensions. With coordinate dimension X and metric dimension G, tensor entries have X^-2 and the scalar has X^-2/G. Use positive coordinate and metric references and supply a callback evaluation budget. Computes the Ricci tensor and scalar with one curvature stencil and one factorization of the metric at `point`. ``` use rssn::kernels::differential_geometry::ricci_curvature; let flat = |_: &[f64]| vec![vec![1.0, 0.0], vec![0.0, 1.0]]; let curvature = ricci_curvature(flat, &[0.0, 0.0])?; assert_eq!(curvature.scalar, 0.0); ``` # Errors Returns an error for an invalid or singular metric at a connection stencil point, or an unrepresentable stencil or result.",
        "Native finite-difference differential geometry over homogeneous physical coordinates and a typed symmetric metric callback. Explicit positive coordinate/metric references normalize derivative step scales; metric/connection/curvature result dimensions are restored. Dimension<=6 with first-failure and callback budget checks. Ricci scalar `R = g^{\u03bc\u03bd} R_{\u03bc\u03bd}` at `point`. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid or singular metric at a connection stencil point, or an unrepresentable stencil or result.",
        "Native finite-difference differential geometry over homogeneous physical coordinates and a typed symmetric metric callback. Explicit positive coordinate/metric references normalize derivative step scales; metric/connection/curvature result dimensions are restored. Dimension<=6 with first-failure and callback budget checks. Ricci tensor `R_{\u03c3\u03bd} = R^\u03bc_{\u03c3\u03bc\u03bd}` at `point`. The derivative and product terms are contracted as they are evaluated. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid or singular metric at a connection stencil point, or an unrepresentable stencil or result.",
        "Native finite-difference differential geometry over homogeneous physical coordinates and a typed symmetric metric callback. Explicit positive coordinate/metric references normalize derivative step scales; metric/connection/curvature result dimensions are restored. Dimension<=6 with first-failure and callback budget checks. Riemann curvature tensor `R^\u03c1_{\u03c3\u03bc\u03bd}` at `point`. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid or singular metric at a connection stencil point, or an unrepresentable stencil or result.",
        "Return the normalized two-dimensional Gross\u2013Pitaevskii density in inverse square length on a grid of positive extents. Planck\u2019s reduced constant, mass and lengths use SI. Imaginary step, contact energy times length squared and trap energy per length squared are scaled in the same reference frame. Zero steps or zero imaginary step return the normalized initial density. Evolves the 2D GPE for a fixed number of imaginary-time steps on a periodic box. The ground-state probability density `|psi|^2` has rows along `y` and columns along `x`, with physical normalization `sum(|psi|^2) dx dy = 1`. Zero steps or a zero time increment return the normalized initial profile. # Errors Returns an error for an empty or oversized grid, unrepresentable spacing, non-positive box lengths, negative imaginary time, non-finite coefficients, or a propagated state whose normalized density cannot be represented.",
        "IO: execute the original reduced-unit128x128,500-step GPE demonstration and write its NPY density to the requested directory. Explicit budget acknowledgement is required; no physical interpretation is inferred for hard-coded model constants. An example scenario that finds the ground state of a BEC, which may contain a vortex. # Errors This function will return an error if the GPE ground state finder fails or if the final density cannot be written to the NPY file. The density is saved as `gpe_vortex_state.npy` inside `output_dir`, which is created if missing.",
        "Native finite-difference calculus with physical reference scales: point,coordinate_scale,value_scale,max_evaluations,[partial index or Jacobian output count],callback. Independent D/E units; derivative E/D, Hessian/Laplacian E/D^2. References are positive. Shared callback errors and finite evaluation budgets are preserved. Gradient of `f` at `point`.",
        "Appends one directed arc with a finite weight in the graph\u2019s declared physical dimension. Loops and parallel arcs are retained. Adds a directed edge with a weight between two nodes. # Arguments * `u` - The index of the source node. * `v` - The index of the destination node. * `weight` - The weight of the edge. The caller must supply a valid destination and a weight in the domain of each algorithm it will call. [`Self::from_edges`] validates input. # Panics Panics when `u` is outside the vertex range.",
        "Returns one vertex\u2019s outgoing endpoints and matching coherent weights, in insertion order. Returns an immutable slice of the neighbors and edge weights for a given node. # Arguments * `u` - The index of the node. # Returns A slice of `(usize, f64)` tuples, where each tuple is `(neighbor_index, edge_weight)`.",
        "Returns shortest distances and optional predecessors from one source. Distances retain the graph\u2019s edge units and are infinite for unreachable vertices. A reachable negative cycle is rejected.",
        "Returns unweighted distance by vertex from the start vertex. Unreachable vertices have the native usize maximum sentinel. Performs a Breadth-First Search (BFS) starting from a node. Returns the unweighted shortest path distances from the start node to all other reachable nodes. Returns `usize::MAX` for unreachable nodes. # Panics Panics on an out-of-range start vertex or encountered edge endpoint.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The canonical code: the `n` vertex colours in canonical order, followed by the `n * n` adjacency matrix rows in canonical order. For graphs of the same directedness, equality of exact canonical codes is equivalent to isomorphism.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The generators of the automorphism group found by the search. They generate the full group when [`is_exact`](Self::is_exact) holds, and a subgroup of it otherwise.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The order of the automorphism group: `n!` for the complete graph, `2n` for the cycle, `120` for the Petersen graph.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether the search finished within its budget, that is, whether the code is guaranteed to be the canonical one and the group the full automorphism group.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Searches for the canonical labelling of `graph` and its automorphism group. Returns the labelling with the smallest code found within `budget` nodes, the generators and orbits of the automorphism group found on the way, and whether the search was exact (see the module docs for the fallback). # Complexity Each node refines the partition by sorting vertex signatures until it becomes equitable. Comparing a leaf takes up to `O(n^2)`. The search visits the individualisation tree after automorphism pruning, with just one node for a graph whose colouring is already discrete.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The canonical adjacency matrix, rows in canonical order.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The number of vertices.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The number of search nodes visited.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The orbits of the automorphism group, each sorted, ordered by their smallest vertex.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The canonical vertex order: `order()[i]` is the original vertex that becomes vertex `i` of the canonical form. When the automorphism group is non-trivial the minimum code is reached by several leaves; any of their orders is returned, and all of them give the same [`code`](Self::code).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The canonical position of an original vertex, or `None` when out of range.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The initial colour shared by the vertices of the cell.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The same for in-neighbours; empty for an undirected graph.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. How many out-neighbours a vertex of the cell has in each cell, per label: `(cell index, label, count)`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The number of vertices in the cell.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The sizes of the cells, in canonical cell order.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The cells, in canonical cell order.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether the graph is directed.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether the colouring is discrete (every cell is a singleton).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The number of cells, that is, of distinct stable colours.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The number of vertices.",
        "Contracts strongly connected components into a DAG. Returns a topologically ordered component label for each input vertex and a two-column matrix of unique arcs between components. Weights are ignored.",
        "Labels an undirected graph represented by symmetric arcs. For asymmetric arcs, use the weakly or strongly connected components operator. Finds the connected components of an undirected graph stored as symmetric arcs. For directed reachability in both directions, use [`strongly_connected_components`]. For weak connectivity of asymmetric arcs, use [`weakly_connected_components`]. Returns a vector where each element corresponds to a node and contains its component ID.",
        "Returns shortest distances and optional predecessors in linear time for a directed acyclic graph. Distances retain the graph\u2019s edge units. Negative weights are supported and all directed cycles are rejected.",
        "Native Dijkstra with nonnegative coherent edge weights; infinity denotes unreachable vertices, and predecessor entries are native Options. Finite edge/path representation and allocation bounds are checked. Finds the shortest paths from a single source node to all other nodes using Dijkstra's algorithm. Dijkstra's algorithm is a greedy algorithm that solves the single-source shortest path problem for a graph with non-negative edge weights. # Arguments * `graph` - The graph to search. * `start_node` - The index of the starting node. # Returns A tuple containing: - A vector of distances from the start node to each node. - A vector of predecessors to reconstruct the shortest paths. Unreachable vertices have infinite distance and no predecessor. When costs tie, the queue visits vertices in increasing vertex order. # Panics Panics on an out-of-range start vertex or encountered edge endpoint.",
        "Returns a row-major all-pairs distance matrix in the graph\u2019s edge units. Unreachable vertices have infinite distance. Negative cycles are rejected. Solves the all-pairs shortest path problem using Floyd\u2013Warshall. The result is a row-major distance matrix. Element `i * n + j` is the distance from `i` to `j`, or positive infinity when `j` is unreachable. A negative diagonal entry detects a negative cycle, in which case distances that can traverse a cycle are undefined. # Panics Panics on an out-of-range edge endpoint or a matrix too large to allocate.",
        "Constructs a directed graph from a two-column endpoint matrix and an equally long weight vector. The vector declares coherent edge units. Loops and parallel arcs are retained in input order.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether two coloured graphs are isomorphic. Returns `None` when the budget ran out before either search finished: the graphs then remain undecided. A `Some(false)` answer may also be reached from the refinement certificate alone, which is cheaper.",
        "Computes a maximum source-to-sink flow and returns its value in capacity units and a Boolean mask for the source side of a minimum cut. Distinct terminals and nonnegative capacities are required.",
        "Computes a minimum spanning forest with Prim\u2019s algorithm. The input contains symmetric, equally weighted arcs and the result retains their physical dimension. Computes a minimum spanning forest using Prim's algorithm. The input is an undirected graph represented by equally weighted arcs in both directions. Every selected arc is included in both directions in the result. Disconnected vertices and components are retained.",
        "Returns the unweighted distance of each vertex from its nearest source, following directed arcs. An unreachable vertex has no distance. Duplicate sources are allowed and weights are ignored.",
        "Construct a directed weighted graph with an explicit positive reference quantity declaring edge-weight units. Edge values and all shortest-path distances use coherent units. Creates a new graph with a specified number of nodes. The graph is initialized with no edges. # Arguments * `num_nodes` - The total number of nodes in the graph. # Returns A new `Graph` instance.",
        "Returns the number of directed arcs, counting loops and parallel arcs. Returns the number of directed arcs, including loops and parallel arcs.",
        "Returns the number of vertices in the weighted graph. Returns the total number of nodes in the graph.",
        "Returns dimensionless PageRank scores. Each outgoing arc counts equally and dangling mass is distributed uniformly. Work is bounded by the graph size and iteration count. Computes the `PageRank` of the nodes in the graph. # Arguments * `graph` - The graph. * `damping_factor` - The probability of continuing following links (usually 0.85). * `tolerance` - The convergence tolerance. * `max_iter` - The maximum number of iterations. # Returns A vector of scores summing to 1, or an empty vector for an empty graph. Parallel arcs each contribute an equal share, and weights are ignored. # Panics Panics on an encountered out-of-range edge endpoint.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The image of `v`, or `None` when `v` is not a point.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. `self \u2218 other` (apply `other` first), or `None` on a length mismatch.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The non-trivial cycles, each starting at its smallest point.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. A permutation from its image list. Returns `None` unless `image` is a permutation of `0..image.len()`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The identity of `n` points.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The image list: entry `i` is the image of `i`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The inverse permutation.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether the permutation acts on no point at all.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether every point is fixed.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The number of points moved by the permutation.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Refines a coloured graph to its coarsest equitable partition: the 1-dimensional Weisfeiler\u2013Leman colouring. The result depends on the isomorphism class only, so two graphs whose certificates differ are certainly not isomorphic. Equal certificates prove nothing beyond the first-order neighbourhood structure of the graph.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The cell index of every vertex.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The sizes of the cells, in canonical cell order.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The cells, in canonical order.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The isomorphism-invariant certificate of the partition.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether the graph the partition came from is directed.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether every cell is a singleton.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The number of cells.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The number of refinement rounds performed.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Checks the result against a graph: the cells partition the vertices, each cell has one colour, and each cell is equitable \u2014 every vertex of a cell has the same neighbour counts (per label) into every cell. Returns `false` when the stated partition conditions fail for `graph`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The default budget for a graph on `n` vertices: `2_000 + 20 n` nodes. The constant term covers small graphs with large automorphism groups (where the pruning makes the tree quadratic), the linear term covers graphs whose colouring stays coarse.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The maximum number of search nodes.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. A budget of `max_nodes` search nodes.",
        "Returns a dense strongly connected component label per vertex, numbered in topological order. Weights are ignored. Labels vertices by strongly connected component in linear time. Vertices with the same label can reach each other along directed edges. Labels are dense and topological: every edge crossing components points from a smaller label to a larger one. Edge weights are ignored. The traversal uses explicit stacks so deep paths do not grow the call stack. # Errors Reports an invalid edge endpoint.",
        "Returns a vertex ordering in which every source precedes its targets. A directed cycle is rejected and weights are ignored. Returns a topological ordering of the vertices using Kahn's algorithm. An edge from `u` to `v` puts `u` before `v`. Parallel edges are counted separately, all vertices are returned, and edge weights are ignored. The traversal takes linear time in the number of vertices and edges. # Errors Reports an invalid edge endpoint or a directed cycle, including a self-loop.",
        "Finds bridges and articulation points in an undirected multigraph. Each row of the two-column endpoint matrix represents one edge. Returns bridge endpoints in depth-first completion order and articulation vertices in vertex order.",
        "Returns a dense component label per vertex after ignoring arc directions and weights. Components are ordered by their smallest vertex.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether the 1-dimensional Weisfeiler\u2013Leman colourings of two graphs agree. A necessary condition for isomorphism, and an exact test on trees; on random and on most structured inputs it decides as well, but it is fooled by every pair of strongly regular graphs with the same parameters (the 4x4 rook graph and the Shrikhande graph, for instance), by which time only [`canonical_labelling`] can tell them apart.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Computes the angle between two vectors in radians.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Computes the barycentric coordinates of a point in a triangle. Returns (u, v, w) where point = u*v0 + v*v1 + w*v2.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Evaluates a cubic Bezier curve at parameter t. # Arguments * `p0`, `p1`, `p2`, `p3` - Control points * `t` - Parameter in [0, 1]",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Evaluates a quadratic Bezier curve at parameter t. # Arguments * `p0`, `p1`, `p2` - Control points * `t` - Parameter in [0, 1]",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Evaluates a Catmull-Rom spline at parameter t. The spline passes through p1 and p2, with p0 and p3 as guide points.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Clamps color values to [0, 1].",
