        "Evaluate the diagonal of the Hessian of a scalar callback at a measured point, returning one value per coordinate in output units divided by squared coordinate units. The common arguments are point, positive coordinate reference, positive output reference and evaluation budget. The with_steps variant takes a positive step per coordinate, in coordinate units, before the callback. Computes the second partial derivatives along the coordinate axes. Uses one central evaluation and two evaluations per axis on an interior stencil. At a finite floating-point boundary it uses three inward samples. An empty point yields an empty diagonal.",
        "Evaluate the diagonal of the Hessian of a scalar callback at a measured point, returning one value per coordinate in output units divided by squared coordinate units. The common arguments are point, positive coordinate reference, positive output reference and evaluation budget. The with_steps variant takes a positive step per coordinate, in coordinate units, before the callback. Computes the Hessian diagonal with a separate nominal step for each axis. If a step would leave a coordinate unchanged, it uses the adjacent representable coordinate and the actual distance in the difference. # Errors Returns [`NumericError::ShapeMismatch`] for a mismatched step count and [`NumericError::Domain`] for nonfinite coordinates or unusable steps.",
        "Native finite-difference calculus with physical reference scales: point,coordinate_scale,value_scale,max_evaluations,[partial index or Jacobian output count],callback. Independent D/E units; derivative E/D, Hessian/Laplacian E/D^2. References are positive. Shared callback errors and finite evaluation budgets are preserved. Hessian of `f` at `point`, in row-major order. Uses central differences, with an inward stencil for diagonal entries at a finite floating-point boundary. The result is symmetric.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The matrix (over `L`'s `k`-simplices, columns `K`'s) of the chain map induced by a simplicial map.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The matrix of `f_*: H_k(K; Q) -> H_k(L; Q)` in the homology bases chosen by this module (rows: `L`, columns: `K`).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether the vertex map sends every simplex of `k` onto a simplex of `l`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Certifies the finite regular endpoints of a homotopy run against the original `system` (Smale's alpha test plus pairwise ball disjointness).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Certifies the finite endpoints of a homotopy run rigorously. Endpoints at infinity, ill-conditioned endpoints and the centroids of clusters of close roots are reported as uncertified; a certified endpoint is a proved simple zero.",
        "Native rigorous homotopy node in explicitly normalized homogeneous projective coordinates. Patch coefficients, gamma and complex continuation time are dimensionless; this proof object retains dimensionless projective coordinates. Certifies one node of a homotopy path: proves that `H(\u00b7, t)` has exactly one zero in a small box around the homogeneous point `x`. `homogenised` and `degrees` are the data of [`crate::kernels::homotopy::homotopy_solve`]'s problem; `x` has `n + 1` entries. Used by the tracker when [`crate::kernels::homotopy::HomotopyOptions::certify_steps`] is set.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Certifies a simple zero of a real-coefficient system near `guess`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Certifies a simple zero near `guess`, trying a ladder of box sizes. Returns `None` when no box in the ladder passes the Krawczyk test (a multiple zero, a singular Jacobian, or an endpoint that is not accurate enough to inflate a certified box around).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether two certified boxes are disjoint (some component separates them, which proves that they cannot contain a common point).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Runs [`homotopy_solve`] and certifies the endpoints. Certification is [`certify_endpoints`]. If the run is in plain `f64` and not every path ends in a certified, distinct zero, the whole solve is repeated with [`crate::kernels::homotopy::Precision::Adaptive`] and the more complete result is returned. # Errors As [`homotopy_solve`].",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Runs [`homotopy_solve`] and certifies the endpoints rigorously. Tracking steps are certified as well ([`HomotopyOptions::certify_steps`] is turned on for the run, which the double-precision arithmetic supports). Runs with [`Precision::Double`] escalate to [`Precision::Adaptive`] when the rigorous report is incomplete and the escalated run certifies more distinct zeros. # Errors As [`homotopy_solve`].",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The homogenised system `H(X, t) = 0` of the total-degree homotopy at a complex `t`, as complex-coefficient terms in the `n + 1` homogeneous variables. `H_i = \u03b3 (1 - t)(X_{i+1}^{d_i} - X_0^{d_i}) + t f_i(X)` for `i = 0..n`, plus the patch equation `-1 + \u03a3_k a_k X_k` (the same rows as [`crate::kernels::homotopy`] evaluates). `None` for a malformed system.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Smale's `beta`, `gamma` and `alpha` for the square polynomial system `system` at the point `x` (one complex number per variable). Returns `None` for malformed inputs, a singular Jacobian, or norms that cannot be computed within the numerical range.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Solves a square polynomial system by total-degree homotopy continuation. Solves the square polynomial system `system[i] = 0` (terms of equation `i`, exponent vectors with one entry per variable) by total-degree homotopy continuation in projective coordinates with a Cauchy endgame and path-jumping protection; see the module documentation. One [`PathEnd`] is returned per start solution (the product of the degrees). Regular finite endpoints are Newton-polished; singular finite endpoints and endpoints at infinity are classified with their winding number. [`HomotopyOptions::precision`] selects double, double-double or adaptive extended-precision tracking. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for an empty system, exponent vectors of the wrong length, nonfinite coefficients, equations without a positive declared degree, or a product of equation degrees above 100 000.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Verifies a zero of a real-coefficient system near `guess`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Verifies a zero near `guess` inside the box of relative half-width `relative` (a complex-coefficient system). Returns the contracted enclosure when the Krawczyk test proves that the box contains exactly one zero; `None` is inconclusive.",
