        "Typed native LIR adapter. Machine instructions use explicit normalized IEEE values, checked register/block/type references and bounded native interpreter steps. Checks types, arities and references. # Errors The first problem found.",
        "Typed native LIR adapter. Machine instructions use explicit normalized IEEE values, checked register/block/type references and bounded native interpreter steps. A new, empty block.",
        "Typed native LIR adapter. Machine instructions use explicit normalized IEEE values, checked register/block/type references and bounded native interpreter steps. Runs `func` in the reference interpreter with at most `max_steps` instructions (a runaway loop bails with [`BAIL_STEPS`]). # Errors The module does not verify, or the arguments do not match the function's parameters, or a read-only slice is passed to a function that writes.",
        "Typed native LIR adapter. Machine instructions use explicit normalized IEEE values, checked register/block/type references and bounded native interpreter steps. The returned values, if the call did not bail.",
        "Typed native LIR adapter. Machine instructions use explicit normalized IEEE values, checked register/block/type references and bounded native interpreter steps. Parameter `i` of the function.",
        "Typed native LIR adapter. Machine instructions use explicit normalized IEEE values, checked register/block/type references and bounded native interpreter steps. A fresh register of type `ty`.",
        "Typed native LIR adapter. Machine instructions use explicit normalized IEEE values, checked register/block/type references and bounded native interpreter steps. Ends the current block with a return.",
        "Typed native LIR adapter. Machine instructions use explicit normalized IEEE values, checked register/block/type references and bounded native interpreter steps. Continues emitting into `block`.",
        "Typed native LIR adapter. Machine instructions use explicit normalized IEEE values, checked register/block/type references and bounded native interpreter steps. The type of a register.",
        "Typed native LIR adapter. Machine instructions use explicit normalized IEEE values, checked register/block/type references and bounded native interpreter steps. The LIR type of the value.",
        "Typed native LIR adapter. Machine instructions use explicit normalized IEEE values, checked register/block/type references and bounded native interpreter steps. Which parameters of `func` may be stored into, by `func` itself or by anything it calls: a slice argument passed for such a parameter must be writable, every other one may be read-only. Slices cannot be created in LIR, only passed along, so the analysis follows each slice register back to the parameters it may alias (through copies, block parameters and call arguments) and iterates over the whole module to a fixpoint (calls may recurse).",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Applies periodic boundary conditions to a position.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Berendsen thermostat. Gently couples the system to a heat bath. # Arguments * `particles` - System particles * `target_temp` - Target temperature * `tau` - Coupling time constant * `dt` - Time step",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Calculates the center of mass of the system. # Errors Returns an error if the particle list is empty.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Coulomb (electrostatic) potential. V(r) = `k_e` * q1 * q2 / r # Arguments * `p1`, `p2` - The two particles (with charge fields) * `k_coulomb` - Coulomb constant (in appropriate units) # Errors Returns an error if the particles have different dimensions.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Creates a simple cubic lattice of particles.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Creates an FCC (face-centered cubic) lattice of particles.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. A stream seeded with `seed`.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. A standard normal deviate.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Harmonic (spring) potential. V(r) = 0.5 * k * (r - r0)\u00b2 # Arguments * `p1`, `p2` - The two particles * `k` - Spring constant * `r0` - Equilibrium distance # Errors Returns an error if the particles have different dimensions.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Initializes particle velocities from Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Langevin dynamics with the BAOAB splitting (Leimkuhler\u2013Matthews): half kick (B), half drift (A), exact Ornstein\u2013Uhlenbeck velocity update with friction `gamma` at temperature `kt` (O), half drift, half kick. Samples the canonical ensemble with configurational error `O(dt\u00b2)`. # Errors Propagates errors of the force calculation.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. The Nos\u00e9\u2013Hoover thermostat: the friction `\u03be` obeys `Q d\u03be/dt = \u03a3 m v\u00b2 - N_f kT`, a deterministic extended system whose invariant `H + Q \u03be\u00b2/2 + N_f kT s` (with `ds/dt = \u03be`) is conserved. Velocity-Verlet with the friction applied as an exact exponential scaling in half steps. Returns the final `(\u03be, s)`. # Errors Propagates errors of the force calculation.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Integrates the equations of motion for a system of particles using the Velocity Verlet algorithm. The Velocity Verlet algorithm is a popular numerical integration scheme for molecular dynamics simulations. It is time-reversible and preserves phase space volume, making it suitable for long-term simulations. # Arguments * `particles` - A mutable vector of `Particle`s. * `dt` - The time step. * `num_steps` - The number of simulation steps. * `force_calculator` - A closure that computes the total force on each particle. It takes `&mut Vec<Particle>` and returns `Result<(), String>`. # Returns A `Vec<Vec<Particle>>` representing the trajectory of the particles over time. # Errors Returns an error if the force calculator fails or if vector operations fail due to dimension mismatch.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Computes the Lennard-Jones potential and force between two particles. The Lennard-Jones potential is a simple mathematical model that describes the interaction between a pair of neutral atoms or molecules. It has a repulsive term at short distances and an attractive term at long distances. # Arguments * `p1`, `p2` - The two particles. * `epsilon` - The depth of the potential well. * `sigma` - The finite distance at which the inter-particle potential is zero. # Returns A tuple `(potential, force_on_p1)` where `potential` is the scalar potential energy and `force_on_p1` is the force vector acting on `p1` due to `p2`. # Errors Returns an error if the particles have different dimensions.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Calculates mean square displacement. MSD(t) = <|r(t) - r(0)|\u00b2>",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Applies minimum image convention for distance calculation.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Morse potential for diatomic molecules. V(r) = De * (1 - exp(-a(r - re)))\u00b2 # Arguments * `p1`, `p2` - The two particles * `de` - Dissociation energy * `a` - Controls the width of the potential well * `re` - Equilibrium bond distance Separations below `1e-9` return infinite energy and a zero force vector. [`morse_radial`] evaluates the scalar curve at all nonnegative distances. # Errors Returns an error if the particles have different dimensions.",
        "Evaluate the Morse energy and signed radial force in a checked reference frame. Energy is zero at the equilibrium distance. Positive force points toward increasing separation. A zero separation returns the one-sided radial force. Returns the Morse energy and the force along increasing distance. The minimum is zero at `r = re`. At `r = 0` the scalar force is the one-sided radial derivative with a negative sign. There is no direction to assign to that force at coincident particle positions. # Errors The distance, depth and equilibrium distance must be finite and nonnegative. The inverse length `a` must be finite and positive. Returns an error when the energy or radial force exceeds finite range.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work.",
        "Explicit reduced-unit native frame; all numeric values are dimensionless and kB equals one.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Distance to another particle # Errors Returns an error if the particles have different dimensions.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Returns the kinetic energy of the particle: KE = 0.5 * m * v\u00b2",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Returns the momentum of the particle: p = m * v",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Creates a new particle with default charge and type.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Returns the speed (magnitude of velocity)",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Creates a new particle with charge.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Calculates instantaneous pressure using the virial theorem. P = (N * `k_B` * T + virial) / V",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Calculates the radial distribution function g(r). # Arguments * `particles` - System particles * `box_size` - Box dimensions * `num_bins` - Number of histogram bins * `r_max` - Maximum distance to consider # Returns (`r_values`, `g_r`) where `r_values` are bin centers and `g_r` are g(r) values",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Removes center of mass velocity from the system. # Errors Returns an error if the particle list is empty.",
        "Run the real native MD engine. Physical thermal targets are Kelvin in SI frames and reduced kBT in normalized frames; LJ energy/length, time, box, count and work bounds are validated. Runs Lennard-Jones MD for a runtime dimension on the const-generic engine. Parameters `(epsilon, sigma, r_cut)` give a shifted-force potential in a periodic box on the const-generic engine ([`crate::sim::md_engine::MdSimulation`]) for a **runtime** dimension of 1, 2 or 3, and writes positions, velocities and forces back into `particles`. Returns the final total energy. # Errors Fails for dimensions other than 1-3, mismatching vector lengths or engine errors.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Soft-sphere potential for avoiding particle overlap. V(r) = \u03b5 * (\u03c3/r)^n for r < \u03c3, 0 otherwise # Errors Returns an error if the particles have different dimensions.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Calculates the temperature from kinetic energy. T = 2 * KE / (dim * N * `k_B`) In reduced units with `k_B` = 1: T = 2 * KE / (dim * N)",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Calculates the total kinetic energy of the system.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Calculates the total momentum of the system. # Errors Returns an error if the particle list is empty.",
        "Physical particle adapter. Coordinates, velocity, mass, charge, force, energy and thermal targets use checked reference units; count, shape and work bounds precede native work. Velocity rescaling thermostat. Rescales velocities to achieve target temperature.",
        "Recompute density, pressure and forces at the current positions of a reduced-unit legacy SPH system. Call the integration operator separately to advance the particles. Updates density, pressure and force for the current positions and velocities. Reuses one neighborhood search for both interaction sweeps.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Computes the density and pressure of each particle.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Computes the forces acting on each particle.",
        "Execute the original hard-coded native demonstration in its explicitly normalized model coordinates. No physical-unit interpretation is inferred. Simulates a 2D dam break scenario using Smoothed-Particle Hydrodynamics (SPH). This function initializes a block of fluid particles (the \"dam\") and simulates its collapse and flow under gravity. It demonstrates the SPH method's ability to model fluid dynamics without a fixed mesh. # Returns A `Vec` of tuples `(x, y)` representing the final positions of the particles.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Integrates the equations of motion for each particle.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Creates a new Poly6 kernel.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Creates a new spiky kernel.",
        "Read particle positions, velocities, forces, density and pressure from the legacy SPH system in reduced units. Rows follow the original particle order. The vector fields have shape [particles,2].",
        "Legacy native educational SPH uses explicitly normalized unit-mass coefficients and3D smoothing kernels on2D sites. This adapter rejects physical quantities rather than assigning inconsistent SI force dimensions; use the general Sph<D> adapters for coherent physical models.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Updates the SPH system by one time step.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Creates a new 2D vector.",
        "Execute the original native analytic multigrid demonstration in normalized coordinates; explicit positive domain/solution references give a physical realization without reinterpreting its hard-coded coefficients. Solves a 1D Poisson problem with a known analytical solution. `-u_xx = 2` on `[0, 1]` with `u(0)=u(1)=0`. Exact solution is `u(x) = x(1-x)`. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_poisson_1d_multigrid` function encounters an error, e.g., if the grid size is invalid.",
        "Execute the original native analytic multigrid demonstration in normalized coordinates; explicit positive domain/solution references give a physical realization without reinterpreting its hard-coded coefficients. Solves a 2D Poisson problem with a known analytical solution. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `solve_poisson_2d_multigrid` function encounters an error, e.g., if the grid size is invalid.",
        "Actual native zero-Dirichlet multigrid on its unit interval/cube, mapped to an explicit physical coordinate and solution reference. RHS has solution/coordinate^2 units.1D input contains interior values;2D/3D input includes boundary nodes. Output is flattened in native x-fastest order with explicit shape. Solves the 1D Poisson equation `-u_xx = f` using the multigrid method. # Arguments * `n` - Number of interior points on the finest grid. Must be `2^k - 1`. * `f` - The right-hand side function values on the finest grid. * `num_cycles` - The number of V-cycles to perform. # Returns The solution vector `u`. # Errors This function will return an error if the grid size `n` is not of the form `2^k - 1`.",
        "Actual native zero-Dirichlet multigrid on its unit interval/cube, mapped to an explicit physical coordinate and solution reference. RHS has solution/coordinate^2 units.1D input contains interior values;2D/3D input includes boundary nodes. Output is flattened in native x-fastest order with explicit shape. Solves `-\u2207\u00b2u = f` on the unit square by running `num_cycles` multigrid V-cycles. Input and output are both flattened with x varying fastest. All boundary values are fixed to zero, and source entries on those boundaries are ignored. With zero cycles, or with a boundary-only 2\u00d72 grid, the result is all zeros. # Errors Returns an error if `n` is not of the form `2^k + 1` for a nonnegative integer `k`, or if `f` does not have exactly `n * n` entries.",
        "Actual native zero-Dirichlet multigrid on its unit interval/cube, mapped to an explicit physical coordinate and solution reference. RHS has solution/coordinate^2 units.1D input contains interior values;2D/3D input includes boundary nodes. Output is flattened in native x-fastest order with explicit shape. Solves the 3D Poisson equation `-\u0394u = f` on the unit cube. Zero Dirichlet boundary, `n` nodes per axis including the boundary, `x` fastest, `n = 2^k + 1`) with `num_cycles` multigrid V-cycles. 3D counterpart of [`solve_poisson_2d_multigrid`]; the general 1D/2D/3D entry point with any boundary conditions is [`solve_poisson_multigrid_nd`]. # Errors This function will return an error if `n` is not of the form `2^k + 1`, if `f.len() != n^3` or if the multigrid hierarchy cannot be built.",
        "Interpolate a rank-one point in axis order using an existing session-local grid. A Dynamic-element point carries each coordinate's dimension separately. A homogeneous measured point supplies one shared dimension. Every coordinate must match its axis and be inside the grid. Returns a value in the grid's stored ordinate dimension. Interpolates an in-bounds point without allocating or rescanning the grid. # Errors Returns an error if the point has the wrong dimension or any coordinate is non-finite or outside its axis.",
        "Create an immutable session-local rectilinear grid. Axes is an ordered rank-one tensor of measured rank-one axis tensors, with independent units and possibly different lengths. Values is a measured rank-one tensor laid out with the first axis varying fastest. Axes must contain at least two finite increasing nodes each. Shape, unit, and allocation bounds are checked at construction. Prepares a shared grid for calls to [`Self::evaluate`]. # Errors Returns an error if there are no axes, any axis has fewer than two finite strictly increasing nodes, the size overflows, or the values are non-finite or have the wrong length.",
        "Native covariance matrix retains squared coordinate units. The row-major `d \u00d7 d` covariance matrix. # Errors [`DistError::Empty`], [`DistError::ShapeMismatch`], or [`DistError::Domain`] on invalid parameters.",
        "Native dimension query. The dimension `d` of the distribution.",
        "Native log density relative to the constructor explicit reference measure, avoiding a logarithm of dimensioned density. The natural logarithm of the probability density at `x`. # Errors [`DistError::Empty`] if the distribution has dimension 0, [`DistError::ShapeMismatch`] if `x` or `cov` has the wrong length, [`DistError::Domain`] if `Dirichlet` parameters are non-positive, or [`DistError::NotPositiveDefinite`] if `cov` is not positive definite.",
        "Native mean vector retains coordinate units. The mean vector. # Errors [`DistError::Empty`] or [`DistError::Domain`] on invalid parameters.",
        "Construct a native multivariate normal from homogeneous mean units and squared covariance units. A positive reference length/scale with mean units defines the dimensionless log-density reference measure. Dimension is bounded to32.",
        "Native density with inverse coordinate units raised to the checked distribution dimension; Dirichlet remains dimensionless. The probability density at `x`. # Errors Propagates validation errors from [`MultivariateDistribution::log_pdf`].",
        "Native FnMut uniform-source specialization using the shared authenticated random stream; output retains coordinate units. Draw one `d`-dimensional sample using a uniform source on `[0, 1)`. # Errors Propagates domain, shape, or positive-definiteness errors.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the Clifford conjugate of the multivector. Conjugation combines reversal and grade involution.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Performs the inner (dot) product.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the inverse of a general `Cl(3,0)` multivector. The product with its Clifford conjugate is central (scalar plus pseudoscalar). Components of the input must be finite. Returns `None` if the determinant is exactly zero or an inverse component overflows.",
        "Multiplies a Cl(3,0) multivector by scalar + pseudoscalar times e123. The two factor coefficients share one physical dimension. The product coefficients have that dimension times the input coefficient dimension. Multiplies by the central factor `scalar + pseudoscalar * e123`. The `e123` blade commutes with every multivector and squares to `-1`, so this operation combines four independent pairs of coefficients.",
