        "Return the exact determinant of a square normalized QMatrix with integer coefficients, using modular elimination and Chinese remaindering. Multi-prime modular determinant of a square integer matrix over $\\\\mathbb{Z}$ using 62-bit Montgomery `GF(p)` Gaussian elimination and Garner CRT reconstruction.",
        "Return the row-style Hermite normal form of a normalized QMatrix with integer coefficients. Row-style Hermite Normal Form (HNF) of an integer matrix over $\\\\mathbb{Z}$. Brings `m` by unimodular row operations into upper-echelon Hermite normal form $H$ where every leading non-zero pivot $H_{r, c} > 0$ and all entries above a pivot satisfy $0 \\\\le H_{i, c} < H_{r, c}$.",
        "Return the row-style Hermite normal form and unimodular row transform satisfying transform * A = form. Row-style Hermite Normal Form $(H, U)$ of an integer matrix over $\\\\mathbb{Z}$ satisfying $U A = H$ with $U$ unimodular ($\\\\det U = \\\\pm 1$).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The identity matrix of size `n`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The inverse of a square matrix.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether a vector is zero.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The product of two matrices (`a` is `r x m`, `b` is `m x c`).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The product of a matrix and a vector.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. A basis of `{x : m x = 0}` for `m` with `cols` columns.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The integer `n` as a rational.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The rank of a matrix whose rows have length `cols`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Reduces `v` modulo the span of the echelon rows `basis` (with their `pivots`, as returned by [`rref`]): the remainder vanishes exactly on the span.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Brings `m` (rows of length `cols`) to reduced row echelon form in place, drops the zero rows and returns the pivot columns.",
        "Return the Smith normal form and unimodular transforms satisfying left * A * right = form.",
        "Return the positive nonzero Smith invariant factors in divisibility order. The normalized QMatrix must have integer coefficients. Computes the non-zero diagonal invariant factors $[`d_1`, `d_2`, \\\\dots, `d_r`]$ of the Smith Normal Form of an integer matrix over $\\\\mathbb{Z}$, with $`d_i` > 0$ and $`d_1` \\\\mid `d_2` \\\\mid \\\\dots \\\\mid `d_r`$.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. A particular solution of `m x = b`, with free variables set to zero. Returns `None` for an inconsistent system, a mismatched right-hand side, or an invalid matrix shape. Pivots are chosen from left to right.",
        "Solve an exact square system by Dixon lifting and residual certification. A singular system returns None. Solves a nonsingular square system $A x = b$ over $\\\\mathbb{Q}$ using Dixon's $p$-adic lifting over a 62-bit prime $p$ and exact residual certification. Returns `None` if `a` is not square, dimensions mismatch, or `a` is singular.",
        "Solve an exact system with multiple right-hand sides. Free variables are zero, and inconsistent systems return None. Solves a matrix linear system $A X = B$ where $A$ has `cols` columns and `B` has matching row count. Free variables in underdetermined systems are set to zero. Uses Dixon $p$-adic lifting for square nonsingular systems of order $n \\\\ge 6$ and sparse-pivot elimination otherwise.",
        "Solve an exact system with full column rank. Inconsistent and underdetermined systems return None. Solves `m * x = b` over `Q` when the system has a unique solution (full column rank `cols`), returning `None` if inconsistent or underdetermined.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. A basis (reduced echelon rows) of the span of `vectors` of length `dim`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The transpose of an `r x c` matrix.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. The zero vector of length `n`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced.",
        "Construct exact rational coefficient algebra after explicit division by a same-unit positive reference. All subsequent Q operations are normalized coefficient-space operations; physical scales are retained by the caller rather than silently erased. Finite Float inputs are converted to their exact binary rational values.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Finds a non-trivial divisor of the odd composite `n`. Returns `Some(d)` with `1 < d < n` and `d | n`, or `None` for invalid input, for a prime, when `n` has more than [`QsLimits::MAX_DIGITS`] decimal digits, when the relation matrix would exceed [`QsLimits::MAX_FACTOR_BASE_COLUMNS`], and when the polynomial or [`QsLimits::max_sieve_positions`] budget is exhausted. An even `n > 2` returns `2` directly. The input may contain small factors: trial division by the primes below 1000 runs first and returns a factor directly. The digit limit is checked before that trial division, so an oversized request never does any sieving work. Perfect powers, prime input and even input are otherwise handled before the sieve. The limits are not clamped upwards or downwards to hide a bad choice: they are used as given, apart from a safety clamp on `bound` and `interval`.",
        "Finds two ordered nontrivial exact factors within the quadratic sieve limits and verifies their product. The input and both factors are dimensionless integers. None means no pair was found within the supplied limits. Splits `n` into two proper factors in ascending order. Uses the same limits and early factor checks as [`qs_factor`]. The product of the returned factors is exactly `n`. Either may be composite.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. [`qs_factor`] with an explicit configuration and run statistics. The same guarantees hold: the returned divisor is always in `(1, n)` and divides `n`; `None` means \"no factor found\", not \"n is composite\" or \"n is prime\".",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. A Baillie-PSW probable-prime test. Deterministic Miller-Rabin with the first twelve prime bases for machine words (which is a proof for all of `u64`), and a base-2 strong probable-prime test plus a strong Lucas test with Selfridge's parameters for larger integers. No composite is known to pass the latter, in particular the smallest strong pseudoprime to those twelve Miller-Rabin bases (318665857834031151167461) does not.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Parameter set for a `digits`-digit number. The factor-base bound is the standard QS optimum `L_n[1/2, 1/2] = exp(0.5 sqrt(ln n ln ln n))`, rounded up to two significant digits; the sieve interval is of the same order. The large-prime multiplier grows with the size, the relation margin is a small fraction of the estimated factor-base width, and the work bound is `L_n[1/2, 1] = exp(sqrt(ln n ln ln n))` capped at `2 * 10^10` positions so that even a hopeless input has a bounded cost. For 80 digits this yields a factor base around five million and a sieve interval of two million, which is beyond the column limit: the attempt returns `None` before allocating the sieve. At 65 digits the matrix is already large (tens of thousands of columns and hundreds of megabytes at the peak), but the work bound still terminates. Beyond about 66 digits the factor base typically exceeds the column limit. The remaining primes are checked for direct divisors before the attempt returns.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced.",
        "IO: execute the original reduced-unit 256 by 256, 300-step double-slit demonstration and write the NPY density. Provide an explicit grid-update budget. An example scenario simulating a wave packet hitting a double slit. # Errors This function will return an error if the underlying `run_schrodinger_simulation` fails or if the final state cannot be written to the NPY file. The last sampled density is saved as `schrodinger_double_slit.npy` inside `output_dir`, which is created if missing.",
        "Advances the two-dimensional periodic Schr\u00f6dinger model with a positive time increment. Input lengths, mass, reduced Planck constant and potential use SI dimensions. Returns inverse-length final amplitudes, inverse-square-length density after steps 1, 11, 21 and so on, and their measured times. Samples have the x coordinate varying fastest. Evolves the periodic 2D wave by Strang splitting and returns density frames. Frames are sampled after steps 1, 11, 21, and so on. Each frame has `ny` rows and `nx` columns. On success the supplied wave becomes the final state. # Errors Invalid geometry or samples, an unrepresentable phase, wave or density, failed allocations, or cancellation.",
        "Evolves a complex wavefunction with inverse-length amplitudes on a prepared periodic grid. The input and final_psi are flattened with x varying fastest. Returns the final wavefunction and time equal to steps multiplied by the signed time increment. Zero steps preserve the initial wave. Uniform potentials can evolve directly to the final state. Returns the final wave and optionally streams earlier wave samples. With a stride `s`, the observer sees completed steps `1, 1 + s, ...` within the requested interval. A zero step count emits no samples. Pass `None` for final-state-only evolution. A uniform potential can then be advanced directly in one pair of Fourier transforms. # Errors Invalid initial samples or shape, an unrepresentable evolved state, failed allocations, cancellation or an error from the observer.",
        "Prepares periodic two-dimensional Strang split-step evolution for repeated wavefunctions. Supply positive lengths, mass and reduced Planck constant in physical units, a signed measured time increment, and one energy sample per grid point in x-fast order. Every grid extent is positive. A zero increment gives the identity. Prepares the signed time increment, grid and physical potential. The evolution call supplies the step count. The `time_steps` field controls the legacy snapshot-returning entry point. # Errors Returns an error for invalid geometry or coefficients, an unrepresentable phase angle, or failure to reserve phase storage.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Finds numerical real roots of the polynomial in ascending order. Root isolation and refinement reuse the same Sturm chain. When distinct roots stay together in a bracket narrower than `tolerance`, the method returns one representative for that cluster. # Errors Invalid coefficients or tolerance, an unrepresentable root bound or Sturm chain, or failure to isolate or refine a bracket.",
        "Finds real roots of a normalized polynomial within the finite closed interval [lower, upper], including endpoint roots. The bounds, absolute tolerance and positive coordinate reference share one physical dimension. The sorted root vector retains that dimension. Finds numerical real roots in the finite closed interval `[left, right]`. Includes roots at either endpoint and returns results in ascending order. Endpoint checks use the original binary coefficients. Only the requested interval is searched, with the tolerance and cluster behavior of [`find_roots`]. # Errors Non-finite or reversed endpoints, invalid coefficients or tolerance, or a Sturm chain that cannot resolve the requested interval.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Isolates the numerical real roots of a polynomial in ascending order. Intervals are closed. A bracket may be wider than `precision` if its Sturm count is one. Roots that stay together in a bracket narrower than `precision` are returned as one cluster interval. # Errors Invalid coefficients or precision, an unrepresentable root bound or Sturm chain, or failure to separate adjacent brackets in floating point.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Refines one isolated root in a closed interval by Sturm bisection. Returns NaN for invalid endpoints, tolerance, polynomial or root counts. If the width cannot be reduced further in floating point, returns an endpoint of the last bracket.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Generates the Sturm sequence for a numerical polynomial. The Sturm sequence is a sequence of polynomials derived from the original polynomial and its derivative. It is used to determine the number of distinct real roots of a polynomial in a given interval. # Arguments * `poly` - The input polynomial as a `Polynomial`. # Returns A `Vec<Polynomial>` representing the Sturm sequence.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Circular equatorial orbit `(E, L, Omega)` at radius `r` (`None` if none exists).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Circular equatorial timelike orbit at radius `r`. # Errors Fails if no circular orbit exists at `r`.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Value of the constraint residual `H + mu^2/2` (zero on the mass shell).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. As [`Geodesic::new`] with the initial data bundled in a [`GeodesicInit`]. # Errors Fails when the constraint has no real solution (forbidden region).",
        "Advances a retained geodesic by up to 65,536 nonzero signed implicit-midpoint steps. Supply Time and ordered positive stopping radii in a physical frame or numerical values in a normalized frame. Returns a record with termination (Steps, Horizon or Escaped) and the final state in frame units. The project flag requests mass-shell projection. Integration and unit conversion must succeed before the retained handle is updated.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Starts a geodesic at `(r, theta, phi)` with conserved `E`, `L`, polar momentum and radial direction (`radial_sign = +-1`), solving the mass-shell constraint for `p_r`. # Errors Fails when the constraint has no real solution (forbidden region).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless.",
        "Advances a retained geodesic by one nonzero signed implicit-midpoint step. Supply Time in a physical frame or an affine step in a normalized frame. The project flag projects the new momentum onto the mass shell. Returns the new state in frame units. The proper_time field carries the affine parameter, which is proper time for a unit timelike mass shell. Integration and unit conversion must succeed before the retained handle is updated.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Hamiltonian `1/2 g^{mu nu} p p` at `(r, theta)` with momenta `(p_r, p_theta)`, energy and angular momentum.",
        "Returns the optional outer Kerr\u2013Newman horizon radius. The result is a measured Length in a physical frame and a Float in a normalized frame. A superextremal metric has no horizon and returns None. The adapter restores the radius without requiring an intermediate normalized radius to fit in Float when the physical result is representable.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Innermost stable circular orbit: the minimum of `E(r)` over timelike circular orbits.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. `cos(theta)` at Mino time `lambda`.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Fundamental frequencies: `(Upsilon_r, Upsilon_theta, Upsilon_phi, Gamma)` in Mino time (`Upsilon_theta = 0` for equatorial orbits, where `theta` is constant) and the coordinate-time frequencies `(Omega_r, Omega_theta, Omega_phi)`.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Orbit specified by periapsis `r_p`, apoapsis `r_a` and the turning latitude `z_minus = cos(theta_min)` (`0` = equatorial); `prograde` selects the sign of `L`. The constants `(E, L, Q)` are found by Newton iteration. The orbit starts at periapsis on the equator-crossing side of the polar motion. # Errors Fails if the iteration does not converge or the resulting orbit is not bound.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Closed-form bound geodesic with constants `(E, L, Q)` starting at `(r0, theta0, phi0)`; `radial_out` / `polar_down` give the signs of `dr/dlambda` and of `dtheta/dlambda` (`polar_down`: theta increasing, i.e. moving toward the south pole). # Errors Fails unless `E < 1`, the radial polynomial has four real roots with `r1 > r2 > r3`, `r2` is outside the horizon, `r0` lies in `[r2, r1]` and the polar motion is allowed.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Mino period of the polar motion (`None` for equatorial orbits).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Mino period of the radial motion.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Radius at Mino time `lambda` (measured from the start of the orbit).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. State `(t, r, theta, phi)` at Mino time `lambda` (`t = 0` and `phi = phi0` at the start).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Radius of the circular equatorial photon orbit strictly outside the outer horizon. The positive angular direction is selected by `prograde`. A branch that coincides with the horizon, has no exterior root, or cannot be represented returns `None`.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Radius of the circular equatorial photon orbit of Kerr (`prograde` or retrograde).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Advances the geodesic by the affine parameter `dtau_total` with an adaptive Dormand-Prince 5(4) integrator (relative tolerance `tol`). Returns `false` if the radius fell below `r_stop` (the state is left at the last accepted step).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Conserved axial angular momentum `L_z = x p_y - y p_x`.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Boyer-Lindquist `(r, theta, phi)` of the position (outside the horizon).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Carter constant `Q = p_theta^2 + cos^2 theta [a^2 (mu^2 - E^2) + L^2/sin^2 theta]` of the current state (off the axis).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Conserved energy `E = -p_t`.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Mass-shell value `1/2 g^{mu nu} p_mu p_nu` (equals `-mu^2/2`).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Starts a geodesic from Boyer-Lindquist data: position `(r, theta, phi)`, constants of motion `(E, L, Q)`, mass-shell value `mu2` (1 timelike, 0 null) and the signs of `dr/d tau` and `d theta / d tau`. # Errors Fails if the position is inside the horizon, on the axis, or the motion is forbidden there.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Kerr radius of the current position.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Schwarzschild black hole.",
        "Kerr shadow for a distant off-axis observer. Mass and spin use geometrized lengths, theta is in radians, and points is the sample count per half (2 through 2048). Returns a [2*points, 2] tensor of alpha and beta in the frame length units.",
        "Trace one Kerr null geodesic from the given observer radius and inclination in radians. Mass, spin, radius and xi use frame lengths and eta uses squared lengths. Returns Captured or Escaped with dimensionless arrival angles. Traces a null geodesic backwards from the observer. The impact parameters are `xi = L/E`, `eta = Q/E^2` from the observer at `(r_o, theta_o)` inwards (initial direction: decreasing `r`, polar motion toward larger `theta` if `polar_down`) by integrating the Mino-time equations with RK4. # Errors Fails if the ray is not allowed at the observer (`R(r_o) < 0` or `Theta(theta_o) < 0`), the step budget is exhausted.",
        "Find the Kerr shadow by tracing up to 64 regularly spaced image-plane directions. Mass, spin and observer radius use frame lengths and inclination is in radians. Returns a [points, 2] tensor in frame length units in increasing image angle.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Advances the geodesic by the Mino time `dlambda` with an adaptive Dormand-Prince 5(4) integrator (relative tolerance `tol`). Returns `false` if the orbit reached the horizon (the state is left at the last accepted step).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Carter constant measured from the current state, `Q = p_theta^2 + cos^2 theta [a^2 (mu^2 - E^2) + L^2 / sin^2 theta]` (off the axis).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Impact parameter `b = L / sqrt(E^2 - mu^2)` (`None` for a parabolic orbit `E^2 = mu^2`).",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Starts a geodesic with constants `(E, L, Q)` at `(r, theta, phi)`; `radial_out` / `polar_down` give the signs of `dr/dlambda` and of `dtheta/dlambda`. # Errors Fails unless `M > 0`, `|a| < M`, `Q >= 0`, `0 < E`, `E^2 >= mu^2` (an orbit that can reach infinity), `r` is outside the horizon and both potentials allow the motion there.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Normalisation `g_{mu nu} u^mu u^nu` of the current state, from `Sigma g^{mu nu} p_mu p_nu = -mu^2` with `p_t = -E`, `p_phi = L`, `p_r = dr/dlambda` and `p_theta = dtheta/dlambda`; it equals `-mu^2` on a geodesic.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. Runs the orbit from its (inward) start until it either falls through the horizon or returns to its starting radius moving outward. The azimuth is completed analytically: the two legs still missing between the start radius and infinity contribute `b (u0 + u1)` to leading order, so the start radius has to be large compared with the impact parameter. # Errors Fails if the state does not move inward, if the orbit is parabolic (`E^2 = mu^2`) or if the step budget is exhausted.",
        "Native geometrized relativity with positive length reference. Mass, spin and charge metric parameters use geometric lengths; coordinate/proper time restore seconds, Mino time uses inverse length, specific energy and angles are dimensionless. State `(t, r, theta, phi)`; `t = 0` and `phi = phi0` at the start.",
        "Session-local reverse AD object. Tape constructor reserves 1..65536 nodes. Linear quantity values use coherent units; addition requires matching dimensions, products/quotients and gradients propagate physical powers; transcendental arguments must be dimensionless. Opaque handles are kind checked, invalid across sessions and after release. Release dependent variables/gradients before their tape. Callbacks scope temporary handles. No native pointer/state is serialized.",
        "Exact native AD with normalized callback variables and independent physical references: point,positive coordinate_scale,positive value_scale,max_evaluations,callback. Forward callback scalar duals are {val,deriv}; vector duals are {val:vector,deriv:vector}; all dual arithmetic is dimensionless and uses native num_dual_* operators. Reverse callbacks use checked NativeAdVar handles on an ephemeral session tape; native operations enforce same-tape identity and a65536-node limit. Output derivatives restore E/D; no numerical differencing or opaque-callback finite-difference fallback. Exact value and gradient `(f(x), grad f(x))` of `f(&[Var]) -> Var` at `x` in a single reverse-mode pass. # Errors [`AdError::Empty`] when `x` is empty.",
        "Native finite-difference calculus with physical reference scales: point,coordinate_scale,value_scale,max_evaluations,[partial index or Jacobian output count],callback. Independent D/E units; derivative E/D, Hessian/Laplacian E/D^2. References are positive. Shared callback errors and finite evaluation budgets are preserved. The first derivative of `f` at `x`, by a central difference with one Richardson extrapolation step.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Degrees of freedom: 3 translations plus the non-degenerate rotations, minus the 3 of the total momentum.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Translational plus rotational kinetic energy.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Maximum deviation of any intramolecular site distance from its rigid value (diagnostic).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Total linear momentum.",
        "Construct the actual native rigid-molecule site driver. Checked CSR partitions identify rigid molecules; the physical forcefield is transferred once. Unitful centers/sites, translational and rotational DOFs retain mass-length-time dimensions; rotational integration remains native. Builds the rigid-body state from the site positions and velocities in `sys`. `molecules[m]` lists the site indices of molecule `m`; the lists must partition the sites. The current site positions must already form rigid molecules (their shape defines the body frame); velocities are projected onto rigid motion. # Errors Fails if the molecules do not partition the sites or the box is not fully periodic.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Molecular pressure tensor `(sum M v v + W_mol) / V` with the virial of the centre-of-mass separations (site virial minus the intramolecular lever arms).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Runs `n` steps using one rotor composition for the entire run. # Errors Propagates the errors of [`Self::step`]. Completed steps remain applied.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Advances the centre of mass by velocity Verlet and applies the selected rotor composition. # Errors Invalid rotation order or non-finite step times are reported before state updates. A force-field error during propagation can leave the state partially advanced.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Writes site positions and velocities from the rigid-body state.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Instantaneous temperature.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Total energy.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Whether the point `(re, im)` lies in every component of the box.",
