        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Grows and flips one Wolff cluster; returns its size (`0` when the correction of a frustrated `J2` boundary rejects the flip). A non-zero field `h` is handled by the **ghost-spin** construction: the field couples every site to an extra ghost spin `sign(h) e_0` with strength `|h|`, the cluster may absorb the ghost, and when it does the cluster's complement is flipped instead (the global symmetry of the extended system), which keeps detailed balance. A ferromagnetic `j2 > 0` joins the bond set like `j`. An antiferromagnetic `j2 < 0` is frustrated with respect to the `j` bonds, so it stays out of the bond set: the cluster grows on the `j` bonds only and the flip is then accepted with `min(1, exp(-beta dE))`, where `dE = 2 J2 sum p_i p_j` runs over the diagonal bonds with exactly one endpoint in the cluster and `p_i = s_i . r` is the projection on the embedding direction. Those projections, not the scalar products `s_i . s_j`, are what the reflection `s_i -> s_i - 2 p_i r` changes: it maps `s_i . s_j` to `s_i . s_j - 2 p_i p_j`, so the perpendicular components drop out (`p_i p_j = s_i s_j` only for `N = 1`). Only the frustrated bonds need the correction \u2014 the Fortuin-Kasteleyn weight of a ferromagnetic bond is the same before and after a cluster flip, while the Boltzmann factor of a frustrated bond is not. The proposal is symmetric (the bond construction uses only bonds inside the cluster, whose products the flip leaves unchanged), so the correction keeps detailed balance. # Errors Fails for `J <= 0`.",
