        "Actual native split-step solver in1D/2D/3D with independent physical coordinate/time references, SI mass and hbar. Potential/energy scale=hbar/time_reference; normalizedmass=mL\u00b2/(hbarT). Wavefunctions carry Length^(-dimension/2), preserving dimensionless norm; contact coupling has Energy*Length^dimension units. Box `[-len/2, len/2]^dim` with `n` points per axis. # Errors See [`SpectralGrid::new`].",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. `\u222b \u03c8* (H \u03c8)` with the nonlinear term `g|\u03c8|\u00b2` counted fully (chemical potential).",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Total energy `\u222b [|\u2207\u03c8|\u00b2/2m + V|\u03c8|\u00b2 + g|\u03c8|\u2074/2]`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Real-time evolution for `steps` steps of size `dt` from time `t0`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Real-time evolution with a complex absorbing potential `-i W(x)` (`W >= 0`): after every potential half step the amplitude is multiplied by `exp(-W dt / 2)`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Real-time evolution in a frame rotating with angular velocity `omega` about the `z` axis: `i \u03c8_t = (-\u0394/2m + V + g|\u03c8|\u00b2 - \u03a9 L_z) \u03c8`. With `omega = 0` it is exactly [`SplitStep::evolve`]. # Panics For a one-dimensional grid, or when a sub-step would rotate by more than `\u03c0/2`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Ground state by imaginary-time propagation with renormalisation. Stops when the energy changes by less than `tol` over a step or after `max_steps` steps.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Kinetic energy `\u222b |\u2207\u03c8|\u00b2 / (2m)`.",
        "Reuses the phases and Fourier plans to evolve a flat complex wavefunction. Physical samples carry Length^(-dimensions/2) and the result keeps that dimension. A zero step count or zero timestep gives the identity. Evolves one wave function for `steps` steps, with no resampling of the potential. A zero step count or a zero time step returns the original samples exactly. # Errors A wrong input length, a non-finite sample, or a nonrepresentable wave function.",
        "Prepares a fixed linear Schr\u00f6dinger propagator on a periodic grid with one to three axes, odd or even. Pass lengths and coordinate reference in Length units, mass in Mass units, flattened real potential in Energy units, and both timestep and time reference in Time units. Samples have the x axis varying fastest. Use Strang or Yoshida4, a negative timestep for inverse evolution, or zero for the identity. Prepares a fixed real potential on a one- to three-dimensional periodic grid. Each positive extent in `n` corresponds to a finite positive box length and the mass must be finite and positive. The potential is sampled at every grid point. `dt` may be negative for inverse time evolution or zero for the identity. # Errors Invalid grid dimensions, lengths, mass, time step or potential, a phase angle outside the representable range, or failure to reserve storage.",
        "Returns the number of points in the grid of a prepared linear split-step propagator. Number of points in the periodic grid.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. `\u222b |\u03c8|\u00b2`, with rescaling when necessary to keep the integrated norm in range.",
        "Prepares a fixed linear propagator using an existing centered split-step solver's grid, mass and references. Use one real potential sample per grid point, a signed timestep, and Strang or Yoshida4. A normalized solver uses dimensionless values. Prepares a fixed real potential sampled at the points of this box. # Errors See [`LinearSplitPlan::new`].",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Rotates the pattern of `psi` counterclockwise by `angle` (`|angle| <= \u03c0/2`) in the `x`-`y` plane about the box centre, as three shears. # Panics For a one-dimensional grid.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Samples a complex function on the grid.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Physical coordinate of point `p`.",
        "Native scaled global/constrained optimization with shared actual host evaluation limit. Nelder: x,xscale,yscale,step,tol,iterations,budget,f. Differential evolution: lo,hi,xscale,yscale,population,generations,budget,seed,f. Annealing: x,xscale,yscale,step,temperature,cooling,iterations,budget,seed,f. Constrained: x,xscale,yscale,equality_scale,inequality_scale,tol,iterations,budget,f,eq,ineq. Constraints independently carry C/H units; SQP returns physical multipliers E/C and E/H, native status and second-order verdict. Second-order: x,lambda,mu,xscale,yscale,eqscale,ineqscale,budget,f,eq,ineq. No global-optimality claim; native iteration/status semantics retained. Sequential quadratic programming for `min f(x)` subject to `h(x) = 0` and `g(x) <= 0`. Each iteration linearises the constraints, solves the quadratic program of [`qp_active_set`] for a step `d`, and takes a backtracking step on the `l1` merit function `f + rho (|h|_1 + max(0, g)_1)`. A least-squares restoration phase is run whenever the iterate is infeasible, so the constraints are satisfied to `tol` rather than merely penalised. The multipliers returned in [`ConstrainedResult::lambda`] and [`ConstrainedResult::mu`] are the ones of the final quadratic program, i.e. the exact multipliers of the Lagrangian `L = f + lambda . h + mu . g`; in particular `-mu_i` is the derivative of the optimal value with respect to relaxing `g_i(x) <= 0` to `g_i(x) <= eps`. `grad` may be `None`, in which case central differences are used for the objective; constraint Jacobians are always central differences. Convergence is declared when the violation is at most `tol` and the KKT residual is at most `max(tol, 1e-8)`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Graph helper: nearest-neighbour bonds of a chain with `l` sites.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Diagonal-operator constant `C`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Number of bonds.",
        "Actual native XXZ with transverse Jxy=one exchange-energy reference, unit spin hbar=1, physical inverse energy temperature and bounded operator storage. Exhausting an explicit work budget invalidates the mutable state rather than exposing a partial directed-loop update. Creates a simulation on the given bonds. # Errors Fails for an empty or unencodable bond list, invalid sites or couplings, a non-positive inverse temperature, or a graph that is not bipartite.",
        "Discard the requested thermalization sweeps, then accumulate XXZ energy, heat capacity and spin moments over measured sweeps. One explicit budget covers the entire run. Exhaustion after starting invalidates the state. A fixed seed reproduces this sampling law. Runs `therm` thermalisation sweeps and `sweeps` measured sweeps and returns the averages `(energy, specific_heat, <m^2>, <m_stag^2>)` with the energy statistical error estimate.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Measurements of the current configuration.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Graph helper: nearest-neighbour bonds of an `lx x ly` square lattice (site `x + lx y`).",
        "Run one XXZ stochastic-series-expansion sweep. A nonempty operator string uses max(8, number of sites) closed-loop updates. An empty string resamples all free spins. The explicit budget counts operator and site passes, starting draws and traversed legs. Exhaustion during a sweep invalidates the state. A fixed seed reproduces this sampling law. One Monte Carlo sweep (diagonal update, directed loops, cutoff adjustment). Fixed worm counts and the zero-order spin refresh reproduce the same trajectory for the same seed and inputs under this schedule. They change the sequences produced by earlier visit-capped releases.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. One-sample Kolmogorov\u2013Smirnov test against a reference continuous CDF. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Empty`] when `sample` is empty.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced.",
        "Evaluates the dimensionless product of positive f(x)/value_reference factors from config.h with steps of config.step. Fractional offsets use analytic continuation of the sum of logarithms. An integral interval that exceeds the callback budget is rejected before the callback runs. Evaluate the indefinite product with an explicit callback budget. # Errors Returns the errors of [`indefinite_sum_with_budget`] or an error if `f` is not positive and finite where needed.",
        "Evaluates the discrete sum from config.h to x with steps of config.step. Fractional offsets use analytic continuation in normalized coordinates. The sum has the callback's output dimension. An integral interval that exceeds the callback budget is rejected before the callback runs. Evaluate the indefinite sum with an explicit callback budget. The exact lattice path takes one callback per step. For a fractional offset, both Euler\u2013Maclaurin shifts are reserved together and Taylor fallback may use the remaining budget. If the shifts exceed the budget, Taylor can use it in full. A finite normalization point itself needs no calls. The callback receives only finite coordinates. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite endpoints, zero or non-finite step, or an offset outside the exact floating-point lattice range. It also errors if no strategy fits the budget or can produce a finite sum. A Taylor fallback that is needed requires at most 64 terms.",
        "Actual Taylor coefficients in the explicitly normalized coordinate u=x/coordinate_reference. Every coefficient retains output units; divide coefficient k by coordinate_reference^k for derivatives in physical coordinates. Taylor coefficients `c_m` of `f` about `center`. They satisfy `f(t) \u2248 \u03a3 c_m (t - center)^m` and come from the interpolating polynomial of degree `terms - 1` through Chebyshev nodes on `[center - radius, center + radius]`. This is accurate for moderate `terms` (up to about 16), unlike repeated finite differences, and converges to the Taylor series for analytic `f`.",
        "Physical native series/sequence convergence. sum_series: start,max_terms,tolerance,value_scale,max_evaluations,term(Float)->E. sequence_limit omits start. Levin: max_terms,value_scale,max_evaluations,term(Int)->E; result includes native error estimate. References/tolerances are positive physical E; callback failures propagate and budgets bound actual host calls. Sums `term(n)` for `n = start, start + 1, ...` (at most `max_terms` terms) and stops, before adding it, at the first term whose magnitude is below `tolerance`.",
        "Native antidifference of coefficients in normalized coordinate u=x/coordinate_reference. Coefficients and result share physical output dimension; this is a discrete sum with unit step in u. Antidifference of the power series `\u03a3 coefficients[m] (t - center)^m`, normalised to zero at `t = center`. For repeated evaluation or differences between nearby points, use [`PolynomialAntidifference`].",
        "Prepare a tape that selects one of 1 to 62 measured source columns for each row. Input zero holds dimensionless offsets, truncated toward zero. Offsets select the subsequent source columns starting at zero. The sources and result use the same positive physical reference. An out-of-range offset is a measured evaluation error. Builds a tape that selects one of `sources` input columns. Input zero supplies a zero-based offset and the remaining inputs hold the sources. [`Inst::Gather`] defines the offset and missing-value rules. Returns `None` if there are no sources or the input count cannot be represented.",
        "Native outward interval enclosure; measured bounds and coordinate references are checked. Infinite endpoints retain their sound interval meaning. Rigorous enclosures of every output over the input boxes `inputs` (interval arithmetic with outward rounding; calls of operator semantics without interval rules give the whole line).",
        "Evaluate native tape using measured heterogeneous coordinates/parameters; returns checked measured Value cells. Plain evaluation of every output.",
        "Native column tape evaluation; each coordinate/output column has independent retained physical references. Evaluates the program at every row of a batch, column-wise. `columns[i][row]` is input `i` (a missing element reads NaN, like a missing input of [`Tape::eval`]); `out[k][row]` receives output `k` for `row < min(out lengths)`. Rows are processed in blocks of [`COLUMN_BLOCK`]: each instruction runs over a whole block before the next one, which turns the per-instruction dispatch of the scalar interpreter into one dispatch per block and lets the arithmetic vectorise. Large pure batches split independent row chunks across the active CPU pool while respecting cancellation and producing the exact same bits as [`Tape::eval`].",
        "Wrap a real native tape in the multiple-output executor. Wraps a lowered program (the host fallback of [`device::DeviceBackend`]).",
        "Retain the exact native TapeFunction type, including its physical frame. Wraps a lowered program (the host fallback of [`device::DeviceBackend`]).",
        "Recover the defining native tape from a retained TapeFunction without serializing internal pointers. The underlying program.",
        "Native forward tape gradient; each returned Value cell has its own output/input unit. The gradient of output `k` with respect to every input at `inputs` (forward mode, one pass per input). This reference path retains its floating-point evaluation order; use [`Self::gradient_reverse`] for one forward pass and one reverse sweep on large input spaces.",
        "Native reverse tape gradient with heterogeneous coordinate units. Gradient of output `k`, in one value pass and one reverse sweep.",
        "Native deterministic structural tape hash. A structural hash, independent of node ids (for compilation caches).",
        "Native hyperdual tape Hessian; output(i,j) has units E/(D_i D_j). The Hessian of output `k` with respect to every input at `inputs`, row-major (`n` rows of `n` entries), by hyper-dual arithmetic. Entry `(i, j)` is the `\u03b5\u2081\u03b5\u2082` component of a run whose two directions are seeded by `e_i` and `e_j`. Every entry is computed by its own pass, so symmetry is a property of the rules rather than of the assembly. The cost is `n\u00b2` forward passes for `n` inputs. Entries are NaN when the tape reaches an opaque operator (`Call`, `Extern1`, `Extern2`) or a domain error; see the module limitations. Large tapes with independent built-in instructions can evaluate rows concurrently in the active CPU pool.",
        "Native Hessian-vector product with a measured coordinate displacement direction; result units E/D_i. `H_k(inputs) direction` without assembling a Hessian. Forward-over-reverse AD uses one directional value pass and one reverse sweep of dual adjoints. Cost and storage are linear in tape size for bounded-arity analytic instructions, independent of input count. Opaque calls have no second-order rule: affected entries are NaN. Non-smooth functions use the documented hyper-dual conventions. An invalid output or direction length returns a vector of NaNs.",
        "Native normalized IEEE scalar operation. NaN/interval-entire sentinels retain their native meaning; no physical quantity units are erased. `x^n` by square-and-multiply (the same sequence the JIT emits).",
        "Lower elementary expressions in declared symbol/parameter order. Every coordinate, parameter and output has an explicit positive physical reference; mixed units stay in checked Value cells. Lowers the concrete terms `roots` to one tape with an output per root, as functions of `inputs` and `params` (in that order). # Errors An unbound symbol, a non-numeric literal or an operator without scalar semantics.",
        "Return the native optimized tape retaining its physical frame. Constant folding, then dead-code elimination (registers renumbered).",
        "Explicit Float specialization of generic native tape execution, preserving physical coordinate/output references. Evaluates every output over an arbitrary number type.",
        "Validate native tape register and output invariants. Validate SSA dependencies, input/parameter indices, calls and outputs. Every operand must refer to an earlier instruction; each input and parameter must lie within the declared arity. Call argument ranges must exist and all outputs must name instructions. Gather offsets are checked at runtime, since their values are data dependent. # Errors Returns [`BackendError::Codegen`] describing the invalid instruction or output. Native backends call this before generating any memory loads.",
        "Native VJP; weights have functional/output units and each answer has functional/input units. Positive functional reference specifies the normalized objective measure. Vector-Jacobian product `weights\u1d40 J`, with respect to inputs. One value pass and one reverse sweep, with storage linear in tape size. Parameters are held constant. Repeated outputs and gathered inputs add their adjoints. Only selected branches receive adjoints; comparison and gather-index derivatives are zero. Opaque calls use the same central differences as forward-mode AD. A wrong weight count, invalid selected output, or NaN active output returns a vector of NaNs.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the cumulative distribution function (CDF) value at `x`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the quantile (inverse CDF) at probability `p`. Returns `NaN` when `p` is outside `[0, 1]`.",
        "Checked native legacy distribution. Continuous parameters and queries retain coherent physical units; inverse CDF restores sample units and PDF has inverse sample units. Creates a new `UniformDist` instance. # Arguments * `min` - The minimum value of the distribution. * `max` - The maximum value of the distribution. # Returns A `Result` containing the `UniformDist` instance, or an error string if parameters are invalid. # Errors Returns an error if `min >= max`.",
        "Checked native method over explicitly normalized values. Session/kind, shape and work/allocation bounds are enforced. Returns the probability density function (PDF) value at `x`.",
        "Session-local reverse AD object. Tape constructor reserves 1..65536 nodes. Linear quantity values use coherent units; addition requires matching dimensions, products/quotients and gradients propagate physical powers; transcendental arguments must be dimensionless. Opaque handles are kind checked, invalid across sessions and after release. Release dependent variables/gradients before their tape. Callbacks scope temporary handles. No native pointer/state is serialized.",
        "Dimensionless serial optimization: initial vector,[lower bounds,upper bounds for lbfgs_b],positive tolerance,max_iterations,max_evaluations,objective callback,gradient callback. Both callbacks share a strict evaluation budget. Gradient length matches initial vector; L-BFGS methods retain ten correction pairs. Returns x,value,iterations,evaluations,converged; callback failures propagate unchanged. Nonlinear Conjugate Gradient (Polak-Ribiere+ with automatic steepest-descent restarts) for smooth unconstrained minimization.",
        "Measured nonlinear problem with explicit coordinate_scale and value_scale, dimensionless tolerance, max_iterations and max_evaluations controls. Solves in normalized coordinates/residuals. Arguments: initial, controls, optional lower/upper, objective/residual, optional gradient. Gradient units are objective/coordinate. Homogeneous coordinate and residual vectors; callback failures propagate and budgets are shared. Nonlinear Conjugate Gradient (Polak-Ribiere+ with automatic steepest-descent restarts) for smooth unconstrained minimization.",
        "Euclidean vector norm. Computes the Euclidean norm ($`L_2`$ norm) of a vector. Formula: $||\\\\mathbf{v}||_2 = \\\\sqrt{\\\\sum_{i=1}^n `v_i^2`}$",
        "Normalize a nonzero vector. Normalizes a vector to have a magnitude of 1. # Errors Returns an error if the vector is a zero vector.",
        "Decode hexadecimal NumPy float32 or float64 bytes into shape and row-major Float storage. Accepts either byte order and C/Fortran layouts, up to 262144 values.",
        "Encode shape and dimensionless row-major Float storage as little-endian NumPy float64 bytes represented in lowercase hexadecimal. Accepts scalars and empty arrays, up to 262144 values.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Cell-centred pressure at flat cell index.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Cell size.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Creates a configuration with all walls no-slip. # Errors For bad dimensions or sizes.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Cell-centred density (`None` for the uniform density of one).",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Returns the net face flux per full cell volume at every cell, x fastest. The face flux uses the aperture of each face in an immersed-body grid. A cut cell's divergence per unit of fluid volume is obtained by dividing its flux residual by the fluid volume fraction.",
        "Reports maximum absolute divergence and a fluid-volume-weighted RMS, both in inverse time. Net outward flux has length^rank/time units, and fluid volume has length^rank units for a 2D or 3D grid. Cut-cell norms use actual fluid fractions. Fully solid domains return zero diagnostics. Measures fluid volume, net flux and norms of the cellwise divergence. Uses active pressure cells with positive fluid volume. With immersed geometry, the norms divide each aperture-weighted residual by that cell's fluid fraction. The net flux integrates the represented open grid faces. An all-solid grid returns zeros. # Errors The velocity buffers or cell spacings are invalid, or the fluid-cell divergence or integrated diagnostics are unrepresentable.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Eddy viscosity `\u03bd_t` of the sub-grid-scale model at every cell centre.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Open fraction of the cell face normal to axis `axis` with face index `i`.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Volumetric flux through the plane `x_axis = index` (face index).",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Force exerted by the fluid on whatever lies inside the box `[lo, hi]`, from the steady momentum balance on its surface: `F = -\u222e [u (u\u00b7n) + p n - \u03bd \u2202_n u] dS` (densities are one). The box must lie in the fluid and the flow must be steady for the result to be the drag of the enclosed body. In 2D the force is per unit length.",
        "Native integral of speed squared divided by two, without density weighting; output has dimensions Length^(dimension+2)/Time^2. For 2D this is the native integral per omitted depth. Kinetic energy `\u00bd \u222b |u|\u00b2` using face values.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Total mass `\u222b \u03c1 dV` over the domain (the volume when `\u03c1 \u2261 1`).",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Maximum speed over the faces (for CFL estimates).",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Cells per axis.",
        "Consumes one native NS configuration and constructs its actual staggered solver. Initial velocity is three compiled physical spatial programs. All callbacks retain parent handles; callback budgets and original failures are preserved. Creates a solver with the given initial velocity (faces) and time step. # Errors For inconsistent periodic faces or singular systems.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Runs `steps` steps. # Errors See [`NsSolver::step`].",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Multilinear interpolation of the cell-centred pressure at `x`.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Multilinear interpolation of velocity component `c` at `x`.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Overwrites the density field and rebuilds the projection operator. The two updates take effect together after the new operator is valid. # Errors Without a density field, for a wrong length, for a non-positive or non-finite value, or when the new pressure operator cannot be prepared.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Overwrites the temperature field. # Errors Without a thermal model or for a wrong length.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Advances one time step. # Errors If a linear solve fails.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Number of steps taken.",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Cell-centred temperature (`None` without a thermal model).",
        "Native staggered flow in explicit Length/Time/Mass/Temperature references. Fields and coordinates are normalized in that frame; physical outputs retain their dimensions. Work, indices, callback domains, session and parent lifetime are checked. Fluid volume fraction of pressure cell `i` (1 without an immersed body).",
        "Solves `x \u2261 remainders[i] (mod primes[i])` via Garner's mixed-radix algorithm, returning the unique canonical solution `x` in `[0, prod(primes))`.",
        "Nonnegative greatest common divisor of two arbitrary-precision integers.",
        "Floor integer square root `floor(sqrt(n))` for nonnegative `n`, or `None` for `n < 0`.",
        "Modular multiplicative inverse of `a` modulo `modulus` in `[0, modulus)`. Returns `None` if `modulus <= 0` or `gcd(a, modulus) != 1` (except `modulus == 1`, which returns `Some(0)`).",
        "Truncated integer `k`-th root of `n` (`Some(r)` with `r^k` closest to `n` toward zero without exceeding `|n|` in magnitude).",
        "Extended greatest common divisor `(g, x, y)` of arbitrary-precision integers such that `g = gcd(a, b) >= 0` and `a * x + b * y == g`.",
        "The Jacobi symbol `(a / n)` for odd positive arbitrary-precision `n`. Returns `None` if `n <= 0` or `n` is even.",
        "Applies the strong Lucas probable-prime test with Selfridge\u2019s parameters. Strong Lucas probable-prime test of an odd integer `n > 2` with Selfridge's parameters (`D` the first of `5, -7, 9, -11, ...` with Jacobi symbol `-1`, `P = 1`, `Q = (1 - D) / 4`): checks `U_d = 0` or `V_{d 2^r} = 0` for some `r < s`, where `n + 1 = d 2^s` with `d` odd.",
        "Batched modular exponentiation computing `bases[i]^exponents[i] mod modulus` in `[0, modulus)`. Uses division-free Montgomery multiplication (`MontU64`) when `modulus` is odd and parallelizes across large batches via [`par::map_indexed`]. # Errors Returns [`NumberExtError::ShapeMismatch`] if `bases` and `exponents` differ in length, or [`NumberExtError::Domain`] if `modulus <= 0`.",
        "Exact `n`-th Catalan number `C_n = C(2n, n) / (n + 1)`. Returns zero for negative arguments.",
        "Chinese Remainder Theorem: solves `x \u2261 remainders[i] (mod moduli[i])`. Returns `(first, period)` with `0 <= first < period`. The period is the least common multiple of the moduli. Non-coprime moduli are supported. # Errors The inputs must be nonempty, of the same length, and have positive moduli and consistent congruences. The period must fit in `i64`.",
        "Arbitrary-precision Chinese Remainder Theorem solving `x \u2261 remainders[i] (mod moduli[i])`. Returns `(first, period)` with `0 <= first < period`, where `period` is the least common multiple of `moduli`. Non-coprime moduli are supported. # Errors Returns [`NumberExtError::ShapeMismatch`] if slice lengths differ, [`NumberExtError::Empty`] if the slices are empty, or [`NumberExtError::Domain`] if any modulus is nonpositive or the congruences are inconsistent.",
        "M\u00f6bius function `\u03bc(n)`, extended to return zero for `n <= 0`.",
        "Computes `\u03bc(0)` through `\u03bc(limit)` with a linear sieve. The value at index zero is zero. Each composite is visited once using its smallest prime factor, so consecutive values need no separate factorizations. The sieve uses `O(limit)` time and storage. # Errors The inclusive length must fit the address space and its storage must be allocatable.",
        "Modular multiplicative inverse in `[0, modulus)`. # Errors The modulus must be positive and relatively prime to `a`.",
        "Modular exponentiation: `base^exponent mod modulus`, in `[0, modulus)`. # Errors The modulus must be positive.",
        "Integer partition function `p(n)` via Euler's pentagonal number theorem. Negative arguments produce zero. The recurrence retains values through `n`. # Panics Panics when the table does not fit the host's address space.",
        "Solves `a * x \u2261 b (mod modulus)` over the integers. A solution `(first, period)` represents exactly the integers `first + k * period` for any integer `k`, with `0 <= first < period`. The period is `modulus / gcd(a, modulus)`. Returns `None` when the congruence has no solution. Modulus one admits all integers. For example, `6 * x \u2261 8 (mod 14)` gives `(6, 7)`. # Errors The modulus must be nonzero.",
        "Unsigned Stirling numbers of the first kind `c(n, k)`. Returns zero when `n < 0` or `k` lies outside `0..=n`. # Panics Panics when the recurrence's workspace length cannot fit the address space.",
        "Signed Stirling numbers of the first kind `s(n, k) = (-1)^(n - k) c(n, k)`. Uses the same domain and workspace as [`stirling_first`].",
        "Exact Stirling numbers of the second kind `S(n, k)`. Returns zero when `n < 0` or `k` lies outside `0..=n`. # Panics Panics when the recurrence's workspace length cannot fit the address space.",
        "Computes Euler's totient `\u03c6(0)` through `\u03c6(limit)` with a linear sieve. The value at index zero is zero and `\u03c6(1) = 1`. Uses pooled prime scratch storage from [`global_pool`] and runs in `O(limit)` time. # Errors Returns [`NumberExtError::Domain`] when the inclusive length overflows `usize` or cannot be reserved in memory.",
        "Extended greatest common divisor `(g, x, y)` such that `a * x + b * y == g` and `g >= 0`. Wide outputs represent `gcd(i64::MIN, 0)` without overflow.",
        "Computes Carmichael's reduced totient function $\\\\lambda(n)$ (`carmichael_lambda(0) = 0`). $\\\\lambda(n)$ is the exponent of the multiplicative group $(\\\\mathbb{Z}/n\\\\mathbb{Z})^\\\\times$, i.e. the smallest positive integer $m$ such that $a^m \\\\equiv 1 \\\\pmod n$ for all $\\\\gcd(a, n) = 1$.",
        "The least common multiple, or `None` when it overflows `u64`.",
        "The least `x >= 0` with `g^x \u2261 h (mod p)`. Accepts prime and composite moduli, including bases that share factors with the modulus. Returns `None` if there is no solution or `p` is outside `2..=2^48`. After removing common factors, baby-step giant-step takes `O(sqrt p)` time and memory in the worst case.",
        "Returns the number of positive divisors `\\\\tau(n) = \\\\sigma_0(n)` (`divisor_count(0) = 0`).",
        "Returns the sum of the `k`-th powers of the positive divisors of `n`, `\\\\sigma_k(n) = \\\\sum_{d \\\\mid n} d^k`, saturating at `u128::MAX` (`divisor_sigma(0, _) = 0`).",
        "Returns all positive divisors of `n` in ascending order (`divisors(0)` is empty).",
        "Given unsigned 64-bit integers a and b, return the exact record {gcd, x, y} satisfying a*x + b*y = gcd(a,b). Coefficients x and y are signed. The extended Euclidean algorithm on `u64` inputs. Returns `(g, x, y)` with `g = gcd(a, b)` and `a * x + b * y = g`. The B\u00e9zout coefficients of `u64` inputs stay within `i128`.",
        "Returns the prime factors of `n` with multiplicity, in ascending order. `0` and `1` have no factors. Small primes are removed by trial division; the cofactor is split with Miller\u2013Rabin and Pollard\u2013Brent rho, so every `u64` factors in milliseconds.",
        "Computes the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two numbers using Stein's binary GCD algorithm after one initial Euclidean reduction step.",
        "Computes the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two 128-bit unsigned integers using Stein's binary GCD algorithm.",
        "Deterministic Baillie\u2013PSW primality test over `u64` (strong base-2 Miller\u2013Rabin test combined with a strong Lucas probable-prime test using Selfridge's parameters, executed in the Montgomery domain).",
        "Performs a deterministic Miller-Rabin primality test over `u64` using division-free Montgomery multiplication (`MontU64`).",
        "Primality test for 128-bit unsigned integers combining deterministic `u64` Miller\u2013Rabin with 13-base strong Miller\u2013Rabin and a strong Selfridge Lucas test (Baillie\u2013PSW) for values above `u64::MAX`.",
        "Floor of the square root of `n`, exact for all `u64`.",
        "Jacobi symbol of a signed 128-bit numerator and a positive odd unsigned 64-bit denominator. Returns -1, 0, or 1. Computes the Jacobi symbol $\\\\left(\\\\frac{a}{n}\\\\right)$ for an odd positive integer `n`. Returns `0` when `n == 0`, `n` is even, or $\\\\gcd(a, n) > 1$. For `n == 1`, returns `1`.",
        "Computes the least common multiple (LCM) of two numbers. Returns `0` when either argument is `0`, and `u64::MAX` when the true value does not fit (use [`checked_lcm`] to tell the two apart).",
        "Legendre symbol of an unsigned 64-bit numerator and an odd prime denominator. Returns zero when the prime divides the numerator. Computes the Legendre symbol $\\\\left(\\\\frac{a}{p}\\\\right)$ for an odd prime `p`. Returns `0` if `p < 3`, `p` is composite, or `p` divides `a`; `1` if `a` is a quadratic residue modulo `p`; and `-1` if `a` is a quadratic non-residue.",
        "Canonical inverse of an unsigned 64-bit integer modulo m >= 2. Returns a domain error when a and m are not coprime. `a^(-1) mod modulus`, or `None` when `gcd(a, modulus) != 1` or `modulus < 2`. The extended Euclidean algorithm terminates in `O(log modulus)` steps with small quotients, which is faster than Fermat exponentiation and works for composite moduli.",
        "Finds the modular multiplicative inverse of a number. Solves for `x` in `ax \u2261 1 (mod m)` using the extended Euclidean algorithm in `i128`. The modulus may be negative (its absolute value is used); `m == 0` has no inverse. The result lies in `0..|m|`, and `|m| == 1` gives `Some(0)`. # Returns An `Option<i64>` containing the modular inverse if it exists, otherwise `None`.",
        "Computes `(base^exp) % modulus` efficiently using modular exponentiation (binary exponentiation).",
        "`num_number_theory_mod_pow_u128(base, exponent, modulus)` raises a u128 base to a u128 exponent modulo a positive u128 integer without overflow. Computes `(base^exp) % modulus` for 128-bit unsigned integers without overflow. Returns `0` when `modulus <= 1`.",
        "`num_number_theory_mul_mod(a, b, modulus)` multiplies two u64 values modulo a positive u64 integer without overflow. `a * b mod m` without overflow. `m` must be non-zero.",
        "`num_number_theory_mul_mod_u128(a, b, modulus)` multiplies two u128 values modulo a positive u128 integer without overflow. Computes `(a * b) % m` for 128-bit unsigned integers without overflow. Returns `0` when `m <= 1`.",
        "Computes Euler's totient function \u03c6(n) (`\u03c6(0) = 0`). Uses [`factorize`], so it is fast for every `u64`.",
        "Returns the prime modulus retained by the prepared square-root plan. The prime modulus used by this plan.",
        "Checks a 64-bit modulus and prepares its square-root method. Returns None for a modulus other than a prime. Modulus two is supported. Prepares an odd prime modulus or `2`. Returns `None` for other moduli.",
        "Reduces the unsigned input modulo the prepared prime. Returns the smaller square root, or None if the input is a quadratic nonresidue. Returns the smaller root of `n` modulo the prepared prime. The argument is reduced modulo the prime. Non-residues give `None`.",
        "Generates primes up to `limit` using the Sieve of Eratosthenes. For `limit <= 1_000_000`, reuses the cached prime table in [`crate::kernels::reuse::global_pool`]. For larger limits, executes an odd-only bit-packed L1-cache-resident segmented sieve leasing scratch storage from [`crate::kernels::reuse::global_pool`]. Returns an empty vector when the sieve table cannot be allocated (`limit` near `usize::MAX`) instead of aborting.",
        "Smallest positive primitive root modulo an unsigned 64-bit integer. Returns a domain error if the multiplicative group has no primitive root. Returns the smallest positive primitive root modulo `n` if the multiplicative group $(\\\\mathbb{Z}/n\\\\mathbb{Z})^\\\\times$ is cyclic ($n \\\\in \\\\{2, 4, p^k, 2p^k\\\\}$ for an odd prime $p$), or `None` otherwise.",
        "A square root of `n` modulo the prime `p`, choosing the smaller root. Accepts `p = 2`. Returns `None` for a composite or invalid modulus or when `n` is a non-residue. Use [`PrimeSqrtPlan`] for repeated queries with the same prime modulus.",
        "Serial adaptive vector IVP: initial time, final time, initial state vector, absolute state tolerance, relative tolerance, positive finite maximal time step, positive attempt budget, RHS callback. RHS(time,state) returns a same-length vector with state/time dimensions. Returns accepted times/states/evaluations/rejected; the rank-two state tensor has one row per accepted time. Coefficients use coherent units. Callback failures propagate unchanged, rejected attempts consume the budget, and numerical error estimates are heuristic. Events, DAEs and dense-output handles use the native Rust API. Variable-order BDF (orders 1 to 5, NDF coefficients) with a finite-difference Jacobian; see [`bdf_jac`]. # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::Invalid`].",
        "Serial adaptive vector IVP: initial time, final time, initial state vector, absolute state tolerance, relative tolerance, positive finite maximal time step, positive attempt budget, RHS callback. RHS(time,state) returns a same-length vector with state/time dimensions. Returns accepted times/states/evaluations/rejected; the rank-two state tensor has one row per accepted time. Coefficients use coherent units. Callback failures propagate unchanged, rejected attempts consume the budget, and numerical error estimates are heuristic. Events, DAEs and dense-output handles use the native Rust API. Dormand-Prince 5(4) integration of `y' = f(t, y)` from `t0` to `t1` (either direction) with dense output and event detection. `f(t, y, dy)` writes the derivative into `dy`. Events are located by Brent iteration on the continuous extension; a terminal event ends the integration at the event time. # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`] or [`OdeError::NonFinite`].",
        "Dimension-aware adaptive requested-method integration: t0,t1,y0,atol,rtol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,rhs. Callback derivative has state/time dimensions. Returns accepted times/states/Hermite rates and actual work counters; no method substitution. Adaptive state-dependent stepping does not preserve fixed-step symplecticity. Integrate the selected RK4 or geometric method forward using step doubling. The finite interval uses `start <= end`. The accepted state is two half steps of the requested method, without extrapolation or method substitution. Both the endpoint estimate and the midpoint defect of its cubic Hermite extension control acceptance. A trial needs a representable time strictly between its endpoints. A bracketed event between adjacent times is reported at the later timestamp. Adaptive state-dependent step selection does not retain fixed-step symplecticity. Gauss\u2013Legendre trials reuse stage storage for the duration of the solve. # Errors Invalid inputs or callback outputs, exhausted attempts/evaluations, a tolerance requiring a smaller step than `h_min`, an unsplittable time grid, or failed implicit stages.",
        "Dimension-aware adaptive requested-method integration: t0,t1,y0,atol,rtol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,rhs. Callback derivative has state/time dimensions. Returns accepted times/states/Hermite rates and actual work counters; no method substitution. Adaptive state-dependent stepping does not preserve fixed-step symplecticity. Integrate the selected RK4 or geometric method forward using step doubling. The finite interval uses `start <= end`. The accepted state is two half steps of the requested method, without extrapolation or method substitution. Both the endpoint estimate and the midpoint defect of its cubic Hermite extension control acceptance. A trial needs a representable time strictly between its endpoints. A bracketed event between adjacent times is reported at the later timestamp. Adaptive state-dependent step selection does not retain fixed-step symplecticity. Gauss\u2013Legendre trials reuse stage storage for the duration of the solve. # Errors Invalid inputs or callback outputs, exhausted attempts/evaluations, a tolerance requiring a smaller step than `h_min`, an unsplittable time grid, or failed implicit stages.",
        "Evaluate a cubic Hermite solution history after validating its storage. # Errors Malformed, empty or nonfinite history, nonincreasing times, or an out-of-span query.",
        "Dimension-aware adaptive requested-method integration: t0,t1,y0,atol,rtol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,rhs. Callback derivative has state/time dimensions. Returns accepted times/states/Hermite rates and actual work counters; no method substitution. Adaptive state-dependent stepping does not preserve fixed-step symplecticity. Integrate the selected RK4 or geometric method forward using step doubling. The finite interval uses `start <= end`. The accepted state is two half steps of the requested method, without extrapolation or method substitution. Both the endpoint estimate and the midpoint defect of its cubic Hermite extension control acceptance. A trial needs a representable time strictly between its endpoints. A bracketed event between adjacent times is reported at the later timestamp. Adaptive state-dependent step selection does not retain fixed-step symplecticity. Gauss\u2013Legendre trials reuse stage storage for the duration of the solve. # Errors Invalid inputs or callback outputs, exhausted attempts/evaluations, a tolerance requiring a smaller step than `h_min`, an unsplittable time grid, or failed implicit stages.",
        "Serial adaptive vector IVP: initial time, final time, initial state vector, absolute state tolerance, relative tolerance, positive finite maximal time step, positive attempt budget, RHS callback. RHS(time,state) returns a same-length vector with state/time dimensions. Returns accepted times/states/evaluations/rejected; the rank-two state tensor has one row per accepted time. Coefficients use coherent units. Callback failures propagate unchanged, rejected attempts consume the budget, and numerical error estimates are heuristic. Events, DAEs and dense-output handles use the native Rust API. Radau IIA(5) integration of `y' = f(t, y)` with a finite-difference Jacobian; see [`radau5_jac`]. # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`], [`OdeError::SolveFailed`] or [`OdeError::Invalid`].",
        "Dimension-aware adaptive requested-method integration: t0,t1,y0,atol,rtol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,rhs. Callback derivative has state/time dimensions. Returns accepted times/states/Hermite rates and actual work counters; no method substitution. Adaptive state-dependent stepping does not preserve fixed-step symplecticity. Integrate the selected RK4 or geometric method forward using step doubling. The finite interval uses `start <= end`. The accepted state is two half steps of the requested method, without extrapolation or method substitution. Both the endpoint estimate and the midpoint defect of its cubic Hermite extension control acceptance. A trial needs a representable time strictly between its endpoints. A bracketed event between adjacent times is reported at the later timestamp. Adaptive state-dependent step selection does not retain fixed-step symplecticity. Gauss\u2013Legendre trials reuse stage storage for the duration of the solve. # Errors Invalid inputs or callback outputs, exhausted attempts/evaluations, a tolerance requiring a smaller step than `h_min`, an unsplittable time grid, or failed implicit stages.",
        "Serial adaptive vector IVP: initial time, final time, initial state vector, absolute state tolerance, relative tolerance, positive finite maximal time step, positive attempt budget, RHS callback. RHS(time,state) returns a same-length vector with state/time dimensions. Returns accepted times/states/evaluations/rejected; the rank-two state tensor has one row per accepted time. Coefficients use coherent units. Callback failures propagate unchanged, rejected attempts consume the budget, and numerical error estimates are heuristic. Events, DAEs and dense-output handles use the native Rust API. Rosenbrock 2(3) (the `ode23s` pair) for stiff problems. The Jacobian and the explicit time derivative are obtained by finite differences at every step. Dense output is the cubic Hermite interpolant of the step ([`OdeSolution::eval`]); events are not supported. # Errors [`OdeError::StepLimit`], [`OdeError::NonFinite`] or [`OdeError::SolveFailed`].",
        "Dimension-aware adaptive requested-method integration: t0,t1,y0,atol,rtol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,rhs. Callback derivative has state/time dimensions. Returns accepted times/states/Hermite rates and actual work counters; no method substitution. Adaptive state-dependent stepping does not preserve fixed-step symplecticity. Integrate the selected RK4 or geometric method forward using step doubling. The finite interval uses `start <= end`. The accepted state is two half steps of the requested method, without extrapolation or method substitution. Both the endpoint estimate and the midpoint defect of its cubic Hermite extension control acceptance. A trial needs a representable time strictly between its endpoints. A bracketed event between adjacent times is reported at the later timestamp. Adaptive state-dependent step selection does not retain fixed-step symplecticity. Gauss\u2013Legendre trials reuse stage storage for the duration of the solve. # Errors Invalid inputs or callback outputs, exhausted attempts/evaluations, a tolerance requiring a smaller step than `h_min`, an unsplittable time grid, or failed implicit stages.",
        "Dimension-aware adaptive requested-method integration: t0,t1,y0,atol,rtol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_evaluations,rhs. Callback derivative has state/time dimensions. Returns accepted times/states/Hermite rates and actual work counters; no method substitution. Adaptive state-dependent stepping does not preserve fixed-step symplecticity. Integrate the selected RK4 or geometric method forward using step doubling. The finite interval uses `start <= end`. The accepted state is two half steps of the requested method, without extrapolation or method substitution. Both the endpoint estimate and the midpoint defect of its cubic Hermite extension control acceptance. A trial needs a representable time strictly between its endpoints. A bracketed event between adjacent times is reported at the later timestamp. Adaptive state-dependent step selection does not retain fixed-step symplecticity. Gauss\u2013Legendre trials reuse stage storage for the duration of the solve. # Errors Invalid inputs or callback outputs, exhausted attempts/evaluations, a tolerance requiring a smaller step than `h_min`, an unsplittable time grid, or failed implicit stages.",
        "Deterministic dot product of `a` and `b` over their common prefix `a.len().min(b.len())`. Each fixed block of `block` elements accumulates with a 4-way unrolled sum, and block sums are folded sequentially in block order so serial execution (`grain = usize::MAX`) and parallel execution produce bit-identical results.",
        "Deterministic Euclidean (`L2`) norm of `a` using fixed blocks of `block` elements. Uses [`dot_fixed`] on the ordinary path and falls back to scale-normalized block accumulation when intermediate squares overflow or underflow, keeping serial (`grain = usize::MAX`) and parallel results bit-identical.",
        "Deterministic inclusive prefix sum of `data` using a two-pass fixed-block scan. Pass 1 scans each contiguous block of `block` elements independently; a sequential scan computes exclusive block offsets left-to-right; pass 2 adds each block's offset to its elements. Serial (`grain = usize::MAX`) and parallel executions produce bit-identical outputs.",
        "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive first-order upwind advection with a multidimensional CFL bound. # Errors Invalid grid, face data, velocities, controls, or exhausted temporal budgets.",
        "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive first-order upwind advection with a multidimensional CFL bound. # Errors Invalid grid, face data, velocities, controls, or exhausted temporal budgets.",
