        "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive heat evolution in one, two, or three spatial dimensions. BE and CN solve their full implicit operators; ADI uses Douglas splitting. Explicit trials are additionally bounded by the diffusion CFL condition. # Errors Malformed grids/BCs, invalid controls, inconsistent state, or failed convergence.",
        "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive heat evolution in one, two, or three spatial dimensions. BE and CN solve their full implicit operators; ADI uses Douglas splitting. Explicit trials are additionally bounded by the diffusion CFL condition. # Errors Malformed grids/BCs, invalid controls, inconsistent state, or failed convergence.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a bounded Helmholtz problem by Chebyshev collocation. Solves the 1D/2D/3D Helmholtz / Poisson equation `-\u0394u + \u03c3 u = f(x)` on a bounded box `[lo, hi]` using Chebyshev\u2013Gauss\u2013Lobatto spectral collocation (`crate::sim::spectral::ChebGrid::solve_helmholtz`) with Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions. Returns `(chebyshev_node_coords, nodal_values)`. # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid degrees (`< 2`), bounds, or BCs.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve periodic linear advection with discontinuous Galerkin elements. Solves 1D linear advection `u_t + a u_x = 0` using Nodal Discontinuous Galerkin (`crate::sim::dg::solve_dg_1d`) of polynomial degree `poly_degree >= 1` on `n_elem` elements over `[x0, x1]` with periodic boundary conditions. Returns `(lgl_nodes_x, nodal_values)` at `t = t_end`. # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] if parameters are invalid.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a one-dimensional reaction-diffusion boundary problem by FEM. Solves the 1D reaction-diffusion BVP `-(k u_x)_x + c u = f(x)` on `[x0, x1]` with `nx` simplex intervals using `P1` or `P2` Lagrange finite elements (`crate::sim::fem_simplex`) and boundary conditions `bc` (Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin). # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] if the mesh, coefficients, or BCs are invalid.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a two-dimensional reaction-diffusion boundary problem by FEM. Solves the 2D elliptic PDE `-\u2207\u00b7(k \u2207u) + c u = f(x, y)` on `[x0, x1] \u00d7 [y0, y1]` triangulated into `2 * nx * ny` triangles using `P1` or `P2` Lagrange finite elements (`crate::sim::fem_simplex`) and per-face boundary conditions `bc` (Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin). # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] if the mesh, coefficients, or BCs are invalid.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a three-dimensional reaction-diffusion boundary problem by FEM. Solves the 3D elliptic PDE `-\u2207\u00b7(k \u2207u) + c u = f(x, y, z)` on a tetrahedral box mesh `[lo, hi]` with `cells = [nx, ny, nz]` using `P1` or `P2` Lagrange finite elements (`crate::sim::fem_simplex`) and per-face boundary conditions `bc`. # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] if the mesh, coefficients, or BCs are invalid.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solves a 2D elliptic PDE `-\u2207\u00b7(k \u2207u) + c u = f(x, y)` on a triangle mesh with adaptive D\u00f6rfler residual-based mesh refinement (`crate::sim::fem_triangles`). # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] if the mesh or parameters are invalid.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Maximum nodal error `max_i |u_h(x_i) - exact(x_i)|`.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solves the 1D Shallow Water Equations (`h_t + (hu)_x = 0`, `(hu)_t + (hu^2 + g h^2 / 2)_x = 0`) using `crate::sim::physics_fvm::solve_shallow_water_1d`. Returns `(height_h, momentum_hu)` at `t = steps * dt`. # Errors [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial_h.len() != initial_hu.len()`, [`FdmError::Domain`] for non-positive parameters.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solves the 1D inviscid Burgers' equation `u_t + (u^2 / 2)_x = 0` using `crate::sim::physics_fvm::solve_burgers_1d` or high-resolution MUSCL-Hancock FVM. # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid or step parameters.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a scalar conservation law with finite volumes. Solves a 1D scalar conservation law `u_t + f(u)_x = 0` on `num_cells` finite volumes covering `[0, domain_size]` using `scheme` (`Upwind`, `LaxFriedrichs`, `MusclMinmod`, or `MusclVanLeer`, leveraging `crate::sim::physics_fvm` limiters and fluxes) with periodic or transmissive boundary conditions. Returns `(cell_centers, cell_averages)` at `t = steps * dt`. # Errors Returns [`FdmError::Domain`] for an invalid grid (including a cell width that rounds to zero), invalid time step or non-finite initial state. Returns [`FdmError::NotConverged`] if flux evaluation produces a non-finite state.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Integrate a periodic reaction-diffusion equation by ETDRK4. Integrates a periodic reaction-diffusion or heat equation `u_t = \\\\alpha \u0394u - \\\\sigma u + N(t, u)` in 1D, 2D, or 3D on a periodic Fourier pseudospectral grid using 4th-order Exponential Time Differencing Runge\u2013Kutta (`crate::sim::spectral::etdrk4`) with 2/3 dealiasing on the nonlinear term. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] for invalid grid extents or time span, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `initial.len()` does not match the grid size.",
        "Actual public high-level PDE entry point, with explicit Length/Time/field references and original boundary, discretization and numerical method semantics. Arrays and controls are normalized in that frame; callbacks take coherent physical arguments. Native storage, work and callback failures are checked. Solve a periodic Helmholtz problem by Fourier inversion. Solves the periodic Helmholtz / screened Poisson equation `-\u0394u + \u03c3 u = f` in 1D, 2D, or 3D on a periodic box of lengths `lengths` and power-of-two grid sizes `extents` using Fourier pseudospectral inversion (`crate::sim::spectral::SpectralGrid`). For `\u03c3 == 0.0` (pure periodic Poisson `-\u0394u = f`), the source `f` must have zero mean and the returned solution has zero mean. # Errors [`FdmError::Domain`] if extents are not powers of two (`>= 4`) or if `\u03c3 == 0` with non-zero mean source, [`FdmError::ShapeMismatch`] if `source.len()` does not match.",
        "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive Fourier ETDRK4 reaction-diffusion evolution in one to three dimensions. Both linear diffusion/reaction and nonlinear callback physical times are retained on each restarted ETDRK4 step. The temporal order remains four. # Errors Invalid periodic grid, shape, coefficients, callback shape/domain or temporal controls.",
        "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive Fourier ETDRK4 reaction-diffusion evolution in one to three dimensions. Both linear diffusion/reaction and nonlinear callback physical times are retained on each restarted ETDRK4 step. The temporal order remains four. # Errors Invalid periodic grid, shape, coefficients, callback shape/domain or temporal controls.",
        "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive wave evolution by variable-step velocity Verlet. Each state concatenates displacement and velocity. `velocity_atol` has displacement/time dimensions, independently of `options.atol`. # Errors Malformed grid/BCs, invalid state/tolerances, or exhausted error/CFL budgets.",
        "Tolerance-controlled PDE evolution with explicit coordinate L, time T, and field U dimensions. Diffusivity is L^2/T; velocity L/T; reaction 1/T; Neumann U/L; Robin alpha dimensionless, beta L, gamma U. Grid/state lengths and boundary pairing/corner data are checked. Controls: t0,t1,rtol,atol,h0,h_min,h_max,max_steps,max_rejections,max_step_calls,max_evaluations. Boundary record: kind,value,flux,alpha,beta,gamma vectors (one broadcast face or two per active axis). Only accepted states are returned; step doubling controls temporal error, independently of spatial error. Optional trailing direction and dimensionless terminal-event callback; wave events receive displacement and velocity separately. FEM uses a fixed simplex mesh; source variants take a vectorized (time,coordinates)->nodal-rate callback with a consistent mass load. FVM preserves periodic/transmissive flux methods; classic LF's timestep-dependent viscosity may prevent strict temporal convergence. DG uses periodic SSPRK3 in one or two dimensions; Fourier uses ETDRK4 and a dimensioned nonlinear rate callback. Budgets and callback failures are explicit errors. Adaptive wave evolution by variable-step velocity Verlet. Each state concatenates displacement and velocity. `velocity_atol` has displacement/time dimensions, independently of `options.atol`. # Errors Malformed grid/BCs, invalid state/tolerances, or exhausted error/CFL budgets.",
        "`p \u2218 q`: first `q`, then `p`.", "`g p g\u207b\u00b9`.",
        "The cycle type: the sorted cycle lengths, fixed points included. # Panics Panics if an image is outside the permutation's degree.",
        "Cycles of a checked bijective permutation (fixed points included). CSR offsets/indices preserve independently sized cycles; degree is bounded by 4096. The cycles of `p` (fixed points included), each starting at its least point. # Panics Panics if an image is outside the permutation's degree.",
        "The order of `p` (the lcm of its cycle lengths). # Panics Panics if an image is outside the permutation's degree.",
        "The identity of degree `n`.", "The inverse permutation.",
        "Whether `p` fixes every point.", "Whether `p` is a permutation of `0..p.len()`.",
        "Whether a transitive group is primitive (no block system other than the trivial ones). Intransitive groups are not primitive.",
        "All native minimal block systems under a finite degree/work budget. Top-level offsets group CSR block rows into systems; no rectangular padding changes block membership. The minimal non-trivial block systems of a transitive group (each a list of blocks), without repetitions.",
        "Minimal block system identifying two checked points. Generators must be bijections of the declared degree, and seed points must be in range. Output uses CSR offsets/indices. The finest block system of a transitive group in which `a` and `b` lie in one block (Atkinson's algorithm): the blocks, each sorted, in order of their least point.",
        "Orbits of generators encoded as rows of a common-degree permutation matrix. Output uses CSR offsets/indices with finite degree and group-work budgets. The orbits of the group generated by `gens` on `0..n`.",
        "`p^k` for `k \u2265 0`, using direct composition for powers that need few compositions and cycle rotations for other powers.",
        "Raise a dimensionless permutation of degree up to 4096 to an exact signed integer power. A negative exponent rotates cycles in reverse, and the magnitude can have at most 65536 bits. Invalid images return an error. Raises a permutation to an arbitrary signed integer power. A negative exponent rotates each cycle in reverse. Returns `None` if the input images do not form a bijection of their degree.",
        "Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse. Moore-Penrose pseudo-inverse through the SVD. # Errors As [`svd`].",
        "Returns the dimensionless hyperbolic geodesic distance between two complex points inside the open Poincare unit disk. Hyperbolic distance in the Poincar\u00e9 unit disk `|z| < 1`.",
        "Fit a polynomial in x/coordinate_scale. Positive scale carries coordinate units; all returned coefficients retain ordinate units, so powers never require a heterogeneous coefficient tensor. The coefficients of the least-squares polynomial of `degree`, ascending. # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`] when the samples differ in length, and [`InterpError::Domain`] when there are fewer points than coefficients.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed.",
        "Compose outer(inner(x)). Inner ordinates must have the dimension of the outer coordinate. The result preserves the inner coordinate scale and the outer ordinate dimension. The maximum result degree is 128. Computes the polynomial composition $P(\\\\text{inner}(x))$ where `self` is $P$. The arithmetic runs in double-double and the result is rounded to `f64` once, at the end. Composition is ill-conditioned whenever the intermediate terms $`c_k` Q(x)^k$ are much larger than the result: a `f64` Horner and a `f64` Brent-Kung composition then disagree in the last digits, not because either is wrong but because they round at different points. Carrying the same two algorithms over [`Dd`] drops the accumulated error to about `1e-32` relative, so both round to the same `f64` and the answer is the correctly-rounded one rather than whichever strategy happened to run.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Returns the degree of the polynomial.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Returns the derivative of the polynomial. The derivative is computed by applying the power rule to each term. For a term `c*x^n`, its derivative is `(c*n)*x^(n-1)`. # Returns A new `Polynomial` representing the derivative.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Divides the polynomial by a scalar. # Arguments * `rhs` - The scalar to divide by. # Returns A `Result` containing the new `Polynomial`, or an error if the scalar is zero. # Errors Returns an error if the divisor scalar is zero.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Evaluates the polynomial at a given point `x` using Horner's method. Horner's method is an efficient algorithm for evaluating polynomials. For a polynomial `P(x) = c_n*x^n + c_{n-1}*x^{n-1} + ... + c_1*x + c_0`, it computes `P(x) = (...((c_n*x + c_{n-1})*x + c_{n-2})*x + ... + c_1)*x + c_0`. # Arguments * `x` - The point at which to evaluate the polynomial. # Returns The value of the polynomial at `x` as an `f64`.",
        "Evaluate a descending-coefficient polynomial at a rank-one tensor of up to 16384 physical coordinates. The output retains the input shape, sample order and polynomial ordinate dimension. An empty tensor produces an empty result. Evaluates the polynomial at a slice of points `xs` using 4-way unrolled Horner evaluation and deterministic multi-core parallelism when the workload exceeds the execution threshold. The points are split into disjoint contiguous chunks; every chunk runs the same unrolled kernel, so each output element is produced by exactly one call and the result is bit-identical to [`Self::eval`] for every thread count.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Finds the real roots of the polynomial. This method combines Sturm's theorem for root isolation with Newton's method for refining the roots. Sturm's theorem provides disjoint intervals, each containing exactly one real root, which are then used as starting points for Newton's method to converge to the root. # Returns A `Result` containing a `Vec<f64>` of the real roots found, or an error string if root isolation or refinement fails. # Errors Returns an error if root isolation or refinement fails.",
        "Build a portable polynomial from up to 128 real roots and a positive coordinate scale in the same units. The result is the monic product in x/coordinate_scale with dimensionless coefficients. Repeated roots retain their multiplicity. An empty root tensor gives the constant one. Builds the monic polynomial $\\\\prod_{i} (x - `r_i`)$ from its roots using a balanced divide-and-conquer subproduct tree.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Returns the indefinite integral of the polynomial (constant of integration = 0).",
        "Build a portable polynomial from 1 to 129 distinct coordinates, their ordinates and a positive coordinate scale. Coordinates and scale share units. The model stores descending coefficients in ordinate units for x/coordinate_scale. Computes the unique interpolating polynomial of degree `< xs.len()` passing through `(xs[i], ys[i])` using Newton divided differences converted to the descending power basis. # Errors Returns an error if `xs` and `ys` have different lengths, are empty, contain non-finite values or duplicate `x` nodes, or the divided differences or power-basis coefficients overflow.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Checks if the polynomial is zero (within epsilon).",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Performs polynomial long division. The divisor must have a nonzero leading coefficient. Use [`Self::try_long_division`] to validate the inputs and results. # Returns A tuple `(quotient, remainder)` as `Polynomial`s. # Panics Panics if the divisor has no coefficients.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed.",
        "Multiply two descending-coefficient polynomials with the same coordinate reference using the native FFT kernel. Coefficient units multiply. Multiplies two polynomials using a packed 2-for-1 real-input complex FFT backed by [`crate::kernels::reuse::fft_plan`] and [`crate::kernels::reuse::fft_scratch_for`]. Falls back to [`Self::mul_karatsuba`] if any input coefficient is non-finite. # Accuracy The FFT accumulates a relative error of a few `f64` epsilons times `log n`, where the schoolbook loop stays at one or two epsilons and Karatsuba at a handful. That is a deliberate trade for the 1.4\u20133x speedup above the threshold; a caller that needs the tighter association should call [`Self::mul_karatsuba`] directly, and [`Self::compose`] sidesteps the question by accumulating in double-double. `pow` inherits whatever tier its operands select, which is why `p.pow(3)` and `p * p * p` agree exactly: both go through this same dispatch.",
        "Multiply two descending-coefficient polynomials with the same coordinate reference using the native Karatsuba kernel. Coefficient units multiply. Multiplies two polynomials using recursive Karatsuba multiplication backed by a reusable scratch buffer from [`crate::kernels::reuse::f64_scratch`].",
        "Multiply two descending-coefficient polynomials with the same coordinate reference using schoolbook convolution. Coefficient units multiply. Multiplies two polynomials using the 4-way unrolled $O(n m)$ schoolbook algorithm.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed. Creates a new `Polynomial` from coefficients (highest degree first).",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed.",
        "Portable descending-coefficient Polynomial of normalized x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients retain ordinate dimensions; derivative and integral restore physical scale powers. Arithmetic requires identical coordinate scales. Kind/version, positive scale, finite values and degree/allocation budgets are validated. Native polynomial algorithms are called directly; no pointer or process-local handle is exposed.",
        "Divide measured descending-coefficient polynomials with the same coordinate reference. Leading zeros in the divisor are skipped. Returns quotient coefficients in dividend units divided by divisor units and remainder coefficients in dividend units. Zero divisors and nonfinite results are errors. Divides finite coefficients by a nonzero descending-coefficient polynomial. Leading zeros in the divisor are skipped. A fused update can retain a finite coefficient when a product overflows before it is subtracted. # Errors Returns an error for zero divisors, nonfinite inputs or nonfinite results.",
        "Aberth-Ehrlich complex roots of a polynomial expressed in powers of x/coordinate_scale. Coefficients may share arbitrary ordinate units; roots carry coordinate units. All complex roots of a polynomial (coefficients ascending) by the Aberth-Ehrlich method with Cauchy-bound initial guesses. Leading zero coefficients are dropped; roots at zero are handled by the iteration itself. # Errors [`RootError::Invalid`] for constant polynomials, [`RootError::NoConvergence`] if 500 iterations do not suffice.",
        "Evaluate coefficients with ordinate units at normalized x/coordinate_scale; returns ordinate units. Evaluates a polynomial given its coefficients in ascending order.",
        "Project a vector onto another; zero direction produces zero. Projects vector `v1` onto vector `v2`. Formula: $\\\\text{proj}_{\\\\mathbf{v2}}(\\\\mathbf{v1}) = \\\\frac{\\\\mathbf{v1} \\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{v2}}{||\\\\mathbf{v2}||^2} \\\\mathbf{v2}$ # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "Householder QR with q square and r matching the input. Householder QR: returns `(Q, R)` with `Q` of size `m x m`, `R` of size `m x n`.",
        "Clenshaw-Curtis nodes and weights on `[-1, 1]` with `n + 1` points (`n` is rounded up to an even number, at least 2).",
        "Gauss-Legendre nodes and weights on `[-1, 1]` with `n` points (Newton iteration on the Legendre recurrence).",
        "The `index`-th point (`index >= 1`) of the Halton sequence in `dim <= 16` dimensions.",
        "Difference between sample maximum and minimum. Computes the range (max - min) of a slice of data.",
        "SVD rank using a nonnegative absolute singular-value tolerance. Numerical rank: singular values above `tol` (or the default `max(m, n) * eps * sigma_max` when `tol` is `None`). # Errors As [`svd`].",
        "RBF interpolation with independent coordinate and value units. Positive coordinate scale nondimensionalizes distances. Kernel names: gaussian, multiquadric, inverse_multiquadric, thin_plate, cubic, linear. Radial basis function (RBF) scattered-data interpolation in `R^d`. # Errors [`InterpError::Empty`] for no samples, [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`] for inconsistent dimensions, [`InterpError::Domain`] for non-finite data, an invalid kernel scale, or an unrepresentable result, and [`InterpError::Singular`] for a singular interpolation matrix.",
        "Reflect about a unit normal supplied by the caller. Reflects vector `v` about a normal vector `n`. Formula: $\\\\mathbf{r} = \\\\mathbf{v} - 2(\\\\mathbf{v} \\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{n})\\\\mathbf{n}$ Note: The normal vector `n` must be normalized. The result can remain finite even when the intermediate correction overflows. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "Computes the Riemann zeta function \u03b6(s) for real `s`. * `s > 1`: Euler-Maclaurin summation (via [`hurwitz_zeta`] with q = 1): the first terms directly, the tail by its integral plus Bernoulli-number corrections; accurate to about 1e-15. * `s = 1`: `+\u221e` (the pole). * `-1/2 < s < 1`: the alternating eta series `\u03b7(s) = (1 - 2^(1-s)) \u03b6(s)` accelerated with Borwein's algorithm (relative error about `1e-15`); `s = 0` is exactly `-1/2`. * `s <= -1/2`: the analytic continuation by the functional equation `\u03b6(s) = 2^s \u03c0^(s-1) sin(\u03c0s/2) \u0393(1-s) \u03b6(1-s)`. The trivial zeros at the negative even integers are returned as exactly `0`, and `\u03b6(-1) = -1/12`, `\u03b6(-3) = 1/120`, ... follow from the same formula. Returns `NaN` for `NaN` or `-\u221e`, and `\u00b1\u221e` where the value overflows (roughly `s < -170`).",
        "Scott's rule bandwidth `1.06 sd n^(-1/5)`. Requires at least two finite observations.",
        "Silverman's rule-of-thumb bandwidth `0.9 min(sd, IQR/1.34) n^(-1/5)`. Uses `sd` when `IQR` is zero. Requires at least two finite observations.",
        "Tukey biweight location estimator (tuning constant `c`, 4.685 typical).",
        "Empirical CDF of `data` at `x`.",
        "Empirical CDF at several query points, in the supplied order. NaN observations are ignored and NaN queries yield NaN. An empty effective sample yields NaN at every query. The ordered sample is prepared once and each query uses a binary search.",
        "Huber M-estimator of location with tuning constant `k` (1.345 is the classical choice), scale fixed to the normalised MAD.",
        "Huber location using a caller-supplied scale in the units of the sample. The clipping distance is `k * scale`. NaNs are ignored. The tuning constant and scale must both be positive and finite. Returns NaN for invalid parameters or an empty effective sample. For a flat interval containing the median, returns the median.",
        "Interquartile range (type 7 quantiles).",
        "Kernel density estimate of `data` evaluated at `x` with bandwidth `h`.",
        "Median absolute deviation; multiplied by 1.4826 when `normal` is true so it estimates the standard deviation for Gaussian data.",
        "Median of `data` (NaNs ignored).",
        "Sample quantile of `data` at probability `p` using the Hyndman-Fan definition `kind` (1..=9; 7 is the NumPy/R default). NaNs are ignored. Returns NaN for empty data or an invalid `p`/`kind`.",
        "Hyndman-Fan sample quantiles in query order, using one shared sort. NaN observations are ignored. An empty sample or invalid definition yields NaN at every query. An invalid probability yields NaN at that query.",
        "Mean after discarding the fraction `trim` from each tail.",
        "Fraction of total weight on observations at or below `x`. Weights must be finite and nonnegative. Returns NaN for a NaN query, unequal lengths, invalid weights, NaN observations with positive weight or zero total weight.",
        "Smallest positive-weight observation whose weighted empirical CDF is at least `p`. At `p = 0` and `p = 1` this gives the minimum and maximum positive-weight observations.",
        "Weighted empirical quantiles, sharing one sort and cumulative weight scan. Zero-weight observations are ignored. Invalid weights yield NaN at every query and an invalid probability yields NaN at that query.",
        "Mean after replacing the lowest and highest fraction `trim` by the nearest retained values.",
        "Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget. Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh: a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules: finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule: tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations. Callback failures propagate unchanged. Romberg integration with Richardson extrapolation of the trapezoid rule. The error is the last difference of diagonal entries.",
        "Multiplies a vector by a scalar. Formula: $\\\\mathbf{r}_i = s \\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{v}_i$ # Arguments * `v` - The vector to scale. * `s` - The scalar factor.",
        "Generalized eigenvalues `alpha_i / beta_i` of `A v = lambda B v`. # Errors As [`qz`].",
        "Solves the continuous-time Lyapunov equation `A X + X A^T = Q`. # Errors As [`sylvester`].",
        "Infinity norm `max_i sum_j |A_{i,j}|`.",
        "Real QZ (generalized Schur) decomposition of `(A, B)`. # Errors [`DenseError::Shape`], [`DenseError::NonFinite`], or [`DenseError::NoConvergence`].",
        "Real Schur decomposition `A = Q T Q^T`, where `Q` is orthogonal and `T` is quasi-upper-triangular (1x1 and 2x2 diagonal blocks).",
        "Solves the continuous-time Sylvester equation `A X + X B = C` by real Schur reduction and block back-substitution.",
        "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates `dX = a(t, X) dt + G(t, X) dW` from `y0`. `drift` returns `n` values; `diffusion` returns `n` values for [`Structure::Diagonal`] (the diagonal entries) or `n \u00d7 m` values in row-major order for [`Structure::General`]. Fixed/uniform-grid schedules use prescribed increments. Adaptive schedules retain the requested method and use a bounded noise tree with coupled step doubling, a separate drift-error check, and explicit resolution/work errors. See the module documentation for tolerance scaling and finite allocations. # Errors `E0802` for shapes/parameters, `E0806` for a non-finite state, `E0807` for a step collapse. No method/structure/step-policy combination is refused with `E1001` any more.",
        "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates `dX = a(t, X) dt + G(t, X) dW` from `y0`. `drift` returns `n` values; `diffusion` returns `n` values for [`Structure::Diagonal`] (the diagonal entries) or `n \u00d7 m` values in row-major order for [`Structure::General`]. Fixed/uniform-grid schedules use prescribed increments. Adaptive schedules retain the requested method and use a bounded noise tree with coupled step doubling, a separate drift-error check, and explicit resolution/work errors. See the module documentation for tolerance scaling and finite allocations. # Errors `E0802` for shapes/parameters, `E0806` for a non-finite state, `E0807` for a step collapse. No method/structure/step-policy combination is refused with `E1001` any more.",
        "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates a jump-diffusion SDE `dX_t = a(t, X_{t-}) dt + G(t, X_{t-}) dW_t + \\\\sum_{q=1}^{dN_t} c(t, X_{t-}, Z_q)` over a uniform step grid specified in `options`. * `drift(t, y)` returns the drift vector `a(t, y) in R^n`. * `diffusion(t, y)` returns the diffusion coefficients (`n` for `Diagonal`, `n * m` for `General`). * `jump_coeff(t, y, z)` returns the state jump increment `c(t, y, z) in R^n` produced by a single jump mark `z` (for example, in Merton's geometric jump-diffusion `dS = \\\\mu S dt + \\\\sigma S dW + S (e^Z - 1) dN`, `c(t, S, z) = [S_0 * (z_0.exp() - 1.0)]`). Supports `EulerMaruyama`, `Milstein`, `HeunStratonovich`, `DriftImplicitEuler`, `ThetaMaruyama`, and `DriftImplicitMilstein` for the continuous diffusion part. A fixed or uniform schedule allows up to 1,048,576 integration steps and simulated jumps each. Stored path and Wiener arrays each allow up to 8,388,608 scalar values. The same value limit also bounds the state and mark components processed across all jumps. The expected jump count must fit the event budget before sampling. If the realized count exceeds the budget, the solver stops before applying that step's jumps. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] for invalid parameters or a non-finite per-step Poisson mean, [`SdeError::StepLimit`] for an exceeded work budget, [`SdeError::Shape`] for invalid shapes, or [`SdeError::NonFinite`] for a non-finite state.",
        "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates a jump-diffusion SDE `dX_t = a(t, X_{t-}) dt + G(t, X_{t-}) dW_t + \\\\sum_{q=1}^{dN_t} c(t, X_{t-}, Z_q)` over a uniform step grid specified in `options`. * `drift(t, y)` returns the drift vector `a(t, y) in R^n`. * `diffusion(t, y)` returns the diffusion coefficients (`n` for `Diagonal`, `n * m` for `General`). * `jump_coeff(t, y, z)` returns the state jump increment `c(t, y, z) in R^n` produced by a single jump mark `z` (for example, in Merton's geometric jump-diffusion `dS = \\\\mu S dt + \\\\sigma S dW + S (e^Z - 1) dN`, `c(t, S, z) = [S_0 * (z_0.exp() - 1.0)]`). Supports `EulerMaruyama`, `Milstein`, `HeunStratonovich`, `DriftImplicitEuler`, `ThetaMaruyama`, and `DriftImplicitMilstein` for the continuous diffusion part. A fixed or uniform schedule allows up to 1,048,576 integration steps and simulated jumps each. Stored path and Wiener arrays each allow up to 8,388,608 scalar values. The same value limit also bounds the state and mark components processed across all jumps. The expected jump count must fit the event budget before sampling. If the realized count exceeds the budget, the solver stops before applying that step's jumps. # Errors [`SdeError::Invalid`] for invalid parameters or a non-finite per-step Poisson mean, [`SdeError::StepLimit`] for an exceeded work budget, [`SdeError::Shape`] for invalid shapes, or [`SdeError::NonFinite`] for a non-finite state.",
        "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates an `\\\\alpha`-stable L\u00e9vy-driven SDE `dX_t = a(t, X_{t-}) dt + G(t, X_{t-}) dL_t^{(\\\\alpha, \\\\beta)}` where `dL_t` has independent `S_\\\\alpha(dt^{1/\\\\alpha}, \\\\beta, 0)` increments. # Errors Returns [`SdeError`] for invalid `\\\\alpha \\\\notin (0, 2]`, `\\\\beta \\\\notin [-1, 1]`, mismatched callback shapes, non-finite states, or a Brownian-only method requested for non-Gaussian stable noise.",
        "Adaptive stochastic path with exact declared callback dimensions and finite budgets. Arguments: t0,t1,positive reference_time,y0,state atol,dimensionless rtol,positive finest cells,proposal/path step budget,callback budget,nonnegative u64 seed,method code 0..9; general variants then take noise count and commutative Bool. Levy variants next take alpha,beta and use dimensionless stable noise at the reference-time scale (diffusion has state units). Jump variants next take intensity (inverse time),dimensionless constant mark vector with independent extent M, then drift,diffusion,jump callbacks sharing that M; other families finish with drift,diffusion callbacks. Brownian diffusion has state/sqrt(time) units. Method codes follow SdeMethod declaration order. Rejections reuse a bounded dyadic noise realization; resolution or work exhaustion is an error. Stable alpha<2 supports only Ito Euler/implicit Euler/theta, while alpha=2 uses the correctly scaled Brownian schemes. Jump times/marks are fixed before rejection and continuous segments stop exactly at each arrival. Local error estimates are heuristic, not global certificates. Integrates an `\\\\alpha`-stable L\u00e9vy-driven SDE `dX_t = a(t, X_{t-}) dt + G(t, X_{t-}) dL_t^{(\\\\alpha, \\\\beta)}` where `dL_t` has independent `S_\\\\alpha(dt^{1/\\\\alpha}, \\\\beta, 0)` increments. # Errors Returns [`SdeError`] for invalid `\\\\alpha \\\\notin (0, 2]`, `\\\\beta \\\\notin [-1, 1]`, mismatched callback shapes, non-finite states, or a Brownian-only method requested for non-Gaussian stable noise.",
        "Evaluates the power series `sum_n coefficients[n] * (x - center)^n` by Horner's scheme. An empty coefficient list gives 0.",
        "Logistic sigmoid. Computes the logistic (sigmoid) function \u03c3(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)).",
        "Returns the amplitude envelope `hypot(x, hilbert(x))` of real samples. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite samples or an unrepresentable envelope.",
        "Forms the complex analytic signal of real periodic samples. The real part is the original signal and the imaginary part is its discrete Hilbert transform. Positive-frequency bins are doubled and negative bins removed. DC and the Nyquist bin of an even-length signal are preserved. An empty input gives an empty result. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite samples or an unrepresentable transform.",
        "Computes sample autocorrelations at lags from zero through `max_lag`. Divides each biased sample autocovariance by the lag-zero autocovariance. The lag-zero result is one. Input scaling cancels before the division, so the correlation remains computable when the variance itself would overflow or underflow. # Errors Returns an error for empty or non-finite samples, a lag at least as large as the sample count, a constant series, or an unrepresentable work extent.",
        "Computes biased sample autocovariances at lags from zero through `max_lag`. For lag `k`, divides the sum of `(samples[t] - mean) * (samples[t + k] - mean)` by the full sample count. Lag zero is the population variance of the input. A constant series gives zero at all lags. Computes short lag ranges directly and uses a zero-padded FFT for sufficiently long ranges. # Errors Returns an error for empty or non-finite samples, a lag at least as large as the sample count, or an unrepresentable autocovariance or work extent.",
        "Generates a Blackman window of length `n`. `w[i] = 0.42 - 0.5 cos(2 pi i / (n - 1)) + 0.08 cos(4 pi i / (n - 1))`, symmetric with both endpoints at zero (in exact arithmetic). # Arguments * `n` - The number of points in the output window.",
        "Digital low-pass Butterworth filter `(b, a)` of order `order` with normalized cutoff `cutoff` in `(0, 1)` (where `1.0` is Nyquist).",
        "Computes the one-dimensional discrete linear convolution of two sequences. Convolution is a mathematical operation that blends two functions to produce a third. In signal processing, it is used to describe the effect of a linear time-invariant system on an input signal. # Arguments * `a` - The first input sequence. * `v` - The second input sequence. # Returns The discrete linear convolution of `a` and `v`. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::signal::convolve; let a = vec![1.0, 2.0, 3.0]; let v = vec![0.0, 1.0, 0.5]; let res = convolve(&a, &v); assert_eq!(res, vec![0.0, 1.0, 2.5, 4.0, 1.5]); ```",
        "Computes the discrete cross-correlation of two sequences. Cross-correlation is a measure of similarity of two series as a function of the displacement of one relative to the other. # Arguments * `a` - The first input sequence. * `v` - The second input sequence. # Returns The discrete cross-correlation of `a` and `v`. # Example ```rust use rssn::kernels::signal::cross_correlation; let a = vec![1.0, 2.0, 3.0]; let v = vec![0.0, 1.0, 0.5]; let res = cross_correlation(&a, &v); // correlation(a, v)[k] = sum_i a[i] * v[i-k] ```",
        "Orthonormal Type-II Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT-II) in `O(n log n)`.",
        "Orthonormal Type-II Discrete Sine Transform (DST-II) in `O(n log n)`.",
        "`num_signal_filtfilt(b, a, samples)` filters forward and backward with odd reflection. Coefficients are dimensionless. The result preserves the length and units of the samples. Checked zero-phase forward-backward digital filter. Uses odd reflection up to `3 * (max(b.len(), a.len()) - 1)` samples on each side, capped at the signal length minus one. Coefficients are normalized by `a[0]`. The returned vector has the same length as `x`. # Errors Rejects empty coefficient vectors, a zero leading denominator, an undefined steady state, non-finite inputs or intermediate values, and an unallocatable output extent.",
        "Designs a symmetric band-pass FIR with unit gain at the passband center. The cutoffs are ordered and normalized to Nyquist, with `0 < low < high < 1`. The window is Kaiser, parameterized by `beta`. # Errors Errors for invalid parameters or unrepresentable coefficients.",
        "Designs a symmetric, unit-DC-gain low-pass FIR using a Kaiser window. The cutoff is normalized to Nyquist and must lie strictly between zero and one. A larger absolute `beta` improves sidelobe attenuation at the expense of transition width. # Errors Errors for invalid parameters or unrepresentable coefficients.",
        "Evaluates a digital transfer function at frequencies in cycles per sample. Uses the coefficient convention of [`lfilter`]. Frequencies are periodic modulo one, with Nyquist at `0.5`. Returns a complex response per frequency. # Errors Errors for empty coefficients, non-finite inputs or a singular or unrepresentable response at a requested frequency.",
        "Generates a Hamming window of length `n`. # Arguments * `n` - The number of points in the output window.",
        "Generates a Hann window of length `n`. # Arguments * `n` - The number of points in the output window.",
        "Returns the discrete Hilbert transform of real periodic samples. A positive-frequency cosine becomes a sine, and DC and Nyquist components contribute zero. See [`analytic_signal`] for the boundary convention. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite samples or an unrepresentable transform.",
        "Inverse of [`dct`] (orthonormal Type-III DCT).",
        "Inverse of [`dst`] (orthonormal Type-III DST).",
        "Reconstructs a real signal from full [`stft`] spectra. Applies the same window on synthesis and divides each sample by its sum of squared window weights. Returns the interval covered by the frames, of length `(frames.len() - 1) * hop + window.len()`. The imaginary part after the inverse transform is discarded. Use a window and hop that have nonzero overlap weights across that interval, or pad the analysis when endpoint samples must be preserved. # Errors Returns an error for invalid parameters, inconsistent or non-finite frames, uncovered samples, output size overflow or non-finite reconstruction.",
        "Generates a symmetric Kaiser window with shape parameter `beta`. Either sign of a finite `beta` produces the same coefficients. For `n > 1`, a non-finite shape parameter returns NaNs.",
        "Linear FIR/IIR digital filter (`a[0] * y[n] = sum_k b[k]*x[n-k] - sum_{k>=1} a[k]*y[n-k]`) evaluated via Direct Form II Transposed with pooled scratch state.",
        "Computes Yule\u2013Walker partial autocorrelations at lags zero through `max_lag`. Uses the Durbin\u2013Levinson recursion on the biased sample autocorrelations. For lag `k > 0`, the result is the final coefficient of the order-`k` autoregression. The lag-zero result is one. After calculating the autocorrelations, the recursion takes `O(max_lag\u00b2)` time and `O(max_lag)` space. # Errors Returns the same input errors as [autocorrelation] and also rejects an autoregression with numerically singular prediction-error variance.",
        "Resamples a real signal by a rational factor using a centered polyphase FIR. Reduces the factors by their greatest common divisor, then designs a Kaiser low-pass filter with 10 taps on each side per rate phase (`beta = 5`). The input is zero extended at the boundaries. Output time zero aligns with input time zero, and the output contains `ceil(samples.len() * up / down)` samples. # Errors Errors for a zero factor, non-finite input or output, or unallocatable filter or output dimensions.",
        "Savitzky-Golay smoothing filter coefficients for odd `window_len` and polynomial degree `poly_order < window_len`.",
        "Applies a Savitzky-Golay smoothing filter of odd `window_len` and degree `poly_order` to `x` with clamped boundary padding.",
        "Applies a zero-state cascade of second-order digital sections. Each row of `sections` is `[b0, b1, b2, a0, a1, a2]`. The coefficients are normalized by `a0` and evaluated with transposed direct form II. An empty cascade is the identity. # Errors Errors for a zero leading denominator, non-finite input, or unrepresentable state or output.",
        "Power spectrogram `|STFT|^2` of `x` with `window` and `hop`.",
        "Upsamples, applies a finite impulse response filter and downsamples. This implements zero insertion by `up`, linear convolution with `taps`, then retention of every `down`th sample starting at index zero. It works directly on filter phases without allocating the upsampled signal or multiplying by its inserted zeros. The boundaries are zero extended. The supplied taps determine gain, passband, alias rejection and delay. If either input slice is empty the result is empty. Otherwise the output length is `floor(((samples.len() - 1) * up + taps.len() - 1) / down) + 1`. # Errors Returns an error for a zero factor, non-finite data, non-finite output or an output extent that overflows or cannot be allocated.",
        "Estimates one-sided cross-spectral density and magnitude-squared coherence. Applies the same complete-frame Welch convention as [`welch_psd`] to both inputs. For the Fourier bins `X` and `Y`, it averages `conj(X) * Y` across frames to obtain the cross-spectral density `Pxy`, and computes `abs(Pxy)^2 / (Pxx * Pyy)`. Auto-spectra and cross-spectra share the same window energy and one-sided scaling. # Errors Returns an error on unequal lengths or on the invalid input domains described by [`welch_psd`].",
        "Estimates the one-sided power spectral density of real samples by Welch's method. Averages the periodograms of complete windowed frames, whose starts are `hop` samples apart. Pass the window coefficients explicitly, for example with [`hann_window`]. The transform uses the window length and performs no detrending. Frequencies have the same unit as `sample_rate`. Only bins with a negative-frequency partner are doubled. Multiplying each bin by the frequency spacing and summing gives the mean window-weighted frame power. Trailing samples that do not complete a frame are ignored. Large batches average fixed blocks in order so the result is independent of the worker count. # Errors Returns an error for an invalid window, hop or sample rate, insufficient samples, non-finite data in a complete frame or non-finite computed power.",
        "Computes arbitrary equally spaced Fourier samples using a chirp convolution. Bin `k` is `sum_n x[n] exp(-2 pi i (start + k * step) n)` with `start` and `step` measured in cycles per input sample. Frequencies are periodic modulo one. This is the unit-circle chirp z-transform. It can resolve a sub-band without evaluating the rest of the spectrum. A zero step repeats one frequency. Zero bins give an empty result and an empty input gives `bins` zeros. Time and storage scale with the input and output extents, using an FFT of length `next_power_of_two(x.len() + bins - 1)`. Repeated moderate transforms with the same input length, bin count, and step can share the transformed chirp, even when the start varies. # Errors Returns an error for non-finite frequencies or input, unrepresentable intermediate arithmetic, or an output or work extent that is too large.",
        "Unnormalised sinc, sin(x)/x. Computes the sinc function sinc(x) = sin(\u03c0x) / (\u03c0x).",
        "The sine integral `Si(x) = \u222b_0^x sin(t)/t dt` (odd).",
        "The hyperbolic sine integral `Shi(x) = \u222b_0^x sinh(t)/t dt` (odd).",
        "Computes the adjusted Fisher-Pearson sample skewness coefficient `G1` (the estimator used by Excel's `SKEW` and by `scipy.stats.skew(bias=False)`).",
        "Softplus function. Computes the softplus function softplus(x) = ln(1 + e^x).",
        "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Integrates `q_t + \u2207\u00b7F(q) = 0` with nodal DG to `t_end`. `bcs` lists `[XMin, XMax, YMin, YMax]`; 1D meshes use the first two. # Errors If the mesh is invalid, periodic faces are inconsistent or the initial data have the wrong number of components.",
        "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Integrates `q_t + \u2207\u00b7F(q) = 0` on a hexahedral mesh to `t_end` with SSP-RK3. `bcs` lists `[XMin, XMax, YMin, YMax, ZMin, ZMax]`. # Errors For an invalid mesh, inconsistent periodic faces, a wrong component count in the initial state or a fixed face used by the spatial operator, or a non-finite wave speed.",
        "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. As [`solve_dg`] with an optional slope / positivity limiter applied after every Runge\u2013Kutta stage. # Errors See [`solve_dg`].",
        "Actual native DG method with explicit geometry/time and per-component physical references; normalized states, separate flux units and coherent compiled callbacks preserve mixed Euler dimensions. Native algorithm and limiter identity are retained; shapes, work and parent lifetimes are checked. Integrates `q_t + \u2207\u00b7F(q) = 0` with nodal DG and the full option set (characteristic / hierarchical / WENO limiters, entropy-stable fluxes). # Errors If the mesh is invalid, periodic faces are inconsistent or the initial data have the wrong number of components.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Writes `M^-1 r` into `z`.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Borrows the selected factors as a [`Preconditioner`] for [`cg_with`], [`gmres_with`] and [`bicgstab_with`].",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Builds the strongest incomplete factorisation that succeeds; the chain always terminates with Jacobi, so this never fails.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. The preconditioner that [`AutoPreconditioner::new`] selected.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Applies the preconditioner `M^-1 = L^-T D^-1 L^-1` by one forward substitution, a diagonal scaling and one backward substitution.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. IC(0) factorisation `A \u2248 L D L^T` (incomplete Cholesky without fill). The lower triangular factor uses the strictly lower pattern of `A`; the upper triangle of `A` is ignored, so the matrix must be symmetric and positive definite for `M = L D L^T` to be a valid CG preconditioner. On a symmetric matrix this is the same operator as [`Csr::ilu0`] written as `L D L^T` instead of `L U`. # Returns `None` for malformed CSR, a missing diagonal, a nonpositive pivot, or unrepresentable lower-triangular factor entries. The factor stores only the strict lower triangle, its transpose and the pivots.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Solves `L U z = r` (applies the preconditioner `M^-1`).",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. ILU(0) factorisation (incomplete LU keeping the sparsity pattern). The factors are stored in the pattern of `A`: the strictly lower entries of `L` (unit diagonal) and the entries of `U` in the same arrays. No fill is created and no pivoting is performed. # Returns `None` when the matrix has no entry on the diagonal of some row or when a zero pivot is met (structurally or after elimination). The factorisation never returns `NaN`: on `None` the caller keeps the unpreconditioned path or falls back to [`Csr::jacobi_inverse`].",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Sparse LU factorisation with a fill-reducing symmetric ordering and threshold partial pivoting (left-looking Gilbert-Peierls with a depth-first symbolic reach); see [`SparseLu`]. # Errors [`SparseLuError::NotSquare`] or [`SparseLuError::Singular`].",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Dimension of the factored matrix.",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Number of stored entries in `L` and `U` (a measure of fill-in).",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. The symmetric fill-reducing permutation used (`perm[new] = old`).",
        "Validated portable native dense/sparse factor definition. Reconstructs native factors from finite defining matrices, preserving method identity and right-hand-side/matrix quotient units. Format/version/kind, CSR sorting, shapes, indices, exact permutations and allocation budgets are checked before native methods. No process-local factor pointers are serialized. Solves `A x = b`.",
        "Computes the n-th Bernoulli number `B_n`. Orders up to `BERNOULLI_TABLE_MAX` are served from a small bucketed table cache (the three buckets are 32, 64 and 128), so repeated polynomial and zeta evaluations do not rebuild the recurrence.",
        "Computes the n-th Bernoulli polynomial `B_n(x)`.",
        "Modified Bessel function of the first kind `I_\u03bd(x)`, by `(1/\u03c0)\u222b_0^\u03c0 e^{x cos \u03b8} cos \u03bd\u03b8 d\u03b8 - (sin \u03bd\u03c0/\u03c0)\u222b_0^\u221e e^{-x cosh t - \u03bdt} dt` (`x < 0` for integer orders by parity).",
        "[`bessel_i0`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].",
        "Exponentially scaled modified Bessel I\u2080, `exp(-|x|) I\u2080(x)`. The scaled form stays finite when the unscaled function overflows. Both infinities map to zero and NaN is propagated.",
        "Bessel function of the first kind `J_\u03bd(x)` for real order `\u03bd` and real `x` (`x < 0` only for integer orders, through `J_n(-x) = (-1)^n J_n(x)`).",
        "[`bessel_j0`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].",
        "[`bessel_j1`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].",
        "Modified Bessel function of the second kind `K_\u03bd(x)` for `x > 0`, by [its positive integral representation](https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.32.E9).",
        "Bessel function of the second kind `Y_\u03bd(x)` for real order and `x > 0`, by `(1/\u03c0)\u222b_0^\u03c0 sin(x sin \u03b8 - \u03bd\u03b8) d\u03b8 - (1/\u03c0)\u222b_0^\u221e (e^{\u03bdt} + e^{-\u03bdt} cos \u03bd\u03c0) e^{-x sinh t} dt`.",
        "Computes the beta function, B(a, b) = \u0393(a)\u0393(b)/\u0393(a+b). Defined here for `a, b > 0`; any other argument (zero, negative, NaN) gives NaN rather than reaching `statrs`, which panics on it.",
        "Computes the Chebyshev polynomial of the first kind `T_n(x)`.",
        "Computes the Chebyshev polynomial of the second kind `U_n(x)`.",
        "[`digamma_numerical`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].",
        "Computes the double factorial n!! n!! = n * (n-2) * (n-4) * ... * (1 or 2)",
        "Returns the dimensionless complete third-kind elliptic integral of the prepared parameters. Returns the complete integral for the prepared parameters.",
        "Evaluates the incomplete elliptic integral of the third kind at an amplitude in radians, tracking complete periods of the prepared parameters. Evaluates the incomplete integral at amplitude `phi` in radians.",
        "Evaluates the incomplete elliptic integral of the third kind in amplitude order. All amplitudes are in radians and share the same prepared characteristic, parameter and complete integral. Evaluates the incomplete integral at amplitude `phi` in radians.",
        "Prepares the third-kind elliptic integral at fixed characteristic n and parameter m. Use finite dimensionless values below one. Prepares a complete integral and its incomplete continuation. # Errors The characteristic or parameter is non-finite or greater than or equal to one, or the computed complete integral is unrepresentable.",
        "[`erf_numerical`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].",
        "[`erfc_numerical`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].",
        "Chebyshev polynomial of the first kind `T_n(x)` over the rationals. Uses logarithmically many integer operations before reduction.",
        "Returns `(T_n(x), U_n(x))` reduced modulo a positive modulus. The residues are in `0..modulus`, including for negative `x` and composite moduli. Runs in `O(log n)` modular operations for any `u64` degree. A zero modulus gives `None`.",
        "Chebyshev polynomial of the second kind `U_n(x)` over the rationals. Uses logarithmically many integer operations before reduction.",
        "`H_n(x)` over the rationals.", "`L_n(x)` over the rationals.",
        "`P_n(x)` over the rationals.",
        "The Anger function `J_\u03bd(x) = (1/\u03c0) \u222b_0^\u03c0 cos(\u03bd\u03b8 - x sin \u03b8) d\u03b8` for real `\u03bd` and `x`.",
        "Appell's first double hypergeometric function `F_1(a; b_1, b_2; c; x, y)` `= \u03a3_{m,n\u22650} (a)_{m+n} (b_1)_m (b_2)_n / ((c)_{m+n} m! n!) x^m y^n`.",
        "Modified Bessel function of the first kind `I_n(x)` for integer order. Use [`bessel_in_scaled`] or [`bessel_in_log_abs`] when the unscaled magnitude is outside the floating-point range. Nonfinite inputs return NaN.",
        "Natural logarithm of the real magnitude `|I_n(x)|` for integer order. The logarithm remains available after either value representation underflows or overflows. The result is even in both arguments. It is zero for `n = 0, x = 0`, negative infinity for nonzero order at zero, and positive infinity for `x = \u00b1\u221e`. A NaN argument returns NaN.",
        "Exponentially scaled modified Bessel function `exp(-|x|) I_n(x)` for integer order. Its limit at either infinity is zero.",
        "Bessel function of the first kind `J_n(x)` for integer order. Small arguments (`x^2/4 <= n + 1`) use the power series; large arguments use the Hankel asymptotic expansion (with the phase reduced exactly, so the accuracy does not degrade with `x`, cost `O(1)`); everything else uses Miller's backward recurrence started at an asymptotically chosen order (`~ max(n, x) + 20 max(n, x)^(1/3)`) and normalised by `J_0 + 2 sum J_{2k} = 1`.",
        "Carlson's `R_D(x, y, z)` for finite non-negative `x, y`, not both zero, and finite positive `z`; invalid arguments or nonconvergence return NaN.",
        "Carlson's symmetric elliptic integral `R_F(x, y, z)`. Arguments must be finite and non-negative, with at most one zero; invalid arguments or failure of bounded duplication return NaN.",
        "Carlson's symmetric elliptic integral of the second kind `R_G(x, y, z)`. The arguments must be nonnegative and finite. Invalid arguments return NaN. Two zero arguments are allowed, with `R_G(0, 0, z) = sqrt(z) / 2`. The implementation uses [DLMF 19.21.10](https://dlmf.nist.gov/19.21.E10) with the median argument in its distinguished position to avoid cancellation.",
        "The Coulomb normalising constant `C_l(\u03b7)` (DLMF 33.2.5), `C_l(\u03b7) = 2^l e^{-\u03c0\u03b7/2} |\u0393(l + 1 + i\u03b7)| / \u0393(2l + 2)`, evaluated in logarithmic form so that large `|\u03b7|` neither overflows nor underflows prematurely.",
        "The regular Coulomb function `F_l(\u03b7, \u03c1)` (DLMF 33.2.4). Domain and accuracy: see [`coulomb_fg`]; `None` where no representation is available.",
        "The derivative `dF_l/d\u03c1` of the regular Coulomb function. Domain and accuracy: see [`coulomb_fg`].",
        "The regular and irregular Coulomb functions and their derivatives. `F_l(\u03b7, \u03c1)` is the solution regular at the origin and `G_l(\u03b7, \u03c1)` the irregular solution normalised by the Wronskian `F' G - F G' = 1` (so that `G ~ cos \u03b8` at large `\u03c1`). The dimensionless variables are the Sommerfeld parameter `\u03b7` and `\u03c1 = k r`; `l \u2265 0` is the integer angular momentum. The result is `None` outside the domain of the implemented representations (`\u03c1 \u2264 0`, non-finite arguments, `|\u03b7| > 10\u2076`, `l > 64`, or `24 < \u03c1 \u2264 34` outside the classically allowed region), and also when Steed's fraction fails to certify the physical solution. References: DLMF \u00a7\u00a733.2, 33.8 and 33.11; the series is evaluated in double-double arithmetic beyond `\u03c1 = 3` to defeat the `e^{2\u03c1}` cancellation.",
        "The irregular Coulomb function `G_l(\u03b7, \u03c1)`, normalised by `F' G - F G' = 1` (DLMF 33.2.6).",
        "The irregular Coulomb function `G_l(\u03b7, \u03c1)` and its derivative from the Tricomi function. (DLMF 33.2.7): `H\u207a = G + iF = e^{i\u03b8} (-2i\u03c1)^{l+1+i\u03b7} U(l + 1 + i\u03b7, 2l + 2, -2i\u03c1)` with `\u03b8 = \u03c1 - \u03b7 ln 2\u03c1 - l\u03c0/2 + \u03c3_l`, and `dU/d\u03c1 = 2i a U(a + 1, b + 1, -2i\u03c1)` for the derivative. Unlike Steed's continued fraction for `H\u207a'/H\u207a`, which converges slowly and loses digits at small `\u03c1`, this needs no certification; its accuracy is that of [`crate::kernels::hypergeometric::hyp_u_cplx`] on the imaginary axis. `None` for `\u03c1 <= 0`, `l > 64` or non-finite input.",
        "The derivative `dG_l/d\u03c1` of the irregular Coulomb function. Domain and accuracy: see [`coulomb_fg`].",
        "The Coulomb phase shift `\u03c3_l(\u03b7) = arg \u0393(l + 1 + i\u03b7)` (DLMF 33.2.10).",
        "Complete elliptic integral of the second kind `E(m)`, `m <= 1`.",
        "Incomplete elliptic integral of the second kind `E(phi, m)`. The amplitude `phi` must be finite and the real parameter `m <= 1`. Invalid amplitudes or parameters return NaN.",
        "Incomplete elliptic integral of the first kind `F(phi, m)`. The amplitude `phi` must be finite and the real parameter `m <= 1`. At `m = 1` the integral has signed infinity at or beyond the first pole `|phi| = pi / 2`. Invalid amplitudes or parameters return NaN.",
        "Complete elliptic integral of the first kind `K(m)`, `m < 1`.",
        "Kummer confluent hypergeometric function `1F1(a; b; x)`.",
        "Gauss hypergeometric function `2F1(a, b; c; z)` for real arguments. * `z = 0` and terminating series (`a` or `b` a non-positive integer) are evaluated exactly for any `z`; * `0 <= z <= 0.6`: power series; * `0.6 < z < 1`: the `1 - z` connection formula, including the logarithmic cases when `c - a - b` is an integer (digamma expansions of Abramowitz-Stegun 15.3.10-15.3.11) and Euler's transformation for negative integer `c - a - b`; * `z < 0`: Pfaff's transformation `z -> z / (z - 1)` followed by the above, which also realises the `1 / (1 - z)` and `1 / z` continuations for large `|z|`; * `z = 1`: Gauss's summation theorem (infinite when divergent); * `z > 1`: the value is complex in general, so NaN, except for polynomials (terminating series, or `c - a`, `c - b` a non-positive integer with integer `c - a - b`). Accuracy is about `1e-13` generically; when `c - a - b` is within `1e-4` of, but not equal to, an integer the evaluation switches to the analytic continuation of the differential equation, which has no cancellation (exact integers use the logarithmic limit formulas). A non-positive integer `c` (other than a terminating series) gives NaN. For `z > 1` use [`hyp2f1_complex`].",
        "Gauss hypergeometric function `2F1(a, b; c; z)` for a complex argument and real parameters, on the principal branch (cut along `[1, inf)`; real `z > 1` gives the limit from the upper half plane).",
        "Jacobi amplitude `am(u | m)`, the continuous real inverse of [`elliptic_f`] for `m` in `[0, 1]`.",
        "Jacobi elliptic functions `(sn, cn, dn)` of `u` with parameter `m = k^2` in `[0, 1]`. Nonfinite amplitudes or invalid parameters give three NaNs.",
        "Evaluates the Jacobi elliptic functions at several points with one parameter. Each output row holds `[sn, cn, dn]` for the corresponding input. Invalid arguments produce the same NaN rows as [`jacobi_elliptic`]. The Landen plan is reused across all rows, and large batches can run in parallel without changing result order or arithmetic.",
        "The Kelvin function `bei_\u03bd(x) = Im J_\u03bd(x e^(3\u03c0i/4))`; domain and accuracy as [`kelvin_ber`].",
        "The Kelvin function `ber_\u03bd(x) = Re J_\u03bd(x e^(3\u03c0i/4))` for an integer order `\u03bd <= 100` and `x >= 0`.",
        "The Kelvin function `kei_\u03bd(x)`, the imaginary part of `e^(-i\u03c0\u03bd/2) K_\u03bd(x e^(i\u03c0/4))`; domain and accuracy as [`kelvin_ker`].",
        "The Kelvin function `ker_\u03bd(x) + i kei_\u03bd(x) = e^(-i\u03c0\u03bd/2) K_\u03bd(x e^(i\u03c0/4))` for an integer order `\u03bd <= 100` and `x > 0`.",
        "The Lerch transcendent `\u03a6(z, s, a) = \u03a3_{k=0}^\u221e z^k / (k+a)^s` for real `s`, real `a > 0` and `z < 1` (the standard range).",
        "The polylogarithm `Li_s(z)` for real `s` and real `z`. Real on `z <= 1` (including the negative axis); for `z > 1` the principal value is complex in general and `NaN` is returned, except for non-positive integer `s`, where `Li_{-n}(z)` is the real rational function `z A_n(z)/(1-z)^(n+1)`. At `z = 1` the analytic continuation `\u03b6(s)` is returned (and `+\u221e` at the pole `s = 1`).",
        "The polylogarithm `Li_s(z)` for complex `s` and `z`, on the principal branch (cut along `[1, \u221e)`).",
        "The Weber function `E_\u03bd(x) = (1/\u03c0) \u222b_0^\u03c0 sin(\u03bd\u03b8 - x sin \u03b8) d\u03b8` for real `\u03bd` and `x`.",
        "[`gamma_numerical`] over a slice. Elements are independent and the outputs are collected in input order, so the result is bit-identical to calling the scalar function on each element, for every thread count.",
        "The generalised Laguerre polynomial `L_n^{(\u03b1)}(x)` by the three-term recurrence.",
        "Computes the (physicists') Hermite polynomial `H_n(x)`.",
        "Computes the Hurwitz zeta function \u03b6(s, q) for real s and q. Defined as \u03a3_{n=0}^\u221e 1/(n+q)^s.",
        "Computes the incomplete beta function, B(x; a, b) = \u222b\u2080\u02e3 t^(a-1) (1-t)^(b-1) dt.",
        "Computes the Laguerre polynomial `L_n(x)`.",
        "The Lambert W function: `W(x) e^{W(x)} = x`. `upper = true` is the principal branch `W\u2080` (defined for `x \u2265 -1/e`); `upper = false` the branch `W\u208b\u2081` (for `-1/e \u2264 x < 0`). NaN outside the domain. Halley's iteration from a branch-appropriate start.",
        "Computes the Legendre polynomial `P_n(x)` using recurrence relation.",
        "Natural logarithm of the modified Bessel function `K_\u03bd(x)` for real order. Closed forms or a positive integral centered and scaled at its peak keep the result available even if `K_\u03bd(x)` itself overflows or underflows. Even in `\u03bd`. At zero the result is positive infinity, and at positive infinity it is negative infinity for finite order. Negative arguments and an infinite argument of infinite order give NaN.",
        "Computes the natural logarithm of the beta function, ln(B(a, b)). Defined for `a, b > 0`; other arguments give NaN (see [`beta_numerical`]).",
        "[`ln_gamma_numerical`] over a slice, with the same bit-identical contract as [`gamma_batch`].",
        "Computes the lower incomplete gamma function, \u03b3(s, x) = \u222b\u2080\u02e3 t^(s-1) e^(-t) dt. Uses series expansion for computation. Computed as `P(s, x) Gamma(s)` from the regularised function (series or continued fraction), so it is accurate for large `x` too.",
        "Computes the polygamma function \u03c8^(n)(z) for real z > 0 and integer n >= 0. The 0-th polygamma is the digamma function.",
        "Computes the regularized incomplete beta function, `I_x(a`, b) = B(x; a, b) / B(a, b). Uses continued fraction expansion for better convergence.",
        "The regularised lower incomplete gamma function `P(s, x)`, accurate in both tails; `P(0, x) = 1` for `x > 0` and NaN for `s < 0` or `x < 0`.",
        "The regularised upper incomplete gamma function `Q(s, x) = 1 - P(s, x)`, computed directly so that `Q` of order `1e-300` keeps its digits.",
        "The upper incomplete gamma function `Gamma(s, x)`, computed from the complementary tail (no cancellation against `Gamma(s)`); defined for every real `s` when `x > 0` (`s <= 0` through the recurrence and `E1`).",
        "Largest absolute eigenvalue. Spectral radius estimate through [`eigenvalues`]. # Errors As [`eigenvalues`].",
        "Clamped cubic spline with explicit endpoint derivatives, each in ordinate per abscissa units. Cubic spline interpolation with selectable boundary conditions (`Natural`, `Clamped`, or `NotAKnot`). # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`], [`InterpError::Empty`], or [`InterpError::Domain`] when knots are not strictly increasing.",
        "Not-a-knot cubic spline with independent coordinate and ordinate dimensions. Cubic spline interpolation with selectable boundary conditions (`Natural`, `Clamped`, or `NotAKnot`). # Errors [`InterpError::ShapeMismatch`], [`InterpError::Empty`], or [`InterpError::Domain`] when knots are not strictly increasing.",
        "Standard error of the mean. Computes the sample standard error of the mean (SEM = `std_dev` / sqrt(n)).",
        "Performs a chi-squared goodness-of-fit test. Tests if observed frequencies match expected frequencies. Returns (chi-squared statistic, p-value).",
        "Benjamini\u2013Hochberg adjusted p-values for false discovery rate control. The guarantee holds under independence or positive regression dependence of the null tests. Results retain the original order. An empty input yields an empty vector. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if any p-value is outside `[0, 1]` or non-finite.",
        "Benjamini\u2013Yekutieli adjusted p-values for false discovery rate control under arbitrary dependence. Results retain the original order. An empty input yields an empty vector.",
        "Holm step-down adjusted p-values for family-wise error rate control. Results retain the original order. An empty input yields an empty vector.",
        "One-way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) across `k >= 2` groups. Returns `TestResult` with `statistic = F`, `df = Some(df_between)`, and upper-tail `p_value`. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if fewer than 2 groups, any group is empty, total observations `<= groups.len()`, or within-group variance is zero.",
        "Pearson's chi-squared goodness-of-fit test. # Errors [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`] if lengths differ, or [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if fewer than 2 categories or any expected count is `<= 0`.",
        "Pearson's chi-squared test of independence on a row-major `rows \u00d7 cols` contingency table. # Errors [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`] if `observed.len() != rows * cols`, or [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if `rows < 2`, `cols < 2`, any entry is negative, or any row/column marginal total is zero.",
        "Gaussian Kernel Density Estimation evaluated at `points`. When `bandwidth` is `None`, uses Silverman's rule of thumb. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Empty`] if `samples` is empty, or [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if `bandwidth <= 0` or cannot be determined from a zero-spread sample.",
        "Kendall's tau-b rank correlation, adjusted for ties in either sample. Lexicographic sorting and merge-based inversion counting take `O(n log n)` time and `O(n)` scratch space. # Errors Returns an error for mismatched lengths, fewer than two observations, non-finite observations, or a constant sample.",
        "Two-sample Kolmogorov\u2013Smirnov test comparing two empirical distributions. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Empty`] when either sample is empty.",
        "Lasso (L1-regularized) linear regression via coordinate descent, minimizing `(1 / (2n)) ||y - X\u03b2||\u2082\u00b2 + alpha ||\u03b2||\u2081`.",
        "Binary logistic regression via Newton-Raphson (IRLS). Expects `y[i]` in `[0, 1]` (typically `0.0` or `1.0`). # Errors [`StatsExtError::Empty`], [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`], [`StatsExtError::Domain`] for invalid `y` or tolerances, or [`StatsExtError::NotConverged`].",
        "Two-sided Mann\u2013Whitney U test (Wilcoxon rank-sum test) with tie-corrected normal approximation.",
        "Ordinary least squares with a minimum-norm solution for deficient designs. # Errors Rejects invalid dimensions, non-finite data or results, or failed decomposition.",
        "Principal Component Analysis via SVD of the mean-centered data matrix. `data` is row-major `rows \u00d7 cols`. If `n_components` is `None`, retains `min(rows, cols)` components. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Empty`], [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`], [`StatsExtError::Domain`] when `n_components` is 0 or exceeds `min(rows, cols)`, or [`StatsExtError::NotConverged`].",
        "Ridge (L2-regularized) linear regression minimizing `||y - X\u03b2||\u2082\u00b2 + alpha ||\u03b2||\u2082\u00b2`. Uses the thin SVD of the design without allocating a square feature matrix. # Errors Rejects invalid dimensions, non-finite data or results, and failed decomposition. A negative or non-finite `alpha` is invalid. For `alpha == 0`, a design without full column rank returns [`StatsExtError::Singular`].",
        "Spearman's rank correlation coefficient (with average ranks for ties). # Errors [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`] when lengths differ, or [`StatsExtError::Domain`] when either sample has zero variance.",
        "One-sample Student's t-test against population mean `mu0`. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Domain`] for fewer than two observations, non-finite observations or null mean, or a sample with zero variance.",
        "Paired-samples t-test on `left - right`. # Errors [`StatsExtError::ShapeMismatch`] if lengths differ, or [`StatsExtError::Domain`] if fewer than 2 pairs or differences have zero variance.",
        "Two-sample t-test with pooled variance or Welch's unequal-variance formula. # Errors [`StatsExtError::Domain`] for fewer than two observations per sample, non-finite data, or zero combined standard error.",
        "Kruskal\u2013Wallis rank test for independently sized groups encoded as CSR offsets/values. Samples share one measurement unit. The tie-adjusted statistic, chi-square right-tail probability and degrees of freedom are dimensionless. All pooled samples tied gives a domain error. Kruskal\u2013Wallis rank test across independent groups. Pools all observations, assigns average ranks to ties, and returns the tie-corrected H statistic and its upper-tail chi-squared approximation with `df = groups.len() - 1`. # Errors Returns [`StatsExtError::Empty`] for no groups or an empty group. Returns [`StatsExtError::Domain`] for one group, non-finite observations, all observations tied, or pooled data that cannot be allocated.",
        "Finds the mode (most frequent value) of a slice of data. For continuous data, rounds to a specified number of decimal places. Returns None if no mode exists (all values unique or empty).",
        "One-way ANOVA on independently sized groups encoded as CSR offsets/values. Input groups have one shared measurement unit; F statistic and p-value are dimensionless. Performs a One-Way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) test. Tests the null hypothesis that the means of two or more groups are equal. # Arguments * `groups` - A slice of slices, where each inner slice is a group of data. # Returns A tuple containing the F-statistic and the p-value.",
        "Measured percentile with a private mutable copy of the input. The native sorting/selection routine cannot mutate the caller's tensor; percentile p is dimensionless in 0 through 100. Computes the p-th percentile of a slice of data. # Arguments * `data` - A mutable slice of `f64` data points. * `p` - The desired percentile (e.g., 50.0 for median). # Returns The p-th percentile of the data (fractional `p` allowed), or NaN for empty data, data containing NaN, or `p` outside `0..=100`.",
        "Least-squares regression on separately measured equal-length predictors and responses. The slope carries response divided by predictor units, and the intercept carries response units. Finite moments are recovered at extreme coordinate scales when possible. Fits the least-squares line `y = intercept + slope * x` to paired samples. Returns `(slope, intercept)`. Empty and nonfinite samples return two NaNs. A constant predictor returns a NaN slope and the response mean. The calculation borrows the observations and rescales centered moments when the unscaled moments exceed the Float range.",
        "Performs an independent two-sample t-test to determine if two samples have different means. # Returns A tuple containing the t-statistic and the p-value.",
        "Computes the variance of a slice of data with a specified variance type (Population or Sample). This implementation uses Welford's algorithm to compute the variance in a single pass, which is highly numerically stable even for very large numbers or shifted datasets. # Arguments * `data` - A slice of `f64` data points. * `var_type` - The type of variance to compute (Population or Sample). # Returns `Some(variance)` if `data.len() >= 2`, otherwise `None`.",
        "Performs Welch's t-test for two samples with potentially unequal variances. Returns (t-statistic, p-value).",
        "Sample standard deviation. Computes the standard deviation of a slice of data. # Arguments * `data` - A slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The standard deviation of the data as an `f64`.",
        "Thin SVD A = U diag(s) V^T; returns u, s and v. Thin singular-value decomposition using the bidiagonal SVD backend. Rank-deficient matrices retain orthonormal null-space columns. No Gram matrix is formed, so small singular values do not lose accuracy through squaring the condition number. # Errors Returns [`DenseError::Shape`] for inconsistent storage or non-finite input, and [`DenseError::NoConvergence`] if the backend does not converge.",
        "Dimension-aware serial quadrature with a strict callback evaluation budget. Fixed rules: a,b,order,max_evaluations,callback. Romberg/tanh-sinh: a,b,tolerance,max_levels,max_evaluations,callback. Semi-infinite rules: finite_bound,tolerance,max_evaluations,callback; left-infinite integrates (-infinity,bound], right-infinite [bound,infinity). Full infinite rule: tolerance,max_evaluations,callback. Adaptive results include value,error,evaluations,converged; fixed rules report value,evaluations. Callback failures propagate unchanged. Tanh-sinh quadrature with an explicit refinement budget. A zero budget returns an unconverged estimate. Refinement is capped at 20 levels to keep its exponentially growing node count bounded.",
        "Undo repeated first differences using the original leading samples. Input vectors share a dimension, which the result preserves. The returned length is the sum of the input lengths. Inverse of [`difference`]. `initial` holds the first `initial.len()` values of the undifferenced series, which fixes the integration order. The returned vector has `d.len() + initial.len()` entries. An empty `initial` copies `d` unchanged.",
        "Filter a homogeneous-dimensional linear Gaussian state-space model with explicit initial mean and covariance. Return filtered means, predicted means, filtered covariance matrices, and log likelihood relative to a positive observation scale. Runs the Kalman filter for a linear Gaussian state-space model `x_t = F x_{t-1} + w_t` with `w_t ~ N(0, Q)` and `y_t = H x_t + v_t` with `v_t ~ N(0, R)`, started from `x_0 ~ N(x0, p0)`. # Errors Returns a diagnostic for invalid shapes, non-finite data, nonsymmetric or indefinite model covariances and an innovation covariance that is not positive definite. Model covariances may be positive semidefinite.",
        "Rauch\u2013Tung\u2013Striebel smoothed state means with explicit initial mean and covariance. State components share a dimension and observations may have a different common dimension. Returns one state per observation. Rauch\u2013Tung\u2013Striebel smoothed state means. Runs the filter and then the backward recursion `x_t^s = x_t + C_t (x_{t+1}^s - x_{t+1})` with `C_t = P_t F' P_{t+1}^{-1}`, returning one smoothed state per observation. # Errors Returns the errors of [`kalman_filter`], plus a message when a predicted covariance is singular and the smoother cannot be formed.",
        "Compute means and variances for complete windows of a finite real time series. Use ddof 0 for population variance or ddof 1 for sample variance. The result contains empty vectors when the positive window width exceeds the input length. Means retain sample units and variances square them. Computes a mean and variance for every complete window in `samples`. Result `i` describes `samples[i..i + window]`. The result is empty when `window` exceeds the input length. Pass `ddof = 0` for population variance or `ddof = 1` for sample variance. Samples are always required to be finite. Windows are evaluated in linear time. Windows of at most 16 samples use two passes around an anchor when the whole series has the same sign, its magnitudes are within a factor of two, and their exponents leave enough headroom to square the deviations. For other series and window widths, fixed blocks of window starts combine suffix and prefix moments. Large inputs use the configured worker pool, with identical arithmetic order for any worker count. # Errors Returns [`RollingError`] for a zero window width, `ddof >= window`, a non-finite sample, an overflowing variance, or host cancellation. # Example ``` use rssn::kernels::timeseries::rolling::RollingMoment; use rssn::kernels::timeseries::rolling::rolling_moments; assert_eq!( rolling_moments(&[1.0, 3.0, 5.0], 2, 0)?, vec![ RollingMoment { mean: 2.0, variance: 1.0 }, RollingMoment { mean: 4.0, variance: 1.0 }, ], ); ```",
        "The homology Betti number `b_k` of a closed complex: `dim ker d_k - rank d_{k+1}`.",
        "The Betti numbers `b_0 ..= b_max_dim` of the Vietoris-Rips complex at radius `epsilon`.",
        "The boundary matrix of `cols` (k-simplices) onto `rows` ((k-1)-simplices); `rows.len()` rows and `cols.len()` columns.",
        "The simplices of `simplices` closed under faces, without duplicates, sorted by dimension and then lexicographically. Each simplex is sorted and deduplicated first; empty simplices are dropped.",
        "The cohomology Betti number `b^k`, from the ranks of the coboundary matrices (the transposes of the boundary matrices).",
        "The dimension of a closed complex (`None` if empty).",
        "The persistence diagrams of a point cloud, `max_dim` and below. This is a compatibility wrapper around [`persistent_homology_rips`]: the diagrams are now exact, not sampled on a grid of radii, so `steps` is ignored. Intervals of classes still alive at `max_epsilon` are closed at `max_epsilon` (the convention of the former grid-based routine; use [`persistent_homology_rips`] to get `f64::INFINITY` instead). If the filtration is too large to build, empty diagrams are returned.",
        "The Euclidean distance between two points.",
        "The Euler characteristic `sum (-1)^k #k-simplices`.",
        "The connected components of a graph given by adjacency lists (BFS). Neighbours outside `0..adj.len()` are ignored.",
        "The triangulated `width x height` grid: two triangles per cell.",
        "The product of two integer matrices (`a` is `r x m`, `b` is `m x c`).",
        "The rank of an integer matrix, computed exactly over the rationals by Gaussian elimination.",
        "Exact persistent homology over `Z/2` of a filtered simplicial complex. `filtration` lists `(value, simplex)` pairs in any order; every proper face of a simplex must be present with a value no larger than the simplex's. A repeated simplex enters at its earliest value, and both signs of zero share a filtration level. Simplices are ordered by value, then dimension, then lexicographically. The boundary matrix is reduced by the standard left-to-right column algorithm with *clearing* (the twist optimisation): dimensions are processed from the top down, and a column that is the pivot of a reduced column of the next dimension is known to reduce to zero and is skipped. Returns one [`PersistenceDiagram`] per dimension `0..=max_dim`; each interval is an exact `(birth, death)` pair of filtration values and a class that never dies has `death = f64::INFINITY`. Zero-length intervals are omitted. Intervals are sorted by birth then death. # Errors [`PersistenceError::InvalidFiltration`] if the input is not a filtration.",
        "Exact Vietoris-Rips persistent homology of a point cloud. The filtration contains every simplex up to dimension `max_dim + 1` whose edges are all at most `max_epsilon` long, entering at its diameter (the longest edge); it is processed by [`persistent_homology`], so every `(birth, death)` pair is exact (a distance between two input points). Classes alive at `max_epsilon` get `death = f64::INFINITY`. # Errors [`PersistenceError::TooManySimplices`] if more than `simplex_limit` simplices are needed (use [`DEFAULT_SIMPLEX_LIMIT`] when in doubt).",
        "The boundary of an oriented simplex: `(sign, face)` pairs, with sign `(-1)^i` for the face omitting vertex `i`. Empty for a vertex.",
        "The `k`-simplices of a complex, in order.",
        "The triangulated torus obtained from an `m x n` grid by identifying opposite edges (a valid simplicial complex for `m, n >= 3`).",
        "The transpose of an integer matrix with `cols` columns.",
        "Whether `d_k d_{k+1} = 0` for every `k`.",
        "Whether the coboundaries satisfy `d^{k+1} d^k = 0` for every `k`.",
        "The Vietoris-Rips complex of a point cloud. It holds every set of points that are pairwise within `epsilon`, up to dimension `max_dim`. Simplices are returned by increasing dimension, lexicographically within a dimension.",
        "The Vietoris-Rips filtration restricted to its 1-skeleton: for each of `steps + 1` radii `max_epsilon * s / steps` the vertices and the edges no longer than the radius.",
        "Inverts [`rfft`]: reconstructs the real signal of length `n` from its `n/2 + 1` non-redundant bins, with the `1/n` normalisation of [`ifft`].",
        "Computes the real-input FFT: the `n/2 + 1` non-redundant bins of the DFT of a real sequence.",
        "Transpose a dense matrix. Transpose (cache-blocked in 32x32 tiles and parallelised for large matrices).",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. Vertices on the boundary (on an edge with a single triangle).",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. The mesh without unused vertices.",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities.",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. Residual error indicators `\u03b7_T` of a P1 solution (one per triangle).",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. The `L\u00b2` error against `exact`, by quadrature on every element.",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. The L-shaped domain `[-1, 1]\u00b2 \\\\ [0, 1] \u00d7 [-1, 0]` with mesh size `1/n`.",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. The maximum nodal error against `exact`.",
        "Prepares the geometry, boundary flags and reference quadrature of a triangular mesh for repeated P1 or P2 solves. Use degree 1 or 2. The plan retains the mesh's physical frame and admits at most 1024 degrees of freedom. Prepares the P1 or P2 finite-element layout on `mesh`. # Errors Rejects non-finite vertex coordinates, invalid indices and degenerate triangles.",
        "Returns the number of nodal degrees of freedom, including prescribed boundary nodes, in a prepared triangular P1 or P2 mesh. The number of finite-element degrees of freedom.",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities.",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. The rectangle `[x0, x1] \u00d7 [y0, y1]` cut into `nx \u00d7 ny` squares, each split into two triangles.",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. Refines the triangles flagged in `marked` (and as many neighbours as conformity needs) by bisection.",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. Solves the problem on `mesh` with elements of `degree`. # Errors For invalid mesh geometry, callback values or a non-finite solve.",
        "Actual triangular FEM with explicit Length/Time/field references, variable compiled diffusion/reaction/source/Dirichlet data, original CG/P1/P2/refinement algorithms and bounded native storage/work. Callback arguments and outputs are coherent physical quantities. Adaptive P1 solution. Solve, estimate, mark the smallest set of triangles carrying the fraction `theta` of `\u03a3 \u03b7\u00b2` (D\u00f6rfler), refine; `steps` times. Returns the final mesh, solution and the estimator history `(degrees of freedom, (\u03a3 \u03b7\u00b2)^(1/2))`. # Errors A degenerate triangle.",
        "Solves a triangular diffusion\u2013reaction problem using a prepared mesh. The problem must use the same physical references. Each solve evaluates its diffusion, reaction, source and Dirichlet data and returns the ordinary triangular solution handle. Solves another problem on the prepared layout. # Errors Rejects invalid callback values or non-finite assembled systems or solutions.",
        "Measured nonlinear problem with explicit coordinate_scale and value_scale, dimensionless tolerance, max_iterations and max_evaluations controls. Solves in normalized coordinates/residuals. Arguments: initial, controls, optional lower/upper, objective/residual, optional gradient. Gradient units are objective/coordinate. Homogeneous coordinate and residual vectors; callback failures propagate and budgets are shared. Trust-region method with the dogleg step. The Hessian is supplied by `hess`; pass `None` for a finite-difference Hessian from gradients.",
        "Tensor-train SVD of row-major data; rank cap must be positive. Returns dimensionless normalized cores, shape, scale, Frobenius truncation error and tolerance status; reconstruction multiplies the core contraction by scale. Compress a finite, nonempty dense tensor with TT-SVD. Each split is assigned relative tolerance / sqrt(order - 1). A rank cap may prevent satisfying that tolerance; inspect `tolerance_met` and the returned truncation estimate. Zero tensors use bond rank one. A one-mode tensor is stored exactly. Input is normalized before decomposition to avoid squaring the input's scale in intermediate norm computations. # Errors Returns an error for invalid shape/options, non-finite input, SVD failure, allocation/size overflow or unrepresentable rescaling.",
        "Returns the dimensionless hyperbolic geodesic distance between two complex points with positive imaginary parts. Hyperbolic distance in the upper half-plane `Im(z) > 0`.",
        "Maps a dimensionless complex point in the open upper half-plane to the open Poincare unit disk. A nonpositive input imaginary part or a result that rounds outside the open disk produces a domain error. Cayley transform mapping the upper half-plane `{ Im(z) > 0 }` to the unit disk `{ |w| < 1 }`: `w = (z - i) / (z + i)`. Returns `None` at `z = -i`.",
        "Population variance; zero for an empty sample. Computes the variance of a slice of f64 values (defaults to population variance). # Arguments * `data` - An immutable slice of `f64` data points. # Returns The variance of the data as an `f64`.",
        "Element-wise sum of equal-length vectors. Adds two vectors element-wise. Formula: $\\\\mathbf{r}_i = \\\\mathbf{v1}_i + \\\\mathbf{v2}_i$ # Arguments * `v1` - The first vector. * `v2` - The second vector. # Returns * `Ok(Vec<f64>)` - The sum of the two vectors. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "`num_vector_axpby(alpha, x, beta, y)` computes alpha*x + beta*y with the native SIMD kernel. Both scaled terms must have the same dimension. Returns a new vector in those units. Computes `y[i] = alpha * x[i] + beta * y[i]` in place (BLAS-1 AXPBY). # Errors Returns an error if `x` and `y` have different lengths.",
        "`num_vector_axpy(alpha, x, y)` computes alpha*x + y with the native SIMD kernel. The scaled increment and the retained input y must have the same dimension. Returns a new vector. Computes `y[i] += alpha * x[i]` in place (BLAS-1 AXPY). # Errors Returns an error if `x` and `y` have different lengths.",
        "Computes the element-wise (Hadamard) quotient `v1[i] / v2[i]`. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "Computes the element-wise (Hadamard) product `v1[i] * v2[i]`. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "Checks if two vectors are orthogonal. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "Checks if two vectors are parallel. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "Linearly interpolates between two vectors. Formula: $\\\\mathbf{r} = (1 - t)\\\\mathbf{v1} + t\\\\mathbf{v2}$ # Arguments * `t` - Interpolation factor, typically in $[0, 1]$. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "`num_vector_outer_product(left, right)` returns a row-major matrix with shape [left length, right length] and product units. The result may contain at most 8388608 entries. Computes the outer product $u v^T$ as a dense matrix of shape `(u.len(), v.len())`.",
        "Element-wise difference of equal-length vectors. Subtracts one vector from another element-wise. Formula: $\\\\mathbf{r}_i = \\\\mathbf{v1}_i - \\\\mathbf{v2}_i$ # Arguments * `v1` - The first vector (minuend). * `v2` - The second vector (subtrahend). # Returns * `Ok(Vec<f64>)` - The difference of the two vectors. # Errors Returns an error if the vectors have different lengths.",
        "Planar Voronoi diagram dual to Delaunay, with measured vertices and dimensionless topology/unit ray directions. Input requires 3..=1024 finite nondegenerate sites. Cells are CSR-style cell_offsets/cell_indices; unbounded cells are described by ray_vertices/ray_directions rather than fabricated finite polygons. Computes the 2D Voronoi diagram of `points` as the dual of [`delaunay_2d`].",
        "Discrete fractional Fourier transform of order `a` of the complex signal `x`.",
        "The row-major matrix of the discrete fractional Fourier transform of order `a`. `a = 1` is the unitary DFT. The unitary transforms compose by addition of their orders. For repeated orders, construct one [`FrftPlan`]. # Errors Returns an error for `n < 2`, a non-finite order, an unrepresentable matrix size or an eigensolver failure.",
        "Reconstructs a signal from the complete leaf level of [`wavelet_packet`]. # Errors [`WaveletError::Shape`] when the number of bands is not a power of two, their lengths differ, or they are empty.",
        "Reconstructs a periodic signal from [`swt`] bands. Uses the dual synthesis filters and averages all decimation phases. Input bands must be equal length and in the order returned by [`swt`]. # Errors Errors for malformed or non-finite bands, unsupported level counts or non-finite reconstruction.",
        "Periodic stationary wavelet transform without decimation. Returns `[approximation_L, detail_L, ..., detail_1]`. Each band has the same length as `samples`. The filter-tap spacing doubles each level, giving a shift-equivariant decomposition. Odd lengths are supported. At least one level is required, and the longest tap spacing must be strictly less than the signal length. # Errors Errors for an unsupported length or level count or non-finite inputs or computed coefficients.",
        "Denoises a signal by zeroing wavelet details below a magnitude threshold. Uses the same decomposition and threshold units as [`wavelet_soft_threshold`]. A detail coefficient exactly at the threshold is retained. # Errors As [`wavelet_soft_threshold`].",
        "Decomposes both the low and high branches of a periodic wavelet transform. Returns `2^levels` equally sized leaf bands in tree order, with each low child before its high sibling. For example, level two returns `LL`, `LH`, `HL`, `HH`. Level zero returns one band containing the input. # Errors [`WaveletError::TooManyLevels`] when the signal is empty or its length is incompatible with the requested depth.",
        "Denoises a signal by soft-thresholding its wavelet detail coefficients. Keeps the coarsest approximation and uses [`wavedec_with`] with the chosen wavelet and boundary rule. Detail magnitudes are shrunk toward zero by the threshold, in the same units as `samples`. # Errors Errors for non-finite data, a negative threshold, an unrepresentable signal or unsupported depth or shape.",
        "Computes the Weierstrass invariants g2 and g3 as a named complex record. The dimensionless arguments omega1 and omega2 are nonzero, non-collinear half-periods. An invalid lattice or a nonfinite result produces a domain error. Weierstrass invariants `(g2, g3)` for half-periods `(omega1, omega2)`.",
        "Numerically evaluates the Weierstrass derivative at z. The arguments omega1 and omega2 are nonzero, non-collinear half-periods, so the basic periods are 2*omega1 and 2*omega2. All arguments and the result are dimensionless. A detected lattice pole, an invalid lattice or a nonfinite result produces a domain error. Weierstrass elliptic derivative `wp'(z; omega1, omega2)` via central complex step or theta.",
        "Sample z scores. Computes the z-scores (standard scores) for each data point. z = (x - mean) / `std_dev`",
        "`ode_asymptotic(eq, y(x), x0, n, x1, k)` evaluates formal solution `k` of `eq` at `x1` using up to `n` terms around singular point `x0`. Returns the real part, imaginary part and scaled magnitude of the first omitted term. If all terms are used, the final supplied term gives the estimate. `k` starts at zero.",
        "`ode_borel(eq, y(x), x0, n, x1, k)` uses Borel\u2013Pad\u00e9 resummation to evaluate formal solution `k` of `eq` at `x1` with `n` terms around singular point `x0`. Returns the real part, imaginary part, error estimate and Stokes indicator. `k` starts at zero.",
        "`ode_classify(ode, y)`: classifies an ordinary differential equation `ode` for dependent function `y`, returning a list of applicable solver classes.",
        "`ode_darboux(dx, dy, x, y, [max_deg])`: finds Darboux polynomials and cofactors `[P, k]` for planar autonomous system `x' = dx, y' = dy`.",
        "`ode_etdrk4(L, f, vars, t, u0, t0, t1, steps)`: `u(t1)` for `u' = L u + f(t, u)` by fourth-order exponential time differencing.",
        "`ode_fourier(ode, y)`: solves linear constant-coefficient `ode` for `y` using Fourier transform.",
        "`ode_frobenius(eq, y(x), x0, n)` expands a homogeneous linear second-order equation about rational `x0` or `oo`, with order `n` up to 60. Returns the solution, indicial roots or formal exponent pairs, and a recurrence equation when available. The recurrence entry is an empty list otherwise.",
        "`ode_imex(f_ex, f_im, vars, t, y0, t0, t1, steps, scheme)`: `y(t1)` for `y' = f_ex + f_im` with explicit `f_ex` and stiff implicit `f_im` using `euler`, `ars222`, or `ars343`.",
        "`ode_jacobi_last_multiplier(dx, dy, x, y, [max_deg])`: computes a Jacobi last multiplier (integrating factor) for planar system `x' = dx, y' = dy`.",
        "`ode_series(ode, y, x0, order)`: computes a power-series solution `y(x) = ...` of `ode` about `x0` up to `order`.",
        "`ode_solve(rhs, vars, t, y0, t0, t1, tol, method)`: `y(t1)` for `y' = rhs(t, y)` with `y(t0) = y0` using `dopri5`, `rosenbrock23`, `radau5`, `bdf`, `sdirk3`, or `exprb32`.",
        "`ode_symmetries(ode, y)`: finds Lie point symmetry generators `[xi, eta]` admitted by `ode` for dependent function `y`.",
        "`ode_symplectic(accel, vars, q0, p0, t0, t1, steps, method)`: `list(q, p)` at `t1` for `q' = p`, `p' = accel(q)` using `verlet`, `yoshida4`, `yoshida6`, or `yoshida8`.",
        "`odeint(f, y, x, x0, y0, x1)`: numerically integrates `dy/dx = f(x, y)` from `(x0, y0)` to `x1`.",
        "`omega(n)`: number of distinct prime factors of `n`.",
        "`one_parameter_subgroup(A, t)`: one-parameter subgroup `exp_map(t * A)` generated by matrix `A`.",
        "`oo`: positive real infinity (`+infinity`).",
        "`op_add(A, B, ...)`: sum `A + B + ...` of linear operators.",
        "`op_apply(A, f)`: apply linear operator `A` to function or vector `f`.",
        "`op_commutator(A, B)`: commutator `[A, B] = A B - B A` of operators `A` and `B`.",
        "`op_compose(A, B, ...)`: composition `A o B o ...` of linear operators (rightmost acts first).",
        "`op_d(x)`: differential operator `f -> d/dx(f)`.",
        "`op_identity()`: identity linear operator `f -> f`.",
        "`op_int(a, x)`: Volterra integration operator `f -> int_a^x f(t) dt`.",
        "`op_laplacian(vars)`: Laplacian differential operator `sum_i d^2 / dx_i^2` over `vars`.",
        "`op_mul(g)`: linear multiplication operator `f -> g * f`.",
        "`op_power(A, n)`: `n`-fold composition `A^n` of linear operator `A`.",
        "`op_scale(c, A)`: linear operator `A` scaled by `c`.",
        "`operator_adjoint(A)`: formal `L^2` adjoint `A*` of differential/multiplication operator `A`.",
        "`or(p1, p2, ...)`: variadic logical disjunction, true when at least one operand is true.",
        "Computes an `nx` by `ny` 2D occupancy histogram of `points` over `[xmin, xmax] \u00d7 [ymin, ymax]`. Computes the density distribution of orbit points in a 2D histogram. # Arguments * `points` - The orbit trajectory. * `x_bins` - Number of bins in x direction. * `y_bins` - Number of bins in y direction. * `x_range` - Range for x axis. * `y_range` - Range for y axis. # Returns A 2D vector of counts for each bin.",
        "Computes the Shannon entropy of a 2D occupancy histogram `density`. Computes the entropy of an orbit from its density distribution. Uses Shannon entropy: H = -`sum(p_i` * `log(p_i)`) # Arguments * `density` - A 2D density histogram from `orbit_density`. # Returns The Shannon entropy of the distribution.",
        "`orbit_iterate(f, z, z0, n)`: symbolic `n`-fold iterate `f^{o n}(z0)`.",
        "`orthogonal_coefficients(f, basis, x, a, b)`: Fourier coefficients `<e_i, f> / <e_i, e_i>` of `f` in orthogonal `basis`.",
        "`orthogonalize(vecs)`: Gram-Schmidt orthogonal basis from a list of vectors.",
        "Constructs a 4x4 OpenGL-style orthographic projection matrix from `(left, right, bottom, top, near, far)`.",
        "`orthonormalize(vecs)`: Gram-Schmidt orthonormal basis from a list of vectors.",
        "`oscillatory_integral(f, g, x, a, b, w, n)`: `list(re, im)` of `int_a^b f(x) exp(i w g(x)) dx` by Levin collocation at `n` Chebyshev points.",
        "`outer(u, v)`: outer product matrix `u v^T` of vectors `u` and `v`.",
        "`pacf(x, max_lag)`: the partial autocorrelation of `x` for lags `1..=max_lag`, by the Durbin\u2013Levinson recursion. Partial autocorrelation function, from the Durbin\u2013Levinson recursion. Returns `max_lag + 1` values with `result[0] = 1`; entry `k` is the last coefficient of the order-`k` autoregression fitted to the sample autocorrelations.",
        "`parabolic_cylinder_d_complex(nur, nui, zr, zi)`: `list(re, im)` of `D_nu(z)`. Parabolic cylinder function `D_nu(z)` via asymptotic expansion for large `|z|` in `|arg(z)| < 3*pi/4` and `1F1` connection formula with `rgamma` elsewhere.",
        "`pareto(scale, shape)`: Pareto distribution with minimum scale `scale` and shape parameter `shape`.",
        "`partition_function(energies, T)`: canonical partition function `sum_i exp(-E_i / (k_B T))` over energy levels `energies`.",
        "`partition_list(n)`: list of all integer partitions of `n` in non-increasing part order.",
        "`partitions(n)`: integer partition function `p(n)`, counting ways to write `n` as a sum of positive integers.",
        "`partitions_distinct(n)`: number of integer partitions of `n` into distinct positive parts.",
        "`partitions_k(n, k)`: number of integer partitions of `n` into exactly `k` positive parts.",
        "`partitions_max(n, k)`: number of integer partitions of `n` whose parts are at most `k`.",
        "`path(g, t, t0, t1)`: parameterized contour `g(t)` as `t` runs from `t0` to `t1`.",
        "`pauli_matrices`: list of the three 2x2 Pauli matrices `list(pauli_x, pauli_y, pauli_z)`.",
        "`pauli_x`: 2x2 Pauli matrix `sigma_x = [[0, 1], [1, 0]]`.",
        "`pauli_y`: 2x2 Pauli matrix `sigma_y = [[0, -i], [i, 0]]`.",
        "`pauli_z`: 2x2 Pauli matrix `sigma_z = [[1, 0], [0, -1]]`.",
        "`pde_classify(pde, u)`: classifies `pde` for unknown `u` by type (elliptic, parabolic, hyperbolic), order, dimension, and linearity.",
        "`pde_complete_integral(pde, u)`: complete integral `u(x, y) = ...` of a first-order PDE via Charpit's method.",
        "`pde_crum_kdv(u, psis)`: iterated Crum-Darboux transformation of KdV seed `u` using eigenfunctions `psis`.",
        "`pde_darboux_kdv(u, psi)`: single Darboux-B\u00e4cklund transformation `u - 2 d^2/dx^2 ln(psi)` for KdV.",
        "`pde_greens_ball(radius, dimension)`: Dirichlet Green's function for the negative Laplacian on a disk (`dimension = 2`) or ball (`dimension = 3`).",
        "`pde_greens_ball_neumann(radius, dimension)`: zero-mean Neumann Green's function for the negative Laplacian on a disk (`dimension = 2`) or ball (`dimension = 3`).",
        "`pde_greens_ball_robin(radius, h)`: Green's function for the negative Laplacian inside a three-dimensional ball with Robin coefficient `h`.",
        "`pde_greens_exterior_robin(radius, h, dimension)`: Green's function for the negative Laplacian outside a disk (`dimension = 2`) or ball (`dimension = 3`) with Robin coefficient `h`.",
        "`pde_greens_function(equation, u(x, \u2026)[, conditions])`: Green's function of a linear constant-coefficient PDE. Homogeneous conditions on a box use eigenfunction series. Supported free-space and orthant cases use fundamental solutions or heat and wave kernels, with reflected sources for homogeneous Dirichlet or Neumann walls. The source coordinate for `x` is `s_x`.",
        "`pde_greens_robin(dimension, h)`: Green's function for the negative Laplacian on a half-space with Robin coefficient `h` (`dimension = 2` or `3`).",
        "`pde_hodograph(sys, vars)`: derives the linear hodograph PDE `(lambda - mu) t_rs + lambda_r t_s - mu_s t_r = 0` for a 2x2 hyperbolic system.",
        "`pde_hodograph_riemann(gamma)`: Riemann function for the Euler-Poisson-Darboux hodograph equation.",
        "`pde_hodograph_solution(F, G, ...)`: hodograph-transformed parametric solution `list(x(r, s), t(r, s))` in Riemann invariants.",
        "`pde_kdv_reflectionless(params, x_t)`: KdV time evolution of the reflectionless potential `-N(N+1) kappa^2 sech^2(kappa x)`.",
        "`pde_kdv_scattering(data, x, t)`: numeric KdV inverse-scattering (Gelfand-Levitan-Marchenko) evaluation at `(x, t)`.",
        "`pde_kdv_soliton(kappas, x_t)`: N-soliton Hirota tau-function solution `-2 d^2/dx^2 ln det(I + A)` of the KdV equation.",
        "`pde_nls_soliton(params)`: bright soliton solution of the focusing nonlinear Schrodinger equation.",
        "`pde_order(pde, u)`: highest partial-derivative order of `u` appearing in `pde`.",
        "`pde_poisson_integral(g, radius, dimension)`: harmonic extension of `g(phi)` on a disk (`dimension = 2`) or `g(theta, phi)` on a ball (`dimension = 3`). The positive `radius` is measured in the same units as `r`.",
        "`pde_riemann_shallow_water(hL, uL, hR, uR, g)`: exact Riemann problem solution `list(h*, u*, left_wave, right_wave)` for 1D shallow-water equations.",
        "`pde_riemann_shallow_water_at(hL, uL, hR, uR, g, xi)`: self-similar shallow-water depth `h(x/t = xi)` from the Riemann solver.",
        "`pde_riemann_shallow_water_velocity(hL, uL, hR, uR, g, xi)`: self-similar shallow-water velocity `u(x/t = xi)` from the Riemann solver.",
        "`pde_separate(pde, u)`: separated-variables ansatz and decoupled ODEs for `pde` and unknown `u`.",
        "`pde_similarity(pde, u, generator)`: similarity reduction `list(u = g F(z), ODE)` along a scaling/symmetry `generator`.",
        "`pde_sine_gordon_soliton(params, x_t)`: kink/antikink or breather soliton solution of the sine-Gordon equation.",
        "`pde_symmetries(pde, u)`: Lie point symmetry infinitesimal generators of `pde` for unknown `u`.",
        "`pde_traveling_wave(pde, u, c)`: traveling-wave ansatz `u = F(x - c t)` and reduced ODE for speed `c`.",
        "`pdf(d, x)`: probability density function (or probability mass function) of distribution `d` at point `x`.",
        "`pdsolve(pde, u, ...)`: general symbolic/numeric PDE solver for unknown `u` with optional boundary/initial conditions. For homogeneous heat or undamped wave equations on disks and balls, the numerical modal fallback accepts general initial data with homogeneous Neumann boundaries.",
        "`pell(D)`: fundamental solution `list(x, y)` to Pell's equation `x^2 - D*y^2 = 1` for non-square `D > 0`.",
